## Misadventures in Trading (Schwager's Personal Trading Account)

<!-- page 14: 4 nuggets -->
<!-- page 15: 3 nuggets -->
<!-- page 16: 3 nuggets -->
<!-- page 17: 4 nuggets -->
<!-- page 18: 3 nuggets -->
<!-- page 19: 3 nuggets -->
<!-- page 20: 3 nuggets -->

*A candid autobiographical chapter in which Schwager describes his own trading
mistakes between the two Market Wizards books. Among the most honest
self-examination in trading literature.*

### Rule: Don't Trade with Money You Can't Afford to Lose

- [MUST KNOW][N013] **"Scared money" virtually guarantees failure** — If your
  trading capital is too important to lose, you will make a cascade of
  predictable errors:
  - Miss the best opportunities (often the most risky ones)
  - Exit perfectly good positions prematurely at the first sign of adversity,
    then watch the market go exactly where you expected
  - Take profits too quickly out of fear the market will take them back
  - Paradoxically, STAY in losing trades because fear triggers indecisiveness
    (like a deer frozen in headlights)
  - The emotional interference destroys the decision-making process entirely

- Schwager's own case: he liquidated his commodity account to help pay for a
  house (specifically, an indoor swimming pool). He intended to wait until he
  had adequate risk capital before trading again — a sound principle he then
  violated under pressure.

### The Mechanism of "Scared Money" Failure

- Trading requires energy. Working a demanding full-time job while writing a
  book drained Schwager's reserves. He explicitly did not want to trade.
  This was a legitimate reason to stay out.

- [MUST KNOW][N014] **Waiting for the truly compelling trade is an accidental
  source of edge** — By not wanting to trade, Schwager inadvertently practiced
  extreme selectivity. He waited until he saw the British pound setup that was
  so compelling he couldn't stand not taking it. This forced patience
  dramatically improved the odds. **James Rogers** articulated this principle
  directly (referenced from Market Wizards):
  > "I just wait until there is money lying in the corner, and all I have to
  > do is go over there and pick it up. I do nothing in the meantime."
  — James Rogers

### The British Pound Setup

- Schwager identified a specific chart pattern: a sharp decline over two weeks
  (straight down, no technical rebound), followed by a narrow sideways
  consolidation. He had strong conviction this preceded another leg down.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N015] **Post-sharp-break sideways consolidation is a bearish
  pattern** — Schwager's analysis: traders who were long realize they were
  wrong and plan to exit on the first rebound. Traders who missed going short
  also plan to sell on the first rally. Since everyone is waiting to sell the
  first rally, the rally never comes. The market just keeps falling. This is
  a behavioral/structural explanation for a chart pattern.

- Schwager procrastinated and missed the close. Ten minutes left on the clock.
  He didn't act. Market closed. He placed an overnight short order anyway,
  feeling he'd made a mistake by missing it. Pound opened down 200+ points.

### The Harvey Trade: A Cascade of Errors

- Schwager's account grew from $0 (with a $4,000 initial deposit quickly
  withdrawn once profits covered margin) to over $25,000 through disciplined
  trading. Then, while traveling on a business trip making hasty decisions
  between meetings, he lost about one-third of his gains in ~one week.

- [MUST KNOW][N016] **Taking tips/following another trader's analysis
  initiates a cascade of trading sins**. Schwager's sequence:
  1. Greed (wanting to recoup losses the easy way via someone else's idea)
  2. Violated his own belief that other people's opinions are dangerous
  3. Had no exit criteria of his own (no basis for deciding when to get out)
  4. Ignored clear market signals that screamed to exit (gap against him,
     contrarian response to news — when bearish news causes an UP move,
     that's a red flag)
  5. Surrendered risk control to a third party (Harvey), leaving himself
     with no risk management mechanism whatsoever
  - "The market is a stern enforcer that unmercifully and unfailingly extracts
    harsh fines for all (trading) transgressions."

- **Harvey** (pseudonym): a practitioner of **Elliott Wave analysis** —
  "a complex theory that attempts to explain all market behavior as part of a
  grand structure of price waves." Harvey called the British pound a "fifth
  of a fifth" (an Elliott Wave terminal structure) and predicted a gap lower
  on Monday.

- [MUST KNOW][N017] **If you enter a trade on someone else's analysis, you
  have no exit mechanism** — Schwager explicitly stated this to Harvey before
  taking the trade: "If I get in on your opinion, I'll have no basis for
  deciding when to get out of the trade." He proceeded anyway. The result:
  when the market gapped 220 points AGAINST him, his own rule said to exit
  immediately (never hold a position that gaps sharply against you right
  after entry), but since it was "Harvey's trade," he deferred. This
  asymmetry — entering on someone else's logic, exiting on your own logic —
  is structurally impossible to execute correctly.

- The market continued to edge higher for weeks. Harvey kept saying "we're
  very close to the top." An event that hadn't yet occurred (the reversal)
  provoked enthusiasm from Harvey — which Schwager correctly read as ominous.
  The market was still going UP seven months later when Schwager finally
  got out at a loss.

### Market Clues Schwager Ignored

- [MUST KNOW][N018] **When a market moves opposite to its expected response
  to news, that is a major warning signal** — On a Friday, negative economic
  news for the British pound came out. The pound traded briefly lower in the
  morning, then reversed and closed UP for the day. This "contrarian response
  to news" should have triggered an exit. Schwager recognized it, called
  Harvey, and stayed short anyway.

- Schwager's own rule: "Never hold a position that gaps sharply against you
  right after you have put it on." The pound opened 220 points higher the
  Monday after he went short. He violated his own rule because he was
  deferring to Harvey.

### Ed Seykota's Insight

- [MUST KNOW][N019] **"Everybody gets what they want out of the market."**
  — **Ed Seykota's** most striking comment from the Market Wizards interview.
  Schwager wanted NOT to be trading (he had no desire to trade, only
  inadequate capital and a full life). Sure enough, he ended up not trading —
  because the market took his money back. His unconscious desire shaped
  the outcome.

- Ed Seykota demonstrated **magazine cover contrarianism** as a presentation
  at a seminar: matching financial magazine cover dates to chart turning points.
  Examples:
  - "Are Interest Rates Going to 20%?" cover → coincided with the bottom
    of the bond market (early 1980s)
  - Withering farm fields cover → coincided with the 1988 grain market
    peak (drought top)
  - "How High Can Oil Prices Go?" cover → top of oil market following
    the Iraqi invasion of Kuwait; Seykota said "we've probably seen the top"
    and was right
  - [SHOULD KNOW][N020] Seykota's punchline: "Now you understand how to get
    all the important information about impending market trends from news and
    financial magazines. Just read the covers and forget about the articles
    inside." — Magazine covers as contrarian timing indicators (sentiment
    at extremes).

### The Core Lessons Distilled

- [MUST KNOW][N021] **You don't always have to be in the market** — Don't trade
  if you don't feel like it, or if it doesn't feel right for whatever reason.
  To win at markets you need BOTH confidence AND the desire to trade. Most
  exceptional traders have both most of the time. For ordinary traders, they
  may come together only occasionally. Trading when you have only one of the
  two is a setup for loss.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N022] **Trading sins cascade** — One violation of your rules
  (greed, following a tip) immediately creates conditions for additional
  violations (no exit mechanism, ignoring signals, surrendering risk control).
  The first error is rarely the expensive one; it's the errors that follow
  FROM the first one. The chain: greed → violated beliefs → no exit plan →
  ignored signals → no risk control → loss.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N023] **Markets do whatever confounds the most traders** —
  In the sideways-after-sharp-break scenario, both longs and shorts are
  waiting to sell rallies. With everyone waiting for the same thing, it
  doesn't happen. This crowd-confounding behavior explains why setups that
  "feel obvious" often fail, and why the most uncomfortable trade is often
  the right one.

- [NICE TO KNOW][N024] Schwager's account went from $0 to $25,000+ through
  disciplined patience, then back toward breakeven through a single British
  pound "Harvey trade." He ultimately quit trading at near breakeven — "a
  quick ride up and down, with little to show for it except some market
  experience." The lesson: sustained discipline is required throughout,
  not just to build an account but to keep it.

<!-- pp. 19-40 -->

## Prologue Conclusion: Schwager's Personal Trading Episode (cont.)

<!-- page 19: 3 nuggets -->

### Ed Seykota — Magazine Covers as Contrarian Indicators

- [MUST KNOW][N001] **Ed Seykota** demonstrated that financial magazine covers are near-perfect
  **contrarian indicators** — the cover story marks the exhaustion of a trend, not its continuation
  - Cover "Are Interest Rates Going to 20%?" appeared at the **bottom of the bond market** in early 1980s
  - Farm-field drought photo → coincided with the **price peak of grain markets** during the 1988 drought
  - "How High Can Oil Prices Go?" → published at the top of oil prices following the Iraqi invasion of Kuwait
  - Seykota's line: **"Now you understand how to get all the important information about impending
    market trends from news and financial magazines. Just read the covers and forget the articles inside."**
  - Mechanism: by the time a trend is dramatic enough to warrant a magazine cover, it has already been
    fully discounted by the market — mass consensus = exhaustion point, not validation

> "Now you understand how to get all the important information about impending market trends from news
> and financial magazines. Just read the covers and forget the articles inside."
> — *Ed Seykota*

### Schwager's Temptation to Cash Out at $20K Profit

- [SHOULD KNOW][N002] Schwager was up ~$20,000 — exactly the price of a car he needed. He was tempted
  to close the account and buy the car (immediate tangible reward vs. trading goal ambiguity)
- He didn't, continued trading, gave it back. Hindsight: cashing in would have been correct
- Lesson: **treating trading profits as real money** (using them for life purchases) is a valid discipline
  — the inability to "realize" gains by spending them is a form of psychological trap

<!-- page 20: 3 nuggets -->

### "Everybody Gets What They Want Out of the Market"

- [MUST KNOW][N003] **Ed Seykota's most famous insight**: "Everybody gets what they want out of the market."
  - Schwager SAID he wanted to trade successfully but his behavior revealed he wanted NOT to trade
  - He couldn't stop himself while winning; the only thing that ended it was losing — which was
    unconsciously what he wanted (a way out)
  - Seykota: "In other words, the only way you could stop trading was by losing. Is that right?"
  - Deeper implication: **traders must audit the hidden psychological agenda behind their behavior** —
    people who keep losing may be satisfying a need to lose; people who always exit early may be
    satisfying a need to avoid large commitment; unconscious goals override conscious intentions

> "Everybody gets what they want out of the market."
> — *Ed Seykota (as recounted by Schwager)*

### Confidence + Desire: Both Required to Trade Well

- [MUST KNOW][N004] Schwager's key conclusion: to win in markets you need **both confidence AND the
  desire to trade** — exceptional traders have both most of the time; for the rest of us, they
  come together only occasionally
  - Schwager's failure: started with confidence (had turned $2K→$100K before) but without genuine desire
  - Result: ended up with neither — gave back the gains AND lost the desire to trade
  - Practical implication: **don't trade if you don't feel like it or if trading doesn't feel right**
    — being in the market requires active psychological consent, not just technical permission

<!-- page 21: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N005] Brief additional signal: Schwager planned that "next time I start trading, I plan
  to have both" — signals that this prologue is autobiographical as a cautionary tale, not just context
- *Page is mostly conclusion of chapter, low density*

