## Essay: Hussein Makes a Bad Trade

<!-- page 22: 3 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N006] Schwager uses **Saddam Hussein's 1990 invasion of Kuwait** as a case study in
  the psychology of a losing trade — the decisions mirror a foundering novice trader at every step
- **Initial trade thesis (Kuwait invasion)** — Hussein had solid fundamentals:
  - Eliminate Kuwait (which consistently exceeded its OPEC quota) → drive up oil prices
  - Annex Kuwait's oil fields — permanent strategic gain
  - Direct access to the Persian Gulf
  - Feed megalomaniacal ambitions
  - **Initial risk seemed limited**: U.S. State Dept. initial response was essentially "not our problem"
    — practically inviting the invasion
- [SHOULD KNOW][N007] The **"market changed" moment**: President Bush committed U.S. forces to defend
  Saudi Arabia and pushed UN resolutions — at this point Hussein could have negotiated a face-saving
  deal (withdraw in exchange for disputed territorial gains and port rights) — a "quick profit"
  - He chose to **stand pat** — classic trader error: holding when the setup has changed

<!-- page 23: 3 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N008] Progressive stages of a losing trade — each mirrors a trader's psychological
  escalation of denial:
  1. **Signal 1 (Bush doubles forces to 400K)**: market clearly changed, Hussein ignored it and stood back
  2. **Signal 2 (Jan 15 deadline)**: profit potential gone, breakeven now available — Hussein held anyway
  3. **Post-deadline bombing**: position clearly losing, market moving down sharply every day
     - Rationalization: "if I hold on long enough, U.S. casualty fear will prompt them to back down"
       — the **long-shot hope** of a desperate trader
  4. **Ground war ultimatum**: Hussein now accepting Soviet peace proposal terms that would have been
     sufficient earlier but were now inadequate — classic "I'll get out at the last relative high"
     with the relative high moving steadily lower

> Hussein's behavior was very much like that of a trader holding a long position in a steadily declining
> market who says, "I'll get out when I'm even," and then, as the situation grows more desperate,
> "I'll get out at the last relative high," with the relative high moving steadily down with the
> passage of time. — *Schwager*

<!-- page 24: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N009] **The "mother of all losses" moral**: Hussein finally capitulated when his army was
  largely decimated — like a trader who finally screams "Get me out at any price!"
  - Moral explicitly stated by Schwager: **"If you can't take a small loss, sooner or later you will
    take the mother of all losses."**
  - The inability to accept small losses is the core pathology — not stupidity, not bad analysis.
    Hussein's initial analysis was reasonable. His sin was **refusing to exit when the environment changed.**

> "If you can't take a small loss, sooner or later you will take the mother of all losses."
> — *Schwager*

- [SHOULD KNOW][N010] The Hussein chapter makes an important structural point: **good decision-making
  in trading and in life share the same elements** — the failure modes are identical. Holding a losing
  geopolitical position and holding a losing trading position involve the exact same cognitive distortions:
  escalation of commitment, denial of disconfirming signals, hope replacing analysis

