## Chapter: Bill Lipschutz — The Sultan of Currencies

<!-- page 26: 3 nuggets -->

### The Foreign Exchange Market — Scale and Structure

- [MUST KNOW][N011] **The forex (FX) market is the world's largest financial market** — larger than
  all equity markets combined, larger than all fixed-income markets combined
  - Estimated volume (at time of writing): **$1 trillion traded EACH DAY**
  - Stocks and bonds are "peanuts" compared to currencies in absolute trading volume
  - Primary venue: the **interbank currency market** — NOT an organized exchange
  - Structure: a 24-hour, decentralized OTC market that literally follows the sun:
    U.S. → Australia → Far East → Europe → back to U.S.
  - Primary function: hedge exchange risk for multinational corporations
  - Speculators also participate, trading expectations about exchange rate shifts

- [SHOULD KNOW][N012] Only a **handful of high-stakes players** operate in the interbank FX market —
  they sometimes take positions measured in billions of dollars yet are virtually unknown to the public
  or even the broader financial community
  - **Bill Lipschutz** was Salomon Brothers' largest and most successful currency trader for 8 years
  - Maintained **near-total public anonymity** deliberately throughout his Salomon career
  - After leaving Salomon, started **Rowayton Capital Management** (initially a subsidiary of Merrill Lynch)

### Lipschutz's Physical Setup — Total Market Immersion

- [NICE TO KNOW][N013] Lipschutz had market monitor screens in every room including the bathroom
  (standing height, "somewhat tongue in cheek") — he rolls over in his sleep to check quotes
  because some of the most active FX periods occur during U.S. nighttime hours
  - Illustrates the 24-hour nature of currency markets — there is no clean "off" time for FX traders

<!-- page 27: 2 nuggets -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N014] Salomon Brothers' FX department grew from zero presence to potentially the
  world's largest currency market player in less than a decade — Lipschutz describes it as a unique,
  synergistic growth story (himself, the department, and the currency options market all started
  simultaneously and grew together)

- [SHOULD KNOW][N015] Lipschutz insisted on an informal "feel right" meeting before agreeing to be
  interviewed — met Schwager at a Soho bar, shared French beers, then consented
  - Illustrates his personal operating style: **relationships and chemistry precede business**

<!-- page 28: 2 nuggets -->

### Lipschutz in Action During Interview — Limit Order Discipline

- [SHOULD KNOW][N016] During the interview, the Australian dollar was in free-fall after negative
  comments from the Australian finance minister. Lipschutz felt the sell-off was overdone and tried
  to buy $20M AUD — but entered at a **limit price just below market**. Market never traded lower;
  he missed the fill. AUD subsequently rallied the rest of the evening.
  - His comment: **"Missing an opportunity is as bad as being on the wrong side of a trade."**
  - Shows the cost of limit order discipline when conviction is high — there are times a market order
    is the correct execution strategy
  - Second episode: wanted to short Deutsche mark, waited for a bounce to sell into, market went lower
    without bouncing. When asked why he didn't just sell at market, he said: **"What! And pay the
    bid/ask spread?"** — possibly half-joking (DM kept going lower)
  - These two anecdotes show Lipschutz's extreme price-consciousness but also the cost of optimizing
    execution over trade capture

<!-- page 29: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N017] Despite obsession with markets and trading, Lipschutz appeared relaxed during
  interviews — "I wouldn't even have known he was watching the markets had he not occasionally made
  references to price movements and placed orders over the phone"
  - Illustrates that great traders can hold positions and monitor markets **without visible anxiety** —
    equanimity as a professional hallmark

<!-- page 30: 2 nuggets -->

### Lipschutz Background — Cornell, Grandmother's Portfolio, Self-Education

- [SHOULD KNOW][N018] **Bill Lipschutz** origin story:
  - Enrolled in Cornell's **architecture** program
  - Grandmother died, left him a portfolio of **100 different stocks worth $12,000** — liquidated at
    great cost because all positions were odd lots
  - Used proceeds as trading capital; found himself spending more and more time in the stock market
  - Architecture: "an Old World profession" — 3-year apprenticeship, years as draftsman before
    having design control. Too slow a path for someone who wanted immediate agency
  - Trading literally "took over his life" — no conscious decision, just gradual absorption

- [NICE TO KNOW][N019] Lipschutz's early self-education: read everything available — annual company
  reports in the library, *The Economist*, *Barron's*, *Value Line*
  - Also watched cable TV stock tape in Ithaca, NY (one of the first cable-TV markets in the U.S.
    due to poor antenna reception from mountains) — spent hours watching and "developed a feel for
    price action"
  - Tape-reading as intuition-building: learned price action BEFORE formal theory

<!-- page 31: 1 nugget -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N020] Lipschutz got **two degrees from Cornell** — architecture (5-year program)
  plus MBA (took business courses concurrently). Never practiced architecture; went directly to Salomon.
  Architecture background = systems thinking, spatial reasoning, structural integrity — possibly
  relevant to how he conceptualizes currency positions and global macro frameworks

<!-- page 32: 1 nugget -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N021] Lipschutz's Salomon connection came through his wife — she had worked for
  **Dr. Henry Kaufman** (world-renowned economist) in Salomon's bond research department the previous
  summer. Through her contacts, Lipschutz got interviews with both Kaufman AND Salomon's trading desk.
  He ended up splitting the summer between both.

<!-- page 33: 2 nuggets -->

### The Sidney Gold Interview — Being Hired as a Trader

- [SHOULD KNOW][N022] **Sidney Gold** (head of Salomon's proprietary equity options desk) interview —
  illustrates how experienced traders evaluate candidates:
  - Gold fired rapid questions while simultaneously watching the tape through a glass wall
  - Cut through formalities after 10 minutes: "OK, forget all this bullshit... You said you're
    trading your own account. What stocks are you trading?"
  - Rejected first two stocks (Exxon, 3M) — "I don't know that stock"
  - Third: U.S. Steel — Gold knew it, asked where it was trading, where it broke out from, where
    it broke out from **three years ago**
  - Lipschutz answered correctly (broke out from ~18, three years prior) → Gold hired him on the spot
  - [SHOULD KNOW][N023] Key insight: **Gold hired based on a trader's actual market knowledge and
    memory depth** — not resume, not credentials, not conceptual answers. The test was "do you know
    this specific chart, this specific level, this specific history?" — technical market memory as
    the hiring screen

<!-- page 34: 1 nugget -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N024] Lipschutz ended up wanted by two departments simultaneously (Kaufman's research
  and Gold's trading desk) — Salomon arranged a split. He worked the first half of summer for
  Dr. Kaufman (bond research) and second half on the options trading desk. Gold then offered him
  a full-time job before his final semester.

<!-- page 35: 2 nuggets -->

### Salomon Equity Options Desk — The Non-Quantitative Era

- [SHOULD KNOW][N025] Salomon's equity options desk in the early 1980s was **highly non-quantitative**
  — "I don't believe anybody there even knew what the Black-Scholes model was"
  - Sidney Gold's approach: "I went to buy a car this weekend and the Chevrolet showroom was packed.
    Let's buy GM calls." — pure sentiment/intuition, no quantitative framework
  - A trader's advice to Lipschutz: "Let me tell you everything you need to know about options.
    You like 'em, you buy calls. You don't like 'em, you buy puts."
  - [SHOULD KNOW][N026] They were **trading options as leveraged outright directional positions** —
    not using options for their volatility or hedging properties at all
  - This actually fit Lipschutz's tape-reading background: he was good at directional calls and
    used options as a leverage vehicle, which is the only use case the desk understood

<!-- page 36: 2 nuggets -->

### Salomon Culture — The Training Program and Institutionalized Identity

- [MUST KNOW][N027] **Salomon Brothers' culture** was the defining feature of the firm — Lipschutz
  describes it as more important than any technical skill learned there:
  - Training course: all the big names at Salomon came in, told their story, "delivered their persona"
  - Result: trainees were **indoctrinated into Salomon culture** — the values, risk appetite, and
    street-fighter ethos were transmitted person-to-person
  - Culture: "street fighters who were betting their own money and who really understood what it
    meant to take risk. It was all about personalities, guts, insight, and honesty and integrity."
  - [SHOULD KNOW][N028] Culture **degraded in the late 1980s**: programs scaled from 120 people/year
    to two programs of 250 people each. More standardized hires (less willingness to take "wild-card
    candidates"). The personal transmission of culture broke down at scale — a cautionary tale about
    institutional scaling diluting the qualities that made an organization great

<!-- page 37: 2 nuggets -->

### John Gutfreund — Sensing Risk Through People

- [SHOULD KNOW][N029] **John Gutfreund** (chairman of Salomon Brothers) was described as having
  an almost supernatural ability to read trader psychology:
  - Sat on the trading floor every day — the largest trading floor in the world at the time
  - "We always said that John could smell death at a hundred paces. He didn't need to know what
    your position was to know what your position was, or how it was going. He could tell the state
    of your equity by the amount of anxiety he saw in your face."
  - Illustrates: **experienced risk managers can read emotional states to assess portfolio health**
    before looking at the numbers — anxiety is a leading indicator of position distress

- [NICE TO KNOW][N030] The "Belgian franc" anecdote — Gutfreund tested a young Lipschutz by asking
  "where did the franc close?" (expecting Swiss or French); Lipschutz asked "which one?"; Gutfreund
  said "Both"; one year later, same scenario, same exchange — Gutfreund's deadpan answer was "Belgian."
  - Point: Gutfreund deliberately cultivated memorable interactions with junior people — he was aware
    of his mentoring effect and used it intentionally
  - Salomon had **virtually no turnover of key personnel** — a sign of deep cultural loyalty

- [SHOULD KNOW][N031] Salomon's risk philosophy: "It wasn't that the company was a risk-seeking firm,
  but it was certainly a firm that was **comfortable with risk or with losing money, as long as the
  trade idea made sense**." — distinguishes between recklessness and intellectual comfort with
  calculated loss

<!-- page 38: 2 nuggets -->

### How Lipschutz Ended Up in FX — Salomon's Homegrown Department

- [SHOULD KNOW][N032] Lipschutz ended up in currencies essentially by accident — he wanted equities
  but was told he was "too quantitative" for the equity desk. The Salomon FX department was being
  formed simultaneously; he was recruited into it.
  - The currency department's founding: a few senior people decided one day to start a foreign
    exchange department. **No business plan. No outside hires with experience.** Just: "Who can run
    it? How about Gil? Okay, Gil, do you want to run it? Sure."
  - **Gil** (from bond arbitrage) had zero currency experience. His idea: hire bright people, let
    them figure it out together, and see if they could make money.
  - [SHOULD KNOW][N033] Lipschutz didn't know what a Deutsche mark was when he joined.
    No one in the department really knew currencies — except one junior person with bank experience.
    "That's not how Salomon did things. At Salomon, everything was homegrown."

<!-- page 39: 3 nuggets -->

### Early FX Advantage — Currency Options at the Philadelphia Stock Exchange

- [SHOULD KNOW][N034] **Competitive moat via unique cross-domain knowledge**: When the
  **Philadelphia Stock Exchange** introduced currency option contracts simultaneously with Salomon
  forming its FX department, Lipschutz had a massive edge:
  - He was the **only person on the Salomon FX desk who knew what a put or call was**
  - He was the **only person with equities background** — everyone else came from fixed income
  - He knew specialists and market makers on the Philadelphia floor — no one else in the department
    had ever even been to the equities floor
  - Gil's conclusion: "You're the only one in the department who knows anything about this, so just do it."
  - The situation was "tailor-made" for him — domain cross-pollination created a temporary monopoly

- [SHOULD KNOW][N035] Structural insight on career positioning: Lipschutz grew as the currency
  options market grew, as the Salomon FX department grew — **three things starting from zero together**,
  compounding simultaneously. He describes this as a "unique, synergistic type of experience."
  - Implication: entering a new market or domain at its inception — even without expertise — can
    be more valuable than entering an established market with credentials, because you grow with
    the domain

- [NICE TO KNOW][N036] The **Hambros book** — the directory of all international FX dealers — was
  the primary way to find counterparties. Lipschutz was so shy that he agonized for 10 minutes
  over how to call someone he didn't know at Morgan Guaranty to place a D-mark transaction.
  Irony: he would later become the world's largest currency trader.

<!-- page 40: 3 nuggets -->

### FX Success Without Experience — Relationships as the Moat

- [MUST KNOW][N037] **Foreign exchange is fundamentally a relationship business**, not an information
  or analysis business:
  - "Your ability to find good liquidity, your ability to be plugged into the information flow — it
    all depends on relationships."
  - Anonymous call: "The mark dealer is in the bathroom; call back later."
  - Known call ("Hey, Joe, it's Bill"): "I was just on my way out the door, but for you I'll see
    what I can do."
  - The same $10M order gets radically different treatment based on relationship — this is the
    **execution alpha** in interbank FX that retail/exchange-based traders cannot replicate

- [SHOULD KNOW][N038] How Lipschutz built relationships without experience — **three factors**:
  1. **Options expertise as perceived genius**: Currency market veterans didn't know options. Lipschutz's
     ability to "derive the Black-Scholes model" made him seem like a genius — even though he admits
     "Hell, I didn't know that much about it." Perception of unique knowledge opens doors.
  2. **Salomon Brothers mystique**: "We don't know what they do, but they make a lot of money." Being
     associated with a mysterious, profitable institution is itself a relationship asset.
  3. **Not acting like an investment banker**: Fixed-income types were seen as "prissy" in FX circles —
     suspenders and Hermès ties, "white-wine-and-arugula-salad" types. Lipschutz's equities
     background made him more approachable and culturally compatible with FX traders' style.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N039] **Perception gap as competitive advantage**: Lipschutz leveraged the fact that
  FX market participants had little knowledge of options while he had some knowledge — the gap between
  his perceived expertise and their baseline was enormous, even if his absolute expertise wasn't
  extraordinary. **Relative knowledge advantage matters more than absolute expertise** in early markets
  or new product introductions.

<!-- pp. 39-60 -->

## Chapter: Bill Lipschutz Interview (continued) — FX Trading at Salomon Brothers

### Unique Cross-Market Advantage: Equities Background in a Fixed-Income World

<!-- page 039: 3 nuggets -->

- **Salomon Brothers FX desk** formed at almost exactly the same time the **Philadelphia Stock Exchange** introduced currency options — Lipschutz and the product grew up together [MUST KNOW][N040]
  - He was the only person on the desk who knew what a put or call was
  - Everyone else came from fixed-income (42nd floor); he came from equities (41st floor)
  - He also knew specialists and market makers on the Philadelphia Exchange floor — no one else in FX even knew what a specialist was
  - Boss Gil's instruction: "You're the only one in the department who knows anything about this, so just do it."
- **Convergence of advantages**: the unique combination of being at Salomon, options literacy, and exchange knowledge was tailor-made — not planned, but recognized and seized [SHOULD KNOW][N041]
- **Key insight on competitive advantage**: you don't need absolute expertise — you need relative expertise at the right moment. Lipschutz didn't know options deeply; he just knew more than anyone else in that specific market [MUST KNOW][N042]

> "The key point I am trying to make is that Salomon's foreign exchange department, Bill Lipschutz as foreign exchange trader, and currency options all started at the same time, and we grew together. It was a unique, synergistic type of experience." — Lipschutz

---

### FX as a Relationship Business (Deeper Detail)

<!-- page 040: 2 nuggets -->

- **Liquidity access is relationship-dependent**: anonymous callers get brushed off ("the mark dealer is in the bathroom; call back later"); known callers get pulled back from the door [MUST KNOW][N043]

> "If I call up at 5 P.M. and say, 'Hey, Joe, it's Bill, and I need a price on the mark,' the response is going to be entirely different: 'I was just on my way out the door, but for you I'll see what I can do.'" — Lipschutz

- **Three pillars of Lipschutz's contact-building as a newcomer** [MUST KNOW][N044]:
  1. Options expertise — perceived as a genius because he could derive Black-Scholes when no one in FX could; reality: nobody else knew it either, so the perception stuck
  2. Salomon mystique — "We don't know what they do, but they make a lot of money"
  3. Cultural fit — he was not a "white-wine-and-arugula-salad type" like fixed-income investment bankers; he matched the rougher, more direct culture of FX traders ("go-out-for-pasta-and-dribble-marinara-sauce-all-over-yourself type")

---

### 24-Hour Commitment as Differentiator

<!-- page 041: 2 nuggets -->

- **First person at Salomon to have a Telerate terminal at home** — management thought it was insane; his logic: "Foreign exchange is a twenty-four-hour market. It doesn't go to sleep when you leave at 5 P.M. The market is really there all night, and it moves!" [SHOULD KNOW][N045]
  - Implication: willingness to be "on" around the clock was a genuine edge when competitors treated it as a 9-to-5 job
- **Information network superiority via interbank acceptance**: traders who stayed aloof from the interbank circle couldn't make money — they ended up calling CME floor clerks for information, while Lipschutz was talking to bankers in Tokyo, London, Frankfurt, and New York simultaneously [MUST KNOW][N046]

> "Those of us who did well were generally the ones who were accepted by the interbank circle. The traders who stayed aloof tended to be the ones who couldn't make any money trading foreign exchange." — Lipschutz

---

### What the Market Is Focusing On: The Regime-Switching Insight

<!-- page 042: 2 nuggets -->

- **Berlin Wall trade as case study in market narrative shifting** [MUST KNOW][N047]:
  - Initial consensus: wall comes down → capital floods into East Germany → Deutsche mark benefits
  - Reality shift: absorption of East Germany would take far longer than expected; East Germans demanded West German welfare state standards without West German work ethic
  - Result: investment community reassessed, capital moved OUT of the mark
  - Lipschutz's edge: being plugged in early to the shift in thinking, not having the contrarian view at inception

- **The market's current narrative > the objectively correct view** — Lipschutz explicitly: "What is important is to assess what the market is focusing on at the given moment." Even if you could have made the same bearish argument on Day 1, it didn't matter because the market wasn't focused on it yet [MUST KNOW][N048]
  - Market switches focus: one day it cares about interest rate differentials (money flows to highest yields); the next it cares about capital appreciation (money flows to strongest economic/political outlook — often the lowest-rate countries)
  - These two frameworks can point in opposite directions; knowing which lens the market is using IS the trade

---

### Early Trade: Philadelphia Exchange Currency Options Arbitrage

<!-- page 043: 2 nuggets -->

<!-- page 044: 2 nuggets -->

- **1983 Philadelphia Exchange arbitrage trade** — a specialist was quoting a deep-in-the-money currency option 100 points off (mispriced); Lipschutz bought 50, immediately sold the underlying, locked in risk-free profit [SHOULD KNOW][N049]
  - Kept buying in 50-contract lots; Goldman Sachs joined; specialist eventually sold 300-400 contracts
  - Specialist had done his arithmetic wrong — thought he had a locked-in arbitrage profit but had it backwards
  - Lipschutz offered to do 1,000 more to test; specialist backed off but was still off by ~100 points
  - Lipschutz then told the specialist directly: "You're off by a big figure on your price"
  - Specialist realized his error and went silent

- **Long-term business decision: Lipschutz broke all trades after the first 50** [MUST KNOW][N050]
  - Rationale: holding him to the full position would have put the specialist out of business, which would have been bad for the Philadelphia Exchange currency options market — which Lipschutz needed to operate
  - This was NOT charity; it was self-interest: protecting his marketplace
  - Contrast: Goldman Sachs refused to break any of their 150 contracts → years later, after the specialist company failed and that individual became head trader of the largest market maker, he made Goldman's life difficult on the floor
  - **Lesson**: short-term maximization of a windfall can destroy the long-term relationship infrastructure you depend on

> "My action of breaking the trades represented a long-term business decision, which I didn't think about a lot at the time, but which I agonized over for years afterward." — Lipschutz

---

### The Hard-Ass Philosophy and Zero Back-Office Errors

<!-- page 045: 2 nuggets -->

<!-- page 046: 2 nuggets -->

- **Lipschutz's reputation: never cut anyone a break on market rules** [SHOULD KNOW][N051]
  - Philosophy: "These are the rules of the game. I don't give any quarter, and I don't expect any quarter."
  - On expired OTC options: traders would call late with excuses; he never accommodated — knew Salomon would never get a break if roles were reversed
  - When setting up management company with Merrill: budgeted ZERO for back-office errors — "If you put in enough fail-safes, you don't make errors"
  - Making an exception for the Philadelphia specialist was deeply out of character and agonized over precisely because of this identity

- **Interbank market convention on mispriced quotes** [NICE TO KNOW][N052]:
  - Convention: if a dealer quotes a wrong price (e.g., 1.9140 vs. correct 1.9240), the recipient should say "Are you sure? Please check your price"
  - If dealer confirms after the warning, the price stands — no recourse
  - Lipschutz: every time this happened to him, the other institution came back wanting to cancel or split the difference. He always refused — they had been asked to verify

---

### Stop-Gunning in Exchange-Traded Markets

<!-- page 047: 2 nuggets -->

<!-- page 048: 2 nuggets -->

- **Story illustrating stops getting "picked off" in pit markets** [SHOULD KNOW][N053]:
  - A Salomon trader in late 1980s: market in narrow range, stops clustered 40-50 ticks above
  - His clerk tipped him: "Tomorrow they're going to gun for the stops above the market"
  - His plan: sell heavily once stops hit (artificial rally → sell-off to follow)
  - Instead, market moved DOWN 200+ points — floor traders went for the stops BELOW the market, which were 200 points away, not the clustered stops 50 ticks above
  - Reason: everyone was already long (positioned for the upside stop run), so greatest price vulnerability was actually the downside
  - **Lesson**: the direction of maximum pain is not always the direction of the obvious catalyst

- **"Fast market" as a floor broker license to steal** [NICE TO KNOW][N054]:
  - In fast market conditions: prices trade at multiple levels simultaneously, floor brokers cannot be held for failing to fill orders within the day's price range
  - Trader screamed to buy hundreds of contracts at 100-200 points down; was only filled on 50, even though thousands of lots traded at those levels
  - They were looking right past his brokers, who were bidding higher
  - Lipschutz: "A fast market gives floor brokers a special license to steal, above and beyond their normal license to steal."

---

### First Major Trade: The Sterling/Dollar Dual-Currency Bond Mispricing

<!-- page 049: 1 nugget -->

<!-- page 050: 3 nuggets -->

<!-- page 051: 2 nuggets -->

- **The dual-currency bond trade** — Lipschutz's first major career trade [MUST KNOW][N055]:
  - Bond: redeemable in either sterling OR U.S. dollars — issued by Salomon Brothers (as lead underwriter), grossly mispriced by Salomon itself
  - UK interest rates were much lower than US rates → forward sterling traded at a HUGE premium to spot
  - **Covered interest rate parity explained**: if two countries have different interest rates, the forward rate of the lower-rate currency must trade at a premium exactly equal to the interest rate differential — otherwise risk-free arbitrage exists
  - The bond was priced as if the sterling redemption option had NO premium over spot — ignoring the massive forward premium
  - This meant: buy the bond + sell sterling forward at the huge premium → locked-in arbitrage profit as forward converges to spot over the bond's life

- **Trade mechanics** [MUST KNOW][N056]:
  - Bond matured in four tranches: 5, 7, 9, and 12 years; two tranches of $100M and $50M
  - Lipschutz spent 9 hours convincing Salomon's quantitative analysts he was right (initial response: he must be wrong)
  - By the time he got approval, $50M of the first tranche had already been sold
  - Over the next 1-2 years he acquired $135M of the $150M total outstanding in the secondary market, always maintaining a standing bid
  - Immediately upon buying, sold 50% of the total amount in the forward sterling market
  - Spot rate (and bond redemption rate): $1.3470; 7-year forward: ~$1.47; 12-year forward: ~$1.60
  - Selling forward at a huge premium when the call is at spot = the put established at a much higher price than the call = locked-in profit gap

- **Position structure and outcome** [MUST KNOW][N057]:
  - Half the position was effectively a CALL (sterling redemption option in the bond)
  - Half was effectively a PUT (forward sterling sale at premium)
  - Put established at far higher price than call → locked-in profit with additional upside if pound moved below $1.3470 or above $1.47
  - What actually happened: UK interest rates reversed from below to above US rates → forward sterling inverted from premium to DISCOUNT → covered the whole position at a huge profit
  - This is a real-world covered interest rate parity arbitrage, exploiting a pricing error by the issuer

---

### G-7 Plaza Accord Trade (September 1985)

<!-- page 052: 2 nuggets -->

<!-- page 053: 2 nuggets -->

<!-- page 054: 3 nuggets -->

- **G-7 Plaza Accord background** [MUST KNOW][N058]:
  - September 1985: G-7 nations agreed to a coordinated policy to lower the value of the dollar — the first time seven industrialized nations issued a coordinated currency policy statement
  - Initial market reaction: dollar sold off modestly, then actually REBOUNDED in Tokyo after initial sell-offs in New Zealand and Australia
  - Reason: the market interpreted it as "just another central bank intervention" — after years of ineffective CB interventions, no one understood the magnitude of what a coordinated G-7 manifesto meant
  - **Lesson**: paradigm-shift events are initially underestimated by markets conditioned to prior ineffectiveness

- **Lipschutz was in Sardinia on vacation** [SHOULD KNOW][N059]:
  - Had a small short dollar position going in — nothing significant
  - Only learned about the G-7 meeting by calling his London office
  - His assistant Andy Krieger was home sick with flu; neither initially knew what was happening

- **Andy Krieger's New Zealand trade** [MUST KNOW][N060]:
  - Krieger, reading the news from home while sick, went into the New Zealand market — the FIRST currency market to open after the weekend
  - Sold $60M in New Zealand (USD against NZD) on very wide two-big-figure spreads because everyone was confused
  - Possible only because Salomon regularly traded $20-50M in New Zealand on Monday mornings — they had established relationships when very few New Yorkers or Europeans had any
  - Krieger was hitting bids six big figures below Friday's close
  - Lipschutz: "I was really impressed that he had that type of insight. I wouldn't have done that."

- **Six-hour phone line from Sardinia** [SHOULD KNOW][N061]:
  - Lipschutz maintained an open line from his hotel room in Sardinia to Krieger (sick in bed in New Jersey), who had open lines to the Philadelphia Exchange floor AND a third line from the neighbor's house to the spot dollar/mark broker
  - Three simultaneous phone lines, operated by a sick man flat on his back — traded the currency options and cash market for six hours
  - Made $6 million that day — more than 25% of their total annual profits at the time
  - Lesson in infrastructure: relationships and market access (established before the crisis) determined who could act when opportunity struck

---

### The $800 Million Yen Option Poker Game (1987)

<!-- page 055: 3 nuggets -->

<!-- page 056: 3 nuggets -->

<!-- page 057: 3 nuggets -->

- **Position setup** [MUST KNOW][N062]:
  - 1987: Lipschutz put on a huge bull call spread on Japanese yen — long 23,000 contracts of yen 54 calls, short 23,000 contracts of yen 55 calls
  - If calls expired in the money: each side represented nearly $800 million — an enormous position
  - Maximum profit: ~$13 million (realized above 55, equal to 1 full point × 23,000 contracts × $625/point, minus cost of spread)
  - When put on: calls were well out of the money

- **The expiration dilemma** [MUST KNOW][N063]:
  - Risk: if yen closes between 54 and 55, the long 54 call gets exercised but the short 55 call does not → net long ~$800 million over the weekend until Tokyo opens Sunday night
  - Cannot hedge effectively near expiration when market is near 55: hedging the long 54 (assuming above 55) and being wrong = net short $800M; not hedging (assuming below 55) and being wrong = net long $800M
  - "It's the uncertainty about whether the market will close above or below 55 that makes it impossible to effectively hedge the position"
  - One market maker was on the opposite side of ~20,000 of the 23,000 lots — at that size you know exactly who is on the other side

- **The poker game / information warfare** [MUST KNOW][N064]:
  - Expiration day: yen closes within ONE TICK of 55
  - Both sides don't know the other's hedging status until Sunday morning (when exercise notices arrive)
  - Sunday night in Tokyo: whoever is net long and whose counterpart is net short faces a massive position battle in a thin market
  - Friday afternoon: Lipschutz learned the other firm was buying yen in the cash market → they had tipped their hand (they had not hedged their short 54 call, and were NOT going to exercise their long 55 calls)
  - Saturday call from the other firm's trader: he was fishing for information on Lipschutz's hedge status
  - Lipschutz told him they had tipped their hand Friday → trader admitted it was a "committee decision" (not his choice), confirmed they would not exercise
  - Lipschutz had NOT hedged and was net long yen — but the other firm now believed he had figured out their position (true) AND had hedged (false)
  - Weekend news pushed dollar sharply lower against yen → Lipschutz's unhedged long yen position was highly profitable
  - **Final P&L: ~$20 million** — but Lipschutz said the mental chess game was more satisfying than the money

> "The thing that was so great about the trade was not the money but the mental chess game that Friday afternoon — all the back-and-forth bluffing." — Lipschutz

---

### The $3 Billion Short Dollar / Gorbachev Shock (Fall 1988)

<!-- page 058: 3 nuggets -->

<!-- page 059: 2 nuggets -->

<!-- page 060: 1 nugget -->

- **Position buildup in low-volatility environment** [MUST KNOW][N065]:
  - Fall 1988: D-mark in very tight trading range, volatility dried up
  - Typical response in low-vol periods: position size GROWS as traders try to capture smaller moves to produce the same P&L results
  - Lipschutz was short ~$3 billion against the D-mark — larger than normal because of this dynamic
  - **This is a recurring trap**: low volatility → overconfidence → position creep → outsized exposure exactly when a surprise hits

- **Gorbachev speech surprise** [MUST KNOW][N066]:
  - Gorbachev made a UN speech announcing troop reductions
  - Market interpreted it as: US could also cut armed forces → beneficial for budget deficit → bullish for the dollar
  - Dollar started moving up in New York with NO liquidity
  - Very quickly up 1% — on a $3 billion position = $30 million loss in one afternoon
  - Lipschutz: "I knew I was in trouble."

- **Key lesson: position sizing in low-vol markets** [MUST KNOW][N067]:
  - The structural logic of growing positions in tight ranges is sound P&L management in normal conditions
  - But it creates extreme vulnerability to a sudden volatility spike — precisely the scenario you cannot hedge quickly when there is no liquidity
  - The Gorbachev trade illustrates why position size must be stress-tested against black swan scenarios, not just calibrated to recent volatility
  - (Interview continues on next pages — outcome of this trade not yet covered in this batch)

<!-- pp. 59-80 -->

## Chapter: Bill Lipschutz (continued) — The Sultan of Currencies

### The Yen Bluff Trade — Information Warfare

- [MUST KNOW][N040] **Deception as edge in OTC markets**: Lipschutz was net long yen and unhedged
  when a counterparty called Friday afternoon to sniff out his position — if the counterparty
  knew Lipschutz was long and unhedged, they could go into New Zealand (first interbank market
  to open) and push the market against him:
  - Lipschutz told the counterparty that they had "tipped their hand" by selling yen on Friday —
    implying he had figured out *their* position (true) and had hedged his own (false)
  - The counterparty, believing Lipschutz had hedged, called back to say they wouldn't exercise
  - News over the weekend broke in Lipschutz's favor — dollar opened sharply lower vs. yen
  - Result: approximately **$20 million profit** on the trade
  - Lipschutz's own verdict: "The thing that was so great about the trade was not the money but
    the mental chess game that Friday afternoon — all the back-and-forth bluffing."
  - Key mechanism: in interbank FX, positions are often known to key counterparties — **managing
    what your counterparties believe about your position is a source of alpha unavailable in
    anonymous exchange-based markets**

<!-- page 59: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N041] **Market intelligence as theater**: The yen bluff trade created a spectacle —
  people were calling Lipschutz's desk all Friday afternoon to ask what was happening between
  his firm and the counterparty. "These were the biggest positions in the market by a hundredfold
  that day." The visibility of a major player's positioning is itself market-moving information,
  which means that managing one's perceived position is a form of market power.

<!-- page 60: 1 nugget -->

### The Gorbachev Trade — Liquidity Misjudgment

- [MUST KNOW][N042] **Low volatility breeds oversizing — the Gorbachev $3B position (Fall 1988)**:
  - D-mark had been in a very tight 1-2% trading range for a period. In low-volatility periods,
    Lipschutz's desk would grow position size to capture smaller moves and still produce the
    same results — a classic **volatility compression → position bloat** pattern
  - Lipschutz was short approximately **$3 billion** against the D-mark
  - Gorbachev made a UN speech about troop reductions → market interpreted this as meaning the
    US could cut its armed forces commitment → bullish for the US budget deficit → **bullish for
    the dollar**
  - Dollar started moving up in New York; "there was no liquidity. Very quickly, it was up 1
    percent, and I knew that I was in trouble."
  - 1% on $3 billion = **$30 million loss in one afternoon** — actually transpired in **eight minutes**
  - Key lesson: **position size in low-volatility periods creates catastrophic risk when volatility
    returns** — the position that was "right-sized" for a quiet market becomes unmanageable the
    moment a genuine catalyst appears

<!-- page 61: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N043] **The failed attempt to cap the market**:
  - Strategy: sell $300-500M in the afternoon New York market to "take the starch out" of the
    dollar rally and buy time until Tokyo opened at 7 PM (where $3B could actually be executed)
  - Sold $300 million — "the market went right through it"
  - At that point, covering the full position in New York was impossible — trying would just push
    the market further against him
  - **Critical insight**: liquidity in late-afternoon New York is insufficient for positions of this
    size, *especially* on a news day. The liquidity assumption embedded in the position size was
    wrong for exactly the market conditions that would create urgency to exit

- [MUST KNOW][N044] **"Tommy, we have a problem" — the crisis conversation with Tom Strauss**
  (Salomon president):
  - Lipschutz walked over and said simply: "I'm short the dollar and I've misjudged my liquidity
    in the market. I've tried to hold the market down, but it's not going to work. And I can't
    buy them back."
  - Strauss asked calmly, "Where do we stand?" — "We're down somewhere between seventy and ninety
    million."
  - Strauss asked, "What do we want to do about this?" — **Lipschutz was struck that Strauss said
    "we" not "you"**. This was the firm's culture of shared ownership under pressure.
  - Plan: wait for Tokyo opening, cover half the position then, reassess
  - Strauss: "We've had a good run on this. Do what you need to do." — entire conversation took
    two minutes
  - Parallel reaction: Lipschutz's #2 man Robert said later he would have put on his coat and
    walked out — he assumed Lipschutz would be fired. The idea of being fired **never entered
    Lipschutz's mind**. "The first thing I thought about was the position."
  - Institutional culture determines whether losing traders can think clearly or panic-exit:
    **Salomon's culture of no-recrimination-until-resolved enabled superior crisis management**

<!-- page 62: 2 nuggets -->
<!-- page 63: continuation of N044 -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N045] **Resolution — an $18M loss that "seemed like a major victory"**:
  - By the time Tokyo opened, the dollar was moving *down* — so Lipschutz held off on covering
    half the position as planned
  - Dollar continued collapsing; he covered the remaining position in Europe
  - Final loss: **$18 million** (vs. the $70-90M it could have been)
  - The planned "cover half at Tokyo open" exit was correctly abandoned when market moved in his
    favor — **good crisis management is dynamic, not mechanical**

<!-- page 64: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N046] **"I'm a much better trader from a bad position than from a good position"**:
  - Schwager notes that Lipschutz could have dumped the entire position at the Tokyo open and
    been relieved — most traders would have. Lipschutz stayed with market judgment despite the
    emotional pressure to escape.
  - Lipschutz's explanation: "The reason that I didn't get out on the Tokyo opening was that it
    was the wrong trading decision."
  - This is a rare self-insight: **fear from a bad position can actually sharpen decision-making**
    in traders with strong analytical frameworks, because the emotional stakes force total
    concentration on what is actually true about the market

- [MUST KNOW][N047] **What Lipschutz learned from the $90M → $18M near-disaster**:
  1. He had **misjudged liquidity** — not market direction. "You can get [market direction] wrong
     all the time. But I had drastically misjudged the liquidity."
  2. He had let himself into a situation with **no control** — a state he'd never experienced
  3. His fear was not of losing his job or reputation: "It was a fear that I had pushed the
     envelope too far — to a risk level that was unacceptable."
  4. His decision process remained **clear and unclothed** even during crisis — the fear was about
     the *situation* not about *what to do*
  - This distinction is critical: the most dangerous trading crises are those where the trader
    loses clarity about *what to do*. Lipschutz never lost that — he just feared the outcome.

<!-- page 65: 2 nuggets -->

### Personal Account Blowout — The Granville Reversal

- [SHOULD KNOW][N048] **$12,000 → $250,000 → blown out in days**:
  - Lipschutz had built his personal account from ~$12,000 to a peak of ~$250,000 over 4-5 years
    while at Salomon — a strong personal track record
  - **September 23, 1982**: The Dow went from down 30 points to closing up 20 — the famous
    **Granville reversal**, which marked the bottom of the bear market
  - **Joe Granville** (extraordinarily popular market advisor at the time) had just put out a sell
    recommendation. The market's violent reversal against his call became known as the Granville
    reversal.
  - Lipschutz was very bearish and heavily long puts. He kept **pyramiding all the way down**.
    "I lost most of the money that Monday, and by Wednesday the account was virtually all gone."
  - Psychological response: "No, I just saw it as one major mistake. I've always had a lot of
    confidence as a trader. The money never had a major effect on me."
  - Key lesson: **pyramiding into a losing position** is the mechanism by which a bad trade becomes
    a blowout. The Granville case was a bear market bottom — the worst possible scenario for an
    increasingly large short position.

<!-- page 66: 1 nugget -->
<!-- page 67: continuation of N048 -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N049] **Decision after blowout — never trade own account again**:
  - Lipschutz decided: all attention should go to doing Salomon work well, not splitting focus
    between firm trading and personal account
  - "I never again traded my own account — not because I had lost money but because I didn't
    want to split my focus, as I saw some other people do over the years."
  - Put all savings in a **government-securities-only money market account** for maximum security
  - The blowout catalyzed increased **risk-control orientation**, though he was "never particularly
    risk averse"
  - Implication: elite performance in trading requires **undivided attention**; even successful
    personal-account side trading is a distraction that reduces edge in the primary activity

<!-- page 68 (partial): 1 nugget -->

### Risk Control Framework

- [MUST KNOW][N050] **Lipschutz's risk control principles** (crystallized after personal account
  blowout):
  1. **Always know exactly where you stand** — no ambiguity about P&L or position
  2. **Don't concentrate too much money on one big trade or group of highly correlated trades**
  3. **Always understand the risk/reward of the trade as it *now* stands** — not as it existed
     when you put it on. The position's current risk profile is what matters, not the original
     thesis's risk/reward.
  4. **"I was only playing with the market's money"** — Lipschutz calls this "the most ridiculous
     thing I ever heard." All money in your account is real money with equal value, regardless
     of where it came from. The house-money fallacy leads to irrational risk-taking on profits.

> "I was only playing with the market's money. That's the most ridiculous thing I ever heard."
> — Bill Lipschutz

- [MUST KNOW][N051] **How to handle a losing streak**:
  - "When you're in a losing streak, your ability to properly assimilate and analyze information
    starts to become distorted because of the impairment of the confidence factor, which is a
    by-product of a losing streak."
  - Solution: **cut back trading size**. Smaller size reduces emotional stakes, allows confidence
    to rebuild, and limits further damage while cognitive function is impaired
  - The mechanism: losing streaks → confidence impairment → analytical distortion → more losses.
    Cutting size breaks the feedback loop by reducing the emotional weight of each trade.

<!-- page 68: 2 nuggets -->

### Why Lipschutz Left Salomon — Management vs. Trading

- [NICE TO KNOW][N052] Lipschutz left Salomon because after **Gil** (the department founder) left
  in 1988, Lipschutz ran the department for a year and a half — and hated the administrative side:
  - Spent his time on personnel problems and travel (Tokyo, London, New York)
  - Wanted a comanager to handle administration while he traded — Salomon's style was to bring
    someone in *above* him instead
  - The person they brought in came from fixed income and "saw everything in the eyes of the bond
    world" — would ask, "Isn't that just like the government bond market?" Lipschutz: "No, it's
    nothing like the government bond market. Forget the government bond market."
  - Implication: **domain mismatch in leadership is toxic** — a bond mindset superimposed on FX
    trading is not just unhelpful but actively counterproductive

<!-- page 69: 1 nugget -->

### Large Size as Advantage in FX — A Counterintuitive Claim

- [MUST KNOW][N053] **Why large size is an *advantage* in foreign exchange** (counterintuitive):
  - Standard view: large size = more slippage, harder exits, bigger losses when wrong
  - Lipschutz's view: if a big buyer comes in and pushes the market 4%, that's an advantage for
    them — they don't need to exit all at once, and **they've just changed market psychology for
    the next few days**
  - "Foreign exchange is a very psychological market." A large player can move price and then
    benefit from the momentum they created — exit is not required immediately, and the psychology
    shift works in their favor while they unwind
  - By 1990, Salomon's FX department had **$80 billion in credit lines** at banks — not from
    segregated capital, but from accumulated credit relationships. The banks extended credit
    because of the Salomon name. Size → credit → more size: a compounding advantage unavailable
    to smaller players.
  - Caveat (demonstrated by the Gorbachev trade): **size is an advantage in normal liquidity
    conditions but a catastrophic liability when news creates an illiquid market and urgency to exit**

<!-- page 70: 1 nugget -->

### How Lipschutz Forms and Tests a View — D-Mark Example

- [MUST KNOW][N054] **Lipschutz's fundamental analysis framework** (illustrated with D-mark view
  at time of interview, ~1991):
  - **Bearish D-mark (bullish USD)** rationale:
    - German unification creating massive infrastructure problems in East Germany — may take a
      decade or longer to solve
    - **Bundesbank** restructuring to include East German central bank representatives → uncertainty
    - **Chancellor Kohl's** government on weak political footing
    - Collectively: **disincentives for capital flows into Germany**
    - On the US side: low interest rates + Federal Reserve signaling continued stimulus + early
      signs of US recovery from recession → US becoming more attractive for investment
  - How he would know he was **wrong**: evidence that Germany was effectively addressing its
    problems, OR evidence the US recession was not ending — "essentially the converse of the
    situation" that made him bullish

- [SHOULD KNOW][N055] **Scaling in and out — Lipschutz's core execution philosophy**:
  - "I am definitely a scale-in type of trader" — never puts on a full position at once
  - If price action fails to confirm his expectations: stays flat and buys small on dips, not
    hugely long. "You have to trade at a size such that if you're not exactly right in your
    timing, you won't be blown out of your position."
  - Builds to larger size **as the market goes his way** — not front-loaded conviction
  - Exits the same way: starts lightening "as I see the fundamentals or price action changing" —
    not a binary exit
  - Result: enables staying with long-term winners far longer than most traders can tolerate
  - "You have to figure out how to make money being right only **20 to 30 percent of the time**."
    — i.e., win rate is secondary to holding winners and cutting losers

> "I don't think you can consistently be a winning trader if you're banking on being right more
> than 50 percent of the time. You have to figure out how to make money being right only 20 to
> 30 percent of the time."
> — Bill Lipschutz

<!-- page 71: 1 nugget -->
<!-- page 72: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N056] **Exit rule when fundamentals change but price keeps moving in your favor**:
  - Question: if the dollar is going up (in your favor) but your fundamental reasons for the
    trade have changed — do you stay or exit?
  - Lipschutz: "I would definitely get out. If my perception that the fundamentals have changed
    is not the market's perception, then there's something going on that I don't understand.
    **You don't want to hold a position when you don't understand what's going on.**"
  - The discipline: favorable price action does NOT override a changed fundamental thesis.
    Staying in a winning trade you no longer understand is taking on unknown risk.

<!-- page 73: 1 nugget -->

### Bank Trading Profitability — The Structural Secret

- [MUST KNOW][N057] **Where bank FX profits *actually* come from — the Citibank study**:
  - Schwager puzzled: how can banks with hundreds of young traders all make money when most
    individual traders lose?
  - A study on **Citibank** (largest and most profitable currency trading bank, ~$300-400M/year
    in trading) concluded: if Citibank only traded the **bid/ask spread** and never took
    directional positions, they would make approximately **$600 million a year**
  - Implication: Citibank's *actual* $300-400M profit implies they **lose ~$200M/year on
    directional trading** — the proprietary trading loses money and the spread income subsidizes it
  - Citibank's justification: "If we weren't in the market doing all that proprietary trading and
    developing information, we wouldn't be able to service our customers in the same way" —
    Lipschutz calls this rationalization
  - Mechanism of spread profit: **many marginal traders and hedgers think of Citibank first** when
    they need to do a currency transaction → Citibank can charge wide spreads on
    unsophisticated trades. Also, Citibank has operations in countries without their own central
    bank — virtually all FX transactions in those countries go through Citibank at wide spreads.
  - **Additional source of "directional" profit**: coattailing large customer orders. Not
    conventional position trading — described below.

<!-- page 74: 1 nugget -->
<!-- page 75: continuation of N057 -->

- [MUST KNOW][N058] **"Frontrunning" that's legal in interbank FX — the coattail mechanism**:
  - Trader Joe quotes bid/ask all day, makes small average profit per transaction
  - A customer comes in and must sell **$2 billion**. The trader sells $2.1 billion — $100M
    for the bank's account, the rest to fill the customer
  - Market breaks 1 percent. The bank's $100M position just made **$1 million** on one trade
  - Schwager: "In a lot of markets that's called frontrunning — and it's illegal."
  - Lipschutz: "It's not illegal in the interbank market. He's not putting his order *in front*
    of the customer's; he's basically riding his coattails."
  - Mechanically: "Generally, the first $100 million would be the bank's." That's the convention.
  - The bank's justification: by accepting the credit risk and providing liquidity for the $2B
    order, it *earns* the right to act on the information
  - **True source of bank trading profits**: (1) bid/ask spread margin, (2) coattailing large
    customer orders — NOT consistent directional trading skill in most cases

> "You know my pet peeve? Is *that* trading? Even at Salomon Brothers, where there's a perception
> that everyone is a trader, it came down to only about a half-dozen people who took real risk.
> The rest were essentially just making markets."
> — Bill Lipschutz

<!-- page 76: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N059] **Mechanics of moving a large FX order ($500M+)**:
  - Dealer stands up, shouts: "I need calls!"
  - Among dealers, junior dealers, clerks, and even telex operators — 40 people making calls
    simultaneously, each with their own call list (no overlap)
  - Average ~3 rounds of calls = **120 calls total** in a matter of minutes
  - Dealer acts as coordinator — staff shouts bids to him, he calls back "Yours! Yours! Yours!"
    tracking the total amount sold
  - "A large bank can move an amazing amount of money in a few minutes." — This is the operational
    reality behind why scale/relationships matter in interbank FX

<!-- page 77: 1 nugget -->

### Credit Risk in Interbank FX

- [NICE TO KNOW][N060] **Counterparty credit risk — how it's managed**:
  - In interbank FX, if the counterparty goes bankrupt, you lose your money — no exchange
    clearinghouse guarantee
  - In practice: surprises almost never happen because it's in everyone's interest to share
    information when a problem exists — **credit officers are very quick to share information**
    when they suspect trouble
  - Evidence: Salomon stopped trading with **Drexel** a year and a half before Drexel went under.
    Stopped trading with **Continental Illinois (Conti)** five months before the Fed bailed them
    out. Those still trading with Conti in the final months weren't ignorant — they were just
    willing to take the risk at higher interest rates. "At a certain interest rate level, you
    would lend any bank money."
  - Mitigation: if a counterparty's creditworthiness is in question, you can require them to post
    **margin** before trading

<!-- page 78: 1 nugget -->

### Dreams, Intuition, and Subconscious Market Processing

- [SHOULD KNOW][N061] **The prophetic dream that Lipschutz didn't trade**:
  - Night before a balance-of-trade number release, Lipschutz dreamt the exact trade figure,
    the revision figure, and the subsequent price sequence (dollar moving up through four
    specific levels, with him buying at each)
  - The next day: trade figure, revision, and price sequence were **exactly** as in the dream
  - "The only difference was [he pauses] I didn't trade at all."
  - Why not: "Because I don't trade on dreams or rumors. I'm a fundamental trader." His assistant
    said: "The day you start trading on dreams is the day we might as well pack it up."
  - Irony: "if I had never had the dream, I might very well have bought dollars" — because the
    market looked good at each price level in the conscious analysis

<!-- page 78: continuation -->

- [MUST KNOW][N062] **Schwager's theory: expert intuition as subconscious information processing**:
  - The "prophetic dream" is not mystical — it's the expert's subconscious connecting dots that
    the conscious mind hasn't fully assembled:
    - Expert traders absorb vast amounts of fundamental and technical information daily
    - They may subconsciously pick up signals (things someone said, positions certain people took)
      suggesting a data release will be out of line with expectations
    - For whatever reason (the expectation seemed irrational, reluctance to trade ahead of numbers,
      prior negative experiences), the conscious mind doesn't act on it
    - Suppressed-but-valid trading thesis "comes out in a dream"
  - **Price projection**: once the data is right, an expert with vast market experience will
    naturally get the price sequence right too — not surprising
  - Schwager: "Trades that we are the most difficult to take are often the successful ones" —
    because the logical mind resists what the market-experienced mind recognizes as correct
  - Practical implication: when an expert trader has a strong but "irrational"-feeling conviction,
    the resistance may itself be a signal. The trades hardest to pull the trigger on may be
    the best opportunities.

> "Trades that we are the most difficult to take are often the successful ones."
> — Jack Schwager

<!-- page 79: 2 nuggets -->

### Gut Decisions vs. Reasoned Trades

- [MUST KNOW][N063] **Lipschutz on gut decisions — when they are and aren't appropriate**:
  - General rule: good traders do NOT make gut or snap decisions. Any trade idea must be well
    thought out and grounded in reason *before* taking the position.
  - Why reason is required:
    1. Must always ask: "If this trade goes wrong, how do I get out?" — especially critical at
       large position sizes
    2. Must evaluate the *best way to express* the trade idea — since Lipschutz rarely uses
       straight long/short, he must find the option combination with the most attractive
       risk/reward for his market expectation. This analysis is inherently deliberate.
  - **When gut decisions DO legitimately occur**: panic situations where no time exists for
    deliberation — e.g., the Australian finance minister saying he didn't care if the currency
    lost 10% overnight. Market down 5% during the chaos. Lipschutz's gut: "There's no way the
    currency could adjust even remotely close to 10% before large buyers would come in."
    - He placed a buy order mid-conversation with Schwager — which proved to be the exact turning
      point. This is **calibrated intuition from deep experience**, not random snap judgment.
  - Key distinction: gut decisions are appropriate in **information-overloaded/time-compressed
    situations** where prior experience creates faster-than-conscious pattern recognition.
    Deliberate situations require deliberate analysis. Confusing the two is dangerous.

<!-- page 80: 1 nugget -->

<!-- pp. 79-100 -->

<!-- page 79: overlap — context only, covered in previous batch -->
<!-- page 80: overlap — context only, covered in previous batch -->

### When Panic Has Run Its Course

<!-- page 81: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N064] **"Gut feel" is really subconscious market experience**:
  - Lipschutz on gauging when a panic has exhausted itself: "It's a combination
    of market experience and innate feel."
  - Mechanism: many currency traders operate under rules requiring liquidation
    if they lose a certain amount — these are NOT rational decisions for a given
    moment but pre-set general rules. Their forced selling creates the panic.
  - Knowing when that forced liquidation is nearly exhausted comes from past
    experience so thoroughly internalized that it operates below conscious
    awareness — i.e., what people describe as gut feel IS subconscious market
    experience, not mysticism or random instinct
  - Key reframe: **gut feel = crystallized pattern recognition from vast
    experience**, operating faster than conscious deliberation can

> "What people describe as gut feel is probably better described as subconscious
> market experience."
> — Bill Lipschutz

### Characteristics of Superior Traders

- [MUST KNOW][N065] **The two non-negotiable traits of elite traders: intelligence
  AND extreme commitment**:
  - Lipschutz analogy: in college, smart people could succeed without hard work,
    and hard workers could succeed without being brilliant. **In trading you need
    BOTH.** The best traders are genuinely quite brilliant AND work very hard.
  - Definition of "working hard" in trading: NOT hours in the office — it's
    **commitment and focus**: developing scenarios, reevaluating scenarios,
    collecting information, reevaluating information. Constantly asking:
    - "What am I doing right?"
    - "What am I doing wrong?"
    - "How can I do what I am doing better?"
    - "How can I get more information?"
  - This self-interrogation loop is obsessive and never-ending. Lipschutz: "It's
    obsessive."
  - Caveat (from Schwager): **Victor Sperandeo does NOT share the view that
    superior intelligence is required** — so this is Lipschutz's personal belief,
    not universal consensus among top traders

<!-- page 82: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N066] **Great traders never truly "turn off"** — market focus
  persists even during leisure:
  - Lipschutz: "Absolutely. Some professional traders may claim that they separate
    their personal life from their business life and are able to completely turn
    off on the weekends. I don't believe that for a second."
  - Even on a golf course or sailboat, at some level, they're still processing
    market information
  - Interview test signal: in job interviews, if a candidate asks "What time do
    I have to come in?" or "How late do I have to stay?" — Lipschutz views this
    as a red flag revealing a fundamental misalignment with what elite trading
    requires. The truly committed don't think in terms of hours.

<!-- page 83: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N067] **Courage is the third essential trait — insight without action
  is worthless**:
  - "It's not enough to simply have the insight to see something apart from the
    rest of the crowd, you also need to have the courage to act on it and to stay
    with it."
  - Being different from the majority is, by definition, what you're doing if
    you're a successful trader — but it is very difficult psychologically
  - Key implication: many traders may have the right analysis and still not profit
    because they lack the nerve to act on out-of-consensus positions and hold them

- [SHOULD KNOW][N068] **Trading is situational, not rule-based**:
  - "Many people think that trading can be reduced to a few rules. Always do this
    or always do that. To me, trading isn't about *always* at all; it is about
    each situation."
  - This is a direct contradiction of mechanical/algorithmic trading philosophies
  - Lipschutz's view: the infinite variety and context-dependence of market
    situations means rigid rules fail because they cannot encode the full
    complexity of what the market presents

### The Pain of Trading

<!-- page 84: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N069] **The psychological reality of holding large positions at
  night — the emotional "pain" most people don't see**:
  - Lipschutz: "It's the middle of the night, everyone else is asleep, and you're
    sitting in front of a machine with glowing green numbers, with a pain in your
    psyche because the market is going against you and you don't know whether the
    fundamentals have changed or whether it's just a meaningless short-term move."
  - His wife (a former bond salesman at Goldman Sachs): not just "understanding"
    but "fully cognizant, supportive, and gets a thrill out of what I do" — the
    distinction matters. Surface support vs. genuine domain understanding.

### Why Trade

<!-- page 85: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N070] **Trading as a game — the "easy score" appeal**:
  - Lipschutz: "I like the game. I think it's a great challenge. It's also an
    easy game to keep score of."
  - Would trade for free: "I'm thirty-six years old, and I almost feel like I
    have never worked. I sometimes can't believe I'm making all this money to
    essentially play an elaborate game."
  - Immediately balanced by: "On the other hand, when you look at all the money
    I've produced over the years, I've been vastly underpaid."
  - This "love of the game" theme recurs throughout Schwager's interviews as a
    common trait among supertraders — they are not primarily motivated by money

### Schwager's Postscript on Lipschutz

- [MUST KNOW][N071] **Evidence of innate talent: Lipschutz's paper trading
  performance and early career track record**:
  - Paper trading in college course: turned hypothetical $100,000 → $29 million
    by course end. Caveat: no real money, rules flawed by unrealistic leverage
    limits — but still striking
  - Inherited $12,000 in real money → built to $250,000 over four years →
    blew up the entire account in one mistake (wildly overleveraging). Schwager
    notes: the blow-up doesn't erase the skill demonstrated in the four-year
    steady build-up
  - At Salomon Brothers: **estimated to have generated over $500 million in
    profits over his eight-year tenure** — he declines to confirm specific figures
  - Lipschutz professed commitment as the principal ingredient of his success.
    Schwager observes: hard work alone is not sufficient for great trading, but it
    is a consistent ingredient across many world-class traders

<!-- page 86: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N072] **Schwager's lessons from Lipschutz — scale-in/scale-out
  as a practical framework**:
  - Core principle: **you don't have to get in or out of a position all at once**
  - Scale-in application: If you're convinced a market should be bought but
    prices have already run up significantly, waiting for a major reaction before
    taking the FULL position risks missing the move entirely. Solution: put on
    PART of the position at market, rest on scale-down → ensures at least partial
    participation if the move continues, without the excessive risk of committing
    fully after a large uninterrupted advance
  - Scale-out application: If long with a large profit and worried about a top —
    getting out of the ENTIRE position risks missing the rest of the move if you're
    wrong; keeping the ENTIRE position risks giving back most of the gain if you're
    right. Scale-out guarantees you never get the best outcome, but also **never
    get the worst outcome**
  - Extra benefit: scale-in/out approach lets you restrict FULL positions to those
    instances where your confidence is greatest

<!-- page 87: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N073] **Marty Schwartz rule: if a feared scenario doesn't materialize
  as badly as feared, stay in the trade**:
  - From *Market Wizards* (Schwartz): if a trade you are very worried about does
    NOT turn out as badly as feared, **don't get out**
  - Reasoning: if there is no follow-through in the adverse direction, there must
    be very strong underlying forces in favor of your position — because the fears
    prompting worry are likely shared by many other market participants
  - Lipschutz example: short a very large dollar position vs. D-mark during a
    sharp dollar rally; needed Tokyo opening for sufficient liquidity to exit.
    By the time Tokyo opened, the dollar had weakened → he held the position and
    recouped most of the loss. He "responded exactly right" by NOT panicking out
    - This trade was the one that "scared him most" — yet the "no follow-through"
      signal was the correct cue to stay
  - Key insight: markets often move against good positions temporarily precisely
    BECAUSE the underlying thesis is correct and is shared by others who will
    eventually act on it

- [SHOULD KNOW][N074] **Schwager's observation on Lipschutz's blow-up of personal
  account — "everybody gets what they want out of the market"**:
  - Lipschutz had strong philosophical objections to simultaneously trading
    personal and company accounts (conflict of interest)
  - He blew up his entire personal stock option account in a few days' time —
    just as he was starting full employment at Salomon Brothers
  - Schwager notes the irony: the blow-up conveniently eliminated the exact source
    of conflict Lipschutz objected to. Lipschutz insisted the timing was
    coincidental (he was only in training, not yet aware of any actual conflict)
  - Schwager references the aphorism: **"Everybody gets what they want out of the
    market."** — implying that Lipschutz's subconscious may have resolved the
    conflict on his behalf. Whether conjecture or fact: "Lipschutz did indeed get
    what he wanted — a perfect job, huge trading profits, and an absence of
    conflict between his personal and company trading."

<!-- page 88: covered above (continuation of postscript) -->

<!-- page 89: 1 nugget — end of Lipschutz chapter -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N075] **Buy on extreme strength after a news event that confirms
  your conviction**:
  - Schwager lesson: if you have strong conviction about a trade AND the market
    makes a large move because of a news event confirming that thesis, "the best
    decision may well be to bite the bullet and buy on extreme strength (or sell on
    extreme weakness)"
  - Example from Lipschutz's group: how they traded the G-7 meeting aftermath —
    not waiting for a pullback, just acting on the confirmed fundamental thesis
    regardless of the price level

