## Part III: Futures — The Variety-Pack Market

<!-- page 90: no content (title page) -->

## Chapter: Futures — Understanding the Basics

<!-- page 91: 3 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N076] **Futures markets now dominated by financial instruments,
  not agricultural commodities**:
  - 1991 U.S. futures volume: exceeded 263,000,000 contracts; average contract
    value at least $40,000 → **total dollar value exceeded $10 trillion**
  - Origins in agricultural commodities, but that sector now accounts for only
    ~1/5 of all futures trading
  - Financial instruments (currencies, interest rates, stock indexes): ~60% of
    all futures trading
  - Energy + metals: nearly half of the remaining 40%
  - Key implication: the word "commodities" is increasingly a **misnomer** for
    futures markets. Most actively traded futures are NOT true commodities.
  - Structural insight: since futures price moves closely parallel cash market
    moves (arbitrage keeps them in line), futures traders in financials ARE
    effectively traders in stocks, bonds, and currencies. Futures trader insights
    apply directly to stock/bond investors.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N077] **Essence and purpose of a futures contract**:
  - A **standardized contract** for a commodity or financial instrument for
    delivery at a FUTURE date (as opposed to current/spot delivery)
  - Primary economic purpose: **hedging** — locking in future prices to reduce
    adverse price-move risk. Example: copper manufacturer buys copper futures
    to lock in costs 6 months out. If copper rises, futures profit offsets higher
    spot purchase cost. If copper falls, futures loss offset by lower purchase cost.
  - Traders (speculators) use futures to PROFIT from anticipated price changes,
    preferring futures over cash markets for structural reasons (see below)

<!-- page 92: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N078] **Seven structural advantages of futures markets over cash
  markets for traders**:
  1. **Standardized contracts** — no need to find a specific buyer/seller to
     initiate or liquidate; standardization enables liquidity
  2. **Liquidity** — all major markets provide excellent liquidity
  3. **Ease of going short** — equal ease of going short as long. In stock
     markets, short sellers must wait for an uptick; no such restriction in
     futures
  4. **Leverage** — initial margin typically 5-10% of contract value. CRITICAL
     caveat: futures "margin" is a **good-faith deposit**, NOT a partial payment
     (no physical transaction until expiration). Leverage is a two-edged sword:
     **undisciplined use of leverage is the single most important reason most
     traders lose money in futures**. Futures prices are no more volatile than
     underlying cash prices or many stocks — the high-risk reputation comes
     entirely from the leverage factor.
  5. **Low transaction costs** — far cheaper than equivalent stock market
     operations (e.g., reducing portfolio exposure via index futures vs. selling
     individual stocks)
  6. **Ease of offset** — position can be offset at any time during market
     hours (except when locked limit-up or limit-down). Limit moves: when free
     market equilibrium would require a price outside the daily limit, market
     moves to the limit and virtually ceases to trade
  7. **Guaranteed by exchange** — clearinghouse guarantee eliminates
     counterparty credit risk

<!-- page 93: covered above (continuation of futures advantages) -->
<!-- page 94: covered above (end of futures advantages) -->

<!-- page 95: 1 nugget -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N079] **Arbitrage ensures futures track cash markets closely**:
  - Arbitrageurs keep deviations between futures and cash prices minor and
    short-lived. Therefore futures price moves closely parallel underlying cash
    markets.
  - Practical implication for readers: insights from futures traders interviewed
    in the book apply directly to investors who have never traded futures —
    because they're effectively discussing the same underlying markets (stocks,
    bonds, currencies)

---

## Chapter: Randy McKay — Veteran Trader

### Background and Career Overview

<!-- page 96: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N080] **Randy McKay's extraordinary track record — consistency over
  raw performance**:
  - **Starting stake: $2,000** in first calendar year (actually seven months)
    → **$70,000** — accomplished during the birth of currency futures trading when
    the pit was so quiet that "trading was probably a distant third to newspaper
    reading and board games"
  - Profitable in **18 out of 20 years** of trading career
  - Cumulative earnings in the tens of millions (conservative estimate)
  - Prior to 1986: **seven consecutive million-dollar-plus years** in his own
    account — streak broken when he switched from floor to home trading, but
    quickly adjusted and registered his first million-dollar gain in his second
    year off the floor
  - Managed family/friends accounts: two oldest accounts (started 1982 with
    $10,000 each) had each generated **cumulative earnings in excess of $1 million**
  - Pattern note: McKay made MORE money each successive year until the floor
    transition — a pattern of compound growth rather than erratic peaks

- [NICE TO KNOW][N081] **The currency futures market was nearly dead at its birth**:
  - When McKay started, currency futures were moribund — the pit was so inactive
    that reading newspapers and playing board games occupied most traders' time
  - The CME's survival of the currency futures division was initially in question
  - Yet McKay built his entire fortune trading in this early, illiquid period —
    suggesting the market's low activity was not a barrier for a skilled trader
    who understood price behavior

<!-- page 97: 1 nugget -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N082] **McKay's entry into trading — accidental opportunity**:
  - Background: Vietnam veteran (drafted marines), originally studying to be a
    clinical psychologist. Brother **Terry** was a floor broker at the **Chicago
    Mercantile Exchange (CME)**
  - Got a job as a runner on the floor → allowed school in afternoons, study
    at night. "Absolutely no intention of getting into this business."
  - In 1972, CME launched the **International Monetary Market (IMM)** to trade
    currencies. CME seats were $100,000 (~$500,000 today) — seemed impossibly
    expensive. To seed the new IMM division, the exchange sold IMM seats for only
    **$10,000** AND gave free IMM seats to all existing CME members.
  - Terry received a free seat, had no need for it, offered it to McKay to use
    in the interim. McKay had become interested in market mechanics from watching
    the floor; liked numbers and strategy games (bridge, chess). Thought trading
    "might be an interesting thing to do."
  - Key lesson: **proximity to a market + intellectual curiosity + accidental
    access** → career-defining opportunity. McKay didn't seek out trading; the
    IMM came to him at the right moment.

### Vietnam: Background and Character Formation

<!-- page 98: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N083] **McKay's Vietnam experience — from rule-follower to
  independent thinker**:
  - Drafted after flunking out of college (addicted to bridge, skipped classes →
    six Fs). Marines could draft recruits only during two specific months in
    1968 (April and May). McKay chose to serve rather than take a cushy
    reserves position his father (a colonel) could have arranged — felt that
    accepting the privileges of citizenship meant accepting its responsibilities
  - Held pre-war view that it was a "stupid war" but that elected leaders make
    decisions → feelings changed drastically during and after
  - **Major character shift from Vietnam**: went from rule-follower to independent
    thinker. "When I realized that the leaders in the country didn't necessarily
    know what they were doing, I became much more independent."
  - Likely relevance to trading: willingness to act independently of consensus,
    distrust of authority, comfort forming one's own judgment

- [NICE TO KNOW][N084] **McKay on the emotional arc in Vietnam: fear → boredom**:
  - Initial response: absolute terror landing in Da Nang, gunfire everywhere,
    rushed into jeep with weapons but no combat experience
  - After a few months: fear shifted to **boredom** as the primary emotional
    state — 16-hour workdays in horrible conditions (110°F dust or knee-deep
    monsoon mud), life-threatening attacks became welcome as a break from the
    monotony
  - "You can't have an anxiety attack every thirty seconds for a year. Eventually,
    your mind forces you to get used to the idea that you might die or lose a leg,
    and you go on."
  - Parallel to trading: the psychological adaptation to constant risk exposure
    — repeated exposure desensitizes the fear response, enabling function under
    chronic uncertainty

<!-- page 99: 1 nugget -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N085] **Vietnam's philosophical impact on McKay**:
  - Guard duty reflection: hearing a noise in the bushes, knowing the worst case
    was the enemy trying to kill him — then realizing the enemy soldier was
    "probably a young kid just like me... as scared as I am, trying to kill me,
    and I'm trying to kill him."
  - Conclusion: "War is insanity. It doesn't make the slightest bit of sense for
    countries to try to settle their political differences by sending their
    children out to kill each other."
  - Effect: became a pacifist. Also felt the two years were largely stolen from
    his life — argued he'd have preferred to be "put to sleep for two years and
    then awakened" rather than go through the actual experience
  - Carries nightmares to this day: being chased by people with rifles, feet
    getting bogged down, unable to run fast enough

<!-- page 100: 1 nugget — McKay becoming a trader -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N086] **McKay's pivot to trading — personality fit with markets**:
  - After Vietnam, worked as floor runner while finishing college, originally
    planning to be a clinical psychologist
  - Found himself drawn to the mechanics of the market: liked juggling numbers and
    playing strategy games (bridge, chess), enjoyed watching prices fluctuate and
    "trying to outguess the market"
  - When the IMM seat opportunity came from his brother, he thought trading
    "might be an interesting thing to do" — took it without strong conviction
  - Key personality traits that translated: mathematical aptitude, competitive
    game orientation, tolerance for uncertainty (hardened by Vietnam)
  - Note: the same bridge obsession that got him expelled from college (and
    drafted) is what oriented him toward the number-heavy, game-like environment
    of futures trading — a remarkable irony of how character weaknesses and
    strengths are often the same trait in different contexts

<!-- pp. 99-120 -->

## Chapter: Chuck McKay — The Floor Trader Who Became a Currency Wizard

*(Chapter begins before p. 99; these notes cover pp. 99–120, mid-interview onward)*

**Chuck McKay**: started as a CME floor runner, went on to become a major currency
speculator, catching the IMM from its founding in 1972 through the 1980s bull runs.

<!-- page 99: 2 nuggets -->

### Vietnam, Independence, and Entering the Markets

- [SHOULD KNOW][N064] **Vietnam as formative experience — becoming an independent thinker**:
  - Key shift: "I went from being a rule follower to thinking for myself. When I
    realized that the leaders in the country didn't necessarily know what they were
    doing, I became much more independent."
  - McKay's verdict on Vietnam: "largely a waste of two years." The discipline of
    boot camp and learning "that war is insanity" were the only useful takeaways.
  - He held a philosophical position that he would have preferred to be "put to sleep
    for two years and then awakened" rather than live through the actual experience —
    an anti-experiential view contrasted with the argument that "any experience
    is worthwhile."
  - Practical implication for trading: willingness to question authority and consensus
    — directly relevant to his later contrarian trades.

> "The major change was that I went from being a rule follower to thinking for
> myself. When I realized that the leaders in the country didn't necessarily
> know what they were doing, I became much more independent."
> — Chuck McKay

<!-- page 100: 2 nuggets -->

### Path to Trading — Psychology and the CME Floor

- [SHOULD KNOW][N065] **How McKay entered trading — accident, not ambition**:
  - Was studying to be a **clinical psychologist**; needed a job allowing school
    simultaneously after returning from Vietnam.
  - Brother **Terry** was a floor broker on the **Chicago Mercantile Exchange (CME)**
    — got him a job as a runner.
  - Worked as runner for two years with "absolutely no intention of getting into
    this business."
  - Turning point: in 1972, the CME launched the **International Monetary Market
    (IMM)** to trade currencies. Regular CME seats were $100,000 (~$500,000 today)
    — impossible for McKay. IMM seats were sold for only $10,000 to attract traders
    into new pits, and all existing CME members received free IMM seats.
  - Brother had no use for his free seat and offered it to McKay to use in the
    interim. McKay accepted, borrowed $5,000 from his brother, put $3,000 in the
    bank for living expenses, and started trading with $2,000.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N066] **Psychology of market observation — floor insight**:
  - While working on the floor during his runner years, McKay noticed that "prices
    moved based on the psychology of the people who were trading."
  - "You could actually see anxiety, greed, and fear in the markets. I found it very
    interesting to follow the customers' moods and to see how these emotions
    translated into orders and ultimately into market price movements."
  - This direct psychology-to-price-movement observation was formative — and
    connected to his psychology studies.

<!-- page 101: 1 nugget -->

### Early Currency Futures — Dead Markets and Huge Profits

- [SHOULD KNOW][N067] **The IMM's near-death in early years — and what massive
  inefficiency looks like**:
  - After the initial novelty wore off, currency futures liquidity "completely dried
    up." The market was essentially dead all day.
  - **Leo Melamed** (president of the exchange, who conceived and spearheaded
    currency futures) would personally collar livestock pit traders after their markets
    closed and cajole them into trading currencies — giving the pit a brief flurry
    of activity at end-of-day, then silence again.
  - McKay made about two trades per day. But: "Currency trading began in May 1972.
    By the end of that calendar year, I had made $70,000, which was a sum beyond
    my wildest dreams."
  - **Explanation for early profitability**: price inefficiencies were enormous due
    to "tremendous amount of ignorance about the currency markets." They didn't
    even realize that banks were trading forward currency markets, which were
    exactly equivalent to futures.
  - Core lesson: early-stage markets with low participation and high ignorance
    create outsized opportunity for even a small edge.

<!-- page 102: 2 nuggets -->

### The British Pound Trade 1976 — The Career-Defining Trade

- [MUST KNOW][N068] **British pound 1976 — anatomy of a landmark trade**:
  - British government announced they would not allow the pound to trade above
    **$1.72** (concerned about import inflation from sterling strength). At the time,
    pound was in the mid-$1.60s.
  - Market responded immediately by jumping to $1.72. Fell back to $1.68, rallied
    again to $1.72, each oscillation by smaller and smaller amounts — price range
    converging tightly just below $1.72.
  - **Consensus view**: "They're not going to let it go above $1.72. We might as
    well sell it. It's a no-risk trade."
  - **McKay's read**: the market looked "locked limit-up." His logic:
    - If the government announces a price ceiling and the market doesn't break,
      there must be **tremendous underlying demand**
    - The converging oscillations signaled buyers absorbing every attempt to sell
    - "This could be the opportunity of a lifetime"
  - Action: went long **200 British pound contracts** — from a previous maximum of
    30-40 contracts. Position was 5-7x his prior maximum.
  - So nervous that he woke up at 5am daily to call the **Bank of England** for cash
    quotes (no Reuters service then), pretending to be from CitiBank or Harris Trust.

> [Limit-up explanation]: "When the market's natural equilibrium price lies above
> this limit price, the market will lock at the limit — that is, trading will
> virtually cease. The reason for this is that there will be an abundance of buyers
> but almost no sellers at the restricted limit-up price."

<!-- page 103: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N069] **How the British pound trade played out — managing conviction**:
  - One morning the Bank of England clerk quoted **$1.7250** — McKay thought it was
    a mistake for $1.7150. It wasn't. The dam had broken.
  - McKay called everyone he knew who was in the trade with him, bought more for
    himself, and "just sat back and watched the market ride all the way up to the
    $1.90 level" — over about **three to four months**.
  - **Exit level rationale**: $1.90 was a psychologically critical round number AND
    a prior important chart point on the way down.
  - Exit execution: had a total of **1,400 contracts** to sell (400 his own, 1,000
    belonging to friends/acquaintances he'd brought into the trade). Hit all the bids
    over about 45 minutes. Was so excited he accidentally sold **400 more than
    intended**. His excess selling moved the bank market down ~100 points, and he
    ended up making money on those accidental extra contracts too.
  - **P&L**: $1.3 million personal. Prior maximum profit had been under $100,000.
  - Significance: "The most important thing about that trade was that it propelled me
    into being a hundred-lot trader."
  - **Foresight about market opportunity**: "I felt the business was just too damn
    easy and that it couldn't possibly last forever" — drove him to scale up as fast
    as possible while inefficiency remained. Confirmed: "trading is much more
    difficult now than it was then."

<!-- page 104: 1 nugget -->

### Markets Changing — The Shift from Prognostication to Execution

- [MUST KNOW][N070] **The 1970s vs. 1990s trading environment — the single most
  important structural shift McKay observed**:
  - **1970s**: inflation drove massive price moves; public participation heavy; timing
    was not critical because moves were so large that "all you had to do was jump
    on the bandwagon."
  - **1980s transition**: declining inflation → fewer large moves → choppier price
    action → moves more often to the downside (public prefers longs) → professionals
    trading against each other instead of against naive public.
  - **1991 state**: "In the 1970s, prognostication was 90 percent and execution
    10 percent, whereas today prognostication is 25 percent and execution
    75 percent."
  - Translation: being right about direction used to be almost sufficient. Now,
    **where you get in and out** matters far more than which way the market goes.
  - Implication: trend-following alone no longer works; precise entry/exit has become
    the dominant skill.

> "I would say that in the 1970s prognostication was 90 percent and execution
> 10 percent, whereas today prognostication is 25 percent and execution 75 percent."
> — Chuck McKay

<!-- page 105: 2 nuggets -->

### The Core Methodology — How the Market Responds to News

- [MUST KNOW][N071] **McKay's forecasting approach: market response to fundamentals,
  not the fundamentals themselves**:
  - "I watch the market action, using fundamentals as a backdrop. I don't use
    fundamentals in the conventional sense. That is, I don't think, 'Supply is too
    large and the market is going down.' Rather, I watch how the market *responds*
    to fundamental information."
  - **The signal**: if the market fails to respond the way the fundamentals say it
    should — especially under high-conviction conditions — that's the real information.
  - **Stock market example (1991)**: "We've had a severe recession — probably worse
    than the government is admitting — the worst real estate bust since the
    depression, and a war. Moreover, the market should have been particularly
    vulnerable after a nine-year advance. In the midst of all this negative news,
    the stock market has hardly budged, and we're still trading just below all-time
    highs. The fact that the stock market has been a lot stronger than it should
    have been tells me that it's likely to go higher."
  - **Gold/Gulf War example**: On the eve of the U.S. air war against Iraq, gold
    was near $400. The night U.S. planes attacked, gold went from $397 to $410 in
    Far East markets — breaking the key $400 level that everyone expected to trigger
    a rally — then closed the session at ~$390. "Gold had broken through the critical
    $400 level, starting the rally that everyone expected, but it finished the evening
    significantly lower, despite the fact that the United States had just entered
    the war." Next morning it opened sharply lower and continued down for months.
  - **Rule**: when a market fails to follow through on a breakout to levels expected
    given the fundamental catalyst, the opposite direction is the real trade.

<!-- page 106: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N072] **Soybean example — bullish factors failing to produce a rally
  as a bearish signal**:
  - Setup: soybeans trading just under $6.00. Simultaneously had drought threat
    (critical growing season) AND dramatically improved Soviet relations (increased
    export expectations). These are the two primary price-boosting factors — and
    both were present at once, with prices already at low levels.
  - Result: "Not only did soybeans fail to manage more than a short-lived, moderate
    rally, but on balance prices actually moved lower."
  - Conclusion: "The more recent price break down to the $5.30 level was almost
    inevitable. If prices couldn't sustain an advance with large exports expected
    to the Soviet Union and the threat of a drought, what could possibly rally
    the market?"
  - This is the mirror of N071: maximum bullish catalyst + failure to rally =
    sell signal. Same logic applies in reverse.

<!-- page 107: 2 nuggets -->

### Landmark Trades — Canadian Dollar Short

- [SHOULD KNOW][N073] **Canadian dollar short, early 1980s — reading government
  intervention as a trade qualifier**:
  - Trade: short Canadian dollar from ~85¢ to under 70¢ during early 1980s. Held
    between **1,000 and 1,500 contracts** for "virtually the entire decline, which
    spanned five years."
  - **Key structural insight**: Canadian government policy at the time was to
    *not* intervene aggressively. They would intervene halfheartedly at round
    numbers (120, 130, 140 to the U.S. dollar) for a few days, then let the
    currency go.
  - McKay was on the **opposite side of the intervention trend** — actively looking
    for weak/ineffectual government support as confirmation that his position was
    right and could be held.
  - When to get out: the government's intervention character *changed* mid-decline —
    price eventually reached 67¢, then opened 120 points higher two successive days
    (costing $1M+ each day). On the third day, **Prime Minister Mulroney** was
    quoted: "We will not allow Chicago speculators to determine the value of our
    currency. Our currency is solid and we will not permit it to fall apart because
    of a bunch of gamblers." McKay exited immediately.
  - Lesson: **halfhearted intervention = trade reinforcement; committed intervention
    = exit signal.** When the political rhetoric escalates to personal and nationalistic
    language, the character of the intervention has changed.

<!-- page 108: 2 nuggets -->

### "Catch the Easy Part" — The Central Philosophy

- [MUST KNOW][N074] **"The easy part" — McKay's core trading philosophy**:
  - "When the trade was easy, I wanted to be in, and when it wasn't, I wanted to
    be out. In fact, that is part of my general philosophy on trading: I want to
    catch the easy part."
  - **Definition of the easy part**: "It's the meat of the move."
    - *Beginning* of a price move: hard because you're not sure whether you're right
      about the direction of the trend
    - *End* of a move: hard because people start taking profits and the market gets
      very choppy
    - *Middle* of the move: the "easy part"
  - Implication: "I never try to buy a bottom or sell a top. Even if you manage to
    pick the bottom, the market can end up sitting there for years and tying up your
    capital. You don't want to have a position before a move has started. You want
    to wait until the move is already under way before you get into the market."
  - Common mistake McKay identifies: "They try to put their own opinion of what will
    happen before the market action."

> "I want to catch the easy part."
> — Chuck McKay

<!-- page 109: 2 nuggets -->

### Carter Dollar Rescue — Reading Deceleration as a Warning

- [MUST KNOW][N075] **November 1978 Carter dollar rescue plan — detecting weakness
  before the crash**:
  - McKay was "extremely long" going into the surprise weekend announcement of the
    Carter dollar rescue plan — which caused a huge overnight price break in foreign
    currencies.
  - But he had **liquidated over half his position a week earlier** — before any
    evidence of weakness.
  - **Why**: "The upmove was decelerating instead of accelerating. It's possible to
    see market weakness even when prices are still going up and setting new all-time
    highs."
  - Specifically: he had been long both Deutsche mark and British pound. He sold his
    **Deutsche mark** position and kept the British pound — suggesting deceleration
    was more visible in the Deutsche mark.
  - On Monday when the market opened drastically lower (locked limit-down in futures;
    cash market trading 1,800 points below the futures permissible limit):
    - Got "a couple hundred contracts sold" on naïve buy orders from traders who
      didn't realize the cash market discounted two additional limit-down days
    - Liquidated the rest in the bank market, which was down ~1,800 points
      (~three limit-down moves in futures)
  - Loss: **$1.5 million** — worst loss to that point.

- [MUST KNOW][N076] **Exit rule when you are hurt in the market — no exceptions**:
  - "There's a principle I follow that never allows me to even make that decision.
    When I get hurt in the market, I get the hell out. It doesn't matter at all
    where the market is trading. I just get out."
  - **Reasoning**: "Once you're hurt in the market, your decisions are going to be
    far less objective than they are when you're doing well. And if the market had
    rallied 1,800 points that day to close higher, I couldn't have cared less.
    If you stick around when the market is severely against you, sooner or later
    they're going to carry you out."
  - Emotional aftermath: "As long as you're in the position, there's tremendous
    anxiety. Once you get out, you begin to forget about it. If you can't put it
    out of your mind, you can't trade."
  - Note: this is a categorical rule, not situational — applies even when the market
    might recover intraday.

> "When I get hurt in the market, I get the hell out. It doesn't matter at all
> where the market is trading. I just get out, because I believe that once you're
> hurt in the market, your decisions are going to be far less objective than they
> are when you're doing well."
> — Chuck McKay

<!-- page 110: 2 nuggets -->

### Missing the Gold Rally 1979 — FOMO as a Dangerous Force

- [MUST KNOW][N077] **Missing the gold rally 1979 — the psychology of FOMO and the
  panic buy at the exact top**:
  - Gold's giant rally into early 1980 was one of the greatest commodity price moves
    in history. McKay missed the entire thing.
  - Why he missed it: "The market simply ran away from me. Every day I thought,
    'If only I had bought it yesterday, I would have been OK.'"
  - Two simultaneous pains:
    1. Watching the greatest commodity move in history from the sidelines
    2. Cash in the bank steadily losing real value because of rampant inflation
  - Resolution: "I finally ended up buying gold on the exact day it made its high.
    I bought fifty contracts. The next day, the market opened $150 lower. I was
    out $750,000."
  - Psychological twist: "I was so relieved that the torture was finally over that
    I couldn't have cared less about the money I lost. In fact, I was actually
    praying for the market to open lower."
  - **Diagnosis**: he "just went long to stop the pain" — the buy was driven by
    FOMO and psychological torture, not by analysis.

- [MUST KNOW][N078] **The lesson from missing the gold rally**:
  - "I learned that you have to be more concerned about the moves you're in than
    the moves you're not in."
  - Confessional: "I didn't always realize that. In those days, if I had a small
    position instead of a big one, I would actually hope that the market would
    open against me."
  - This is a fundamental maturity shift: early McKay valued participation and size
    of opportunity *not yet captured* more than protection of *current* positions.
    The reverse is correct — **manage what you own**.

> "I learned that you have to be more concerned about the moves you're in
> than the moves you're not in."
> — Chuck McKay

<!-- page 111: 1 nugget -->

### Worst Loss Ever — The 1988 Canadian Dollar Disaster

- [MUST KNOW][N079] **Canadian dollar 1988 — the "second-to-last play" that blew up**:
  - Context: McKay had a life goal since early in his career to take **$50 million
    out of the market** — $25M in a bank account to live off interest, $25M to play
    with (buy a newspaper or baseball team). He called this structured plan his
    "last two plays."
  - The 1988 Canadian dollar was supposed to be his **second-to-last play**: long
    2,000 contracts above 80¢ (market had just broken through the psychologically
    critical 80-cent level). Target: 87-88¢, expected profit ~$15M. Then one
    more trade ($30M), then done.
  - Complication: he was having a house built in Jamaica, had to fly back every few
    weeks to supervise construction.
  - One Sunday evening, while leaving to catch a connecting flight to Miami, he
    checked the screen and saw the Canadian dollar **100 points lower in Far East
    markets**. The Canadian dollar "rarely moves much in the Far East" and "doesn't
    even move 20 points." He rationalized it as a data error and walked out the door.
  - **It wasn't a mistake.** The market opened 150+ points lower on the IMM next
    morning. He had no phone in Jamaica — had to go to a hotel to use public phones.
    By the time he got through, he was down **over $3 million**.

<!-- page 112: 1 nugget -->

<!-- page 113: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N080] **What caused the Canadian dollar collapse — political shock**:
  - The **1988 Canadian election** was about a month away. PM **Mulroney** had an
    enormous lead (24 points) over opponent **Turner**, who espoused very liberal
    views including support for an independent Quebec.
  - In a Sunday debate, Turner "destroyed" Mulroney. Polls showed Mulroney's
    24-point lead had **shrunk to 8 points overnight**. What had been a foregone
    conclusion was suddenly a toss-up.
  - Additional risk: Canada-U.S. trade agreement negotiations were underway — a
    Turner victory would have also jeopardized that deal.
  - McKay's response: got out of about 400 contracts, but "couldn't see it going
    down much further." Over the next 2-3 days, market broke even more. At -$7M,
    told his clerk "Get me out of everything."
  - Exit was the **exact bottom**. Within a month, price was back to pre-debate
    levels. He missed the entire recovery.
  - Net result: had made $2M on the rally, lost $7M on the break. Instead of making
    the planned $15M, he **lost approximately $5M net**.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N081] **The biggest loss wasn't the most anxiety**:
  - The Jamaica Canadian dollar disaster was McKay's worst loss ever (net -$5M),
    but NOT his most anxious experience.
  - Most anxiety: the **British pound November 1978** Carter rescue plan loss —
    because it was his **first big loss**.
  - Implication: the psychological impact of losses is calibrated to novelty, not
    just magnitude. First major loss hits hardest even when subsequent larger losses
    feel more familiar.

<!-- page 114: 2 nuggets -->

### The 1982 Stock Market — Applying Currency Intuition to Equities

- [SHOULD KNOW][N082] **McKay's entry into the 1982 stock market — and his broker
  selection method**:
  - In 1982, McKay noticed on evening news that the Dow was up almost every day.
    Developed strong bullish feelings — "This was the first time I had ever had any
    market feel based on something other than watching futures."
  - First broker: a friend who was a stockbroker. McKay wanted "a cross section of
    stocks" because he felt the market in general was going higher. But the broker's
    method was "exactly opposite" — buy the *weakest* stocks on the premise they
    could rise the most. "Well, that certainly wasn't my theory." After three months
    (Dow up, account down), McKay discovered broker was buying stocks near lows while
    McKay's instinct was to buy stocks moving higher. Closed the account.
  - **Solution**: walked into a **Merrill Lynch** office with a $1 million cashier's
    check and asked the branch manager to bring him the **least experienced broker**
    — "I wanted somebody without any opinions."
  - Instructions to the rookie: invest ¾ of the money in "a wide variety of stocks,
    all of which are at or near all-time highs." Each week, send a list of stocks
    broken down by market sector, ranking them by proximity to all-time highs.
  - Result: "He followed my instructions exactly, and I did very well in that
    account."
  - Takeaway: **the expert with wrong methodology is more dangerous than the novice
    who executes your methodology correctly.** McKay explicitly chose inexperience
    over experience to avoid unwanted opinion contamination.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N083] **McKay's stock methodology vs. conventional thinking**:
  - His approach: buy stocks at or near all-time highs, in strongest sectors
    (by proximity to highs). Send weekly sector rankings.
  - This is exactly his currency approach applied to equities — **buy strength, not
    weakness**. Mirrors "catch the easy part" and "wait until the move is already
    under way."
  - When the CME launched the **S&P 500 futures contract** (also 1982), McKay
    switched entirely: "I thanked my broker for his efforts, closed the account, and
    switched into buying S&P futures." The index future gave him a cleaner vehicle
    for his market-direction read without stock-picking.
  - Caught the S&P from **120 to 300** — an extraordinary run.

<!-- page 115: 2 nuggets -->

### Why McKay Was Bullish on Stocks in 1982

- [SHOULD KNOW][N084] **What made McKay bullish on stocks in 1982 — applying the
  market-response-to-fundamentals method**:
  - Stock market up almost every day without particular supporting news.
  - Fundamental backdrop was actually *negative*: inflation, interest rates, and
    unemployment were all still very high.
  - Key structural insight: "The stock market was virtually unchanged from its level
    twenty years earlier, while inflation had skyrocketed in the interim. Therefore,
    in real dollar terms, stock prices were extremely low."
  - Sentiment clue: "I liked the fact that most of the experts weren't particularly
    bullish." Specifically mentioned **Joe Granville** — a popular analyst who kept
    getting more bearish each time the market made a new high, even though he was
    supposed to be a technician.
  - The combination: market strength in the face of negative news + depressed real
    valuations + bearish expert consensus = classic McKay setup (market telling you
    something the fundamentals don't).

