### Trading Mistakes — Early Lessons on Cutting Losses

- [MUST KNOW][N085] **First significant loss — the lesson on cutting losses immediately,
  extended cost beyond the direct loss**:
  - McKay was short the **Deutsche mark** when the market went **limit-up**. He
    *could* still have gotten out at the limit-up price, but didn't. Next day:
    limit-up again. He ended up doubling his loss, and it took two months to recover
    to the account size he had before the trade.
  - Lesson: "You must get out of your losses immediately."
  - **Expanded cost framework** (key insight beyond the obvious): "It's not merely
    a matter of how much you can afford to risk on a given trade, but you also have
    to consider how many potential future winners you might miss because of the effect
    of the larger loss on your mental attitude and trading size."
  - Translation: losses are not just the direct dollars lost — they are also the
    opportunity cost of the **impaired mental state** and **reduced position sizing**
    that follow. A loss that takes 2 months to recover from means 2 months of
    sub-optimal trading psychology and size.
  - This is the most complete argument for strict loss limits: the real cost
    is 2x (direct loss + compounded opportunity cost).

> "It's not merely a matter of how much you can afford to risk on a given trade,
> but you also have to consider how many potential future winners you might miss
> because of the effect of the larger loss on your mental attitude and trading size."
> — Chuck McKay

<!-- page 120: 2 nuggets -->

### Market Structure Change — Why Breakouts Stopped Working

- [MUST KNOW][N086] **How professional dominance changed short-term market
  behavior — breakouts becoming traps**:
  - When most market participants were unsophisticated: retail traders waited for
    markets to hit headlines and make new highs before buying. This meant there was
    always a pool of **sideline money willing to buy** on breakouts — so breakouts
    *worked*.
  - With professional dominance: professionals are only on the sidelines when there
    is a large move in the **opposite direction** (i.e., they're waiting to buy
    dips, not buy breakouts). This means:
    - The "sideline fuel" that previously powered breakout continuation is absent
    - Breakouts now frequently attract professional counter-trend traders, turning
      them into false moves
  - McKay's conclusion: "The breakouts that work look similar to the breakouts that
    are sucker plays. In fact, the false breakouts probably outnumber the valid
    signals. Consequently, trading on breakouts is a strategy that I no longer employ."
  - **Adaptation**: major trends are now frequently **preceded by a sharp price
    change in the opposite direction**. McKay now "buy[s] on breaks and sell[s]
    on rallies" — the exact opposite of the breakout strategy that worked in the 1970s.
  - Caveat: "with a few exceptions" — the rule isn't absolute, but it's now the
    default.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N087] **Risk of waiting for reactions before entering**:
  - Schwager asks: if you always wait for a reaction before entering, don't you
    risk missing major moves?
  - *[Content continues past p. 120 — to be captured in next batch]*

---

### Summary of McKay's Core Principles (synthesized from pp. 99–120)

- [MUST KNOW][N088] **McKay's integrated trading framework — the complete picture**:
  1. **Market response over fundamentals**: don't trade what the news *says*; trade
     what the market *does* with that news. Bullish news that fails = sell. Bearish
     conditions that fail to break = buy.
  2. **Catch the easy part**: skip tops and bottoms. Enter after the move is clearly
     underway; exit before the choppy end. The meat of the move is the goal.
  3. **Government intervention as trade signal**: halfhearted intervention confirms
     the trend; forceful, rhetorical, nationalistic intervention = exit.
  4. **Exit immediately when hurt**: impaired objectivity + reduced size = extended
     opportunity cost beyond the direct loss.
  5. **Deceleration = warning**: market weakness can appear even while prices set
     new highs. Momentum change precedes direction change.
  6. **FOMO is a liability**: the most psychologically painful trades (missed moves)
     are traps. Manage positions you have; don't chase ones you missed.
  7. **Buy strength, not weakness**: in stocks and currencies, buy at or near
     all-time highs. The weakest-stock-up-most theory is the enemy of this approach.
  8. **Prognostication has declining value**: by 1991, execution (entry/exit timing)
     was 75% of the game vs. 10% in the 1970s. The market got more efficient.
  9. **Breakouts are now mostly traps**: professional dominance means false breakouts
     outnumber valid signals — buy breaks, sell rallies instead.

<!-- pp. 119-140 -->

<!-- page 119: 2 nuggets -->

### McKay on the 1982 Bull Market Entry (continued)

- [MUST KNOW][N089] **Contrarian expert consensus as a buy signal — the 1982
  S&P trade from 120 to 300**:
  - McKay's bullish read was NOT based on fundamentals — in fact,
    "inflation, interest rates, and unemployment were all still very high"
  - Signal came from price behavior: market was rising almost every day
    without supporting news
  - Secondary signal: stock prices were virtually unchanged in nominal
    terms from 20 years earlier, while inflation had skyrocketed — in
    REAL dollar terms, stocks were extremely cheap
  - Contrarian signal: "most of the experts weren't particularly bullish."
    **Joe Granville** — a popular analyst at the time — got MORE bearish
    every time the market made a new high. McKay found this "particularly
    amusing" since Granville was supposed to be a technician
  - Mechanism: bearish expert consensus = supply of sideline skeptics who
    would eventually have to buy in. When even technicians are bearish at
    new highs, you have a classic contrary opinion setup

- [MUST KNOW][N090] **The true cost of not exiting immediately — Deutsche
  mark limit-up lesson**:
  - McKay's first significant loss: short Deutsche mark when market went
    **limit-up**. He could have gotten out at limit-up but didn't
  - Next day: limit-up again. Result: doubled his loss AND took two months
    to recover to his prior account size
  - The deeper insight: "It's not merely a matter of how much you can
    afford to risk on a given trade, but you also have to consider how
    many potential future winners you might miss because of the effect of
    the larger loss on your **mental attitude and trading size**"
  - This reframes the cost of holding a loser: it's not just the dollar
    loss — it's the cascade of missed winners during recovery. A $1M loss
    that takes 2 months to recover costs you all the P&L you would have
    made in those 2 months trading at normal size and confidence

<!-- page 120: 1 nugget (overlap with prior batch, continuity) -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N091] **Answer to the FOMO risk in "always wait for a
  reaction" strategy**:
  - Schwager's question: if you always wait for a reaction, don't you risk
    missing major moves?
  - McKay: "Certainly, but so what? I've got thirty-eight markets on my
    screen. If I miss moves in ten of them, there will be ten others that
    have a price move."
  - Core principle: opportunity is abundant; the dangerous scenario is not
    missing moves but **being right on direction and still losing money**
    (i.e., buying rallies and selling breaks — being directionally correct
    but entering at the worst possible time)
  - The risk of chasing is worse than the risk of missing

<!-- page 121: 1 nugget -->

### McKay on Markets as a Chess Game

- [SHOULD KNOW][N092] **Markets as chess — different strategy vs.
  different opponent types**:
  - Schwager frames it: "When your opponent is a farmer or a dentist, you
    play one way, and when your opponent is a professional, you play
    another."
  - McKay: "No doubt about it. That's exactly right. You have to keep
    adapting to changes."
  - Implication: the strategy that worked when the market was populated by
    naive retail traders (buy breakouts) no longer works when the market
    is dominated by professionals (buy breaks, sell rallies). The market
    is not a static game — the correct strategy is a function of who you
    are playing against

<!-- page 122: 1 nugget -->

### McKay — Off the Floor

- [NICE TO KNOW][N093] **McKay's motivation for leaving the floor — life
  design as deliberate choice**:
  - Stopped floor trading when first child was born; "I was determined not
    to be one of those fathers that spends an hour with his kids before
    bedtime and that's it"
  - Used the financial freedom of self-employment to optimize for both
    wealth AND quality of life simultaneously
  - Transition difficulty: the first year off the floor was his only year
    in 12-13 years where he made less than the prior year
  - Floor edge: can make quick hits on prices being out of line by a tick
    (bid-ask arbitrage). Off the floor: must trade longer term because you
    have an execution **disadvantage** (you pay the spread instead of
    collecting it)

<!-- page 123: 2 nuggets -->

### McKay's Early Success Habits

- [MUST KNOW][N094] **Systematic trade review as the foundation of early
  success — why winners win and losers lose**:
  - Every day on the floor, McKay made copies of his trading cards and
    reviewed them at home that night
  - Goal: determine why the winning trades won and why the losing trades
    lost
  - Outcome: became more **selective** — knowing which setups had a track
    record of failure allowed him to avoid those categories entirely
  - This is not just journaling — it's classification with feedback:
    winners and losers sorted into types, with causal attribution for each
  - Key insight: a trader who analyzes his trades gains an empirical edge
    over one who doesn't — not by predicting markets better, but by
    filtering out his own worst tendencies

- [MUST KNOW][N095] **McKay's position-sizing drawdown protocol — the
  mechanics of never going broke**:
  - Core principle: "never let a loser get out of hand. You want to be
    sure that you can be wrong twenty or thirty times in a row and still
    have money in your account"
  - Protocol:
    - Normal: risk 5–10% of account per trade
    - After one loss: drop to ~4% regardless of conviction
    - After second loss: drop to ~2%
    - Keep reducing as long as losing
  - Scale demonstrated: went from trading **3,000 contracts** per trade
    to as few as **10 contracts** during cold spells, then back again
  - The psychological rationale: size reduction is not just risk
    management — it's a reset to avoid the hope-and-wish mentality that
    dominates when you're losing big

<!-- page 124: 1 nugget -->

### The Self-as-Trend Framework

- [MUST KNOW][N096] **Treating your own performance as a trend — the
  logic of trading yourself**:
  - Schwager: "In essence, then, you treat McKay as a trend as well."
  - McKay: "Definitely, and there's a logical reason for that. When you're
    trading well, you have a better mental attitude. When you're trading
    poorly, you start wishing and hoping. Instead of getting into trades
    you **think** will work, you end up getting into trades you **hope**
    will work."
  - The distinction between "think" and "hope" is the line between
    analysis-driven and emotion-driven trading
  - Practical implication: the only way back to the right frame of mind is
    by winning, and you can't win big if you're betting big while in the
    wrong mental state. Reduce size to rebuild confidence via small wins
  - McKay's position size varied by a factor of **100:1** between hot and
    cold streaks — this is not modest adjustment; it's a complete mode
    switch

<!-- page 125: 2 nuggets -->

### Winners vs. Losers — Profile Differences

- [MUST KNOW][N097] **Trading style must match personality — the single
  most important separator of winners and losers**:
  - McKay's observation: "virtually every successful trader I know
    ultimately ended up with a trading style suited to his personality"
  - Example: McKay's brother — meticulous, detail-oriented, loves
    organizing. He became a **spreader** (seeking profit from price
    discrepancies between related contracts, simultaneously long and short,
    net-neutral on market direction). He thrived because this matched
    his personality exactly
  - Contrast: McKay's speculator friends are risk-embracing thrill-seekers
    — would "fly off to Las Vegas at a moment's notice or climb a mountain
    in Africa"
  - The style categories where this applies: market arena, system vs.
    discretionary, fundamental vs. technical, position vs. spread,
    short-term vs. long-term, aggressive vs. conservative
  - Key counterintuitive point: "it doesn't make any difference what one's
    personality is, as long as there's no conflict between personality and
    trading style" — ANY personality can be a winning trader in the right
    arena

- [SHOULD KNOW][N098] **McKay's own personality-style integration —
  applying risk-taking and conservatism in their correct domains**:
  - Conservative upbringing (seminary, Catholic, "straight-down-the-line"
    kid) → became a risk-taker in personal life as an adult (Africa trip
    before it was popular, personal adventures)
  - His trading style integrates both: "I take the risk-oriented part of
    my personality and put it where it belongs: trading. And, I take the
    conservative part of my personality and put it where it belongs: money
    management."
  - The division of self: one part makes bold bets; the other ensures
    those bets never threaten the whole account

<!-- page 126: 1 nugget -->

### Advice for Losing Traders

- [SHOULD KNOW][N099] **Selectivity as the cure for most losing traders**:
  - McKay's diagnosis: "Sometimes the reason people lose is that they're
    not sufficiently selective"
  - Prescription: analyze all trades, identify which types consistently
    produce profits, concentrate only on those, abandon the rest
  - Hard truth: "if a trader analyzes his trades and still can't make
    money, then he probably should try another endeavor"
  - Implication: trading skill is knowable through analysis. A trader who
    cannot improve after systematic self-analysis is probably not suited
    to trading — not a temporary slump

<!-- page 127: 1 nugget (Schwager commentary begins) -->

### Schwager's Commentary on McKay (Chapter Synthesis)

- [MUST KNOW][N100] **Schwager's diagnosis: personality-style mismatch is
  the norm, not the exception**:
  - Common misconception: surely every trader chooses a method compatible
    with their personality? "Absolutely not!"
  - Common mismatch patterns Schwager documents:
    - Traders good at system development who constantly override their
      systems (self-sabotage)
    - Long-term oriented traders who trade short-term due to impatience or
      compulsion to "do something"
    - Natural floor traders who abandon the pit for portfolio management
      and become mediocre
    - Theoretically-minded arbitrage developers who become position traders
      — requiring risk tolerance far beyond their comfort zone
  - Root cause: individuals adopt the diametrically opposite style to
    "fulfill some emotional need"
  - The matching of personality to style "may be a matter of common sense,
    but it is certainly not common"

<!-- page 128: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N101] **McKay's unconventional use of fundamentals —
  market response as the signal, not the news itself**:
  - Although predominantly a technical trader, fundamentals play a
    "critical role" in McKay's strategy
  - His use is unconventional: he does NOT try to gauge whether
    fundamentals are bullish or bearish, nor does he place direct weight
    on whether news is bullish or bearish
  - Instead: "he focuses on the market's response to fundamental news"
  - Example: if the market is shrugging off a barrage of bearish news,
    McKay views that as evidence of an impending bull move
  - This is the same framework that undergirds his government intervention
    signals and his breakout analysis — the **market's reaction** to an
    event is more informative than the event itself

