## Chapter: William Eckhardt — The Mathematician

<!-- page 129: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N102] **William Eckhardt — background and the Turtle bet**:
  - Partner of **Richard Dennis** (perhaps the best-known futures
    speculator of his era)
  - Eckhardt bet Dennis that trading skill **could NOT be taught**; Dennis
    bet it could. The group of traders trained to resolve the wager became
    known as the **Turtles** — one of the most famous trading experiments
    in financial history
  - Eckhardt is a mathematician who nearly earned a Ph.D. in mathematical
    logic at the **University of Chicago**, traded on the floor for years,
    then transitioned to systems-based trading
  - Track record: average return of **62%** on managed accounts over five
    years (range: −7% in 1989 to +234% in 1987); averaged better than
    **60% per year** in his own account since 1978; 1989 was his only
    losing year
  - Why emerging from anonymity (at time of interview): "I got tired of
    seeing my students managing hundreds of millions while I was managing
    comparatively paltry amounts"

- [NICE TO KNOW][N103] **Eckhardt's intellectual passions outside trading
  — a trader as scientist**:
  - Trading is the means by which he funds his personal scientific
    research interests
  - Areas of study: **Bell's theorem** (quantum mechanics) — how
    measurements on distantly separated particles can determine each other
    with no possible influence passing between them; **sexual
    reproduction** riddle (why did evolution produce a mechanism where
    organisms pass on only 50% of genes vs. 100% in asexual reproduction?);
    **the nature of time** (his premise: the passage of time is an
    illusion)
  - This background matters because it shapes his approach to trading:
    rigorous, paradox-seeking, comfortable with counterintuitive conclusions

<!-- page 130: 1 nugget -->

### How Eckhardt Became a Trader

- [NICE TO KNOW][N104] **Eckhardt's accidental entry into trading — a
  dissertation blocked by academic politics**:
  - Was writing doctoral dissertation on mathematical logic at U of Chicago
    under a world-famous mathematician
  - New faculty member whose specialty was mathematical logic joined the
    department → supervisory role shifted to him → he wanted Eckhardt to
    write a completely different thesis
  - After completing all coursework, exams, and three-quarters of his
    dissertation, his progress was "stymied" by academic politics
  - Dennis suggested a sabbatical to try floor trading → Eckhardt tried it
    → never returned to school
  - Lesson: sometimes the detour IS the destination

<!-- page 131: 2 nuggets -->

### Floor Trading — The Bid-Ask Edge vs. Market Theory

- [MUST KNOW][N105] **Why floor trader success is NOT what it appears —
  the bid-ask spread as the hidden engine**:
  - Eckhardt's floor strategy: "buy when weak hands were selling and sell
    when they were buying" — but in retrospect, not sure that strategy had
    anything to do with success
  - Key insight: "if you assume that the true theoretical price is
    somewhere between the bid and the offer, then if you buy on the bid,
    you're buying the market for a little less than it's worth. Similarly,
    if you sell on the offer, you're selling it for a little more than
    it's worth."
  - Result: "on balance, my trades had a positive expected return,
    regardless of my strategy. That fact alone could very well have
    represented 100 percent of my success."
  - **Why pit traders using "bogus systems" still profit**: many floor
    traders subscribe to moving averages, lunar cycles, etc. When they get
    signals, they buy on the bid or sell on the offer. At month end, they
    have a profit and attribute it to their system — but the system is
    vacuous. The edge is structural, not analytical
  - Implication: **off-floor traders cannot rely on this structural edge**
    — they must have genuinely valid market ideas or systems because they
    pay the spread rather than collect it

- [MUST KNOW][N106] **Transaction costs as the primary reason small
  customer accounts fail**:
  - Small traders fail "not because they're so inevitably wrong, but simply
    because they can't beat their own transaction costs"
  - Transaction costs = commissions PLUS **slippage** (the skid between
    the price when you decide to trade and the fill you actually get)
  - As a pit trader, Eckhardt was "on the other side of that skid" —
    he was profiting from the very friction that destroys customer accounts
  - This is a structural asymmetry that even a correct forecast cannot
    overcome if the trading frequency is high

<!-- page 132: 1 nugget -->

### Statistics and Trading — Robust vs. Delicate Estimators

- [MUST KNOW][N107] **Why classical statistics fail in trading — the
  distribution assumption problem**:
  - Classical statistics assume data follows a normal distribution (or
    another known form). "If your distribution assumptions are even a
    little bit off, the error is enough to derail the delicate statistical
    estimators"
  - In those cases, "cruder, robust estimators will yield more accurate
    results"
  - Eckhardt's belief: **price distributions are "pathological"** — they
    have more variance than expected under normal distribution theory
  - **Benoit Mandelbrot's conjecture** (originator of the concept of
    fractional dimension): price change distributions may have
    **infinite variance** — the sample variance just grows larger and
    larger as you add more data
  - If true: most standard statistical techniques are invalid for price
    data, and any classically-derived estimate of risk will be
    **significantly understated**

<!-- page 133: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N108] **Infinite variance illustrated — the coin toss
  waiting-time paradox**:
  - Example: in a random walk (fair coin toss), measure the average waiting
    time between successive ties (heads total = tails total)
  - Typically the wait is short — but occasionally heads or tails gets far
    ahead, and the wait for the next tie can be enormous; additional tosses
    are just as likely to increase the discrepancy as reduce it
  - Result: the sample consists of many short waits and a few very large
    outliers. The distribution has **no finite average** — as you add data,
    the sample average grows inexorably
  - Market analog: most days are small moves, but occasionally extreme
    events occur that dwarf all prior observations. **October 19, 1987**:
    an 8,000-point drop in the S&P — normal distribution theory says
    a one-day move this large should happen "a few times in a millennium."
    It happened within a decade of the S&P contract's inception
  - Conclusion: "if market prices don't have a finite variance, any
    classically derived estimate of risk will be significantly understated"

<!-- page 134: 2 nuggets -->

### Robust Approaches — Indicator Weighting and Trade Sizing

- [MUST KNOW][N109] **Binary indicator weighting — the counterintuitive
  optimal from robust statistics**:
  - Question: when combining multiple indicators for a market, how should
    you weight them?
  - Naive approach: assign optimized weights based on each indicator's
    historical contribution
  - Robust statistics answer: "in most circumstances, the best strategy is
    not some optimized weighting scheme, but rather weighting each
    indicator by **1 or 0**"
  - Logic: if an indicator is good enough to use at all, it's good enough
    to weight equally with the others. If it can't meet that minimum
    standard, don't use it at all
  - Same principle for **trade sizing**: either a trade is good enough to
    take at full size, or it's not worth taking at all. Partial positions
    are rarely optimal under robust assumptions
  - This is a direct challenge to portfolio optimization theory and Kelly
    criterion variants that rely on precise return estimates

- [MUST KNOW][N110] **Chart angles (Gann, 45-degree lines) are
  mathematically invalid — the scale-invariance test**:
  - Rule: "any meaningful approach must be invariant to the choice of
    units"
  - **Bar chart angles fail this test**: the angle of a line on a bar
    chart depends entirely on the choice of price and time scales — which
    are arbitrary
  - Proof: draw the same market on two chart services with different price
    and time scales. A 45-degree angle on one chart will be a completely
    different angle on the other. They will intersect the price series at
    different places → they cannot both be describing a property of the
    price series
  - What IS scale-invariant: **trend lines connecting two or more price
    points** — these intersect the price series at exactly the same places
    regardless of the chart's scale
  - Psychological trap: "how sharply a trend slopes on a chart is often a
    psychological consideration in making a trade" — if you're influenced
    by this, you're letting the **chart maker's aesthetic choices** impinge
    on your trading
  - Computer advantage: if you tried to program angle-sensitivity into a
    system, it would immediately become obvious that the slope value
    depends on arbitrary scale choices → forces intellectual honesty

<!-- page 135: 1 nugget -->

### Analytical Pitfalls in System Design

- [MUST KNOW][N111] **Postdictive errors — the most common and damaging
  system design flaw**:
  - **Postdictive**: using information that can only be available after the
    fact
  - Obvious form: programming error — e.g., using the closing price in a
    computation to decide whether to initiate a trade *before* the close.
    These usually betray themselves via unrealistically good backtests
  - Subtle form: the highest prices in any data set are followed by lower
    prices "nearly by definition." If you build rules that depend on
    finding peaks (e.g., seasonal high rules), you've used future knowledge
    — the rule works on past data only because you know where the peak was
  - Hard to detect because they feel legitimate until you understand the
    mechanism

<!-- page 136: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N112] **Overfitting and degrees of freedom — the core
  tension in system design**:
  - **Degree of freedom**: a parameter that yields a different system for
    every allowed value (e.g., the number of days in a moving average)
  - More degrees of freedom = more ability to fit the system to past data
    = less validity for future data
  - "Bad" degrees of freedom: a parameter that only affects a small number
    of large trends in your data. By fitting to the idiosyncrasies of those
    few outlier events, such a parameter can substantially overfit even if
    the total parameter count is manageable
  - Also: "hidden" degrees of freedom — structural alternatives within the
    system that aren't named parameters but still give the system multiple
    chances to conform to past data
  - Eckhardt's practical limit: **3–4 degrees of freedom is fine; 7–8 is
    too many**
  - Method for calibrating intuition: "add bogus degrees of freedom and
    see what you can get" — this teaches you how much spurious benefit
    freedom gives you

- [MUST KNOW][N113] **The optimization dilemma — finding the sweet spot
  between underfitting and overfitting**:
  - Too little optimization: system is "vastly inferior to what it could be"
  - Too much optimization: system "tells you more about the past than the
    future"
  - No algorithmic escape from this trade-off — must develop intuition
    through experience across "hundreds of examples" with many different
    systems

<!-- page 137: 2 nuggets -->

### System Validity and Falsification

- [MUST KNOW][N114] **The "sock you in the eye" test for system validity —
  and the Popper falsification principle**:
  - First validity check: "If the performance results of the system don't
    sock you in the eye, then it's probably not worth pursuing. It has to
    be an outstanding result."
  - Corollary: "if you need delicate, assumption-laden statistical
    techniques to get superior performance results, then you should be very
    suspicious of the system's validity"
  - **Karl Popper's falsification principle** applied to trading: all
    progress in knowledge comes from efforts to *falsify*, not confirm,
    theories. Applied to trading research: "you have to try your best to
    disprove your results. You have to try to **kill your little
    creation**"
  - "The better a system looks, the more adamant you should be in trying
    to disprove it" — human nature wants to confirm; rigorous research
    demands the opposite
  - "If you challenge your system by sincerely trying to disprove it,
    then maybe, just maybe, it's valid"
  - This directly counters the typical trader/system-developer incentive to
    cherry-pick supporting evidence

- [MUST KNOW][N115] **Why chart patterns mostly fail — the human
  pattern-recognition bias**:
  - Eckhardt's verdict: "Most things that look good on a chart — say, 98
    percent — don't work"
  - Root cause 1: "The human mind was made to create patterns. It will see
    patterns in random data." Classic statistics warning: "Too fine an eye
    for pattern will find it anywhere"
  - Root cause 2: humans don't look at data neutrally — "when the human
    eye scans a chart, it doesn't give all data points equal weight.
    Instead, it will tend to focus on certain outstanding cases, and we
    tend to form our opinions" from those unrepresentative samples
    (classic **availability bias** and **salience bias**)
  - Implication: a pattern that looks compelling visually has likely
    already been distorted by the observer's tendency to over-weight
    memorable examples and find structure in noise

