<!-- pp. 139-160 -->

## Chapter: William Eckhardt (continued — interview begins before p. 139)

*Note: William Eckhardt is Richard Dennis's partner and co-developer of the
Turtle trading program. This batch picks up mid-interview on system design.*

<!-- page 139: 3 nuggets -->

### Degrees of Freedom and Optimization

- [SHOULD KNOW][N089] **Degrees of freedom limit in a trading system**:
  - 3 or 4 free parameters = fine
  - 7 or 8 = probably too many
  - More parameters = more curve-fitting risk; past performance becomes
    less reliable as a predictor of future performance
  - You develop intuition about the trade-off between degrees of freedom
    and out-of-sample reliability over time

- [MUST KNOW][N090] **The optimization dilemma — caught between two bad
  outcomes**:
  - *Avoid optimization entirely*: system will be vastly inferior to what
    it could be
  - *Optimize too much*: system tells you more about the past than the future
  - No clean solution — must "mediate between the two extremes" with
    methodological care
  - Without care, optimization produces results that are "not reproducible"
  - Practical implication: optimization is legitimate but demands rigor

- [MUST KNOW][N091] **The outstanding-result test for system validity**:
  - "If the performance results of the system don't sock you in the eye,
    then it's probably not worth pursuing. It has to be an outstanding result."
  - Corollary: if you *need* delicate, assumption-laden statistical
    techniques to demonstrate superior performance, be very suspicious
    of the system's validity
  - The bar must be high enough that the edge is obvious, not statistical

<!-- page 140: 3 nuggets -->

### The Falsification Mindset

- [MUST KNOW][N092] **Karl Popper's falsification principle applied to
  system testing**:
  - **Karl Popper** championed the idea that all progress in knowledge
    results from efforts to *falsify*, not confirm, theories
  - Applied to trading research: "You have to try your best to disprove
    your results. You have to try to kill your little creation."
  - "The better a system looks, the more adamant you should be in trying
    to disprove it." This goes against human nature, which wants the
    historical performance to look as good as possible.
  - Only if the system survives sincere falsification attempts does it
    have a chance of being valid
  - Practical: think of everything that could be wrong with the system
    and everything suspicious about it

> "If you challenge your system by sincerely trying to disprove it, then
> maybe, just maybe, it's valid." — William Eckhardt

- [SHOULD KNOW][N093] **Chart patterns: 98% don't work**:
  - "Most things that look good on a chart — say, 98 percent — don't work"
  - Root cause: human mind is built to create patterns. It will see
    patterns in random data
  - A turn-of-the-century statistics textbook: "Too fine an eye for
    pattern will find it anywhere"
  - The eye does not scan charts neutrally — it gives unequal weight to
    data points, focusing on outstanding cases (the big wins), and forms
    opinions from those special cases
  - Result: researcher walks away thinking the system is far better than
    it really is

<!-- page 141: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N094] **Confirmation bias is universal — even careful hand
  testing is infected**:
  - Human nature picks out stunning successes and overlooks grinding
    day-in, day-out losses
  - Even careful hand research has a strong tendency to bias results —
    this is why science uses double-blind tests
  - "Even the most honest researcher will tend to bias data toward his or
    her hypothesis. It can't be helped."
  - Eckhardt's practice: when doing research by hand, he discounted
    results by 20–50 percent to compensate
  - Only computer testing can remove the researcher's thumb from the scale

- [MUST KNOW][N095] **Hand testing anecdote — the stochastics illusion**:
  - Eckhardt tested a system on a San Francisco–New York flight using
    stochastics in an unconventional way; it looked "terrific" on multiple
    markets
  - When computer-tested, the system lost money
  - Cause: his visual alignment of the indicator (bottom of chart) and
    price (top) was off by about one day
  - Signals occurred during rapid price movement — being off by one day
    was the difference between being on the wrong side of a 500-point move
    vs. the right side of a 1,000-point move in the S&P (~$5,000 difference)
  - "What had actually looked like a great system proved to be totally
    worthless. Ever since then, I've been very cautious about drawing any
    conclusions from hand testing. I now wait until the computer results
    are in."

<!-- page 142: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N096] **Pattern-finding bias = same quirk as superstition,
  astrology, fortune-telling**:
  - The desire to find patterns that convinces people of astrology/
    fortune-telling validity is identical to the chart-pattern trap
  - Successes are more startling than failures; the oracle's hits stand
    out while ambiguous or wrong predictions are forgotten

- [SHOULD KNOW][N097] **Chart reading as discretionary practice has
  psychological costs**:
  - Pattern recognition chart traders tend to size up trades they "really
    like" — inconsistent sizing is generally a bad idea
  - Worse: if you believe your eye creates the profitable situation, you
    feel "excessively responsible" when the trade doesn't work —
    "very destabilizing"
  - Mechanical system advantage: losses are "just an expected part of the
    system" — your judgment is on the line for the *system's* overall
    performance, not any single trade

<!-- page 143: 2 nuggets -->

### Mechanical Systems vs. Discretionary Intervention

- [MUST KNOW][N098] **When to override a mechanical system**:
  - Systems are "rote algorithms" — simpleminded and cannot anticipate
    all situations
  - Any system traded at "a level large enough will occasionally stray
    into overly risky terrain"
  - Scaling down to the worst cases is one solution but too costly in
    overall performance
  - Better approach: "trade at a reasonable level, and when you find
    yourself with too much exposure, override your system and cut back"
  - Also: a good system will occasionally direct something stupid — "your
    own judgment is vital"
  - **Rule**: override only when the system is "clearly violating the
    intentions of its design" or when exposure becomes dangerously large
  - "Don't get into the habit of finagling the system day in and day out.
    Save your ingenuity and creativity for research."

<!-- page 144: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N099] **1987 crash Eurodollar override — system violation
  of design intent**:
  - On October 19, 1987 (Black Monday): Eckhardt was short S&Ps *and*
    short Eurodollars
  - S&Ps fell 8,000 points; Eurodollars fell only 5 points (should have
    fallen 40–50 in sympathy)
  - Despite the system still being short Eurodollars, he covered — the
    market action violated his expectations
  - Result: Eurodollars opened ~300 points *higher* the next day → the
    override was correct
  - Key principle: when the market responds in a way that is inconsistent
    with the rationale of the trade, it is a valid reason to override

- [SHOULD KNOW][N100] **When NOT to systematically add rules for every
  override situation**:
  - Don't change the system every time it does something you don't like
  - Change only for: (a) repeatedly encountered structural flaws, or
    (b) identified persistent problem types
  - Adding a rule for something that happens less than once a year is
    adding an unnecessary degree of freedom
  - "A designer cannot anticipate all possible situations"

<!-- page 145: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N101] **Gulf War override — missing a good trade is worse
  than a bad trade**:
  - Gulf War presented an "unprecedented situation" (war by deadline):
    instinct was not to trade
  - Eckhardt's view: "missing an important trade is a much more serious
    error than making a bad trade"
  - Reasoning:
    - Any worthwhile system has *backups* to protect you when you take
      a bad trade (stops, exits)
    - Missing a good trade has *nothing* to protect you — no mechanism
      to guarantee you eventually get in
    - Missing a major trade is demoralizing and destabilizing; can take
      weeks to get back on track — costs more than just the money not made
  - Override decision: he took the trades but cut normal position size
    in half
  - Result: "I got clobbered, or, more accurately, half-clobbered"
  - Lesson: the override principle (reduced size for unprecedented
    uncertainty) was sound even if the outcome was bad

<!-- page 146: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N102] **Override that backfired — emotional profit-taking
  as countertrend impulse**:
  - After a longer-than-usual losing streak, Eckhardt was long currencies
    that moved sharply higher over a weekend
  - Rationale given for taking profits on half the position: "reducing
    exposure because of increased volatility"
  - Real reason: after much losing, "I couldn't stand the idea of giving
    back all that profit"
  - Actual exposure across all markets was light — he could have easily
    afforded the position
  - Shortly after, currencies had another upside explosion *exceeding the
    first*
  - "Such willfully missed opportunities hurt more than losses"
  - Pattern: emotional override under stress leads to exiting winning
    positions prematurely — the countertrend impulse disguised as
    risk management

<!-- page 147: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N103] **Empirical test: mechanical system outperformed
  discretionary trading**:
  - Eckhardt simultaneously traded his own account (with overrides) and
    managed an associate's account run *purely mechanically*
  - Result: "the account trading entirely on the mechanical system
    definitely did better"
  - His prior belief: he could outperform the system in a mediocre year
    even if the system wins in windfall years
  - "This experience indicated otherwise"
  - Why overrides felt like they helped: the dramatic "smart" moves stick
    in memory; the day-in, day-out slippage from tinkering is forgotten
  - "Clearly, my overriding was costing me money, even though I thought
    otherwise" — a concrete instance of the confirmation bias in real
    trading decisions

- [SHOULD KNOW][N104] **Revised policy on overriding**:
  - Should be "a far more selective process"
  - Express ingenuity by doing research at night, *not* by overriding
    during the day
  - Override only in unexpected circumstances — and then only with
    "great forethought"
  - "If you find yourself overriding routinely, it's a sure sign that
    there's something you want in the system that hasn't been included"
    → fix the system, don't patch it daily

<!-- page 148: 2 nuggets -->

### Entry Philosophy: Don't Buy Retracements

- [SHOULD KNOW][N105] **Against buying retracements — the "false comfort"
  trap**:
  - If the market is going up and you think you should be long, Eckhardt
    prefers to "buy when the market is strong than wait for a retracement"
  - Buying on retracements is "psychologically seductive" — feels like
    getting a bargain vs. earlier price
  - Reality: if the market has retraced *enough to matter*, the trade is
    no longer as good — enhanced chance the trend is turning
  - A strategy of waiting for retracements "will often result in your
    missing the trade entirely or being forced to buy at an even higher
    price"
  - "Buying on retracements is one of those ploys that gives psychological
    satisfaction rather than providing any benefits in terms of increased
    profits."
  - General rule: "avoid those things that give you comfort; it's usually
    false comfort"

### Why Trend-Following Works

- [SHOULD KNOW][N106] **Mechanism behind trend-following — threshold
  discontinuity theory**:
  - People focus on a few broad probable outcomes and ignore
    low-probability scenarios
  - As certain outcomes become less or more likely, neglected scenarios
    of small probability suddenly "pop into view" — a **threshold
    phenomenon**
  - The market must discount these "new" possibilities somewhat
    *discontinuously* — not gradually
  - Trend-following works because moves of a characteristic size are
    more than randomly likely to be the beginnings of these discontinuous
    adjustments
  - The inference problem: distinguishing the initial part of a genuine
    discontinuous adjustment from a random swing

<!-- page 149: 2 nuggets -->

### Commercial Systems and Contrary Opinion

- [SHOULD KNOW][N107] **Commercial systems are nearly all worthless**:
  - Eckhardt reviewed ~50 publicly sold systems
  - Of those 50: only 1 had any value — and not as a complete system,
    but as an element he could use later
  - Root cause: they "tend to overfit the past data"
  - Whether this is naïveté or deliberate: "at this late date, it's
    probably predominantly disingenuous"
  - If a system truly had good profitability, low volatility, and
    durability, a developer who invested years building it would want to
    use it, not sell it
  - "Something good isn't discovered on a Greyhound bus while leafing
    through the charts; it's something developed over a period of years"
  - Practical advice: if you have the resources to evaluate systems,
    your time is better spent developing your own ideas

- [SHOULD KNOW][N108] **Contrary opinion: theoretically sound, empirically
  flawed**:
  - Contrary opinion = trading against the majority in individual trades
  - Theoretically might work given the *right* information about market
    composition
  - In practice: information used (consensus numbers from newsletters,
    advisory services) models a "very nonrepresentative group of traders —
    those who trade on market-letter advice"
  - Eckhardt's empirical research finding: consensus figures are
    marginally profitable to *buy* (not sell) a market with an extremely
    high bullish consensus — the opposite of the standard contrary opinion
    application

<!-- page 150: 3 nuggets -->

### RSI, Stochastics, and Popular Indicators

- [MUST KNOW][N109] **RSI and stochastics: nearly worthless as trading
  tools**:
  - "I think these indicators are nearly worthless."
  - Net expected profit near zero: "What these patterns make during market
    consolidations, they lose during trends"
  - Why they *look* good on charts: the eye picks up the times they
    accurately called minor tops and bottoms; it misses all the false
    signals and the full extent to which they were wrong during trends
  - Distinction between research and trading: "you can be very promiscuous
    in your research, but not in your trading" — studying them is fine;
    trading them is not

- [MUST KNOW][N110] **The prior/posterior probability confusion — why
  most technical signals fail**:
  - Classic mistake: observing that a large fraction of tops/bottoms have
    property X and concluding that property X signals a top/bottom
  - The flaw: you want P(top | property X), not P(property X | top)
  - Example: "It's true that a lot of extremes have reversal days." A
    **reversal day** = market reaches new high (low), then reverses,
    closing below (above) one or more immediately preceding daily closes
  - This only tells you P(reversal day | price extreme) is high
  - What matters: P(extreme — i.e., sustained trend change | reversal day)
    — a very different, much lower probability
  - "If 85 percent of all tops and bottoms have property X, but property X
    also occurs often enough in other places, using that indicator as a
    signal will rip you to shreds"
  - Also: these indicators appeal because they play into human tendencies
    to trade countertrend or abbreviate trend-following trades — "it's
    always tempting to liquidate a good trade on flimsy evidence"

<!-- page 151: 2 nuggets -->

### Cyclical Analysis

- [MUST KNOW][N111] **Cyclical analysis definitively fails rigorous
  scientific testing**:
  - **Fourier analysis** (invented in the 19th century to understand heat
    transfer) is the gold standard mathematical tool for detecting cycles
  - It has been applied to market prices starting with **Louis Bachelier**
    (French mathematician, late 19th century)
  - Result: "All this scientific research has failed to uncover any
    systematic cyclic components in price data"
  - Key insight: the techniques for finding cycles are *much stronger*
    than the techniques for finding trends — and they find nothing
  - Alleged cycles: markets go up and down (loose sense), but sine waves
    can be fitted "pretty closely even to purely random patterns"
  - If you allow cycle periods to shrink and expand, skip beats, and even
    invert — as "cycle cranks" do — you can fit cycles onto any fluctuating
    data series
  - "Rigorous statistical techniques, such as Fourier analysis, demonstrate
    that these alleged cycles are practically random"

<!-- page 152: 2 nuggets -->

### AI, Random Walk, and System Proliferation

- [SHOULD KNOW][N112] **AI will eventually dominate trading**:
  - Eckhardt: eventually cybernetic devices will outperform humans at
    every task including trading
  - "I can't believe that just because we're made of carbon and phosphorus
    there are things we can do that silicon and copper can't"
  - Cybernetic devices lack many human limitations → eventual superiority
  - "I have no doubt that eventually the world's best trader will be an
    automaton" — though not soon, probably within the next few generations
    *(note: said circa early 1990s)*

- [MUST KNOW][N113] **The case against random walk — evidence is
  "staggering"**:
  - Academic view when Eckhardt started: mechanical trading was
    "crackpot stuff"
  - "The evidence against the random walk theory of market action is
    staggering. Hundreds of traders and managers have profited from
    price-based mechanical systems."
  - The "just luck" counter-argument: systems worked year after year,
    were taught to others, those people managed others' money —
    probability of this all being chance is "infinitesimally small"
  - Academic shift: "System trading has gone from a fringe idea to being
    a new kind of orthodoxy"
  - Caveat: Eckhardt finds it "unsettling" that what began as renegade
    is now conventional wisdom

<!-- page 153: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N114] **Random walk as a negative proposition — can't
  be definitively proved**:
  - "Although the contention that the markets are random is an affirmative
    proposition, in fact you're trying to prove a negative" — that there
    is no systematic component
  - Analogy: proving there are no chocolate cakes orbiting Jupiter — may
    be true, very hard to prove
  - In the absence of contrary evidence, random walk might have been
    plausible; given the current evidence, "any academic who still espouses
    the idea that the markets are random is not looking at the realities"

<!-- page 154: 2 nuggets -->

### System Proliferation and the Red Queen Problem

- [MUST KNOW][N115] **The Red Queen Hypothesis applied to systems trading**:
  - Question: will the proliferation of system traders eventually kill
    the trend-following edge?
  - Two conflicting evidence streams: (1) quantitative statistics show
    systems continue to work; (2) qualitative argument that preponderance
    of system traders must eventually change markets enough to eliminate
    the profit opportunity
  - Eckhardt's view: "System traders still have an important old ally:
    human nature. Human nature has not changed."
  - But: "there's no question that the game has become much more difficult"
  - **Red Queen Hypothesis** (from evolutionary biology, *Alice in
    Wonderland*): in a country where you must "run as fast as you could
    just to stay in place" — competition so severe a species must evolve
    at maximum speed just to remain viable
  - Applied to system trading: "there is such strong competition in the
    systems trading niche that the trader has to develop systems as fast
    as he or she can to merely stay in place"
  - Implication: system traders must continuously innovate, not rest on
    historical methods

- [SHOULD KNOW][N116] **The recent-data dilemma for system development**:
  - Common proposed solution to historical invalidation: develop systems
    using only recent data
  - The problem: "Recent data has to be less statistically significant
    than long-term historical data simply because there is a lot less of it"
  - Systems developed solely on recent data are "flimsily supported"
  - There is no clean resolution: long historical data may be invalid due
    to market changes; recent data may be statistically insufficient
  - Eckhardt prefers systems with lower theoretical performance if he
    believes they have the property of being *different* from what most
    other system traders are using

<!-- page 155: 2 nuggets -->

### Money Management

- [MUST KNOW][N117] **Money management is more important than the price
  model — and easier**:
  - "If I were starting out again, I would concentrate more on money
    management. To my regret, it was something that I ignored in my
    early years."
  - Paradox: "even though money management is more important than the
    price model, mathematically, it's the more tractable problem"
  - Implication: most traders optimize the wrong thing (entry/exit
    signals) while neglecting the higher-leverage variable (position
    sizing and risk management)

- [MUST KNOW][N118] **The logarithmic utility function problem and the
  St. Petersburg Paradox**:
  - Many money management schemes assume a **logarithmic utility function**
    — additional wealth has constant utility for equal *percentage*
    increases in wealth
  - Problem: this function is **unbounded** — it will eventually tell you
    to "bet the ranch"
  - The **St. Petersburg Paradox** demonstrates why unbounded utility is
    wrong: with a billion-dollar net worth and an unbounded utility
    function, there must exist some prize for which you'd flip a coin
    against your entire net worth — "there's no amount of money for which
    a sane person would gamble away a billion-dollar net worth on the
    flip of a coin"
  - Therefore unbounded utility functions are inherently flawed as a
    model of rational decision-making
  - Eckhardt's solution: use only **bounded utility functions**
  - Added desirable property: optimal investment fractions are
    *independent of absolute wealth level* — the same system works
    whether you have $100K or $100M

<!-- page 156: 3 nuggets -->

### Position Sizing: The Whale Graph

- [MUST KNOW][N119] **The position-sizing "whale graph" — never optimize
  bet size**:
  - Plot performance against position size: get a graph resembling a
    "rightward-facing, high-foreheaded cartoon whale"
  - Left side (small position): nearly linear — increasing size yields
    proportionate increase in performance
  - Past the optimum ("where the whale's blow-hole would be"): slope
    flattens, then graph plummets sharply
  - The plummet occurs because increasingly large drawdowns force you to
    trade smaller, inhibiting recovery from losing streaks
  - Critical finding: "an average position size only modestly larger than
    the theoretical optimum gives a negative performance"
  - The optimum is right at the precipice
  - **Rule**: "Trading size is one aspect you don't want to optimize."
    Your trading size should lie at the "high end of the range in which
    the graph is still nearly straight" — before the curve begins
  - Practical guideline: never plan to risk more than 2% on a single trade
    (acknowledging gaps can force more)

<!-- page 157: 2 nuggets -->

### Intelligence, Emotional Makeup, and the Turtle Program

- [SHOULD KNOW][N120] **Intelligence is not the key to trading success**:
  - "I haven't seen much correlation between good trading and intelligence."
  - Some outstanding traders are quite intelligent; a few aren't; many
    outstandingly intelligent people are horrible traders
  - "Average intelligence is enough. Beyond that, emotional makeup is more
    important."

- [MUST KNOW][N121] **The Turtle program — and Eckhardt being proven wrong
  about whether trading can be taught**:
  - Background: Eckhardt and **Richard Dennis** had a disagreement about
    whether successful trading could be taught
  - Eckhardt argued it *couldn't* be: "just because we could do it didn't
    necessarily mean that we could teach it. I assumed that a trader added
    something that couldn't be encapsulated in a mechanical program."
  - Result: "I was proven wrong. The Turtle program proved to be an
    outstanding success. By and large, they learned to trade exceedingly
    well."
  - Conclusion: "The answer to the question of whether trading can be
    taught has to be an unqualified yes."

<!-- page 158: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N122] **Whether the Turtle systems have degraded with
  proliferation**:
  - With hundreds of millions under management, if the Turtles were still
    trading the same way, it would be hard for that not to affect the
    systems' performance
  - Eckhardt assumes many Turtles are now doing things differently
  - Tied to the broader Red Queen point (N115): once a system becomes
    widely known, its edge is at risk

- [MUST KNOW][N123] **Trading can be taught, but only a small percentage
  will actually succeed**:
  - "Anyone with average intelligence can learn to trade. This is not
    rocket science."
  - BUT: "it's much easier to learn what you should do in trading than to
    do it"
  - "Good systems tend to violate normal human tendencies"
  - Of the people who can learn the basics, only a small percentage will
    be successful traders — the barrier is psychological, not intellectual
  - Market structure analogy: in any long-running betting game, equity
    flows from the many to the few; skill accelerates this concentration
  - "In the long run, the majority loses. The implication for the trader
    is that to win you have to act like the minority."
  - "If you bring normal human habits and tendencies to trading, you'll
    gravitate toward the majority and inevitably lose."

<!-- page 159: 3 nuggets -->

### Human Nature as the Core Enemy of Trading

- [MUST KNOW][N124] **The two instinctive biases that destroy traders:
  take profits, play with losses**:
  - Decision theorist experiments: subjects choose a *sure gain* over a
    lottery with higher expected gain — but will choose a *worse lottery*
    over a sure loss (as long as the lottery offers a chance of coming
    out ahead)
  - These instincts are "evidently" hardwired: prefer certainty on the
    upside, prefer gambling on the downside
  - Applied to trading: "take your profits, but play with your losses"
    — the exact opposite of what produces profits
  - Culturally reinforced: "Seize opportunities, but hold your ground in
    adversity" — the conventional wisdom is precisely wrong for traders
  - Better trading advice: "Watch idly while profit-taking opportunities
    arise, but in adversity run like a jackrabbit"

> "One common adage on this subject that is completely wrongheaded is:
> You can't go broke taking profits. That's precisely how many traders
> *do* go broke." — William Eckhardt

  - Amateurs go broke by taking large losses; professionals go broke by
    taking small profits
  - Root problem: "human nature does not operate to maximize gain but
    rather to maximize the chance of a gain"
  - The desire to maximize *number of winning trades* (or minimize
    *number of losing trades*) works against the trader
  - "The success rate of trades is the least important performance
    statistic and may even be inversely related to performance"

<!-- page 160: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N125] **"The call of the countertrend" — why humans
  instinctively trade wrong**:
  - "There's a constellation of cognitive and emotional factors that makes
    people automatically countertrend in their approach."
  - People want to buy cheap and sell dear — which by definition makes
    them countertrend
  - The problem: *cheapness/dearness must be anchored to something*
  - People anchor to prices they're used to seeing as "normal"; prices
    that move away from those levels are perceived as "aberrant"
  - This leads to trading *against* emerging trends on the assumption that
    prices will eventually return to "normal"
  - "Therein lies the path to disaster"
  - This is not just inexperience — it is a deeply rooted cognitive bias
    tied to anchoring and mean-reversion intuition that does not reflect
    market reality

- [SHOULD KNOW][N126] **Chapter ends mid-sentence** — content continues
  in the next batch. The final question "What other aspects of human
  nature impede trading success?" is introduced but not answered on
  page 160.

<!-- pp. 159-180 -->
<!-- pages 159-160: overlap with previous batch — see prior notes for N124-N126 -->

## Chapter: William Eckhardt (continued)

### Long-Run Distribution vs. Current Position Focus

<!-- page 161: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N127] **The cardinal sin of over-weighting current positions**:
  - What really matters is the long-run distribution of outcomes from your
    trading techniques, systems, and procedures
  - But psychologically, what seems of PARAMOUNT IMPORTANCE is whether
    the positions you have *right now* are going to work
  - Current positions feel crucial beyond any statistical justification
  - This leads to bending your rules to make current trades work —
    assuming long-term statistics will take care of future profitability
  - Both cardinal sins of trading — giving losses too much rope AND
    taking profits prematurely — are attempts to make *current* positions
    more likely to succeed, to the severe detriment of long-term
    performance
  - The fix: recognize that every individual trade is just one data point
    in the long-run distribution; treat current positions with the same
    indifference as you'd view a historical backtest

- [MUST KNOW][N128] **You must feel the pain of loss to survive**:
  - Survivors avoid snowball scenarios: bad trades → emotional
    destabilization → more bad trades
  - They also feel the pain of losing — this is not optional
  - Analogy: people born without pain sensors burn their hands on stoves;
    no pain = no survival signal
  - "If the losses don't hurt, your financial survival is tenuous"
  - Evidence: Eckhardt knew several multimillionaires who started trading
    with inherited wealth — in each case, they lost it all because they
    didn't feel the pain during formative years; they felt they could
    afford to lose
  - Counterintuitive implication: **starting with a tiny account is an
    advantage** — the trader who can't afford to lose is forced to develop
    discipline
  - "I'd rather bet on somebody starting out with a few thousand dollars
    than on somebody who came in with millions"

<!-- page 162: 2 nuggets -->

### Transforming a Losing Trader

- [SHOULD KNOW][N129] **Two types of losing traders — only one can be fixed**:
  - Type 1: Doesn't know why he's losing → hopeless unless he discovers
    what he's doing wrong
  - Type 2: Knows what he's doing wrong → advice is deceptively simple:
    stop doing it
  - If Type 2 cannot change his behavior → the solution is to become a
    **dogmatic system trader** (remove discretion entirely)

- [SHOULD KNOW][N130] **Eckhardt's soybean catastrophe — first risk management lesson**:
  - First year of trading: went long soybeans a few cents from limit-up
  - Market went from limit-up to limit-down without an uptick — in about
    three minutes
  - He then got short at limit-down; two minutes later, market was
    limit-up again
  - Lost more than half his equity in five minutes on two trades
  - Recovery method: **trading small** — making many small decisions
    rather than trying to make a few blockbuster trades
  - Purpose of story: even expert traders carry formative catastrophes
    that shaped their risk discipline; the lesson was size, not direction

<!-- page 163: 2 nuggets -->

### Large Profits Are More Dangerous Than Large Losses

- [MUST KNOW][N131] **Large profits destabilize more than large losses**:
  - "In many ways, large profits are even more insidious than large losses
    in terms of emotional destabilization"
  - On a big winning streak: temptation to think you're doing something
    special → start believing you can afford shoddy decisions
  - "As a general rule, losses make you strong and profits make you weak"
  - Mechanism: losses force discipline and re-examination; profits breed
    overconfidence and complacency
  - Eckhardt personally made some of his worst trades after long winning
    periods

- [MUST KNOW][N132] **Trading is a positive monetary game but a negative emotional game**:
  - Example: positions open sharply against you, almost to your loss
    cutoff — the loss feels crushing; you wonder if risk was set too high
  - Then miraculously, you're NOT stopped out; by midday markets have
    recovered by the same amount
  - "There's nothing in the elation that would approach compensating for
    the morning's distress. The profit seems large, of course, but it
    doesn't seem to help nearly as much as the earlier loss hurt."
  - This is not mere emotional exaggeration — loss aversion is real and
    asymmetrical (a gain of equal magnitude does not compensate for an
    equivalent loss)
  - Implication: trading is a negative-sum proposition emotionally, even
    when positive monetarily — the only rationale for doing it is financial

<!-- page 164: 2 nuggets -->

### The Asymmetry Principle for Thinking About Retracements

- [MUST KNOW][N133] **Asymmetric thinking rule: what is comforting is wrong,
  what is discomforting is right**:
  - When losing and market is at your stop point: thinking about
    retracements is COMFORTING — but it's hopeful thinking that makes a
    trader give a loss more room. The concept should be **off limits**.
  - When winning big and the market has moved sharply your way: thinking
    about retracements is DISCOMFORTING — but it's exactly right.
    Sharpness of move indicates volatility has increased; a windfall
    profit might dissipate rapidly. The concept should be **at the
    forefront.**
  - "Trading is full of such asymmetries that make it an emotionally
    negative-sum proposition"
  - Rule of thumb: if a thought about market behavior is comforting you
    when you're in a bad trade, it's wrong. If it's making you
    uncomfortable when you're in a good trade, it's probably right.

- [MUST KNOW][N134] **Trading addiction: intermittent reinforcement is more
  addictive than positive reinforcement**:
  - Behavioral psychologists: intermittent reinforcement — positive AND
    negative dispensed randomly (the rat doesn't know if it'll get
    pleasure or pain when it hits the bar) — is the **most addictive**
    alternative of all
  - More addictive than positive reinforcement only
  - This describes both the compulsive gambler AND the futures trader
  - Key difference: the trader actually *can* make money — but this
    distinction barely matters
  - "As with most of the 'affective' aspects of commodity trading, its
    addictiveness constantly threatens ruin"
  - "Addictiveness is the reason why so many players who make fortunes
    leave the game broke"
  - Implication: the emotional enjoyment of trading is itself a warning
    sign; the game is rigged to keep you playing past the point of wisdom

<!-- page 165: 3 nuggets -->

### Emotional Management Advice

- [MUST KNOW][N135] **"If it feels good, don't do it" — the Turtle rule**:
  - **Richard Dennis** used to say, somewhat facetiously: "If it feels
    good, don't do it"
  - One Turtle rule: when all criteria are in balance, **do the thing you
    least want to do**
  - Core decision traders must make: are you playing for fun, or for
    success? You can't optimize for both.
  - "If you're playing for the emotional satisfaction, you're bound to
    lose, because what feels good is often the wrong thing to do"

- [SHOULD KNOW][N136] **Focus on your response, not the market's behavior**:
  - Old trader's advice: "Don't think about what the market's going to
    do; you have absolutely no control over that. Think about what you're
    going to do if it gets there."
  - Corollary: spend NO time thinking about the roseate scenarios where
    the market goes your way — in those situations, there's nothing more
    to do
  - Focus instead on things you LEAST want to happen and what your
    response should be
  - Practical implication: pre-plan your reactions to adverse scenarios;
    leave nothing to be decided under emotional pressure

- [SHOULD KNOW][N137] **During losing periods, channel anxiety into research**:
  - Don't be preoccupied with losses; channel that energy into research
  - At C&D (Dennis and Eckhardt's partnership): their best research
    breakthroughs happened when they were losing
  - Probable mechanism: motivation to improve is greatest when performance
    demands it

### The Counterintuitive Finding: Most Traders Worse Than Random

<!-- page 166: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N138] **Most traders perform worse than a purely random trader**:
  - Annual charting contest: contestants predict settlement prices of
    several futures by a given deadline
  - **Dale Dellutri** (in Eckhardt's office) decided, prankishly, to use
    the **random walk model** — he simply used the current settlement
    prices as his predictions
  - He fell just short of winning; his name was among the first five of
    ~50 close runners-up out of hundreds of entrants
  - "More than 95 percent, and probably more than 99 percent, of the
    contestants scored worse than blind randomness. This is no mean feat."
  - Eckhardt's hypothesis: "The majority of people trade worse than a
    purely random trader would"
  - Admits no hard evidence, but has observed the phenomenon many times
  - Why this matters: it implies that human instincts in trading are not
    just unhelpful — they are actively harmful enough to destroy even
    random-walk level performance

- [MUST KNOW][N139] **The market as a tutor teaching bad habits**:
  - "The market behaves much like an opponent who is trying to teach you
    to trade poorly"
  - Not intentional — analogy to evolutionary theory: natural selection
    acts *as if* it intends outcomes without any bird ever having
    "intended" to evolve a wing
  - Traders who've traded for a while feel "they are out to get you" —
    this illusion is well founded even if the mechanism is mindless
  - What the market specifically teaches:
    1. Most small/moderate profits vanish → take them before they escape
       (reinforces profit-taking habit)
    2. Market spends more time consolidating than trending → buy dips and
       sell rallies (reinforces countertrend habit)
    3. Prices revisit the same levels again and again → hold bad trades
       on the assumption prices will return to "normal"
    4. High success rate techniques feel good → lulled into false security
       of methods that work often but lose disastrously long-term
  - **Key insight**: "What works most of the time is nearly the opposite
    of what works in the long run"

<!-- page 167: 2 nuggets -->

### Schwager's Synthesis: Overfitting and Reprogramming

- [MUST KNOW][N140] **Overfitting warning: the more a system fits the past,
  the worse it performs in the future**:
  - The more closely a system is fit to past price behavior, the more
    impressive the historical simulation AND the better the trader feels
    about using it
  - BUT: beyond a very limited point, the more effort expended to fit
    past behavior → the WORSE actual future performance will be
  - Desire to design impressive-looking systems leads traders to accept
    favorable simulated results without scrutiny
  - "Very often, great results are simply the consequence of error or
    naive methodology"
  - Eckhardt's advice: system designers should believe results only after
    they have done **everything possible to disprove them**

- [MUST KNOW][N141] **The first step in trading success: reprogramming behavior**:
  - [Schwager's synthesis of Eckhardt's core thesis]
  - Most people don't lose merely because they lack skill to beat random
  - They lose because natural human traits entice them into behavioral
    patterns that lead to WORSE than random results
  - Comfortable actions (locking in profits, giving losses room,
    overfitting systems, countertrend trading) all degrade performance
    below random
  - "The first step in succeeding as a trader is reprogramming behavior
    to do what is correct rather than what feels comfortable"
  - This is why intelligence alone is insufficient: the problem is not
    ignorance — it's instinct

