## Chapter: The Silence of the Turtles

<!-- page 168: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N142] **The Turtle experiment: the most famous bet in trading history**:
  - Chapter opens with fictional dialogue (modeled on Mark Twain's
    "The £1,000,000 Bank-Note"):
    - **Duncan**: "Anyone can be taught to be a superior trader — no
      rare talent involved, just appropriate rules"
    - **Colin**: "You just think your success is due to the system. Your
      success is a function of your talent. It cannot be taught."
    - Resolution: pick ten people, teach them the system, give each £1M
      to trade — if up 25%+ on average after one year, Duncan wins;
      otherwise Colin wins
  - The real-life version: **Richard Dennis** (transformed ~$thousands
    into ~$200 million) vs. **William Eckhardt**
  - Dennis: trading success can be taught
  - Eckhardt: scoffed at the idea

<!-- page 169: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N143] **The Turtle program — specifics and results**:
  - Dennis and Eckhardt placed ad in *Wall Street Journal* seeking
    trading trainees
  - Selection: written applications + exam + interviews; ~1,000
    respondents → 13 selected
  - Training: approximately two weeks; Dennis taught all specifics of his
    systems — "no holds barred"
  - First group performed so well Dennis repeated with second group of 10
  - Collectively known as **"the Turtles"** — name from Dennis's visit to
    an Orient turtle farm where thousands were raised in huge vats;
    perfect analogy for growing traders
  - Dennis was funded these traders and sent them off independently
  - Schwager's discovery six years later: scanned *Managed Accounts
    Reports* blindly (looking only at performance stats, not names)
  - Checked off 18 of 100+ CTAs with exceptional performance
  - **8 of 18 (44%) turned out to be Turtles** — astounding concentration
  - "Richard Dennis was obviously right"
  - Caveat: 1991 proved tough year for many Turtles (results less
    dramatic a year later)

<!-- page 170: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N144] **The Turtles refused to talk — an extreme case of
  proprietary secrecy**:
  - Many Turtles refused to participate even after Schwager promised
    final approval rights over any copy used
  - Those who agreed were "largely tight-lipped about anything of
    interest"
  - All had signed NDAs; even questions remote from the system were
    deflected
  - Representative interview:
    - "How do you pick your trades?" → "I basically use the system, but
      I can't say much more than that."
    - "What are the trading rules you live by?" → "The same general
      rules I'm sure you've heard everywhere."
  - Gulf War gold trade example: market had been rising (Turtle was long);
    at outbreak of air war, gold initially rose then dropped $30+
    overnight; Turtle simply said "I got out" and refused to explain the
    reasoning
  - Schwager applied trading principle: cut losses short on Turtle
    interviews after first few — moved on to two willing exceptions

<!-- page 171: no new nuggets — representative silent interview content;
  context noted above -->

<!-- page 172: 1 nugget -->

