## Michael Carr (Turtle Interview)

- [SHOULD KNOW][N145] **Michael Carr's background and Turtle selection**:
  - Was creative management staff at **TSR** (Dungeons & Dragons game
    company); started when only a few employees, grew to 300+ before
    hard times forced layoffs
  - Lost job with 200 others; picked up *Wall Street Journal* same day
    Dennis ran his ad — purely coincidental
  - No trading experience; had studied commodity markets while developing
    a game idea (took a 6-session extension course with a commodity broker)
  - Believes he was selected because of game company background:
    "A lot of commodity trading is based on game theory and probability.
    Therefore, it's not much of a jump to believe that someone with
    experience in that area might bring something to the table."
  - Of the 13 selected: **1/3 had no experience, 1/3 had significant
    experience, 1/3 had some**
  - Performance under Dennis (1984–1988): averaged **57% annually**
    (down moderately in first third of 1988 when Dennis terminated
    the program)
  - Own CTA from August 1989; up **89%** from that starting point as
    of late 1991

<!-- page 173: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N146] **Carr's core lesson from Turtle training**:
  - "Don't worry about what the markets are going to do, worry about
    what you are going to do in response to the markets"
  - Echoes Eckhardt's advice (old trader: "Think about what you're
    going to do if it gets there") — the Turtle training reinforced
    this principle explicitly
  - Practical implication: shift mental energy from market prediction
    to personal contingency planning

<!-- page 174: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N147] **Carr on who should (and shouldn't) trade**:
  - "A large segment of the population should never trade the markets"
  - Gambling analogy: wise and prudent gamblers would also be wise
    investors — they have a detached view of the value of money
  - Those who get caught up in the excitement of the *amount of the
    wager* (whether gambling or investing) are likely to be
    destabilized by losses
  - Carr's personal motivation: trading has many elements of a game;
    for someone always interested in games, hard to imagine a better job
  - Implication: emotional detachment from the money itself — not
    from the outcome — is a prerequisite for trading survival

