## Howard Seidler (Turtle Interview)

<!-- page 175: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N148] **Howard Seidler — profile of the most ebullient Turtle**:
  - Most upbeat of all Turtles interviewed; exuded genuine enjoyment
    of trading emotionally and intellectually
  - Schwager assumed (incorrectly) that Seidler must be on a winning
    streak given his attitude — in fact, the six months prior to the
    interview was his **second-worst performance ever** (down 16%)
  - Long-term performance: **averaged 34% compounded since 1984**
  - First exposure: father dabbled in markets during Seidler's childhood
  - In high school: became aware of futures markets; attracted by the
    symmetry of going short as well as long, and by leverage
  - Reaction to discovering "hardly anyone ever succeeds" at futures:
    treated it as "throwing down the gauntlet" — a challenge to be met

<!-- page 176: 1 nugget -->

<!-- pages 177-178: 1 nugget across both pages -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N149] **Seidler's $1,000 high school account**:
  - Too young to open own account → opened under his father's name
  - $1,000 saved from chores (shoveling snow, mowing lawns)
  - Lost the entire amount in a little over a year
  - Retrospective: "I wasn't too thrilled about it at the time. However,
    as I got older, I realized that I had really done pretty well
    considering the circumstances. I certainly did get my money's worth
    in terms of experience."
  - Key point: even complete inexperienced loss can be formative if the
    lessons are extracted — the small scale made the tuition affordable

<!-- page 179: 1 nugget -->
<!-- page 180: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N150] **Seidler's "fortunate" potato market disaster — the
  leverage-size lesson**:
  - Believed potato market would break sharply → went short one contract
  - Market moved in his direction; with a small profit, decided to double
    his position (the second contract was beyond what his tiny account
    could support)
  - Market turned up; fear made him liquidate the added contract at a
    loss — and that fear also caused him to exit the ORIGINAL contract
    far too early
  - Two days later: market collapsed exactly as anticipated; he missed
    the move entirely
  - Why he called it "fortunate": if he had stayed and made several
    hundred percent, "I would have thought that I knew it all"
  - The actual lesson, learned cheap: **"You can't win if you're trading
    at a leverage size that makes you fearful of the market"**
  - If he'd learned this lesson later with real money, "it would have
    been far more expensive"
  - Before becoming a Turtle, Seidler had worked as an economic
    consultant and then left to become a full-time trader — saw Dennis's
    WSJ ad shortly after that transition

<!-- pp. 179-200 -->

## Chapter: The Turtles (conclusion — unnamed Turtle trader)

### Leverage Fear and the Cost of Oversizing

- [MUST KNOW][N127] **Trading at leverage that makes you fearful is a
  guaranteed path to bad decisions**:
  - Potato market story: trader went short one contract, market moved in
    his favor; doubled position even though account was so tiny that
    even one contract was "pushing it"
  - Added contract prompted panic — liquidated the added contract at a
    loss, then exited original position early; market then collapsed
    exactly as originally anticipated
  - Calls this "fortunate": if he had stayed and made several hundred
    percent, he would have thought he knew it all — and learned nothing
  - The lesson would have been learned later at far greater expense
  - "You can't win if you're trading at a leverage size that makes you
    fearful of the market"
  - Mechanism: fear distorts decision-making; every adverse tick becomes
    a crisis; you exit good trades early and stay in bad trades hoping

<!-- page 179: 1 nugget -->

### The Turtles: Background and Selection

- [SHOULD KNOW][N128] **How one Turtle was selected from ~1-in-100
  applicants**:
  - Dennis wasn't specifically looking for trading experience — but
    being a trader didn't disqualify you either
  - Philosophy alignment with Dennis probably helped; MIT engineering
    degree likely sparked Dennis's curiosity
  - Left job as economic consultant to become a full-time trader
    shortly before seeing Dennis's Wall Street Journal ad
  - Background: started trading in high school with $1,000 saved from
    chores; opened account under father's name (too young); lost it
    all in just over a year
  - In retrospect: lasting a year with a tiny account as a complete
    novice was actually doing well — "got my money's worth in experience"

<!-- page 180: 1 nugget -->

### Advice from a Turtle

- [MUST KNOW][N129] **The trading plan as the single most important
  element**:
  - A plan forces discipline — "an essential ingredient to successful
    trading"
  - A plan gives a benchmark to measure performance against something
    other than raw P&L
  - Critical distinction: you can follow your rules exactly and still
    lose money in the short run — that does NOT mean you traded poorly
  - "In the short run, conformance to a trading plan is more significant
    than short-term equity fluctuations"
  - Persistence required: human nature will tempt you to abandon a
    winning approach if you get enough negative feedback over the short
    run

- [MUST KNOW][N130] **Respect for the market vs. fear of the market —
  two different things**:
  - Respect = essential for capital preservation
  - Fear = prevents making correct decisions; paralysis disguised as
    prudence
  - You must have one without the other — the line between them is
    real and consequential

<!-- page 181: 2 nuggets -->

### Turtle Chapter Conclusion

- [SHOULD KNOW][N131] **The chapter's core message on Turtle success**:
  - No definitive answer for what made Turtles successful as a group
  - The important message: it IS possible to develop a system that
    significantly beats the market
  - If you can discover such a system and exercise the discipline to
    follow it, you can succeed without being a "born trader"
  - Implication: trading skill is at least partially learnable and
    systematizable, not purely innate

<!-- page 182: 1 nugget -->

