## Chapter: Monroe Trout — The Best Return That Low Risk Can Buy

### Who Is Monroe Trout

- [MUST KNOW][N132] **Monroe Trout's extraordinary return/risk
  performance — best of 100+ managers**:
  - Schwager consulted *Managed Accounts Reports* covering 100+
    commodity trading advisors (CTAs)
  - Trout's combination of return-to-risk: best of any manager in the
    survey
  - **5-year performance stats**: 67% average annual return; largest
    drawdown during entire 5-year period was just over **8 percent**;
    profitable in **87% of all months**
  - Some CTAs had larger average returns, and some had smaller
    drawdowns — but none came close to matching both simultaneously
  - Even **Paul Tudor Jones** — described as legendary — did not
    approach Trout's return/risk figures over the same period
  - Became a public money manager in **1986**
  - Trout does not trumpet his successes — Schwager met him before
    realizing how well he was doing; Trout made no mention of his
    performance
  - Self-description: "Some people make shoes. Some people make houses.
    We make money, and people are willing to pay us a lot to make money
    for them."

<!-- page 183: 1 nugget -->
<!-- page 184: no new nuggets (intro text continued) -->

### Early Career and Education

- [SHOULD KNOW][N133] **Trout's path to markets — from data entry
  to Harvard thesis**:
  - At 17, got job for futures trader **Vilar Kelly** in New Canaan,
    Connecticut (1978) — typed price data from newspapers into Kelly's
    Apple computer (data not available on diskette at the time)
  - Also learned basics of futures markets and computer programming
  - By sophomore year at **Harvard**, knew he wanted to be a trader;
    took every available markets course
  - Wrote senior thesis on **stock index futures market**
  - After graduating with honors, skipped graduate school entirely:
    "I knew what I wanted to do — trade. Graduate school would only
    have delayed that goal."

- [MUST KNOW][N134] **Harvard thesis: two conclusions that shaped
  Trout's entire approach**:
  - **Conclusion 1 — Fat tails**: probability of very large price
    changes, while still small, is much greater than standard
    statistical assumptions (normal distribution) would imply
  - Therefore: risk control methodology must be prepared to deal with
    events that statistically seem nearly impossible — because they
    are NOT impossible
  - Practical consequence: Trout uses less leverage than he would have
    otherwise — Oct 1987 and Oct 1989 stock market crashes confirmed
    this: under normal distribution, probability of those daily moves
    would be "virtually zero"
  - **Conclusion 2 — Price dependence**: prices are NOT independent —
    statistically significant patterns exist, markets are not fully
    random

<!-- page 185: 1 nugget -->
<!-- page 186: 1 nugget -->

### Floor Trading Education at NCZ Commodities

- [SHOULD KNOW][N135] **How Trout broke in — Victor Niederhoffer and
  the floor**:
  - Harvard athletic director **Jack Reardon** knew **Victor
    Niederhoffer**, who headed **NCZ Commodities** (NYC trading firm)
  - Niederhoffer had graduated from Harvard in 1964; was world's best
    squash player at one point
  - Within two weeks of joining, Trout was trading stock index on the
    floor of the **New York Futures Exchange**
  - Mostly scalped for himself on a profit-sharing deal
  - **Scalping**: two principal methods — (1) capturing bid/ask spread
    by taking opposite side of customer orders; (2) exploiting
    temporary price discrepancies between related positions (e.g.,
    March vs. June stock index contract)
  - The pit is a fast learning environment: ask lots of questions, find
    the traders who know something and will talk; you can learn quickly
    — but eventually hit a plateau

<!-- page 187: 1 nugget -->

### Transaction Costs: The Hidden Competitive Edge

- [MUST KNOW][N136] **Transaction costs are far more variable — and
  more controllable — than most traders realize**:
  - Floor experience gave Trout detailed understanding of true
    transaction costs across different markets
  - **T-bonds**: common assumption is bid/ask spread = 1 tick ($31.25)
    in transaction cost; reality: with a good broker who is patient,
    you often get filled at the bid — so actual cost is ~half a tick.
    With a bad broker, maybe one full tick.
  - Key insight: a T-bond system with a small expected gain that most
    would discard might be VIABLE — if you have good execution
  - **S&P**: opposite problem — the apparent bid/ask is 1 tick ($25),
    but often costs more because when you try to buy at the offer, it
    disappears; actual slippage regularly exceeds quoted spread
  - **Practical implication**: the same system can be profitable for
    Trout and unprofitable for a typical trader, purely due to
    execution differences

- [MUST KNOW][N137] **Transaction cost advantage quantified —
  ~12% per year edge over competitors**:
  - Round-trip commission: lower than 99% of funds in the business
  - Why: several hundred million AUM + frequent turnover = massive
    trade volume → negotiated extremely low commission rates (under
    $10/round trip vs. ~$20 for others)
  - Saves ~**6% per year** from reduced slippage via careful broker
    selection and monitoring
  - Saves another ~**6% per year** from low commissions ($10 vs $20)
  - Slippage monitoring: at end of every day, staff provides summary
    sheet of slippage in each market; brokers constantly evaluated and
    replaced if underperforming; can put 10 people on phones to a
    single pit simultaneously
  - Example: bond system averaging $40/lot profit is tradeable at <$10
    commission + half-tick slippage ($16); for trader paying $30 +
    one-tick slippage, same system is a money drain

<!-- page 188: 2 nuggets -->

### Stop Placement Intelligence from the Floor

- [SHOULD KNOW][N138] **Where traders cluster their stops — and why
  to avoid those spots**:
  - Floor experience revealed: people most commonly place stops right
    above the prior day's high and right below the prior day's low
    (sometimes one tick, sometimes a couple of ticks above/below)
  - Many floor locals make money specifically by identifying where stops
    are and moving the market to trigger them — legal as long as not
    done illegally
  - **Practical advice**: avoid the obvious stop locations; instead,
    put stops either 10 ticks below the high (so you're out before the
    stop-hunt happens) or 10 ticks above the high (so the stop-hunt
    won't reach you)
  - Trout himself uses mental stops + beeper alerts rather than actual
    stop orders: "When we start losing money, a warning will go off,
    alerting us to begin liquidating the position"
  - Trout uses floor knowledge to TRADE the stop clusters: enters
    before the stop area is reached, sometimes tries to "set the stops
    off" himself — then "the market will be off to the races"

<!-- page 189: 1 nugget -->

### The Magnet Effect

- [MUST KNOW][N139] **"The magnet effect" — markets are drawn to
  round numbers, and this is tradeable**:
  - Trout's observation: markets almost always reach round numbers
    (e.g., Dow 3,000) when they get close
  - Strategy: buy before the round number is reached and ride the
    magnet effect (e.g., buy at Dow 2,950, target 3,000)
  - Execution filter at the round number: when approaching the target,
    everyone in the trading room gets on the phone with a different
    broker to listen to floor noise level
    - If floor is loud and large orders are transacting → hold position
    - If floor is quiet and order sizes are small → dump position
      (market probably going to fall off)
  - Real example: crude oil approaching $20 during the Persian Gulf
    crisis — floor was loud, market continued higher
  - Cannot be systematized — requires real-time floor intelligence:
    "What numbers are people looking for?"
  - This is Trout's favorite discretionary trade (outside of
    system-generated signals)

<!-- page 190: 1 nugget -->

### Liquidity Patterns and Market-Moving

- [SHOULD KNOW][N140] **The U-shaped liquidity curve — and how to
  exploit illiquid periods**:
  - Market liquidity follows a consistent U-shape: high at open, falls
    to nadir at midday (~11:30 Chicago time), recovers to secondary
    peak at close — holds in "almost every market"
  - Why it matters for large traders: irrelevant for 1-contract
    traders; critical when trading thousands of contracts
  - **Exploiting illiquidity**: during midday illiquid period, Trout
    supports his position by placing scale-down buy orders (e.g., 10
    lots every tick down) — costs far fewer contracts to hold the
    market up during illiquid hours than at other times
  - **Moving the market intentionally**: Trout explicitly states he
    moves the market every single day — e.g., if S&P is near
    yesterday's high and he's long and wants to exit, he pushes prices
    through the high to generate excitement and boost volume, making
    it much easier to dump the position

<!-- page 191: 1 nugget -->

### Trading Models — Design and Edge

- [SHOULD KNOW][N141] **Why Trout's short-term models work when
  most short-term systems fail**:
  - Typical short-term systems (1 week or less avg holding period)
    don't beat transaction costs for most traders
  - Trout's models hold positions from **a day to a week**
  - Two reasons his models survive at this timeframe:
    (1) More statistically rigorous design
    (2) Transaction costs dramatically lower than competitors
  - If Trout blindly followed systems without discretionary timing of
    entries/exits, he estimates making **half** of current returns —
    maybe less
  - Could give exact systems to 10 other CTAs; some still wouldn't
    make money — execution and discretion matter that much

<!-- page 192: 1 nugget -->
<!-- page 193: no new nuggets (slippage measurement methodology detail) -->
<!-- page 194: no new nuggets (transaction cost quantification, already captured in N137) -->

### Defining Success: The Daily Sharpe Ratio

- [MUST KNOW][N142] **Trout's definition of the best trader: highest
  daily Sharpe ratio**:
  - Standard Sharpe ratio measured on monthly data — only captures
    month-to-month equity variability
  - Trout measures on **daily** data — far more granular, much harder
    to fake or obscure with monthly smoothing
  - The Sharpe ratio normalizes return by risk: two traders making
    identical trades but one uses double the contracts gets double the
    return but also double the risk → same Sharpe ratio
  - Trout's view: trading performance should incorporate risk down to
    day-to-day equity variations, not just monthly swings
  - Key implication: raw return is an incomplete and potentially
    misleading metric; Trout's 67% return with 8% max drawdown is
    more impressive than a 100% return with 40% drawdown

<!-- page 195: 1 nugget -->

### Five Pillars of Trout's Success

- [MUST KNOW][N143] **The five factors Trout attributes his success
  to — and the "skin in the game" element**:
  - (A) Good research → genuine statistical edge
  - (B) Rational, practical approach to money management
  - (C) Very low commissions
  - (D) Executions among the best in the business
  - (E) **Alignment of incentives**: most staff keep a large portion
    of their net worth in the fund; Trout himself has **over 95% of
    his net worth** in the fund he manages
  - Trout pulls no money out — rents a condo, drives a cheap car
  - Motivation: "At this point, it's more a matter of keeping score...
    I like to trade. When I was a kid, I loved to play games. Now I get
    to play a very fun game, and I'm paid handsomely for it."
  - Hard stop: "The minute I don't have fun trading, or I don't think
    I can make a profit, I'm going to quit."

<!-- page 196: 1 nugget -->

### Pattern Validation: Logic Must Match Statistics

- [MUST KNOW][N144] **Statistical significance is not enough — a
  pattern must also make logical sense**:
  - Trout runs massive computer searches across all combinations of
    market and interval relationships — easier to test everything than
    to pre-select which combinations to test
  - Filter: any statistically significant relationship must pass a
    logical sense test before being used
  - Example: if British pound price 40 days ago is statistically
    significant in predicting today's S&P — discard it. Why would
    the pound 40 days ago affect the S&P? No causal mechanism → the
    pattern is almost certainly coincidental
  - Survivorship bias of pattern testing: if you test enough patterns,
    some will be profitable by chance alone (the coin-toss argument)
  - Defense against this: require BOTH statistical significance AND
    a plausible causal or behavioral explanation
  - Markets becoming more efficient: patterns Trout used to trade
    are being eliminated as others discover them (e.g., buying 2 days
    after an up day is now obsolete)

<!-- page 197: 1 nugget -->

### Diversification Across Strategies and Time

- [SHOULD KNOW][N145] **Diversifying over both trading strategies
  AND time frames simultaneously**:
  - Dozens of different models/patterns running at any given time
  - Time diversification: same pattern applied to hourly, daily, and
    weekly data — and staying ready to trade throughout the entire
    session
  - Strategy diversification reduces the correlation of losing periods
    across different approaches
  - ~**50% of trades** are system-determined; ~**50% are discretionary**
    — though the line blurs because a system may say "buy 1,000
    contracts today" while Trout decides when to buy them

<!-- page 198: 1 nugget -->

### Discretionary Trades: Magnet Effect and Reverse-Local

- [SHOULD KNOW][N146] **The reverse-local trade — reading what is
  moving the market**:
  - When Trout's floor contacts reveal that a price move was caused
    primarily by locals (not by public order flow or fundamentals),
    he goes in the **opposite direction**
  - Reasoning: locals go home flat — they must cover their positions
    before end of day, which brings the market back to where it came
    from
  - This trade is only possible with constant real-time floor
    intelligence — clerks calling every minute with information on
    who is doing what and how much
  - If respected traders (whom Trout tracks via floor contacts) are
    doing the same thing, it may reinforce or prompt Trout to take
    a similar position or add to an existing one

- [SHOULD KNOW][N147] **Execution timing is worth roughly half the
  total return — and cannot be delegated to systems alone**:
  - Blind system following (buying on open, close, or fixed intervals)
    would produce roughly half of Trout's actual returns — maybe less
  - "I could give ten CTAs the exact systems we use, and some of them
    still wouldn't make any money"
  - The discretionary element — knowing when to pull the trigger,
    how to read floor noise, when to hold vs. dump — is not a minor
    refinement; it is approximately equal in value to the system
    itself

<!-- page 199: 2 nuggets -->
<!-- page 200: no new nuggets (floor contacts and staff role, captured in N146) -->

<!-- pp. 199-220 -->

