<!-- page 200: no new nuggets (magnet effect unsystematizable; floor contacts, captured in N146/N147) -->

### The Magnet Effect (Discretionary Trade Detail)

<!-- page 201: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N148] **The magnet effect: markets are drawn to round
  numbers and critical levels**
  - When the market approaches a round number or a key chart point,
    Trout plays for it to reach that level
  - Cannot be systematized: the recognition that a level is a "key
    point" sometimes comes from sudden intuition, sometimes from floor
    contacts asking "What numbers are people looking for?"
  - The information is inherently qualitative and real-time — no
    formula can replicate it
  - This is the single most common type of discretionary trade Trout
    mentions explicitly by name

### Managing Shock News Events: Size Changes Everything

<!-- page 202: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N149] **January 9, 1991 (Baker/Iraq meeting) as a
  case study in how large size changes news-event response**:
  - Secretary of State **James Baker** met the Iraqi ambassador
    January 9, 1991 to avert war; markets expected a peaceful
    outcome — optimism was reasonable
  - Baker opened his press statement with "Regrettably…" — traders
    sold before hearing the second word; a wave of selling hit
    stocks and bonds instantly
  - Trout was long ~700 S&P contracts; lost **1,200 S&P points**
    in a half-hour, most in the first ten seconds
  - **Dollar loss: ~$9.5 million — lost in approximately ten seconds**
  - At that size, trying to sell into the panic would have amplified
    the decline and likely resulted in getting filled at the day's low
  - **Rule that activated: down 4% on the day → exit ALL positions,
    wait until next day**; this rule had been activated only twice
    in two years; January 9 was one of those days
  - Trout's response: devised an orderly exit plan, then LEFT the
    trading room so the liquidation could be executed rationally
    without emotion clouding the process

- [MUST KNOW][N150] **Small size vs. large size: fundamentally
  different optimal responses to sudden adverse news**
  - Small size (e.g., 10 S&P lots): get out immediately the moment
    you hear bad news — the market is liquid enough to absorb the
    exit without moving against you materially
  - Large size (e.g., 700 S&P lots): the initial panic is essentially
    impossible to exit through; trying to do so worsens your fill
    dramatically and can move the market itself
  - At large size: wait until the market stabilizes, then exit
    methodically over the remainder of the session
  - **Trigger for "stabilized enough to start exiting"**: when the
    loss has exceeded the per-trade pain threshold (1.5% of equity),
    the exit is no longer optional — the rule decides, not the emotion

<!-- page 203: 2 nuggets -->

### Trout's Four-Layer Risk Management Framework

- [MUST KNOW][N151] **Monroe Trout's complete risk management
  system — four rules in order of time scale**:
  1. **Per-trade maximum loss: 1.5% of total equity** — if any single
     position loses more than 1.5%, exit that position entirely.
     No exceptions.
  2. **Daily maximum loss: 4% of total equity** — if the portfolio
     is down 4% on any single day, close ALL positions and wait
     until the following day before re-entering anything.
     Activated only twice in two years; Jan 9, 1991 was one.
  3. **Monthly maximum loss: 10% of total equity** — if ever hit,
     exit all positions and wait until start of the next month.
     Has NEVER been triggered.
  4. **Monthly position size caps** — at the start of each month,
     determine maximum allowable position size for each market;
     never exceed that cap regardless of how bullish or bearish
     conviction becomes. "This rule keeps me in check."
  - **The purpose of rules 2 and 3**: prevent the kind of
    catastrophic loss that you can't psychologically or financially
    recover from — "I want to make sure I'll be around to play
    tomorrow"
  - **Counterintuitive**: on Jan 9, Trout would have been better off
    holding — but he still endorses the 4% rule because without it,
    you're vulnerable to a true October 1987 scenario where
    procrastinating would have been catastrophic

<!-- page 204: 1 nugget -->

### Emotional Psychology of Large Losses

- [SHOULD KNOW][N152] **Trout's emotional experience of large
  losses — anger as primary but manageable response**:
  - First emotion when losing money: anger — primarily at the market,
    not himself (though he acknowledges the market isn't personal)
  - Has tried for years to eliminate anger on losses; concluded it is
    impossible: "Until the day I die, I don't think I'm ever going to
    be able to look a big loss in the face and not get angry"
  - Key: anger doesn't affect his trading — he keeps it contained
  - Jan 9 was so fast he was speechless rather than angry — the usual
    emotional sequence didn't have time to engage
  - Once the loss exceeds 4%, he forms an exit plan and literally
    leaves the trading room, delegating execution to traders —
    **removing himself physically from the situation** to prevent
    emotion from corrupting the liquidation
  - Sleep: doesn't sleep well at any time — "that's one of the prices
    you pay for being a trader." Oddly, sleeps WORSE on good days
    because he gets too excited
  - Recovery from a major loss: started forgetting about January 9
    within a day; mostly over it within a few days

<!-- page 205: 1 nugget -->

### Dollar-Profit Screen for Evaluating CTAs

<!-- page 206: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N153] **Percentage returns vs. total dollar profit —
  the definitive CTA screening test**:
  - Trout: "There are plenty of traders I know who show track records
    with an amazing cumulative winning percentage. I've seen situations
    where they might be up 1,000 percent over a five-year period, but
    if you examine their track record in terms of net dollars made or
    lost, you discover they are actually down."
  - **Mechanism**: they generated the big percentage returns while
    managing small capital; then as AUM grew, they couldn't replicate
    the performance and actually lost money in dollar terms
  - **Trout's first screen if evaluating CTAs**: total dollar profit
    pulled out of the market — if negative, eliminate from
    consideration regardless of percentage return
  - This is a size-scaling test: can the strategy actually work at
    real money levels?

<!-- page 207: 2 nuggets -->

### Charts as Crowd-Psychology Map

- [SHOULD KNOW][N154] **How Trout uses charts — not for price
  prediction but to model WHERE OTHER TRADERS WILL ACT**:
  - "I look at charts primarily to figure out where traders are going
    to get interested in a market. I know the types of patterns they
    like to look at."
  - Charts as a map of collective attention — breakout levels, support/
    resistance zones matter because thousands of traders are watching
    the same charts and will react at those points
  - This is a second-order use of technical analysis: not "what does
    the pattern predict?" but "what will other people do when they see
    this pattern?"

### Indicators: What Works vs. What Doesn't

- [SHOULD KNOW][N155] **Trout's indicator verdicts**:
  - **Useful**: Moving averages — "They'll work if you watch your risk
    management. I believe you can make an above-average return by using
    moving averages, if you're smart about it."
  - **Overrated / found nothing useful in**: Fibonacci retracements,
    Gann angles, RSI, stochastics
  - Caveat: these are Trout's findings from his own research — not
    universal law, but notable given his track record and systematic
    research depth

<!-- page 208: 2 nuggets -->

### Position Sizing with Momentum (Streaks)

- [MUST KNOW][N156] **Bet bigger when winning, smaller when losing —
  Trout's anti-mean-reversion sizing philosophy**:
  - "The better I'm doing, the bigger I play, and the worse I'm doing,
    the smaller I play."
  - Trout believes in streaks — not just in trading but in life
    generally: "If a team has won eight games in a row, you don't bet
    against them winning their ninth game."
  - **Mechanism**: strong performance indicates the edge is working
    and conditions are favorable → increase size. Poor performance
    indicates something is off → reduce exposure and preserve capital.
  - This is the OPPOSITE of mean-reversion thinking ("it can't keep
    going up, time to reduce") — Trout explicitly rejects that
    instinct

### Stop Orders: Why Large Traders Avoid Them

- [SHOULD KNOW][N157] **Why Trout avoids placing stop orders in
  the market — the size-amplification problem**:
  - "If I put a large stop order in the market, not only is it going
    to have a tendency to get hit, but when it does get activated,
    prices are likely to run. So I will not only get stopped out, but
    I will get filled at an average price significantly worse than
    my stop."
  - **Double penalty for large traders**: (1) the stop is a visible
    target for floor traders who push prices to trigger it, and
    (2) when it triggers, the large size itself moves the market
    further against you during execution
  - **Alternative**: occasionally use stop orders as a tool to get
    price to move toward a desired level, then cancel before the
    order can be triggered — a tactical feint
  - Mental stops with discretionary execution replace hard stops at
    large size

<!-- page 209: 2 nuggets -->

### Commercial Systems: How to Evaluate Them

- [SHOULD KNOW][N158] **Monroe Trout's approach to commercial
  trading systems**:
  - Has bought many commercial systems; now delegates evaluation to
    staff
  - **Has never used any commercial system as-is** — uses them
    exclusively as a source of ideas for building proprietary systems
  - Systems for public sale almost always have two fatal problems:
    1. **Insufficient observations** to be statistically significant
    2. **Returns based on exchange minimum margin** rather than realistic
       account size — this inflates percentage returns astronomically
       while masking the true drawdown-to-capital ratio. If you actually
       traded with only minimum margin, drawdowns would wipe you out.
  - Trout admits making this same mistake in his senior thesis —
    assumed account size of double the exchange minimum margin,
    which was "grossly inadequate"
  - **"If someone developed a really good system, they wouldn't be
    selling it"** — Trout agrees with this "to some degree"; a truly
    exceptional system developer should be able to find capital from
    friends or family instead of selling the system

- [NICE TO KNOW][N159] **Resources for evaluating commercial systems**:
  - **Club 3000**: newsletter formed by traders who paid ~$3,000 for a
    system they felt was worthless; members share information about
    various systems. Named after the founding frustration price.
  - Publications: *Futures*, *Technical Analysis of Stocks and
    Commodities*, *Commodity Traders Consumers Report* — all review
    commercial systems
  - Mandatory: test any purchased system on your OWN data, not the
    vendor's backtested samples

<!-- page 210: 1 nugget -->

### Efficient Markets: Practitioner Rebuttal

- [MUST KNOW][N160] **Trout's direct rebuttal of the Efficient
  Market Hypothesis (EMH)**:
  - "The markets are clearly not a random walk. The markets are not
    even efficient because that assumption implies you can't make an
    above-average return. Since some people can do that, I disagree
    with the assumption."
  - On why academic research misses what practitioners find: "A person
    in an academic setting might think that they have tested all
    possible types of systems. However, when you have real money on
    the line, you can start to think pretty creatively. There is always
    something else to test."
  - Academic studies are too short and too narrow: "Certainly, if you
    just spend a short time doing an academic study, you're not going
    to find anything significant. It can't be any other way. If it
    were, everyone would be rich."
  - **The practitioner advantage**: "But if you spend every day of
    your life researching the markets and have adequate computer
    support, you can find stuff that works."
  - Also pointed: "That's probably why they're professors and why I'm
    making money doing what I'm doing."

<!-- page 211: 1 nugget -->

### Confidence and Trading Success

- [SHOULD KNOW][N161] **The asymmetric relationship between
  confidence and trading success**:
  - Confidence is correlated with success, but not 100%: "Some people
    are just confident, but if they don't have an edge in the market,
    it doesn't matter; they're still going to lose money."
  - **Direction matters**: Nearly ALL good traders are confident, but
    NOT all confident people are good traders
  - Confidence without edge = losing money with conviction
  - Confidence WITH edge = the psychological foundation that lets you
    execute consistently, hold winners, and weather drawdowns
  - Trout became confident when he "knew he was getting statistically
    significant results" — i.e., confidence derived from rigorous
    evidence, not from a hot streak

<!-- page 212: no new nuggets (EMH rebuttal continues from p.210, captured in N160) -->

<!-- page 213: 2 nuggets -->

### Traits and Advice for Aspiring Traders

- [SHOULD KNOW][N162] **What makes a successful trader — Trout's
  framework**:
  - Five traits: **rational, analytical, able to control emotions,
    practical, profit-oriented**
  - Advice for someone wanting to become a trader:
    1. Learn a lot of **statistics**
    2. Learn to use a **computer**
    3. Find some **systems that work**
    4. Develop simple **risk management rules**
  - Books given to every new trader at Trout's firm:
    - *The Complete Guide to the Futures Markets* — **Jack D. Schwager**
      (John Wiley & Sons, 1984)
    - *The Handbook of Futures Markets* — **Perry Kaufman** (John Wiley
      & Sons, 1984)
    - *The Commodity Futures Game: Who Wins? Who Loses? Why?* —
      **Richard J. Tewles** and **Frank J. Jones** (McGraw-Hill, 1987)
    - *Market Wizards* — "a good motivational work" (Schwager's prior
      book — Trout recommends it to his own traders)

### Common Misconceptions and Mistakes in Markets

- [MUST KNOW][N163] **The two root causes of retail trader failure —
  Trout's diagnosis**:
  - **Misconception**: "They believe you can make tons of money with
    little work. They think you can make 100 percent a year doing a
    little bit of research on the weekends. That's ridiculous."
  - **Actual mistake #1**: Entering the market without any edge. "They
    get in the market because their broker told them that the market
    is bullish. That is not an edge."
  - **Actual mistake #2**: Betting too much too soon. "Most small
    speculators will never be around long enough to find out whether
    their system could have worked, because they bet too much on their
    trades, or their account is too small to start."
  - **The trap**: A trader might actually have a viable edge but will
    never discover it because overleveraging knocks them out before the
    strategy has a chance to prove itself over a statistically
    meaningful sample
  - Also: blaming others — Trout was "galled" by a *Wall Street
    Journal* article about a trader who sued his brokerage firm for
    losses even though he was calling his own trades. Personal
    responsibility is absolute in his worldview.

<!-- page 214: no new nuggets (misconceptions section continues from p.213, captured in N163) -->

<!-- page 215: 1 nugget -->

### 24-Hour Operation and the Cost of Trading at the Top Level

- [NICE TO KNOW][N164] **The lifestyle cost of elite trading —
  Trout's personal example**:
  - 24-hour operation: night traders staffed with instructions to call
    and wake Trout if something important happens (~4 times/year)
  - Uses a hand-held quotation device to check markets at home every
    night — acknowledges this is "overbearing" and he has a tendency
    to become compulsive
  - In a 1.5-year period: only **3 days off total**
  - Allots himself 10 vacation days per year; takes none
  - "I firmly believe that for every good thing in life, there's a
    price you have to pay."
  - Replaced basketball (was college team captain) with trading as his
    consuming passion — no nostalgia for it because "I'm on to the
    next big thing: trading"

<!-- page 216: no new nuggets (lifestyle/personal section, continuation of N164) -->

<!-- page 217: 1 nugget -->

### Final Trading Rules and Goals

- [MUST KNOW][N165] **Trout's trading rules distilled to their
  essence — and the one goal that defines his benchmark**:
  - **Rule 1**: Make sure you have the edge. Know what your edge is.
  - **Rule 2**: Have rigid risk control rules.
  - **The hierarchy**: "Good money management alone isn't going to
    increase your edge at all. If your system isn't any good, you're
    still going to lose money, no matter how effective your money
    management rules are. But if you have an approach that makes money,
    then money management can make the difference between success and
    failure."
  - **Edge is necessary; money management is multiplicative on edge,
    not a substitute for it**
  - **Goal**: **30% annual return** with **no peak-to-valley drawdown
    greater than 10%** — this ratio (3:1 return-to-drawdown) is the
    benchmark he measures himself against
  - "If I ever don't feel [excited and confident about the future],
    I will stop trading." — confidence as an ongoing prerequisite, not
    a permanent state

<!-- page 218: 1 nugget -->

### Schwager's Post-Interview Analysis of Trout

- [MUST KNOW][N166] **Schwager's synthesis: why Trout's return/risk
  profile is exceptional and what explains it**:
  - Schwager spent many years developing technical systems; found
    systems that approach Trout's return levels, but they "invariably
    exhibit much greater volatility" — drawdowns of 25% commonplace,
    worst-case exceeding 50%
  - Reducing leverage to reduce volatility would bring returns down to
    mediocre levels — the high return AND low drawdown combination
    cannot be replicated by systems alone
  - **Key observation**: every trader Schwager interviewed who
    displayed high return combined with very low risk "invariably
    proved to be a discretionary trader" — Trout is an anomaly who
    appears systematic but executes like a discretionary trader
  - **Schwager's explanation**: Trout uses statistical systems (not
    trend-following) PLUS exceptional discretionary timing of entry
    and exit. The timing skill alone accounts for roughly half his
    return by his own estimate.
  - **"He may reach his trading decisions in a similar fashion to that
    of system traders, but he executes these decisions like a
    discretionary trader."**
  - **Trout's twofold message** (Schwager's summary):
    1. You must have an edge — everything else is secondary; great
       money management with no edge still loses money
    2. Once you have an edge, you must exercise rigid risk control
       because tail events (sudden large price moves) occur far more
       often than standard statistical assumptions suggest — as proven
       in Trout's own senior thesis on fat tails

<!-- page 219: 1 nugget -->

