## Chapter: Al Weiss — The Human Chart Encyclopedia

### Al Weiss: Track Record Overview

- [MUST KNOW][N167] **Al Weiss's track record — potentially the
  best return/risk ratio for any long-term CTA**:
  - Trading since 1982 under **AZF Commodity Management**
  - Average annual return: **52%** from 1982–1991
  - $1,000 invested in 1982 → ~$53,000 by end of 1991 (10 years)
  - **Low drawdowns**: largest single equity drawdown in entire period
    was **17% in 1986**
  - In the most recent four years (1988–91), refined risk control
    further: worst annual drawdown averaged **under 5%** while average
    annual return **exceeded 29%**
  - **Return-to-drawdown profile**: roughly 6:1 in recent years
    (29% return / <5% drawdown) — exceptional by any standard
  - Until 1991, refused to grant ANY interviews: "I didn't feel my
    methods were proven until I had realized at least a decade of
    superior performance." Also worried interviews would attract the
    wrong type of investors.
  - AUM cap: ~$100 million — beyond which he believes performance
    would be negatively affected; regularly turns away new investors
  - Exception for small accounts ($100K) if the investor impresses
    him — e.g., accepted one investor who had gone through the track
    records of 500 CTAs before making a selection

<!-- page 220: 1 nugget (intro continues, chapter beginning) -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N168] **Why Weiss kept a low profile for a decade —
  and what changed**:
  - Principle: results must be proven over a MINIMUM of 10 years
    before claiming genuine alpha vs. luck
  - Fear of attracting wrong investors: early investors who don't
    understand the strategy may panic and redeem at the wrong time,
    damaging the fund's ability to execute
  - Once AUM hit the maximum manageable level (~$100M), the concern
    about investor type became moot — no new investors needed
  - The "impressed investor" criterion: Weiss still makes exceptions
    for small accounts when an investor demonstrates genuine diligence
    (e.g., reviewing 500 CTA track records before choosing) — signals
    someone who will be a long-term, rational partner rather than a
    hot-money chaser

<!-- pp. 219-240 -->

<!-- page 219: 1 nugget -->

### Trout Conclusion: Edge + Risk Control as Universal Formula

- [MUST KNOW][N148] **The two-part formula that Schwager distills
  from Trout (and echoes across all market wizards)**:
  1. You must have a demonstrable edge — if you can't prove it,
     you can't win. Many traders enter markets with no evidence
     of an edge whatsoever.
  2. You must exercise rigid risk control against the fat-tail events
     that standard statistical assumptions massively underestimate —
     as Trout's own PhD thesis showed, sharp price moves are far
     more frequent than a normal distribution implies
  - Traders wiped out by sudden large adverse moves are "not simply
    unlucky" — these events happen often enough that they must be
    planned for
  - **Cross-trader validation**: Trout (directional trader) and
    **Blair Hull** (arbitrageur) use completely different methods
    but arrive at virtually the same conclusion — edge + money
    management controls is the universal answer

