## Chapter: Al Weiss — The Human Chart Encyclopedia

<!-- page 220: 2 nuggets -->

### Track Record Overview

- [MUST KNOW][N149] **Al Weiss's performance as a CTA (AZF Commodity
  Management, 1982–1991) — arguably the best return/risk ratio in
  the industry**:
  - **52% average annual return** since 1982
  - $1,000 invested in 1982 → ~$53,000 by end of 1991
  - Largest single equity drawdown in the entire period: **17%**
    (in 1986)
  - 1988–1991: worst annual drawdown averaged **under 5%**;
    average annual return **over 29%** — a ratio of ~5.8x
  - Managing approximately **$100 million** — the level at which
    Weiss believes scale would hurt performance
  - Refused all interviews until 1991, saying: "I didn't feel my
    methods were proven until I had realized at least a decade of
    superior performance"
  - Also turned away large institutional money ($30M from a French
    bank) while accepting a sincere $100K individual investor —
    demonstrating priorities were not AUM maximization

- [SHOULD KNOW][N150] **Weiss's personal investment portfolio of
  external traders — built as a composite, not individual bets**:
  - Reviewed ~800 traders' track records over the years; selected
    ~20 for personal investment
  - Goal: NOT to pick individual supertraders but to **blend traders
    whose composite reflects both good returns AND very low
    drawdowns**
  - The group's 1988–91 composite: **19% average return, under 3%
    maximum annual drawdown** (ratio = 6.3x, nearly matching Weiss's
    own 5.8x ratio for the same period)
  - The group performs like a "Weiss clone" — he has essentially
    replicated his own equity curve using other people's strategies

<!-- page 221: 1 nugget (interview logistics — low information density) -->
<!-- page 222: no new nuggets (interview atmosphere/logistics only) -->

<!-- page 223: 2 nuggets -->

### How Weiss Developed His Approach

- [SHOULD KNOW][N151] **Four years of pre-trading research before
  risking any serious capital**:
  - "It was not an overnight process. I spent four years of solid
    research before doing any serious trading."
  - Literally thousands of hours poring over charts going as far
    back in history as available data allowed
  - Grain markets: studied charts back to **the 1840s**
  - Motivation: "I'm a risk-averse person. I wanted to have
    confidence in my approach before I started."
  - Financial support during research: had pioneered the
    **urethane skateboard wheel** in his early twenties —
    a major financial success — then invested proceeds in real
    estate, so had full financial independence to research

- [MUST KNOW][N152] **Why long historical data is not optional —
  it's required for interpreting current chart patterns correctly**:
  - Markets behave differently in different economic cycles
  - Recognizing repeating and shifting long-term patterns requires
    extensive history — short history cannot reveal cycle-dependent
    behavior
  - Identifying where you are in an economic cycle
    (inflationary vs. deflationary phase) is **critical to
    interpreting the chart patterns evolving at that time**
  - Most technical traders have gone back only 30 years; Weiss went
    back **100–150 years** in the markets where data existed

<!-- page 224: 2 nuggets -->

### What Separates Weiss From Other Technical CTAs

- [MUST KNOW][N153] **Three specific differentiators that explain
  why Weiss outperforms other technical system traders**:
  1. **Depth of historical research** — most technicians go back
     30 years; Weiss goes back 100+ years (grain: 1840s)
  2. **Economic cycle context** — he does NOT interpret the same
     pattern the same way in all conditions; where he believes we
     are in the long-term economic cycle substantially changes his
     conclusion from the same chart formation
  3. **Patterns within patterns** — he does not look at classical
     patterns (head-and-shoulders, triangles) as independent
     formations; instead he looks for specific **combinations of
     patterns**, or patterns within patterns — these more complex
     multi-pattern combinations signal much higher probability trades
  - These three factors together can produce "very substantial
    differences" in conclusions vs. traders not incorporating them

<!-- page 225: 3 nuggets -->

### On Pattern Accuracy and Risk Control

- [MUST KNOW][N154] **A 50%-accurate pattern is still highly
  profitable with proper risk control — win rate is not the
  point**:
  - Most popular chart patterns are accurate only about 50% of
    the time
  - "That's not a drawback. A pattern that works 50 percent of
    the time can be quite profitable if you employ it with a good
    risk control plan."
  - Implication: edge is about **expected value** (win rate ×
    payoff), not win rate alone. The entire framework of pattern
    trading collapses without the accompanying risk management.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N155] **Technical analysis is both art and science
  — and becomes a science only when you mathematically define
  your patterns**:
  - Art dimension: ask 10 traders to define a head-and-shoulders
    pattern → 10 different answers
  - Science dimension: for any individual trader, the definition
    CAN be made mathematically precise
  - "Chart traders are artists until they mathematically define
    their patterns — at which time they become scientists."
  - Implication: the reason most chart traders fail is that they
    never cross this threshold — they stay at the discretionary/
    artistic phase without forcing precision

- [MUST KNOW][N156] **Why fundamentals alone fail in commodity
  markets — psychology dominates, and the interim can wipe you
  out even when you're eventually right**:
  - Fundamental traders in commodity markets have "usually ended
    up losing" — the markets operate more on psychology than
    fundamentals
  - Example: silver's true fundamental value may be ~$8. In 1980
    commodity inflation boom, silver reached **$50** — completely
    disconnected from any fundamental value
  - Eventually the market returned to base value (always does in
    history — Weiss can't think of a single commodity that didn't)
  - BUT: anyone trading purely on fundamentals during the run-up
    would have been **wiped out** in the interim
  - Lesson: being fundamentally correct and being solvent are two
    different things; psychology governs the path, not just the
    destination

<!-- page 226: 2 nuggets -->

### Memorable Trades and System Override Philosophy

- [SHOULD KNOW][N157] **The Iraq/Kuwait oil trade — following
  signals without questioning them (summer 1990)**:
  - While on vacation in the Bahamas, Weiss was updating charts
    at a picnic table under palm trees
  - Noticed **buy signals in all the energy markets**
  - Found this odd because it's unusual to get a heating oil buy
    signal in summer
  - "I didn't question the trade and simply phoned in the orders"
  - Three days later, Iraq invaded Kuwait and oil prices exploded
  - Core lesson: the system saw the setup forming before the news
    event that explained it — trusting signals even when they feel
    counterintuitive

- [SHOULD KNOW][N158] **System override philosophy: 90%+ system
  compliance, with selective discretionary improvement**:
  - Follows system "well over 90 percent of the time"
  - Occasionally deviates to try to outperform the system
  - Key condition: uses deviations "very selectively" — not
    frequently
  - The selective discretion has "improved performance overall"
  - 1987 crash example: system still had a short T-bill position,
    but Weiss anticipated a flight to T-bills following the equity
    crash and **closed the entire short ahead of any system reversal
    signal** — the T-bill market then rallied sharply

<!-- page 227: 2 nuggets -->

### Market Cycles — Inflation/Deflation and Market-Specific Cycle Lengths

- [MUST KNOW][N159] **The 47-60 year inflation/deflation long-wave
  cycle and its implications for chart interpretation**:
  - "There are cycles in everything — the weather, ocean waves,
    and the markets."
  - Most important long-term cycle: **inflation → deflation**,
    repeating roughly every **two generations (47–60 years)**
  - In commodity markets, each deflationary phase has historically
    lasted **8–12 years**
  - At time of interview (~1991): commodity deflation began in 1980,
    making it the 12th year — Weiss believed commodity prices were
    very close to a major bottom
  - This cycle context is why the same chart pattern has different
    meaning in an inflationary vs. deflationary environment —
    it's not just what the pattern looks like, it's WHEN in the
    cycle it appears

- [SHOULD KNOW][N160] **Cycle frequency varies dramatically by
  market — not all markets cycle at the same pace**:
  - Grain markets (weather-dependent): major bull markets occur
    **~5 times per 20 years**
  - Gold market: a major bull cycle may occur only **3–5 times
    per century**
  - Implication: a trader trying to "play the next gold bull wave"
    may wait decades — gold is "very frustrating" for traders who
    don't understand this low-frequency cycle structure
  - Practical application: position sizing, patience level, and
    strategy must be calibrated to the specific market's cycle
    frequency

<!-- page 228: 1 nugget -->

### Weiss's Core Belief About Markets

- [MUST KNOW][N161] **Markets are ultimately based on human
  psychology — charts are its graphic representation**:

> "The essential element is that the markets are ultimately based
> on human psychology, and by charting the markets you're merely
> converting human psychology into graphic representations. I
> believe that the human mind is more powerful than any computer
> in analyzing the implications of these price graphs."
> — Al Weiss

  - Schwager's synthesis: Weiss succeeds through the combination
    of vast historical research AND a natural ability to perceive
    complex patterns that others miss
  - Key practical input for others: chart analysis reliability
    is greatly enhanced by treating patterns as **parts of more
    complex combinations** rather than in isolation
  - Proof of concept: Weiss's consistent high returns + low max
    drawdowns provide compelling evidence that **pure chart
    analysis** can yield an extraordinarily effective trading
    approach

<!-- page 229: no new nuggets (Part IV title page — Fund Managers and Timers) -->

