## Chapter: Stanley Druckenmiller — The Art of Top-Down Investing

<!-- page 230: 2 nuggets -->

### Track Record and Background

- [MUST KNOW][N162] **Stanley Druckenmiller's performance profile —
  top-down macro at multibillion-dollar scale**:
  - Took over **Quantum Fund** from **George Soros** (his mentor
    and idol) — managing $2.0–$3.5 billion
  - In three years running Quantum: **average annual return over
    38%** on those assets
  - Own **Duquesne Capital Management** (founded 1980): averaged
    **37% annually** since inception; from mid-1986 (when he
    restructured to flexible multi-asset approach): **45% annually**
  - Strategy evolution: started as conventional stock portfolio
    manager → transformed into eclectic approach trading bonds,
    currencies, and stocks from both long AND short side
  - Left Dreyfus (where he managed 7 funds simultaneously) because
    the strain was too great and he wanted to work with Soros,
    whom he considers "the greatest investor of our time"

- [SHOULD KNOW][N163] **Career trajectory — the eccentric mentor
  Speros Drelles and the value of naive inexperience**:
  - Dropped out of economics PhD program (found it "overly
    quantitative and theoretical, with little emphasis on real-life
    applications"); joined Pittsburgh National Bank as management
    trainee
  - Within months: hired as bank and chemical stock analyst by
    **Speros Drelles**, director of investments — a brilliant,
    eccentric mentor who kept chart books (considered a "kook"
    by colleagues)
  - At age 25, with only ~1 year of experience, Drelles promoted
    Druckenmiller to **director of equity research** — leapfrogging
    all senior analysts with MBAs and decades of experience
  - Drelles's reason: "For the same reason they send eighteen-year-
    olds into war. Because they're too dumb to know not to charge."
  - Context: small-cap stocks had been in a bear market for 10
    years (1968–1978); Drelles believed a huge liquidity-driven
    bull market was coming — and wanted someone without the
    psychological "scars" of a decade of failure

<!-- page 231: no new nuggets (Druckenmiller's Dreyfus tenure details, same as page 230 context) -->

<!-- page 232: 2 nuggets -->

### Drelles's Most Important Lesson: What Makes Stocks Move

- [MUST KNOW][N164] **The key analytical question that reoriented
  Druckenmiller's entire career: "What makes the stock go up and
  down?"**:
  - Druckenmiller submitted a thorough, comprehensive paper on the
    banking industry — felt proud of it
  - Drelles's response: "This is useless. What makes the stock
    go up and down?"
  - This comment forced Druckenmiller to abandon comprehensive
    fundamental coverage in favor of identifying the **specific
    factors most strongly correlated with a stock's price
    movement**
  - His findings by sector:
    - **Bank stocks**: key factor = earnings
    - **Chemical stocks**: key factor = **capacity** (NOT earnings)
      - Best time to buy: after lots of capacity has left the
        industry + a catalyst that will trigger demand increase
      - Best time to sell: when new plant expansion announcements
        appear — NOT when earnings turn down (too late)
      - Reason: new plants mean earnings will fall in 2–3 years;
        market anticipates this well in advance
  - "Frankly, even today, many analysts still don't know what
    makes their particular stocks go up and down."

<!-- page 233: 1 nugget -->

### The Oil Stocks Concentration Call and Naive Conviction

- [SHOULD KNOW][N165] **The Shah of Iran deposition → 70% oil
  stock concentration — inexperience as competitive advantage**:
  - When the Shah was deposed, Druckenmiller (25 years old, newly
    unprotected research director) immediately decided to put
    **70% of the portfolio in oil stocks**, 30% in defense stocks
  - "This course of action seemed so logical to me that I didn't
    consider doing anything else. At the time, I didn't yet
    understand diversification."
  - Used his authority as research director to restrict stock
    selection committee presentations primarily to oil and defense
  - Other department heads argued against him for political reasons
    ("just for the sake of taking the opposite view")
  - The call was exactly right — outperformed S&P 500 by multiples;
    result: named director of investments (Drelles's former position)
    at 25/26 years old
  - Druckenmiller's own verdict: "Of course, now I would never even
    dream of putting 70 percent of a portfolio in oil stocks, but at
    the time I didn't know better. Fortunately, it was the ideal
    position to have."
  - **Lesson**: there are moments in a career when everything aligns,
    and naive conviction (without the scars of past failures)
    allows the right bold move to be made

<!-- page 234: no new nuggets (continuation of above episode, director of investments appointment) -->

<!-- page 235: 2 nuggets -->

### Leaving the Bank, Launching Duquesne, Near-Death Experience

- [SHOULD KNOW][N166] **The 1980 NY presentation and the offer
  that launched Duquesne Capital Management**:
  - At a 1980 presentation in New York, an audience member
    exclaimed: "You're at a bank! What the hell are you doing
    at a bank?"
  - Within minutes: offered $10,000/month just to speak with him
    — no reports required
  - Context: as director of investments at the bank,
    Druckenmiller was still earning only $48,000/year
  - Launched **Duquesne Capital Management** in February 1981 with
    one analyst, a secretary, and $1 million AUM (generating
    $10,000/year in fees — vs. $120,000/year consulting retainer)
  - Caught the small-cap rally immediately but then: went 50% cash
    in mid-1981 (valuations extreme, rates at 19%), yet STILL lost
    12% in Q3 1981 on the remaining position
  - Druckenmiller's revelation: "This is criminal. We have never
    felt more strongly about anything than the bear side of this
    market and yet we ended up down for the quarter."
  - **Policy change**: if ever that bearish again → go **100%
    cash** — no half measures

<!-- page 236: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N167] **Being right on direction but still losing
  due to excessive leverage — the T-bill futures disaster of 1982**:
  - Drysdale Securities (Duquesne's consulting client) collapsed,
    instantly eliminating the $120,000/year consulting revenue
  - Overhead: $180,000/year; new revenue: $70,000 (1% on $7M AUM);
    firm assets: under $50,000
  - Druckenmiller was "absolutely convinced" interest rates were
    coming down — took all firm capital and put it into T-bill
    futures
  - **Lost everything in four days**
  - "The irony is that less than a week after we went bust,
    interest rates hit their high for the entire cycle."
  - He was correct on direction but levered himself into ruin
    before the move materialized
  - "That was when I learned that you could be right on a market
    and still end up losing if you use excessive leverage."
  - Recovery: sold 25% of Duquesne to a client for $150,000;
    one month later the bull market began; AUM climbed to $40M
    within a year

<!-- page 237: no new nuggets (same Drysdale/recovery episode continued from 236) -->
<!-- page 238: no new nuggets (Dreyfus joining context, same as page 230-231) -->

<!-- page 239: 2 nuggets -->

### The 1987 Crash — Setup, Positioning, and the Shift to Short

- [SHOULD KNOW][N168] **How Druckenmiller positioned going into
  the 1987 crash — went net short in June, two months early**:
  - First half of 1987: bullish, prices went straight up
  - June 1987: flipped to **net short** — then suffered two
    rough months as market continued rising against the position
  - Multiple funds were up **40%–85%** at the time he turned bearish
    (Dreyfus Strategic Aggressive Investing Fund: up ~40% in just
    its first quarter of operation)
  - The eventual crash vindicated the call — but the interim pain
    from fighting an uptrend for two months illustrates that even
    correct macro calls require tolerating painful mark-to-market
    losses

<!-- page 240: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N169] **Valuation for magnitude, liquidity and
  technicals for timing — Druckenmiller's framework for market
  turning points**:
  - What triggered the 1987 bearish shift (combination of factors):
    1. **Valuations extremely overdone**: dividend yield down to
       **2.6%**; price/book value ratio at all-time high
    2. **Fed tightening**: Fed had been tightening since January
       1987
    3. **Poor breadth**: market strength concentrated in large-cap
       stocks; broad market lagging badly — classic **blow-off**
       pattern where leadership narrows before collapse
  - Druckenmiller's key distinction on valuation:
    - **"I never use valuation to time the market."**
    - Valuation only tells him HOW FAR the market can go once a
      catalyst enters to change direction — it sets the magnitude
      of potential move, not the timing
    - Timing comes from **liquidity** (Fed policy, dollar behavior)
      and **technical analysis**
  - 1987 liquidity signal: Fed tightening since January 1987;
    dollar was "tanking" — suggesting further Fed tightening was
    coming → bearish liquidity environment confirmed the valuation
    extreme's danger

> "I never use valuation to time the market. I use liquidity
> considerations and technical analysis for timing. Valuation
> only tells me how far the market can go once a catalyst enters
> the picture to change the market direction."
> — Stanley Druckenmiller

<!-- pp. 239-260 -->

