## Chapter: Richard Driehaus — The Art of Bottom-Up Investing

### Background and Track Record

<!-- page 258: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N187] **Richard Driehaus — bottom-up growth stock
  investor with 30%+ net annual returns since 1980**:
  - Got hooked on markets as a teenager; invested $1,000 from
    newspaper route at age 13; early broker/columnist-guided
    investments failed — prompted him to educate himself via
    library newsletters and financial periodicals
  - Key early influence: **John Herold's** newsletter *America's
    Fastest Growing Companies* — showed 10x and 20x returns on
    growth stocks over 10 years; charts superimposing price and
    earnings over time made the earnings-growth thesis visually
    obvious
  - Career path: research analyst → institutional trading at
    **A. G. Becker** → director of research at Mullaney Wells and
    Jessup Lamount → own firm (Driehaus Capital Management) in 1980
  - At A.G. Becker (1970-73): rated in the **top 1% of all
    portfolio managers** by Becker's Fund Evaluation Service
    (the largest fund rating service at the time)
  - Since 1980: averaged **30%+ annual return net of fees** vs.
    S&P 500 at 16.7% and Russell 2000 at 13.5% (compounded)
  - **Dollar comparison**: $1 invested in the Russell 2000 in 1980
    = $4.56 by end of 1991; same $1 in Driehaus's Small Cap Fund
    = **$24.65** — nearly 5.4x more

<!-- page 259: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N188] **Driehaus's Bull and Bear Partnership Fund —
  market-neutral pure stock selection vehicle**:
  - Concept: remove the impact of general market direction by
    **approximately balancing long and short positions** at all times
  - Market directional exposure near zero — performance entirely
    dependent on **individual stock selection skill**
  - First two years (1990-1991): **67% and 62% annual returns**
    (before 20% performance fee payout)
  - Only 3 of 24 months registering a loss; largest monthly loss
    only 4%
  - Philanthropic note: **Richard H. Driehaus Foundation** started
    in 1984 with $1M contribution of TCBY stock; grown to ~$20M by
    1991; distributes 5% of equity annually to charities

### Driehaus Early Career and Core Thesis Formation

<!-- page 260: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N189] **Driehaus's core thesis — earnings growth
  drives price over time; buy growth regardless of current P/E**:
  - Thesis formed in his teens via John Herold's newsletter:
    "It seemed logical that if a company's earnings were growing
    over a long period of time, its stock price had to go in the
    same direction."
  - Herold's charts showing price and earnings growth in harmony
    over 10-year periods was "a very powerful image"
  - **Why high-P/E growth stocks are systematically underowned**:
    - First job: brokerage research analyst; his best growth stock
      recommendations were largely ignored
    - Reason: high P/E multiples are **psychologically difficult
      to buy** — if brokers weren't turned off by high P/Es,
      their clients were
    - Brokers were primarily sales-oriented, not portfolio managers
    - This creates a **structural opportunity**: the market
      consistently underweights the highest-quality growers
      because the entry price *looks* expensive on backward-
      looking metrics
  - Moved to A.G. Becker institutional trading department because
    own recommendations were outperforming both the other
    portfolios and the firm's own research recommendations — given
    $400K of the **A.G. Becker Profit-Sharing Fund** to manage
    personally in early 1970
  - First live management period coincided with a **bear market**
    — suffered early large losses but stayed with the approach
  - Example: bought **Bandag** at $37; stock fell before eventually
    confirming the thesis — illustrates why conviction in the
    approach is necessary to survive drawdowns in high-P/E stocks

- [SHOULD KNOW][N190] **Driehaus's foundational lesson — broker and
  columnist recommendations are not investable; self-education
  is required**:
  - At age 13, invested $1,000 following financial columnist and
    broker recommendations — results were "very disheartening"
  - Lesson drawn: if you want to make money, you need to understand
    **what actually makes stock prices move** — not follow consensus
    opinion from people with misaligned incentives (brokers are
    salespeople, columnists are entertainers)
  - Went to the library daily, devoured every financial newsletter
    and periodical he could find — self-directed research
  - This habit formed the core of his investment philosophy before
    he ever had a job in finance
  - **Broader implication**: the market is populated by
    professionals with structural incentives misaligned with
    investor returns; alpha comes from thinking independently of
    that consensus

<!-- pp. 259-280 -->

## Chapter: Richard Driehaus — Winning Big with Small Stocks (continued)

<!-- page 259: 3 nuggets -->

### Origins: From Newspaper Route to Growth Stock Evangelist

- [SHOULD KNOW][N170] **Driehaus's formative investing education — from
  broker recs to self-directed library research**:
  - Age 13: invested $1,000 saved from newspaper route; followed
    financial columnist and broker recommendations; fared poorly
  - Reaction: didn't accept failure — went to local library on
    regular basis, read financial periodicals and newsletters
  - Most influential discovery: **John Herold's** newsletter
    *America's Fastest Growing Companies*
  - What made it compelling:
    1. Showed stocks up **tenfold and twentyfold** over ten years
    2. Herold's charts superimposed a stock's **price and earnings**
       over ten years — both graphs showing dramatic growth
    3. Core insight: if earnings grow over long period, price must
       follow — price/earnings harmony as a visual image
  - Early lesson: professional broker recommendations are not
    the path to wealth — independent research is

- [SHOULD KNOW][N171] **First job friction: growth stocks with high P/Es
  never got implemented**:
  - After college: securities analyst at small Midwestern brokerage
  - Discovered most of his best recommendations were never
    implemented in client portfolios
  - Why: **P/E multiples too high** — psychologically difficult
    for brokers AND clients; even if broker wasn't put off, client
    usually was
  - Added problem: brokers were primarily sales-oriented, not
    portfolio managers — didn't follow through
  - Left after ~2 years for **A.G. Becker** institutional trading
    department; published in-house recommendation letter
  - Management noticed his recommendations significantly
    outperforming; at **beginning of 1970** they gave him
    ~**$400,000** of the A.G. Becker Profit-Sharing Fund

<!-- page 260: 2 nuggets -->

### Early Test: Bear Market and the Bandag Lesson

- [SHOULD KNOW][N172] **First real money managed — coincided with 1970
  bear market; faith required**:
  - Starting $400K mandate coincided with bear market in stocks
  - Had to suffer early, large losses — key lesson: "you have to
    have faith in your approach in order to succeed"
  - First stock: **Bandag** (truck retreading), bought at **$37**
  - Dropped to **$22** — painful early mark-to-market loss
  - In the ensuing 1971–72 bull market: Bandag went up tenfold

- [MUST KNOW][N173] **The Bandag mistake — why you shouldn't sell a
  winner prematurely**:
  - About a year later, on a business trip, called office — Bandag
    up $5 that day, hitting new high of **$47**
  - Decided to take profits "with the idea of buying the stock
    back later"
  - Bandag then went straight up to a high of **$240** over the
    next year — couldn't rebuy at reasonable prices
  - Lesson: "It's not that easy to buy back a good stock once
    you've sold it"
  - Reinforced: **there's great advantage and comfort to being a
    long-term investor**
  - Practical caveat: despite this lesson, Driehaus still maintains
    shorter-than-average holding periods (see N174) — the lesson
    applies to stocks with intact fundamentals AND technicals

<!-- page 261: 2 nuggets -->

### The "Fastest Horse" Principle and High Turnover as Risk Reduction

- [MUST KNOW][N174] **High portfolio turnover as active risk management,
  not speculation — Driehaus's counterintuitive framework**:
  - Question: why hold shorter than other money managers?
  - Answer: always seek the best potential performance at the
    **current time** — not the best pick from six months ago
  - "Even if I think a stock I hold will go higher, if I believe
    another stock will do significantly better in the interim,
    I'll switch"
  - The "fastest horse" principle: want the fastest horse even
    if your current horse is still going in the right direction
  - More important: **get off the horse if it starts heading in
    the wrong direction**
  - Contrarian claim on turnover: "Most people believe high
    turnover is risky, but I think just the opposite"
  - Mechanism: high turnover REDUCES risk when it results from
    **taking a series of small losses to avoid larger losses**
  - Key discipline: does NOT hold stocks with deteriorating
    fundamentals or price patterns
  - This is different from churning — it's disciplined
    replacement, not random activity

<!-- page 262: 1 nugget -->

### Career Path: A.G. Becker → Mullaney Wells → Independent Management

- [NICE TO KNOW][N175] **Career path and first outside client**:
  - Fall 1973: left A.G. Becker to become research director for
    **Mullaney, Wells and Company** (small regional brokerage)
  - A.G. Becker let him continue managing their account
  - A woman in A.G. Becker's reconciliation department had
    noticed his stock-picking skill → gave him **$104,000** of
    her own money (most of her liquid assets) to manage
  - Timing: late 1973 was start of the worst bear market
    since the 1930s (**1973–74 bear market**)
  - Was fully invested — account dropped from $104K to
    under **$60,000** at worst point
  - Client's confidence never wavered — she stayed the entire
    time; account eventually grew to **$5.8 million after taxes**
  - Quote: "This stuff really works!"

<!-- page 263: 2 nuggets -->

### Home Shopping Network: The Largest Position Ever

- [MUST KNOW][N176] **Home Shopping Network (HSN) trade — largest
  position ever; anatomy of a growth stock pick**:
  - Source: analyst he'd sent to cable TV conference weeks before
    HSN went public in 1986
  - Fundamental story at IPO:
    - First six months of operation: sold **$64 million** in
      merchandise, earned **$7 million fully taxed**
    - "About the best results I had ever seen for a new company"
    - Still had enormous unrealized potential: only reaching
      limited number of subscribers, adding new subscribers
      fast, cable systems got profit share so easy to expand
  - IPO price: **$18** per share; first trade in low $40s
  - Could only get **100 shares** at IPO; bought bulk of position
    in low $40s to low $50s range
  - Did not find it hard to buy at 2x+ IPO: "the growth was
    tremendous, the company was making lots of money, and the
    potential seemed open-ended"
  - Within five months: stock reached **$100**
  - By early 1987: reached **$200**

<!-- page 264: 2 nuggets -->

### HSN Exit: Fundamentals + Technicals Both Signal Sell

- [MUST KNOW][N177] **How Driehaus exited HSN — dual signal (fundamental
  crack + technical breakdown) = aggressive sell**:
  - Sent analyst to Florida investor meeting hosted by HSN
    management in early 1987 (stock at $200)
  - Red flag from management's own admission:
    - Almost all growth was coming from **new subscribers**
    - Growth in order rates from **existing cable system customers**
      was not great
    - This meant the business was dependent on perpetual subscriber
      expansion, not deepening engagement
  - Simultaneously: stock had started to **break down technically**
  - Driehaus: "That was all I needed" — sold aggressively,
    eliminated entire position over the next few weeks
  - Key principle: fundamentals tell you **what** to own;
    when both fundamental story AND chart break simultaneously,
    that's the clearest exit signal

- [SHOULD KNOW][N178] **"Left brain" vs. "right brain" stock picks —
  U.S. Surgical as a right-brain story**:
  - **Left brain (micro) factors**: growth rates, multiples,
    margins, current earnings acceleration, relative strength,
    institutional sponsorship
  - **Right brain (macro) factors**: qualitative understanding
    of a market opportunity BEFORE the numbers confirm it
  - U.S. Surgical (USS) example — uncovered in **late 1989**:
    - Didn't yet have the great "left brain" characteristics
    - But had powerful fundamental story: best pipeline of
      **noninvasive surgical products** — an innovative sector
      Driehaus believed would be the fastest-growing medical
      market of the 1990s
    - "This new procedure was not heavily covered by Wall Street
       back in 1989"
    - Key: had to appreciate the potential of this market
      **before** the numbers came through powerfully
    - Stock subsequently showed all the left-brain characteristics
      in 1990 and 1991 as the thesis played out
  - Implication: early-stage thesis investing requires tolerating
    the absence of confirming quantitative signals

<!-- page 265: 2 nuggets -->

### Danek Group: No Universal Decision Rules

- [MUST KNOW][N179] **Danek Group (DNKG) trade — illustration that
  decision-making has no universal rules; context determines
  everything**:
  - Spinal implant manufacturer; bought at IPO in May 1991
  - Had everything Driehaus looks for: accelerating revenues/
    earnings, proprietary products in rapidly expanding market
  - Also in hot sector: healthcare-related stocks were in strong
    demand in 1991
  - Exploded from **$19 to $43** in first three months after IPO
  - Then: unsubstantiated rumor circulated that DNKG would have
    trouble at the **FDA** → stock cracked from $43 to $34
    in just a couple of days
  - This was his **second largest position** at the time
  - His normal rule: sell a portion when there's a problem
    (FDA trouble definitely qualifies)
  - Why he held full position this time (three reasons):
    1. Didn't believe the rumors
    2. Medical stocks were in strong demand marketplace-wide
    3. Trading-oriented judgment (not just investment-oriented)
  - Result: DNKG recovered and hit new highs, exceeding **$60**
    by end of 1991
  - Critical nuance: "If the market weren't strong, or if the
    medical products group had been weakening, then I might
    have sold the entire position. There are a lot of different
    inputs that can affect a decision, and there are **no
    universal decision rules**."
  - Lesson: same setup, different market conditions = different
    correct action — context cannot be abstracted away

<!-- page 266: 1 nugget -->

### Blockbuster Entertainment: Franchise Expansion Story

- [SHOULD KNOW][N180] **Blockbuster Entertainment (BV) — how a franchise
  expansion story builds conviction over time**:
  - Source: bullish research report from a Texas brokerage firm
  - Initial skepticism: growth rate estimates seemed aggressive;
    but technical indicators improving and concept compelling
  - Credibility factor: founded by **Wayne Huizenga** (founder
    and former chairman of **Waste Management**, another company
    Driehaus had been early in)
  - Conviction built at next quarterly earnings: growth rates
    impressive, made prior street estimate look conservative
    rather than aggressive
  - Thesis: VCR sales still growing substantially; Blockbuster's
    competition was mom-and-pop video stores with small
    selection; superstores with thousands of videocassettes
    were a structural competitive advantage
  - Increased position over following months; stock more than
    doubled

<!-- page 267: 2 nuggets -->

### TCBY: "You Can't Make a Harvest in the Wintertime"

- [MUST KNOW][N181] **TCBY trade — how good stocks fail in the wrong
  market environment; the "harvest in wintertime" principle**:
  - Summer 1984: broker friend pitched **This Can't Be Yogurt
    (TCBY)**; Driehaus skeptical ("I don't really like yogurt")
  - Broker sent prospectus AND product sample — tasted like
    ice cream
  - Prospectus showed **70–80% earnings growth**
  - Bought large position at IPO price of **$7**
  - Stock dropped to **$4.25** — client questioned large holding
  - Driehaus held AND bought more (waited for uptick before
    adding): "earnings growth was so spectacular that the stock
    was still a buy"
  - Couldn't figure out why stock fell: concluded small-cap stocks
    were so out of favor that TCBY was being dragged down with
    the group regardless of its own results
  - Eventually the stock reached **$200**
  - What turned it around: **market environment** changed to
    appreciating growth stocks — not just the company
  - Key quote and principle:

> "You can't make a harvest in the wintertime."
> — Richard Driehaus

  - Mechanism: company fundamentals were excellent the whole
    time; the constraint was the market's unwillingness to
    VALUE those fundamentals — only when the environment
    rotated to growth stocks did the price reflect the reality
  - Practical implication: even when you're right on fundamentals,
    you can be early; holding requires conviction AND tolerance
    for extended periods of no price recognition

- [SHOULD KNOW][N182] **How Driehaus decides to cut losses — the
  fundamentals vs. price action balance**:
  - Triggers for cutting: (1) change in fundamentals
    (disappointing earnings turn), OR (2) price action
  - "It's not purely deterministic; there's an element of art"
  - Must balance underlying faith in the company with the
    price action signal
  - In TCBY case: conviction on fundamentals was strong enough
    to override negative price action — the conviction level
    itself is the key variable
  - Implication: there is no mechanical stop rule —
    the appropriate stop is calibrated to one's degree of
    fundamental conviction

<!-- page 268: 2 nuggets -->

### Technical Analysis: Role and Method

- [MUST KNOW][N183] **How Driehaus uses technical analysis — confirms
  ideas, times entries, triggers exits**:
  - Has used charts for ~25 years ("that probably says as much
    as anything about how helpful and reliable I have found them")
  - Primary value: "a very unemotional insight into a stock in
    an otherwise emotional market"
  - **Hard rule**: will NOT buy a stock when it's dropping, even
    if he likes the fundamentals — "I have to see some stability
    in the price action before I buy"
  - Charts can also trigger SALE of current holding
  - Relative strength requirement: stock must be in top **10%**
    of the market, or at least top **20%**
  - On P/E ratios: "not a key variable" for high-growth stocks;
    long-term high-growth stocks often sell at higher multiples,
    especially newer companies; P/E "really measures investors'
    emotions, which swing wildly from fear to greed, and is
    only significant at extremes"
  - Summary of framework: **Fundamentals determine WHAT to buy.
    Technical analysis times WHEN to buy and sell.**

<!-- page 269: 1 nugget -->

### Buying on Fundamental News Breaks — The Dataram Example

- [SHOULD KNOW][N184] **Large one-day moves on significant news continue
  in the same direction — and you must buy at market**:
  - **Dataram Corporation** example: makes memory products for
    PCs and workstations
  - Earnings report: quarterly EPS up from $0.32 to $0.75;
    revenues up from $7M to $11M
  - Stock had closed at $26.375 the previous day; shot up $4 on
    the news → bought 25,000 shares at average cost of $30.25
  - Schwager: "Wasn't it hard to buy after a big move?"
  - Driehaus: comfortable buying because numbers were very
    strong AND market was moving toward technology stocks anyway
  - Eventually bought almost 4% of the company at average
    cost of $31.50; stock subsequently reached $58
  - General principle: "If there's a large move on significant
    news, either favorable or unfavorable, the stock will usually
    continue to move in that direction"
  - Execution: **must use market orders** not limit orders in
    these situations — limit orders won't get filled when a stock
    is gapping; "you basically have to bite the bullet"
  - Caveat: "It's taken time to get good at it" — not easy
    psychologically; a learnable skill

<!-- page 270: 2 nuggets -->

### Market Misconceptions: Short-Term Volatility vs. Long-Term Risk

- [MUST KNOW][N185] **Biggest market misconception: equating short-term
  volatility with long-term risk — and the "buy low" myth**:
  - Most investors confuse short-term price volatility with
    actual long-term risk — they are NOT the same thing
  - "The longer the time period, the lower the risk of holding
    equities"
  - Investors look at all price movement as negative; Driehaus
    looks at movement as a **constructive element**
  - Contrarian argument on "safe" investments: "Investment
    vehicles that provide the least short-term volatility often
    embody the **greatest long-term risk**. Without significant
    price movement, you can't achieve superior gains."
  - The real risk for many investors: **insufficient exposure
    to high-returning, more volatile assets**
  - On the "buy low, sell high" dogma: "I believe that far more
    money is made **buying high and selling at even higher prices**"
  - Mechanism: buying stocks with high relative strength means
    buying stocks in demand by other investors — the market
    is telling you something
  - Risk preference: would rather buy an increasing stock and
    risk it beginning to decline than invest in a declining
    stock and guess when it will turn around
  - Practical implication: **comfort is the enemy of
    superior performance** — the most comfortable approach
    (low P/E, near lows) is why most investors underperform

- [MUST KNOW][N186] **The market timing trap — the 40-day statistic**:
  - Since January 1980: market averaged **17% annual compounded
    return**
  - If you were out of the market on the **40 best days** (only
    **2% of trading days**): return dropped to **under 4%**
  - Moral: penalty for being out of the market on the wrong days
    is severe
  - And: "human nature being what it is, those are exactly the
    days that most people are likely to be out of the market"
    — people tend to reduce exposure after large losses, exactly
    when the best days cluster (sharp bounces after panic)
  - This is a direct empirical argument against market timing
    as a strategy

<!-- page 271: 1 nugget -->

### Traits of Successful Traders and Teaching Philosophy

- [SHOULD KNOW][N187] **Driehaus on trader traits and whether trading
  can be taught**:
  - Traits: **open-minded, flexible**, and risk takers "because
    they believe in what they're doing"
  - Has trained several people at his firm; all had zero previous
    market experience before joining; all doing very well
  - Can be taught — BUT only if the person has an open mind:
    "The mind is like a parachute — it's only good when it's open"
  - Caveat: each person must still develop an **individual
    philosophy** and tailor basic trading concepts to their
    own personality — trading philosophy cannot be simply
    transferred, must be acquired through research

<!-- page 272: 2 nuggets -->

### The October 1987 Crash — Driehaus's Experience

- [SHOULD KNOW][N188] **How Driehaus fared in October 1987 — saw it
  coming but couldn't fully exit**:
  - Small Cap Fund: down **34%** in October
  - But was up **46%** coming into October → finished year
    down only **3%**
  - Anticipated negative event ~**one week before** the crash
  - Warning signals he observed:
    1. Buying had dried up
    2. Sense of fear in the market
    3. Worried about **burgeoning use of portfolio insurance**
       (recognized as a systemic risk amplifier)
  - Had substantial portion of portfolio **up for sale** on
    Thursday and Friday before crash — couldn't liquidate
    as much as wanted

<!-- page 273: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N189] **The October 19 liquidity crisis — size problem
  in thin stocks during a crash**:
  - Were trying to sell at market — not limit orders
  - But: holdings were very thin stocks; size wanted to sell was
    too large relative to what the market could handle
  - Example: stock nominally trading at **$36 bid / $38 offered**;
    willing to sell 30,000-share position at $34 — but there
    were **no bids of any size** even well below the market
  - Key lesson for small-cap concentrated managers: **liquidity
    risk compounds in a crash** — it's not just price decline,
    it's the absence of any buyer at any price
  - Stopped trying to sell late in the day: "the break was so
    severe that it didn't seem to make any sense to try to
    sell unless the financial world was coming to an end"
  - Emotional state on that day: "very calm — felt detached,
    as if I had transcended the situation"

<!-- page 274: 1 nugget -->

### Resilience: Prior Experience with Greater Loss

- [SHOULD KNOW][N190] **Why prior crash experience builds resilience —
  1973–74 as psychological anchor**:
  - After smoke cleared: had lost roughly **one-third of his
    wealth in one day** (keeps almost all his money in his
    own funds)
  - Reaction: laughed and said "get it back!"
  - Why: "Actually, I had lost much more than that in 1973–74"
  - Having already survived something worse gave confidence that
    he could survive and recover from 1987
  - Quote: cited **Nietzsche** — "What does not destroy me,
    makes me stronger"
  - Source of sustained confidence: when A.G. Becker fund
    placed in **top 1%** of all funds surveyed — "My confidence
    in this trading philosophy has never wavered" since then

<!-- page 275: 1 nugget -->

### Core Philosophy: The Essential Element for Sustained Success

- [MUST KNOW][N191] **Having a core philosophy as the essential
  element for sustained outperformance**:
  - "The essential element is having a core philosophy. Without
    a core philosophy you're not going to be able to hold on
    to your positions or stick with your trading plan during
    really difficult times."
  - Requirements for a functional core philosophy:
    1. Must **fully understand** it
    2. Must **strongly believe** in it
    3. Must be **totally committed** to it
  - How to achieve that mental state: do a great deal of
    **independent research** — philosophy must be earned,
    not borrowed
  - "A trading philosophy is something that cannot just be
    transferred from one person to another; it's something
    that you have to acquire yourself through time and effort"
  - Final advice: "If you reach high, you just might amaze
    yourself"

> "Without a core philosophy you're not going to be able to
> hold on to your positions or stick with your trading plan
> during really difficult times. You must fully understand,
> strongly believe in, and be totally committed to your
> trading philosophy."
> — Richard Driehaus

<!-- page 276: 1 nugget -->

### Schwager's Commentary on Driehaus

- [MUST KNOW][N192] **Schwager's synthesis of what makes Driehaus's
  method work — the anti-comfort principle and the home-run
  requirement**:
  - Core thesis: price follows growth — picking companies with
    best potential earnings growth is the key; everything else
    is secondary
  - Structural problem with conventional "prudent" approach:
    buying only stocks with average-to-below-average P/Es
    **automatically eliminates many of the best performers**
  - Market inefficiency edge: Driehaus targets stocks NOT
    followed (or only lightly followed) by industry analysts
    → greater inefficiency → greater profit opportunity
  - Technical confirmation layer: before buying, wants price
    rising AND high relative strength; typically buys near
    recent highs — "fortunes are made by jumping on board
    the strongest fundamental and technical performers,
    not by picking bargains"
  - The psychological barrier: "Think of a broker espousing
    the same strategy in a telephone solicitation. 'Hello,
    Mr. Smith... What is the P/E ratio? Well, it's 60 to 1.
    How far is it from its low? Well, it's making new highs.
    Mr. Smith? Hello? Mr. Smith?'"
  - Broader principle: **successful strategies often require
    doing what most people find instinctively uncomfortable**
  - The reason most investors underperform: their natural
    inclination toward comfort (buying near lows, low P/Es)
    is the exact wrong approach for superior returns

<!-- page 277: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N193] **A small percentage of huge winners accounts for
  the bulk of superior performance — two requirements for
  capitalizing on them**:
  - "You don't have to be right the majority of the time, but
    you do have to take advantage of the situations when you
    are right"
  - Two essential elements:
    1. **Take larger positions** when high degree of confidence
       exists (e.g., HSN was Driehaus's largest position ever)
    2. **Hold long enough** to realize most of the potential —
       avoid temptation to take profits after a double or triple
       if fundamental and technical conditions still point higher
  - "The steely patience necessary to hold such positions to
    fruition is one of the attributes that distinguishes the
    Market Wizards from less skilled traders"
  - Cross-reference: "Even though Driehaus and Druckenmiller
    employ dramatically different approaches, 'home run' trades
    are an essential ingredient to the success of each"
  - Anti-comfort principle applied to selling: immediately
    liquidate a holding on a sharp one-day decline if negative
    news has changed the outlook — even though it feels wrong
    to sell into a one-day panic
  - Rule: "Do what is right, not what is comfortable"

