## Chapter: Gil Blake — The Master of Consistency

<!-- page 279: no content (chapter title page only) -->

<!-- page 280: 3 nuggets -->

### Gil Blake: The Statistics of Consistency

- [MUST KNOW][N194] **Gil Blake's performance record — possibly the
  most consistent track record in the book**:
  - Company name: **Twenty Plus** — business card/stationery
    shows probability curve with +20% return falling **two
    standard deviations to the left of the mean**
  - Implication: **95% probability** of realizing at least 20%
    annual return
  - Logo is more than marketing — probability curve doesn't
    even extend to 0% return, let alone negative territory
  - 12-year track record:
    - **45% average annual return**
    - **Never had a year below 20%** (worst year: 24% gain in
      1984 — and that was a "subpar" year to him)
    - In his worst year: made money in **all 12 months**
    - **134 out of 139 months** (96%) were breakeven or profitable
    - Had a streak of **65 consecutive months without a loss**
      (Schwager: "a feat that would qualify him as the Joe
      DiMaggio of trading" — DiMaggio's streak ended at 56)

- [MUST KNOW][N195] **Blake's fee structure — unique alignment showing
  extreme confidence in his own consistency**:
  - Charges clients: **25% of total annual profits**
  - Unusual guarantee: also agrees to pay clients **25% of any
    losses** AND **100% of losses** incurred in a new account
    during the **first twelve months**
  - "Obviously, he has not had to pay out on these guarantees yet"
  - This fee structure is only rational if you have
    extraordinary confidence in never having losing years —
    Blake's track record is the proof behind the promise
  - Contrast with standard hedge fund model (20% of profits,
    no downside participation): Blake's arrangement aligns
    manager and client interests far more completely

- [NICE TO KNOW][N196] **Blake stopped accepting client funds — why**:
  - Stopped accepting new client funds **five years ago**
  - Has made only two exceptions since — both for close friends
  - Implication: either managing capacity constraints or
    personal preference for a small, known client base;
    sets up the chapter's exploration of what his actual
    edge IS (to be continued in subsequent pages)

<!-- pp. 279-300 -->

## Chapter: Gil Blake — The Master of Consistency (continued)

<!-- page 279: no content (chapter title page — covered in previous batch) -->

<!-- page 280: covered in previous batch (N194–N196) -->

<!-- page 281: 3 nuggets -->

### Blake's Method: Mutual Fund Timing

- [MUST KNOW][N197] **Blake is a mutual fund timer — specific mechanics**:
  - Does NOT merely switch between a single fund and money
    market; also selects WHICH sector fund offers the best
    opportunity on any given day
  - Uses **purely technical models** — no fundamental analysis
  - **Holding period: 1–4 days** (very short-term)
  - Can show consistent monthly profits **even in months when
    the funds he traded registered significant declines**
  - Key insight: you don't need the underlying fund to go up
    to make money — you just need to correctly time the
    short-term direction within the fund's moves

- [SHOULD KNOW][N198] **Blake's background — a Wall Street outsider**:
  - Cornell graduate → 3 years as naval officer on a
    **nuclear submarine** → Wharton Business School
    (graduated with **highest honors**) → 3 years at
    **Price Waterhouse** → 9 years as **CFO of Fairfield
    Optical**
  - During this entire period: **no serious thoughts about
    trading**; still believed in the **random walk theory**
    (markets cannot be beaten)
  - Became a trader **fifteen years after graduating from
    college** — purely by accident
  - Context: his background is accounting/finance (numbers),
    not Wall Street — arguably an advantage (no bad habits)

- [NICE TO KNOW][N199] **Nursery school teacher's note about Blake**
  (saved by his mother):
  > "His claywork, painting, and carpentry all show an amazing
  > meticulous precision. He enjoys working with small things,
  > and is a perfectionist about it. Everything he does is
  > made up of many small parts instead of one, big splashy
  > form... He has an extraordinary interest and grasp of
  > numbers, and shows a real talent toward things mathematical."
  - Schwager raises "born or made?" question — this note
    suggests some traits were present from the very start
  - The pattern-finding in small increments, mathematical
    precision, and detail orientation describe his trading
    approach exactly

<!-- page 282: 1 nugget -->

### Origin Story: Discovering Nonrandom Behavior

- [SHOULD KNOW][N200] **How Blake became a trader — the accidental
  discovery**:
  - A colleague (**"Nick"**) showed Blake data on a Fidelity
    municipal bond fund he owned — interest rates were 10-11%
    tax-free, but NAV was steadily eroding
  - Nick noticed the **NAV had declined approximately
    twenty-two consecutive days** and asked: why not switch to
    cash when it goes down and back in when it goes up?
  - Blake's dismissive reaction: cited **_A Random Walk Down
    Wall Street_** and said "I don't think the markets work
    that way" — told Nick to get more data and he'd disprove it
  - Nick got more data. The persistence held up.
  - Blake: **"I quickly became convinced that there was
    definitely something nonrandom about the behavior of
    municipal bond funds"**
  - Mechanism of conversion: Blake was trying to DISPROVE
    an anomaly, and ended up confirming it — the right way
    to approach any trading edge

<!-- page 283: no content (transition/continuation only) -->

<!-- page 284: 3 nuggets -->

### The "One Penny" Rule and First Trading

- [MUST KNOW][N201] **The "one penny" rule — Blake's original edge**:
  - With two years of data: **approximately 83% probability**
    that any uptick or downtick day in a municipal bond fund
    NAV would be followed by a move in the **same direction**
    the next day
  - Called it the "one penny" rule — smallest directional
    signal was sufficient
  - Began trading Fidelity's municipal bond fund in his own
    account in **spring 1980** based on this observation
  - Critical context: this is an 83% hit rate on a
    zero-transaction-cost instrument — extraordinarily high
    for any market, and the reason it compounded so
    dramatically over time

- [MUST KNOW][N202] **Why the bond fund timing worked despite the
  bond market crashing — the mechanism**:
  - When Blake started (March 1980), NAV was ~$10.50
  - By end of 1981, NAV had eroded to ~$5.65 — **nearly 50%
    drop** in the underlying fund
  - Yet Blake **achieved gains in excess of 20% per year**
    on top of interest income adding another ~10%
  - Explanation: no transaction costs, and the NAV smoothing
    process meant short-term direction signals still worked
    even while the long-term trend was deeply negative
  - There was a 3-month period in early 1981 when there were
    **virtually no upticks** — NAV was all down or flat —
    while the underlying bonds DID have uptick days
    (Blake: "I don't know the answer. Maybe someone can
    explain it to me some day")
  - During declining periods: simply held cash — couldn't
    short a fund — but avoidance of losses + timing of gains
    during uptick periods was sufficient

- [MUST KNOW][N203] **Blake's conviction: mortgaging his home to trade**:
  - Took out **four successive second mortgages over a
    three-year period** to increase trading capital
  - Rationale: housing prices in Northeast were rising fast,
    so equity was available; the odds were so favorable it
    made no sense not to leverage
  - "Of course, I had to overcome the conventional wisdom.
    If you tell someone that you're taking out a second
    mortgage to trade, the response is hardly supportive.
    After a while, I just stopped mentioning this detail
    to others."
  - Key point: Blake's conviction came from data, not hope —
    he had personally verified the 83% edge; the mortgages
    were a rational bet at a known edge, not reckless gambling

<!-- page 285: 2 nuggets -->

### Fidelity's Switch Limits and Blake's Workaround

- [SHOULD KNOW][N204] **Fidelity's 4-switch guideline — how Blake
  navigated it**:
  - Fidelity's guideline: **4 switches per year per fund**
  - Blake was making **20–30 trades per year** because
    trends were so persistent (daily signal = ~20-30
    direction changes per year)
  - Fidelity's unofficial stance: "Just don't abuse it
    too much" — rule existed as a fallback provision,
    not strictly enforced initially
  - Later: Fidelity sent letters citing the guideline;
    Blake's solution: **rotate through different funds**
    — 4 trades in municipal bond fund, then 4 trades in
    high-yield municipal bond fund, then 4 trades in
    limited-term municipal bond fund, and so on
  - Technically complied with the letter of the rule while
    doing 20–30 total trades per year
  - Lesson: rules create friction but not always a wall;
    understanding the rule's purpose lets you find
    compliant alternatives

- [SHOULD KNOW][N205] **The edge degraded — and Blake's response**:
  - By the mid-1980s, the municipal bond fund pattern
    deteriorated:
    - Daily volatility dropped from **0.25–0.5%** (1979–
      early 1984) to only **0.1–0.2%**
    - Reliability/persistency of price direction dropped
      from **80%+ to below 70%**
    - Potential annual return fell to ~**20%** (which
      Blake considered insufficient)
  - Blake's response: **go back to the library** — fell
    1984 through spring 1985, he "practically lived there,"
    extracting years of data on ~100 mutual funds from
    microfilm
  - Key mindset: when an edge degrades, don't try to
    squeeze more out of it — research a new one

<!-- page 286: no content (transition only) -->

<!-- page 287: no content (transition only) -->

<!-- page 288: 2 nuggets -->

### The Sector Fund Breakthrough

- [MUST KNOW][N206] **How Blake discovered sector funds — the
  breakthrough insight**:
  - Equity funds showed tradable patterns but only **60%
    daily persistency** and **20–25% prospective annual
    returns** — not good enough
  - Initially ignored Fidelity sector funds because of
    **$50 fee per switch** — seemed unnecessary when many
    funds had zero fees
  - Two insights combined to change his mind:
    1. **Commodity-related funds** (gold, oil sectors)
       worked better than broadly based funds
    2. Technology, oil, and utility **indexes** were each
       significantly less random than a broad market index
  - Conclusion: the more HOMOGENEOUS the constituents,
    the more tradable the fund
  - "I couldn't believe that I had ignored sectors during
    my earlier research. I had almost missed it."
  - The $50 fee was worth it because the volatility was
    5x+ higher than bond funds — making the probability
    edge worth far more per trade

- [MUST KNOW][N207] **Sector fund statistics — the core edge**:
  - A price change **larger than the average daily move**
    in a given sector had **70–82% probability** of being
    followed by a move in the same direction the next day
  - Daily volatility comparison:
    - **Biotech**: ~0.8% per day average move
    - **Gold sector**: ~1.2% per day average move
    - **Municipal bond funds** (by then): 0.1–0.2% per day
  - Same 70–82% "batting average" applied to markets with
    5–10x the profit potential
  - Sector funds: **unlimited switching** (vs. 4-switch
    limit on other Fidelity funds) — just pay $50/trade
  - Combined: high volatility + high persistency +
    unlimited switching = the optimal system

<!-- page 289: 2 nuggets -->

### Why Sectors Are Tradable: The Mathematical Explanation

- [MUST KNOW][N208] **Blake's mathematical explanation for sector
  tradability — the "school of fish" / binomial model**:
  - Individual stock with relative strength: only ~**55%
    probability** of continuing in same direction next day
    — after commissions and bid/ask spread, not tradable
  - BUT: analogy to biased coins — if each coin has 55%
    probability of heads, and you flip 9 coins, odds of
    majority heads rises to **62%**; flip 99 coins →
    **75%**. This is the **binomial probability distribution**
  - Applied to markets: 99 chemical stocks each with 55%
    edge, all up 1–2% today while broad market is flat →
    the **entire chemical sector** behaves like a school
    of fish: odds of sector being up tomorrow jump to
    **~75%**
  - The aggregation of correlated, slightly-biased
    individual outcomes creates a much stronger sector-level
    signal
  - This is WHY sectors are more tradable than individual
    stocks OR broad indexes: they hit the sweet spot of
    sufficient homogeneity to aggregate the edge, without
    so much diversity that signals cancel out

- [SHOULD KNOW][N209] **Homogeneity = tradability: the proof**:
  - Fidelity's **leisure fund** (least homogeneous — included
    Budweiser, Pan Am, Holiday Inn) was **the least
    persistent** of all sectors
  - **Savings and loans** and **biotech** (most homogeneous)
    were consistently the **most persistent**
  - Broader market indexes: adding MORE stocks beyond a
    given sector actually REDUCES the next-day persistency
    probability — diversification destroys the edge
  - Implication: the key variable in sector selection is
    not industry type but internal homogeneity of members

<!-- page 290: 2 nuggets -->

### Execution: Anticipating the Signal

- [MUST KNOW][N210] **How Blake anticipated sector direction without
  waiting for the close**:
  - Mutual fund NAVs are set at the close; a one-day lag
    would lose most of the profit (average holding 2–3
    days, ~50% of profit on day 1)
  - Solution: **sample 10–20 stocks in each sector** during
    the trading day → get a very good estimate of where
    that sector will close
  - This allowed Blake to **anticipate the signal by one
    day**, entering before the NAV was set
  - The stock sample didn't need to be the exact Fidelity
    holdings: **any 15 stocks in the sector worked as
    well** as 15 specifically held by Fidelity
  - As long as sample size was large enough relative to
    the sector's total stocks, the estimate was reliable

- [SHOULD KNOW][N211] **Blake's position sizing and diversification
  philosophy**:
  - Bet **100% of the account** on one sector at a time
    — never split across multiple sectors simultaneously
  - Rationale: "I'm not a big fan of diversification.
    You can diversify very well by just making enough
    trades per year. If the odds are 70 percent in your
    favor and you make fifty trades, it's very difficult
    to have a down year."
  - Key distinction: **temporal diversification** (many
    trades over time) vs. **simultaneous diversification**
    (splitting across positions at same time)
  - At 70% win rate × 50 trades, the law of large numbers
    provides effective protection — no need to dilute
    the best signal with second-best signals

<!-- page 291: 1 nugget -->

### Sector Selection System

- [SHOULD KNOW][N212] **Blake's "flashing lights" sector selection
  system**:
  - Mental model: imagine a board with one light per sector
    — **red = avoid/liquidate**, **green = buy/hold**
  - Ranked each sector on combination of **volatility** and
    **historical reliability** (Blake's term: "persistency")
  - Strategy: always take the "**brightest green light**";
    when it turns red, switch to next brightest green
  - Typical pattern: when one sector turned red, they
    ALL turned red simultaneously → went entirely to cash
  - Needed only ONE green light to be invested; zero green
    lights = cash
  - The system made him a sector PICKER (not just a timer)
    — always in the best available opportunity at any time

<!-- page 292: 2 nuggets -->

<!-- page 293: 1 nugget -->

### Client Business and Fee Structure

- [SHOULD KNOW][N213] **Why Blake took on clients and how he
  recruited them**:
  - Took first client in **1982** — driven by economics:
    borrowing at **15%** via second mortgages from
    Phil Rizzuto at **The Money Store** while earning
    35–40% on the money; managing $100,000 of client
    money at 25% profit share was equivalent to
    effectively borrowing $25,000 for free
  - All clients were personal connections — friends and
    neighbors; never advertised
  - Recruitment method: invite a neighbor for a beer,
    say "I've been doing this for a couple of years
    and I'd like you to take a look at it"
  - Blake's observation on investor types:
    1. Give you money immediately with zero homework
    2. Would never give you money regardless of evidence
    3. Do serious homework and ask the right questions
  - Blake's clients: category 3 — they asked the right
    questions and communicated their risk tolerance
  - Account sizes: **$10,000 to $100,000**

- [MUST KNOW][N214] **Blake's fee structure — the full terms**:
  - **25% of capital gains**
  - **25% of any losses** (paid by Blake to client)
  - **100% of losses** if account is down after first
    twelve months
  - Schwager: "Heads you win one; tails you lose four."
  - Blake's answer: "What I was confident in was the
    probability of winning after fifty trades per year."
  - The guarantee remains active — he still offers it
    to new clients (only 2 accepted in last 5 years)
  - This structure is only rational with an extraordinary
    edge AND the statistical understanding that 50 trades
    at 70%+ virtually eliminates yearly losses

<!-- page 294: 2 nuggets -->

### Why Blake Stopped Taking New Money

- [SHOULD KNOW][N215] **Why Blake stopped accepting new accounts —
  market impact, not capacity**:
  - Not about managing too much money — about **market
    impact on other fund shareholders**
  - Very sensitive to impact on fund manager and on
    passive shareholders who don't switch as actively
  - Preferred to limit his trades to **~$2 million in
    a fund of several hundred million** so impact
    is minimal
  - Signal that he's running counter to consensus:
    when placing orders, often told "You're getting out?
    Most of the calls we're getting today are getting in"
  - Fund employees confirmed: **most active switchers
    in their own accounts end up doing WORSE than if
    they had done nothing**
  - Blake's edge partly depends on being a contrarian
    to the mass of unsuccessful market timers

- [SHOULD KNOW][N216] **Psychological dimension of client money ratio
  to own capital**:
  - 25% profit/loss sharing = exact equivalent of leverage
  - In 1986 took on new money too rapidly → found himself
    rationalizing only taking **50% positions** (e.g.,
    trading $200,000 of a $400,000 account) because full
    positions felt uncomfortable
  - Result: was reducing clients' return by half — not
    acceptable
  - Decision: **fewer clients, trade all of them fully**
    — never cut position size to manage psychological
    discomfort
  - Lesson: the right way to manage discomfort is to
    reduce AUM, not to reduce position size — the latter
    hurts clients while fooling yourself about risk

<!-- page 295: 2 nuggets -->

### Blake's Loss Psychology and Risk Management

- [MUST KNOW][N217] **Blake's "rehearsal" approach to losses —
  a practical psychological system**:
  - Routine: **before taking any position**, imagine the
    worst-case scenario and internalize how it would feel
  - Purpose: minimize confusion and emotional reaction
    IF that scenario actually materializes
  - Key rule: "If I can't get over the emotions of
    taking a loss in twenty-four hours, then I'm trading
    too large or doing something else wrong"
  - The **24-hour rule**: inability to recover emotionally
    within 24 hours is diagnostic — it means position
    sizing is wrong, not that the loss is unusually bad
  - Losses are "a very important part of trading. When
    a loss happens, I believe in embracing it."
  - This desensitization through pre-trade rehearsal
    allowed Blake to adapt to **growing dollar risk** as
    AUM expanded 15–20% per year — the same percentage
    loss means larger and larger absolute dollars, and
    emotional adaptation must keep pace

- [SHOULD KNOW][N218] **Blake's deliberate AUM growth limitation**:
  - AUM was growing ~50%/year until a few years before
    the interview; deliberately slowed to **15–20%/year**
  - Methods: encouraged client withdrawals, asked clients
    with multiple accounts to close one
  - Reason: comfort level with growing dollar risk requires
    gradual adaptation — forced rapid growth creates the
    size-discomfort-position-cutting spiral he experienced
    in 1986

<!-- page 296: 2 nuggets -->

### Notable Client Story: Trust and the Nervous Investor

- [SHOULD KNOW][N219] **The disastrous fall 1985 healthcare trades
  and what they revealed**:
  - Took on one non-personal client (via referral) —
    someone investing inherited money his wife received
  - Fall 1985: two consecutive disastrous healthcare
    trades:
    1. Entered healthcare the day industrialized nations
       announced a plan to weaken the dollar → dollar
       cracked, drug stocks soared → next day, plan
       deemed unlikely to work, dollar rebounded,
       drug stocks clobbered → big loss
    2. A week later, re-entered healthcare →
       Hospital Corporation of America reported
       surprisingly bad earnings → sector hit again
  - Each trade: about **2–3% loss**, account went from
    $70,000 to ~$67,000
  - Blake's extraordinary response: voluntarily went to
    the bank and sent the client a **cashier's check for
    $3,000** even though the guarantee didn't require
    payment until year-end; told him "Just hold onto the
    check; it's as good as cash"
  - Account recovered to over $70,000 about a month later
  - Client cashed the check — Blake later confirmed this
  - When reducing clients, this client was **first to go**:
    cashing the check was "indicative of a lack of trust"
  - Lesson: trust is the foundation of a manager-client
    relationship; the nervous client who monitors every
    move and cashes emergency checks despite recovery
    is not a good long-term fit

<!-- page 297: 2 nuggets -->

### Fidelity's Crackdown — The Energy Service Pattern

- [MUST KNOW][N220] **The energy service sector — Blake's most
  reliable pattern ever**:
  - After Fidelity introduced **hourly pricing** of sector
    funds in 1986, Blake discovered the energy service fund
    had an **extraordinarily strong tendency to trade near
    its daily low at the beginning of the day**
  - Hourly data analysis: **90–95% probability** of realizing
    a gain by buying at **10 a.m., 11 a.m., or 12 p.m.**
    and selling at the close
  - Pattern: one of the highest-probability edges Blake
    ever found; held up for the **full year or two** he
    traded it before Fidelity restricted it
  - Notably: he rotated clients through the fund (buying
    at 10 for some clients, 11 for others, 12 for others)
    — all profitable due to the intraday pattern
  - In November 1989, Fidelity charged **0.75% on trades
    held less than thirty days** — killed the short-term
    pattern's profitability

- [SHOULD KNOW][N221] **Fidelity's crackdown — directly targeting
  Blake after a Wall Street Journal article**:
  - Early 1989: _Wall Street Journal_ article mentioned
    Blake's track record and that he traded Fidelity
    sector funds
  - **Two days** after the article appeared: Blake called
    to place an order and was told: "I'm sorry, Mr. Blake,
    but the hourly investment limit in energy service
    has been lowered to $100,000" (previous limit: ~$500,000
    = 1% of fund assets)
  - Blake's interpretation of Fidelity's broader motive:
    "I suppose they thought that the profits being made
    by some market timers were coming out of shareholders'
    pockets. I would submit, however, that the profits
    were coming mostly from unsuccessful market timers."
  - Implication: successful market timers actually
    ADD liquidity and may benefit funds; the majority of
    active switchers (per fund employees) underperform
    buy-and-hold — so restricting ALL timers hurts the
    bad ones but also eliminates a source of liquidity

<!-- page 298: 2 nuggets -->

### Continuous Research and Method Evolution

- [MUST KNOW][N222] **Blake's research posture: finding new
  methods constantly**:
  - Spends **most of his day** researching new methods
    — actual fund management takes only a tiny fraction
    of his time
  - Finds new viable strategies "**all the time**"
  - Has **many methods on the shelf** that he's not
    currently using — can't use everything simultaneously
  - Key implication: his edge is not a single fixed
    pattern but a **research process** that continuously
    discovers and evaluates patterns; the current method
    is just the best currently available from a pipeline
    of options

- [SHOULD KNOW][N223] **Blake on system loyalty vs. system
  questioning**:
  - "For me, it's important to be loyal to my system.
    When I'm not, which happens occasionally, then,
    win or lose, I've made a mistake. I usually remember
    these for years."
  - BUT: distinguishes between **overriding** (a mistake)
    and **questioning** (allowed)
  - Questioning a trade: if prior experience makes a
    trade "look wrong," he can complete research before
    the close; if results dictate, he makes a **permanent
    amendment to the rules** — NOT a one-time exception
  - Critical distinction: ad hoc overrides are always
    wrong; research-driven rule amendments are how
    the system improves

<!-- page 299: 2 nuggets -->

### Fund Performance Is Less Important Than You'd Think

- [MUST KNOW][N224] **Why the underlying fund's direction barely
  matters for Blake's returns**:
  - Counterintuitive: the difference between trading a
    fund that rises 20% in a year vs. one that loses
    20% is **surprisingly small**
  - Math: with 1% average daily volatility, a fund up
    20% annually means ~**54% of days were up, 46% down**;
    a fund down 20% means ~**46% up, 54% down**
    (at 250 trading days, 20% net = 135 up, 115 down)
  - For Blake's strategy: the difference between a fund
    that's up 54% of the time vs. 46% of the time is
    **not significant** — his edge comes from correctly
    calling WHICH days those are, not from the fund's
    annual bias
  - This explains how he made money through the 1980-1981
    bond market collapse: being right on daily direction
    50%+ more often than the base rate swamps the
    underlying fund's long-term drift

<!-- page 300: 1 nugget -->

### Memorable Trades

- [NICE TO KNOW][N225] **Blake's most memorable trades — character
  reveals**:
  - **First big trade**: early in career, didn't get the
    closing price over the phone; woke in the middle
    of the night, called, found it was twice what he
    expected — made a few thousand dollars; "I was so
    excited that I couldn't sleep. I was like a kid
    at Christmas."
  - **July 7, 1986**: stock market fell 62 points
    (a record then); Blake knew that morning he'd be
    out of positions at the close, but had a windsurfing
    lesson that afternoon; got blown to the other side
    of the lake; paddled back as hard as he could to
    beat the 4 p.m. close; didn't make it — "it cost me"
  - Both stories reveal personality: the excitement of
    discovery/validation (not greed) and the human
    reality that even the most systematic traders
    have moments where life intrudes

<!-- pp. 299-320 -->

## Chapter: Gil Blake — The Master of Consistency (continued)

<!-- page 299: 3 nuggets -->

### System Loyalty vs. Questioning Trades

- [MUST KNOW][N197] **Blake on mechanical discipline — the distinction
  between loyalty and questioning**:
  - "For me, it's important to be loyal to my system. When I'm
    not, which happens occasionally, then, win or lose, I've
    made a mistake. I usually remember these for years."
  - BUT: "It's not disloyal, however, to question a trade.
    Sometimes, based on prior experience, a trade just doesn't
    look right. If I can complete the necessary research before
    the close and the results so dictate, then I make a permanent
    amendment to the rules."
  - Key distinction: **deviation** = mistake (emotional). 
    **Questioning** + researching → **permanent rule amendment**
    = legitimate system improvement
  - The system evolves through research, not through gut overrides

- [SHOULD KNOW][N198] **Fund performance impact on Blake's
  strategy — a counterintuitive finding**:
  - Question: does it matter which fund you're switching into?
  - Blake: "It's not nearly as important as you would think."
  - Reasoning: if a fund has **1% average daily volatility**,
    a fund rising 20% annually implies ~54% up days / 46% down
    days; a fund losing 20% annually implies ~46% up days /
    54% down days
  - [250 trading days × 1% move/day = 20% net return = 135 up
    days out of 250 = 54%]
  - For Blake's switching strategies: "the difference between
    trading a fund that's up 54 percent of the time and one
    that's up 46 percent of the time is not that significant"
  - Implication: his edge comes from **timing the switch**, not
    from picking the best-performing underlying fund

### Memorable Trades

- [NICE TO KNOW][N199] **Blake's first big win — emotional
  memory of trading's earliest thrill**:
  - Early in his career: didn't get closing price by phone, knew
    it had been a good day
  - Woke up in the middle of the night, called, discovered it
    had been "twice what I had expected" — made a few thousand
    dollars
  - "I was so excited that I couldn't sleep. I was like a kid
    at Christmas. I'll always remember that feeling."
  - Illustrative of how early wins cement emotional attachment
    to trading

<!-- page 300: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N200] **The July 7, 1986 windsurfing incident —
  cost of being unable to exit**:
  - Stock market fell 62 points (a record at the time)
  - Blake knew that morning he needed to be out at the close
  - Complication: **hourly switching was not yet available**, so
    he couldn't act intraday — needed to call before 4pm close
  - Had a windsurfing lesson that afternoon; got blown to the
    other side of the lake, paddled back hard to beat the close
  - "I didn't make it, and it cost me."
  - Practical lesson: **execution infrastructure matters** — a
    system that requires timely exits is only as good as your
    ability to actually execute those exits

<!-- page 301: 3 nuggets -->

### Breaking Discipline: The Gold Sector Trade (August 1987)

- [MUST KNOW][N201] **Blake's gold sector violation — the
  compounding cost of a single rule breach**:
  - August 1987: took a position in the gold sector fund
  - The day after entry, the fund dropped — giving a **red light**
    (exit signal). Blake stayed in.
  - Next day: gold stocks down big; technology giving a bright
    green light (strong entry signal)
  - Blake faced: (a) exit gold at a loss he shouldn't have taken,
    then (b) immediately reinvest in technology — already up
    sharply — at maximum risk exposure
  - "I found it extremely difficult to exit all my clients from
    the gold sector at a big loss — a loss that I knew I
    shouldn't have taken in the first place — and immediately
    place them at risk again in the technology fund"
  - "I think the thing that made it so difficult was that I had
    violated my rule."
  - Outcome: technology gain more than made up for gold loss —
    but **the psychological difficulty was the real lesson**
  - Breaking a rule once creates a cascading problem: not only
    do you take the loss, you're psychologically unable to take
    the next correct action

- [MUST KNOW][N202] **Blake's discipline analogy — why a single
  rule breach is catastrophic**:
  > "If you break a discipline once, the next transgression
  > becomes much easier. Breaking a diet provides an appropriate
  > analogy — once you do it, it becomes much easier to make
  > further exceptions."
  - This is not about the size of one violation; it's about the
    **erosion of the psychological barrier** to future violations
  - Each breach lowers the activation energy for the next one

### Markets Changed?

- [SHOULD KNOW][N203] **Blake on whether markets have changed —
  micro vs. macro distinction**:
  - "In a micro sense — yes; in a macro sense — no."
  - "Opportunities change, strategies change, but people and
    psychology do not change."
  - "If trend-following systems don't work as well, something
    else will. There's always money being lost, so someone out
    there has to win."
  - Key insight: **the presence of consistent losers is the
    structural guarantee that consistent winners are possible**

<!-- page 302: 4 nuggets -->

### Major Market Myths

- [SHOULD KNOW][N204] **Blake on the two contradictory market myths**:
  - Wall Street myth #1 (academic): "No one can consistently beat
    the market year in, year out, with steady returns of 20 or
    30 percent a year"
  - Wall Street myth #2 (sales side): "it can be done by anyone"
  - "Neither is really the truth."
  - Blake's view: a very small number can do it, but not because
    they bought someone else's system or because they got lucky

### Why Traders Lose

- [MUST KNOW][N205] **Blake's two-part framework for why traders
  fail**:
  1. "Most traders don't have a **winning strategy**"
  2. "Even among those traders who do, many don't **follow their
     strategy**"
  - "Trading puts pressure on weaker human traits and seems to
    seek out each individual's Achilles' heel."
  - Practical: having an edge is necessary but not sufficient —
    **execution discipline is its own separate requirement**

### What Makes a Good Trader

- [MUST KNOW][N206] **Blake on the profile of a good trader —
  the maverick + artistic/scientific blend**:
  - "A critical ingredient is a **maverick mind**."
  - "It's also important to have a blend between an artistic side
    and a scientific side."
  - **Artistic side**: imagine, discover, and create trading
    strategies — the generative function
  - **Scientific side**: translate those ideas into **firm trading
    rules** and execute those rules — the disciplinary function
  - Without the artistic side: nothing original to trade
  - Without the scientific side: nothing rigorous enough to
    follow mechanically under pressure

### Purchased Systems

- [SHOULD KNOW][N207] **Blake on buying trading systems —
  why they don't transfer**:
  - "Systems tend to be more useful or successful for the
    originator than for someone else."
  - "It's important that an approach be personalized; otherwise,
    you won't have the **confidence to follow it**."
  - Also possible: "individuals who become successful traders are
    not the type to use someone else's approach and that
    successful traders don't sell their systems"
  - Self-selection problem: if the system worked, you wouldn't
    sell it; if you're selling it, it either doesn't work or
    it won't fit the buyer's personality

<!-- page 303: 3 nuggets -->

### Response to Burton Malkiel

- [SHOULD KNOW][N208] **Blake on Burton Malkiel's *A Random Walk
  Down Wall Street* — partial agreement, with a data challenge**:
  - "I'm more in agreement than in disagreement with him."
  - "The markets *are* mostly random, and most people *can't* beat
    or 'time' the market."
  - "One hundred money managers each believe that they can
    consistently outperform the market. My feeling is that the
    number is a lot closer to zero than one hundred."
  - "Trading is probably more of an art than most people want
    to believe."
  - BUT: "I guess I'd also be tempted to show him my numbers."
  - The numbers (96% profitable months, 65-month winning streak)
    are his empirical rebuttal to pure randomness

### Five Steps to Becoming a Successful Trader

- [MUST KNOW][N209] **Blake's five-step framework for trading
  success**:
  1. **Focus on trading vehicles, strategies, and time horizons
     that suit your personality**
  2. **Identify nonrandom price behavior**, while recognizing
     that markets are random most of the time
  3. **Absolutely convince yourself that what you have found is
     statistically valid**
  4. **Set up trading rules**
  5. **Follow the rules**
  - Summary: "Do your own thing (independence); and do the right
    thing (discipline)."
  - This framework implies: most people fail at step 1 (wrong fit),
    step 3 (not rigorous enough), or step 5 (can't execute)

### Schwager's Commentary on Blake

- [MUST KNOW][N210] **Schwager's synthesis: open-mindedness as
  Blake's core trait**:
  - Blake began as a true believer in market randomness
  - When confronted with contradictory evidence: "he didn't dig
    in his heels and argue his position — the reflexive response
    most people would have in such a situation. Instead, he
    researched the question, and when the evidence suggested that
    his prior views had been wrong, he changed his mind."
  - "The ability to change one's mind is probably a key
    characteristic of the successful trader. Dogmatic and rigid
    personalities rarely, if ever, succeed in the markets."

<!-- page 304: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N211] **Schwager's synthesis: Blake's adaptability
  as performance sustainer**:
  - Blake started in municipal bond funds; when that edge
    diminished, he didn't keep repeating the same strategy —
    he launched an intensive research project yielding "an
    entirely new and even more effective approach"
  - When Fidelity imposed prohibitively high switching fees,
    Blake "switched to different fund families and different
    strategies"
  - "Blake's ability to adapt has allowed him to maintain a
    remarkable consistency of performance, despite profound
    changes in his trading environment."
  - Critical distinction: **loyalty to rules within a system**
    (required) vs. **loyalty to a specific system** (not
    required — adaptability wins)

- [MUST KNOW][N212] **Schwager's final verdict on Blake's
  meaning — proof that markets can be beat**:
  - "Blake's amazingly consistent track record provides
    compelling empirical evidence that the markets are indeed
    nonrandom."
  - "Blake's ability to win in an astounding **twenty-five months
    for every month he loses**, allows us to say 'Yes, Virginia,
    the markets can be beat.'"
  - How? "Certainly not by buying the answer. Even if the answer
    were for sale, the odds are that it wouldn't fit your
    personality."
  - "Each trader must find his or her own solution to the market
    puzzle."
  - Two remaining steps once you find statistically valid patterns:
    **devise your trading rules** and then ***follow* your
    trading rules** — the second step being harder than the first

