## Chapter: Victor Sperandeo — Markets Grow Old Too

<!-- page 305: no content (OceanofPDF.com filler page) -->

<!-- page 306: 3 nuggets -->

### Victor Sperandeo: Background

- [SHOULD KNOW][N213] **Victor Sperandeo's career path — from
  quote boy to market wizard**:
  - Started on Wall Street straight out of high school:
    minimum-wage **quote boy** → statistical clerk at **Standard
    & Poor's** (copying numbers — "stupifyingly boring"; fired
    for too many errors)
  - Accounting department at another Wall Street firm → talked
    his way into an **OTC options trading position**
  - Two years as a **dealer in the over-the-counter (OTC) options
    market**, matching buyers and sellers ("making the middle")
  - 1969 bear market: switched firms for more autonomy;
    offered a percentage of spread per transaction (vs. flat fee)
  - Earned **$50,000 in commissions in six months** at a firm
    where his boss earned $50,000 annually — boss responded by
    trying to convert him to a $20,000 salary + ambiguous bonus
  - Left; new firm ate his profits via expense allocations
  - Conclusion: "if he were going to get a fair deal, he would
    have to make it himself"

<!-- page 307: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N214] **Sperandeo's firm: Ragnar Options (1971)**:
  - Founded with a partner; claim: **first option dealer to offer
    guaranteed quotes without exceptionally high premiums**
  - If no existing contract met a buyer's request, they wrote the
    option themselves (at the time, options were tailor-made to
    customer specs, not standardized exchange instruments)
  - Result: within six months, **Ragnar was the largest OTC option
    dealer in the world**
  - Ragnar later merged with another Wall Street firm
  - 1978: joined **Interstate Securities** — given company account
    + private accounts to manage at **50/50 split** (profits AND
    expenses) — "complete independence to trade any markets in
    any way he desired, capital backing, and a meaningful split"
  - Interstate went public in 1986 and dissolved its trading
    group; Sperandeo then launched **Rand Management Corporation**

- [MUST KNOW][N215] **Sperandeo's performance record — 18
  consecutive winning years**:
  - Throughout career: emphasis on **loss avoidance** over
    scoring large gains
  - **18 consecutive winning years** before first loss in 1990
  - **Average annual gain: 72%**
  - Range: single loss of **35% in 1990** to five years of
    **triple-digit gains**
  - Paradox he notes: "I found it somewhat ironic that my first
    losing year in the markets occurred after having gained more
    knowledge than ever" — knowledge doesn't insulate from loss;
    in his case, managing OPM for the first time introduced new
    pressures and dynamics

<!-- page 308: 2 nuggets -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N216] **Sperandeo's reading habit and "office"**:
  - Self-educated: read extensively in markets, economics,
    psychology, philosophy — estimates approximately **2,500 books**
    on these subjects despite never finishing his college degree
  - Interview conducted in basement "office" converted into a
    lounge: fifteen-foot bar, seating for seventy-five, elaborate
    sound system
  - Schwager: "You almost expect Bill Murray to pop up and do
    his 'Saturday Night Live' lounge singer act."
  - Notable personality trait: "instantaneously likable" — relaxed
    and friendly despite unconventional setup

- [SHOULD KNOW][N217] **Why Sperandeo started a money management
  firm — the 1987 catalyst**:
  - "In 1987 I did enormously well catching the huge break in the
    stock market."
  - His success generated **unsolicited offers to manage large
    sums of money**
  - Realization: "if I had been managing money, as opposed to
    simply trading my own account, my profit potential would have
    been enormously greater"
  - Classic asymmetry: trading own account caps upside at personal
    capital base; managing OPM scales upside with AUM

<!-- page 309: 2 nuggets -->

### How Sperandeo Became a Trader

- [SHOULD KNOW][N218] **Sperandeo's poker-to-trading path —
  the probability mindset origin**:
  - As a teenager: "literally earned a living by playing poker"
  - Method: "read every book I could find on the game and quickly
    learned that winning was a matter of managing the odds"
  - "If you played only the hands in which the odds were in your
    favor and folded when they were not, you would end up winning
    more times than you lost."
  - Memorized the odds of every important card combination
  - Career decision at age twenty: surveyed the *New York Times*
    employment section — only three professions offered >$25,000
    starting salary: physicist, biologist, **OTC trader**
  - Reasoning: "OTC trading sounded a little bit like playing
    cards — they both involved odds"
  - Got the job by claiming (falsely) to have a photographic
    memory, then backing it up by having memorized all stock
    symbols after taking a memory course years earlier
  - Poker mindset → **speculation as probability management** =
    the core Sperandeo framework

<!-- page 310: 1 nugget -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N219] **Milton Leeds — Sperandeo's inspiration
  at Pershing**:
  - At Pershing & Company, **Milton Leeds** — described as "a
    legendary tape reader and trader of his day" — worked there
  - Sperandeo found observing him inspirational, but didn't work
    directly for him
  - Self-taught by "reading everything on the subject that I
    could get my hands on"
  - The deliberate pursuit of the most esoteric instrument
    (options, when only ~1% of stockbrokers understood them in
    the late 1960s) as competitive moat: "if I undertook the
    most esoteric form of trading and mastered it, then I would
    have to make a lot of money"

<!-- page 311: 2 nuggets -->

### Trading vs. Gambling

- [MUST KNOW][N220] **Sperandeo's distinction between speculation
  and gambling — a fundamental frame**:
  - "*Gambling* involves taking a risk when the odds are against
    you. For example, betting on a lottery or playing a slot
    machine are forms of gambling."
  - "Successful trading, or poker playing for that matter,
    involves **speculating** rather than gambling."
  - "Successful speculation implies taking risks when the odds
    are in your favor."
  - "Just like in poker, where you have to know which hands to
    bet on, in trading you have to know when the odds are in
    your favor."
  - Practical implication: the question is never "should I take
    risk?" but "do I currently have a positive expected value?"

### How Sperandeo Defines "The Odds"

- [MUST KNOW][N221] **The 1974 catalyst: Sperandeo's market
  life-expectancy research**:
  - In 1974, missed the huge October-November rally in the stock
    market — that failure drove a **two-year intensive study**
  - Research questions: "How long do bull and bear markets last?
    What are the normal percentage price moves a market makes
    before it forms a top or a bottom?"
  - Finding: "market price movements are like people — they have
    **statistically significant life-expectancy profiles** that
    can be used to measure risk exposure"
  - Flagship stat: **median extent for an intermediate swing in
    the Dow during a bull market = 20%**
  - Clarification: "This doesn't mean that when the market is up
    20 percent, it's going to top... However, when the market is
    up more than 20 percent, **the odds for further appreciation
    begin to decline significantly**"
  - Practical use: "if the market has been up more than 20
    percent and you begin to see other evidence of a possible
    top, it's important to pay close attention to that information"

<!-- page 312: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N222] **The life insurance analogy — why context
  of market age changes the meaning of technical signals**:
  - Life insurance underwriting: a twenty-year-old vs. an
    eighty-year-old face radically different mortality odds —
    even an out-of-shape twenty-year-old is likely to survive;
    even a healthy eighty-year-old (Jack LaLanne capable of
    200 push-ups, swimming the English Channel) represents
    higher actuarial risk
  - BUT: an eighty-year-old who smokes three packs of Camels,
    drinks a quart of scotch, and has pneumonia → you definitely
    don't write the policy
  - "**The older the individual, the more significant the
    symptoms become.**"
  - Direct market application: "In a market that is in a stage
    of old age, it is particularly important to be attuned to
    symptoms of a potential end to the current trend"
  - "Most people who become involved in the stock market don't
    know the difference between a twenty-year-old and an
    eighty-year-old."

- [MUST KNOW][N223] **Why most technical analysis fails —
  context-blindness to market age**:
  - "One reason why many types of technical analysis don't work
    too well is because such methods are often **applied
    indiscriminately**."
  - Example: a head-and-shoulders pattern in a "twenty-year-old"
    market (early in the move, up only 10%) → "the odds are that
    the market is not likely to die so quickly"
  - Same head-and-shoulders in an "eighty-year-old" market (up
    25-30%, extended duration) → "there's a much better chance of
    that pattern being an accurate indicator of a price top"
  - "Trading the market without knowing what stage it is in is
    like selling life insurance to twenty-year-olds and
    eighty-year-olds at the same premium."
  - **Actionable inversion**: technical signals should be weighted
    by market age/extent, not applied uniformly

<!-- page 313: 2 nuggets -->

### Defining Bull Moves

- [SHOULD KNOW][N224] **Sperandeo's definition of "intermediate
  swing" and the 20% figure's robustness**:
  - "Intermediate" = price move lasting a **minimum of three
    weeks to a maximum of six months**
  - Analogous figures exist for all other move types: categorized
    by (a) type of market (bull or bear), (b) length of move
    (short/intermediate/long term), (c) location within market
    cycle (first swing, second swing, etc.)
  - **Remarkable consistency**: the 20% median held across all
    years since **1896** — when checked only through 1945, the
    figure was virtually identical at **19%**
  - Two explanations for the stability:
    1. **Value**: Roger Ibbotson studied sixty years of data;
       stock market averaged **9.2% annually** including
       dividends. Rising 20% in ~107 days (median duration of
       intermediate upmove) "squeezed out a lot of value in a
       very short amount of time" — market becomes expensive
    2. **Psychology**: like supermarket tomatoes rising from
       $1.00 to $1.50 — people start looking for substitutes;
       "you don't want to pay up beyond a certain number"
       regardless of absolute income level

<!-- page 314: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N225] **Statistical profiles are NOT price/time
  targets — the insurance company analogy**:
  - "To use extent and duration profiles to predict exact market
    turning points would be like an insurance company telling
    you when and how you will die on the day you buy your policy.
    They don't have to do that to make a profit. All they need
    to do to make a profit is to know what the odds are."
  - The profiles are **risk-adjustment tools**, not forecasting
    tools: they tell you when to pay more attention, not when
    to sell
  - When market exceeds median historical age: "any method you
    use to analyze the market, whether it be fundamental or
    technical, is likely to be **far more accurate**"
  - Example: chartist sees top formation at +10% from low →
    "odds are high that the projection will be incorrect."
    Same formation at +25-30% → "should be given a great deal
    more weight"

### The December 1974 Bottom — Correct Call, Lost Money

- [SHOULD KNOW][N226] **Sperandeo's paradox: buying the exact
  bottom and losing money**:
  - After missing the October-November 1974 rally, Sperandeo
    waited for a major relative low
  - Went long "the world" on **December 6, 1974** —
    "that day proved to be the exact bottom of the market"
  - "Ironically, I actually ended up losing money on the trade"
  - Signals that convinced him it was the bottom:
    - **Dow Theory buy signal**: Dow Jones Industrial index made
      new low, but Transportation index and S&P 500 had not
      (non-confirmation = classic Dow Theory divergence)
    - Volume on break to new lows was **relatively light**
    - Bear market had lasted a long time; **bearish sentiment
      was pervasive**
    - **Bad news was losing its impact** — bearish news stories
      came out and stocks "would essentially lie flat"

<!-- page 315: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N227] **How Sperandeo lost money at the December
  1974 low — the wrong-expiration options error**:
  - Had an incredibly profitable year up to that point; took
    one-third of annual profits and bought **out-of-the-money
    calls expiring in January** (too short a duration)
  - Example: Kodak trading at 64; bought January 70 calls near 1
  - January 27 (expiration day): Kodak had rebounded to 69 —
    calls expired worthless
  - One week later: Kodak at **80**
  - "The same type of experience was repeated in a dozen other
    positions."
  - His actual mistake: "I didn't allow enough time until
    expiration" — NOT that calls were too far out of the money
  - After completing his 1976 study on duration/magnitude of
    price moves, he realized: "second legs of bull markets tend
    to be relatively extended" — he would have bought April
    options instead
  - Lesson: **option time selection must account for the
    statistical duration of the expected move**

<!-- page 316: 2 nuggets -->

### Sperandeo's Bull/Bear Market Classification System

- [SHOULD KNOW][N228] **Sperandeo's objective criteria for
  classifying bull and bear markets**:
  - **Uptrend criterion**: you do NOT take out a prior major
    relative low
  - To qualify as a **bear market**: price decline must (a) last
    a **minimum of six months** AND (b) equal at least **15%**
  - Application: the June 1983-July 1984 price decline met BOTH
    criteria → qualified as a bear market → July 1984 upmove
    was the beginning of a **new** bull market (not a
    continuation of 1982's)
  - By this classification: the 1982 bull market ended November
    1983; the July 1984-October 1989 bull market was the
    **second longest since 1896** (start of his statistical survey)
  - The **1987 crash was NOT a bear market** by these criteria:
    "the subsequent price break lasted only three months" — did
    not meet the six-month minimum time duration requirement
  - Implication: even though 1987's decline was "extraordinarily
    sharp," it was a severe correction within an ongoing bull
    market, not a new bear market

<!-- page 317: 2 nuggets -->

### Position Sizing Based on Market Age

- [MUST KNOW][N229] **Sperandeo's position-sizing rule — scaling
  exposure to statistical risk**:
  - "I analyze risk by measuring the **extent and duration** of
    price swings."
  - "If the market has risen 20 percent in roughly 107 days,
    even if I'm still extremely bullish I'll have a **maximum
    position size of 50 percent**, because statistically we've
    reached the median historical magnitude and duration of an
    upmove."
  - This is NOT a forecasting claim (he doesn't predict a top);
    it's a **risk-management discipline**: reduce exposure when
    statistical odds against continuation increase
  - Comparison: like an insurance underwriter who doesn't predict
    the exact time of death but adjusts premiums by age

### Predetermined Exit = Emotional Discipline

- [MUST KNOW][N230] **The XYZ stock example — how the absence of
  a predetermined exit causes psychological collapse**:
  - Broker tip: "Stock XYZ going to be a takeover play, trading
    at $20, could go to $60!" Trader buys.
  - $20 → $18: "action just doesn't seem right; I'll get out
    when I'm even"
  - $18 → $17: "I'll get out on the first rally"
  - $17 → $15 (trading on margin): "lost half his money — pure
    panic"
  - $15 → $14 → sells at **$13** (on a call to broker in
    "desperation") — that's the bottom
  - Relief when finally out — "somewhat ironic since he had just
    lost 70 percent of his money"
  - Psychological sequence: **lure → anxiety → onset of panic →
    pure panic → desperation → relief** — all driven by having
    no exit plan
  - "There's nothing logical about this process. It's all an
    emotional pitfall."
  - "**Planning where to get out before putting on the trade is
    a means of enforcing emotional discipline.**"
  - Sperandeo has done this since his first day in the business:
    "I've always had a point where I knew that I was getting out"

<!-- page 318: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N231] **Sperandeo's goal: stay in business, not
  get rich** (from page 318, continued onto 320):
  - "My goal on Wall Street was **never to get rich but to stay
    in business**. There's a big difference. If you're out of
    the business, you can never get rich."
  - When trading large: "you need to have an especially short
    fuse in regard to cutting losses"
  - When trading at your biggest, "you should be making money
    **instantaneously**" — if a large position isn't working
    immediately, cut it

<!-- page 319: 2 nuggets -->

### The November 1984 $1 Million Loss — Violating His Own Rule

- [MUST KNOW][N232] **Sperandeo's worst deviation — the $1M
  November 1984 loss**:
  - Fed was easing; he was convinced they were in a bull market
  - Market sold off because Congress was considering a **tax code
    change** reported as "revenue neutral" — Sperandeo went long
  - Position size: smallest position was 100 options; largest
    was **2,000 options** ("I'm not exaggerating")
  - What he missed: the "revenue neutral" tax plan called for
    **taking money from corporations and giving it to
    individuals** — bad for stocks ("when you're long stocks,
    you don't want any plan that takes money away from
    corporations")
  - Market fell **four consecutive days** — at which point he
    knew he was wrong and his rule required him to exit
  - He did NOT exit on day four: "the decline seemed extremely
    overdone" — waited for the first rally day to liquidate
  - Market fell **nine consecutive days** total
  - Exited on day six of the decline — "If I had sold after the
    fourth down day, my loss would have been only half as large"
  - Cost: over **$1 million**

<!-- page 320: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N233] **Lessons from the November 1984 loss —
  three rules extracted from the experience**:
  1. **Legislative/tax uncertainty = go flat immediately**: 
     "Whenever there's a tax proposal, or some other major
     legislative uncertainty, I now get flat immediately. The
     market will always run to the sidelines until it knows
     what will happen."
  2. **When trading at your biggest, you must be making money
     instantaneously**: if a large position isn't immediately
     profitable, cut it — don't wait
  3. **Primary goal is to stay in business**: "That's why you
     have to be especially cautious when you're trading a larger
     position size" — the bigger the bet, the shorter the fuse
     must be on losses
  - Meta-lesson: the $1M loss happened because **two violations
    compounded**: (a) he violated his rule by staying past the
    fourth down day, AND (b) he substituted judgment ("this looks
    overdone") for a rule that existed precisely to remove
    judgment in such moments
  - Rule-breaking is never "just this once" — it is always a
    surrender of the very protection the rule was designed to
    provide

