### The S&P Expiration Day Trade — From +$800K to +$100K

- [SHOULD KNOW][N234] **S&P expiration day trade (1985 or 1986) —
  the near-miss that made $100K from a potential $800K**:
  - **Major Market Index (MMI)** trading at ~349
  - Spread action between S&P and OEX convinced Sperandeo a
    major **buy program** was about to hit
  - Bought **4,000 of the 355 options at 1/8** and **500 of the
    350 options at 1¾**
  - At 3:30 PM (30 minutes before close): 355s at 2, 350s at 5½
  - Sold the 500 350 options — gave him a **free ride** on the
    remaining 4,000 options
  - Then a major Wall Street firm hit the market with an
    **extremely large sell program** — prices collapsed
  - With less than 30 minutes to expiration and options now out
    of the money, no exit was possible
  - Market finished sharply down; ended up making only $100,000
  - Quote to bartender at Michael's One: **"I need a drink. I
    just made $100,000 today — the only problem is that an hour
    ago I was ahead $800,000."**
  - Lesson: even free-ride positions can go to zero if a
    catastrophic event hits in the final window

### The Trading Company Experiment

- [SHOULD KNOW][N235] **Sperandeo's attempt to build the
  "McDonald's of trading"**:
  - Made substantial withdrawals from his account during the
    1980s and invested in starting a trading company
  - Hired other traders, taught them his methods, gave them
    capital to trade
  - Ambition: "I wanted to be the McDonald's of trading rather
    than an egotistical, solitary trader"
  - Result: "This venture, of course, didn't work out as well
    as McDonald's"
  - Practical outcome: over 5 years, trained **38 people**,
    each spending several months by his side learning virtually
    everything he knew about the market
  - **Only 5 of 38 made money** — a 13% success rate despite
    full access to his methods and capital

<!-- page 324: 2 nuggets -->

### Intelligence vs. Emotional Discipline — The 38-Trainee Experiment

- [MUST KNOW][N236] **Intelligence is not correlated with trading
  success — the dropout and the genius**:
  - Sperandeo was "not scientific" in selection — went with
    instincts/whims, chose a diverse group
  - Asked: "Was there any correlation between intelligence and
    success at trading?" Answer: **"Absolutely, but not in the
    way you think."**
  - **Success case**: A high school dropout (phone clerk on the
    American Stock Exchange) who:
    - Had served in Vietnam and had a hand grenade explode near
      him — shrapnel left in his pancreas
    - "As a result of this experience, he was always afraid of
      everything"
    - In trading: more worried about losing than winning →
      **took losses very quickly**
    - Was one of the 5 who made Sperandeo "a great deal of money"
  - **Failure case**: A genius with a **188 IQ** who had been on
    Jeopardy and answered every question correctly → **never
    made a dime in trading during five years**
  - Core insight: "The key to trading success is **emotional
    discipline**. Making money has nothing to do with
    intelligence. Think of all the bright people that choose
    careers on Wall Street. If intelligence were the key, there
    would be a lot more people making money trading."

<!-- page 325: 2 nuggets -->

### Why Intelligence Is an Impediment to Trading

- [MUST KNOW][N237] **High intelligence creates an ego trap that
  makes admitting mistakes nearly impossible**:
  - Sperandeo's mechanism: Brilliant Harvard summa cum laude grad
    gets a $5M portfolio within one year → "what would you
    believe about yourself? Most likely, you would assume that
    you're very bright and do everything right"
  - When the market goes against their position: reaction =
    **"I'm right."** Reason: "Because everything you've done
    in life is right. You'll tend to place your IQ above the
    market action."
  - "People who are very bright don't make very many mistakes.
    In a sense, they generally *are* correct. In trading,
    however, the person who can easily admit to being wrong is
    the one who walks away a winner."
  - **Counterintuitive implication**: being frequently correct
    in other domains is a liability in trading — it trains
    you to resist the evidence of the market
  - Key phrase: "To be a successful trader, you have to be able
    to admit mistakes."

- [MUST KNOW][N238] **In trading, you cannot hide from your
  failures — rationalization is a guaranteed road to ruin**:
  - Lawyer analogy: loses a big case after poor preparation
    (went out, stayed up half the night). He can always blame
    the jury, claim he "did the best I could." Will never have
    to admit his own actions caused the loss. "He'll probably
    push the truth so far into his subconscious that he'll
    never admit to himself that his own actions caused the
    loss of the case."
  - **Trading is different**: "Your equity provides a daily
    reflection of your performance."
  - "The trader who tries to blame his losses on external events
    will never learn from his mistakes. For a trader,
    rationalization is a guaranteed road to ultimate failure."
  - This is why the market uniquely demands psychological
    honesty — no other profession forces such immediate and
    undeniable accountability

<!-- page 326: 2 nuggets -->

### Trainee Program Results and Economics

- [SHOULD KNOW][N239] **Financial mechanics of the trainee
  program — how the math worked out**:
  - Starting capital: typically **$25,000–$50,000** per trainee;
    some accounts as large as **$250,000**
  - Cutout point: "when they lost it all" — "I never had to fire
    anyone, they just self-destructed"
  - Net result: the **5 successful trainees made more money than
    all 33 others combined lost** — so the program was
    profitable overall
  - Irony: "The only unfortunate thing was that these five people
    made so much money that they quit. That was one outcome I
    didn't consider at the onset."
  - Bottom line: net profit, but modest given the effort. "I
    certainly wouldn't do it again."
  - Key implication: 87% of trainees with full access to a
    proven trader's methods, his capital, and months of
    mentorship still failed → the bottleneck is NOT knowledge

<!-- page 327: 2 nuggets -->

### Emotional Discipline — The Dieting Analogy

- [MUST KNOW][N240] **Why most traders fail: emotional discipline,
  not knowledge — the dieting parallel**:
  - "They lacked what I call **emotional discipline** — the
    ability to keep their emotions removed from trading
    decisions."
  - **Dieting analogy**: "Most people have the necessary
    knowledge to lose weight — that is, they know that in
    order to lose weight you have to exercise and cut your
    intake of fats. However, despite this widespread knowledge,
    the vast majority of people who attempt to lose weight are
    unsuccessful. Why? Because they lack the emotional
    discipline."
  - The parallel is exact: everyone knows "cut losses short,
    let winners run," yet most traders still fail to do it
  - If Sperandeo were to repeat the experiment: **select
    trainees on psychological traits**, not background or
    intelligence
  - Key trait to screen for: **"the ability to admit mistakes
    and take losses quickly"**
  - Why most people lack this: "Most people view losing as a
    hit against their self-esteem. As a result, they postpone
    losing. They think of all sorts of reasons for not taking
    losses. They select a mental stop point and then fail to
    execute it. They abandon their game plan."

<!-- page 328: 1 nugget -->

### The Greatest Misconception — Buy-and-Hold

- [MUST KNOW][N241] **Buy-and-hold is the greatest misconception
  about markets — extreme bear markets destroy it**:
  - "The greatest misconception is the idea that if you buy and
    hold stocks for long periods of time, you'll always make
    money."
  - Evidence:
    - **1896 low to 1932 low**: 36-year period where buy-and-hold
      lost money (not even counting opportunity cost on funds)
    - **1962 low to 1974 low**: another extended period of
      buy-and-hold losses
    - **1929–1932**: market dropped an average of **94%**
    - **1973–74**: the **"nifty fifty" stocks** lost over **75%**
      of their value
  - Sperandeo's principle: "If something happens once, I think
    logic tells you it can happen again. Actually, I believe
    that anything can happen, but certainly if it has happened
    before, it can happen again."
  - "People who have the notion that buying and holding for the
    long term is the way to go can easily go bankrupt."
  - Note: Sperandeo's own duration/magnitude rules would not
    lead him astray in an 80–90% bear market — he uses
    statistical studies as "only one among many tools"

<!-- page 329: 2 nuggets -->

### Managing Losing Streaks

- [MUST KNOW][N242] **How to handle losing streaks — size down,
  step back, restore confidence before scaling up**:
  - Principle: expand involvement when winning; cut position size
    when losing
  - "The idea is to lose as little as possible while you're in
    a losing streak. Once you take a big hit, you're always
    on the defensive."
  - In all months he lost money: "I always ended up trading
    small — sometimes trading as little as **1 percent of the
    account**"
  - During losing streaks: reads a nonfiction book to learn
    something new — "First, it takes my mind off trading;
    second, by enhancing my knowledge, I help improve my
    self-esteem. The key is to do something positive."
  - Sometimes pulls away from markets entirely for **1–2 months**
  - Reason for extended breaks: "You don't want to keep losing
    if you're in a rut, because that will only destroy your
    self-confidence even more."
  - **Ty Cobb analogy** for getting back in: Cobb was never in
    a slump because whenever he felt one coming, he wouldn't
    try to get a hit — he'd just try to make contact with the
    ball. Translation: **"When you're in a slump, try to be
    patient and wait for a trade that you feel very confident
    about and keep the bet size small. Your goal should not be
    to make lots of money but rather to get your confidence
    back by making correct decisions."**

<!-- page 330: 2 nuggets -->

### Why People Lose + Trading as a Business

- [MUST KNOW][N243] **#1 reason people lose: failing to cut
  losses short — and the three-tier trading business hierarchy**:
  - "The single most important reason that people lose money in
    the financial markets is that they don't cut their losses
    short. It is a curiosity of human nature that no matter how
    many books talk about this rule, and no matter how many
    experts offer this advice, people still keep making the
    same mistake."
  - Mistake #2: "They don't approach trading as a business."
  - Sperandeo's **three-tier hierarchy for trading as a
    business** (in strict priority order):
    1. **Preservation of capital** — first question when looking
       at any trade: "What is the potential loss I could
       suffer?" (not "What is the potential profit?")
    2. **Consistent profitability** — balance risk relative to
       accumulated profits or losses; "Consistency is far more
       important than making lots of money."
    3. **Superior returns** — achieved by increasing bet size
       only *after* periods of high profitability; pyramid gains
       when in a profitable period and the right situation
       presents itself
  - Underlying philosophy: "The key to building wealth is to
    preserve capital and wait patiently for the right
    opportunity to make the extraordinary gains."

<!-- page 331: 2 nuggets -->

### Chart Analysis — The Three-Step Trend Change Method

- [SHOULD KNOW][N244] **Sperandeo's three-step method for
  identifying major trend changes**:
  - Backstory: not a pure fundamentalist (made too much money on
    technical observations), but considers TA insufficient as a
    *sole* method
  - 1974 anecdote: hired a technical analyst at $125/week who
    worked 16-hour days studying charts — but could never give
    a straight buy/sell recommendation ("This stock might be
    forming a top"). Most memorable detail: "his shirtsleeves
    were frayed and he ate homemade tuna fish sandwiches for
    lunch." (Implied conclusion: chart study alone doesn't pay.)
  - Prefers to "keep it simple" — **three-step process** to
    define important trend changes (example: identifying a top):
    - **Step 1**: Wait for the **uptrend line** to be broken
    - **Step 2**: Look for an **unsuccessful test of the recent
      high** — price reverses *below* the prior high, OR (more
      reliable) penetrates the prior high briefly then breaks
      back below it
    - **Step 3**: **Downside penetration of the most recent
      relative low** — confirms the trend change
  - Correct way to draw an **uptrend line**: from the lowest
    low to the highest low *immediately preceding* the highest
    high, ensuring no prices between the two chosen points
    touch the line

<!-- page 332: 2 nuggets -->

### Price Failures — The Most Reliable Chart Pattern

- [MUST KNOW][N245] **Why price failures at prior highs/lows are
  the most reliable chart patterns — floor mechanics explain it**:
  - A price failure that penetrates the prior high before
    reversing can "sometimes catch the virtual exact high or
    low" — "probably the most reliable and important chart
    patterns"
  - **Mechanism (floor trading dynamics)**:
    - Most traders set stops at or near prior highs or lows —
      this is widely known
    - Floor **locals** (market makers) know where stop clusters
      are concentrated
    - As price approaches a cluster of buy stops above the
      market, locals buy in anticipation — riding the upcoming
      stop-triggered extension
    - After stops trigger and price briefly extends, locals
      liquidate for a quick profit
    - **If the move to a new high had fundamental reasons → it
      extends.** If driven by local stop-hunting → price
      reverses once stops are filled
    - "The triggering of the stops represents the **market's
      last gasp**."
  - Same dynamic applies both ways: locals will also sell ahead
    of heavy sell-stop clusters below the market
  - Caveat: this is most explicit in open-outcry futures markets,
    but an analogous process operates in specialist-driven
    stock markets

<!-- page 333: 2 nuggets -->

### Specialist Market Dynamics and Technical Indicators

- [SHOULD KNOW][N246] **Specialists in stock markets have the
  same stop-hunting incentive as floor locals**:
  - The specialist trades one or several stocks, makes the
    market, is paid per hundred shares traded
  - "It's in the specialist's interest to have prices move to
    the levels that will result in the execution of the largest
    amount of orders" — just above prior highs or just below
    prior lows
  - Advantage: the specialist knows **all pending orders in
    advance** — unlike other participants
  - Floor locals on stock exchanges also share this interest in
    triggering stops
  - Conclusion: "Key chart patterns are often based on the
    activity of the professionals on the floors." — i.e.,
    chart patterns are not mystical; they reflect the mechanics
    of how professional floor traders profit

- [SHOULD KNOW][N247] **200-day moving average study — 18%
  annual return vs. buy-and-hold over 50 years**:
  - Sperandeo's most-weighted technical indicator in the stock
    market: the **200-day moving average**
  - Would not recommend as sole input, but useful supplement
  - Study he references: using the 200-day MA on **Dow Jones
    stocks** → average annual return of **18%** over a 50-year
    survey period — approximately **double** the return of
    straight buy-and-hold
  - Mechanism not explained — just cited as empirical evidence
    that simple mechanical rules can substantially outperform
    passive holding

<!-- page 334: 2 nuggets -->

### Economics as a Trading Edge — The Mitterrand Trade

- [MUST KNOW][N248] **François Mitterrand's 1981 election —
  economics knowledge converted into a "sure thing" currency
  trade**:
  - **Mitterrand** won a surprise victory in the 1981 French
    presidential election as a self-proclaimed socialist
  - Campaign promises: nationalize segments of industry +
    introduce massive social welfare programs
  - Sperandeo's reasoning: "The economic implications of
    Mitterrand's programs would spell disaster for the French
    franc."
  - Action: immediately **sold the franc** at approximately
    a **4-to-1 exchange rate to the dollar**
  - Covered **three weeks later** when franc trading at **6-to-1**
  - Long-term: "the franc eventually sank to a ten-to-one rate
    against the dollar"
  - Sperandeo's assessment: "That trade was about as close as
    you can get to a sure thing."
  - Key principle: macroeconomic and political analysis can
    generate high-conviction, low-uncertainty trades — the
    edge comes from understanding the *direction* of consequences
    even when timing is uncertain

<!-- page 335: 1 nugget -->

### The 1987 Crash — Prediction and Timing

- [MUST KNOW][N249] **How Sperandeo called the 1987 crash one
  month early in Barron's — and timed the short entry**:
  - Published September 1987 prediction in **Barron's** that a
    major top was forming
  - **Valuation/duration signal** (cautionary but not
    sufficient alone):
    - Market up **23% in 96 days** at the August 1987 high
    - These figures matched the historical medians for magnitude
      and duration of **intermediate bull market moves** —
      suggested the move was statistically "mature"
  - **Multiple converging signals** (all screaming caution):
    - **Advance/decline ratio** did NOT confirm the Dow's new
      high → classic **bearish divergence**
    - **P/E ratio** at its highest level in **25 years**
    - Government, corporate, AND consumer debt at **record
      levels**
    - "Virtually any indicator you looked at was screaming
      caution against the possibility of an impending collapse."
  - **Timing the entry — two triggers**:
    1. **October 5**: Greenspan quoted in *Wall Street Journal*
       saying rates could become "dangerously high" if inflation
       fears mushroomed; hinted the discount rate might have to
       be raised
    2. **October 15**: **Dow Theory** gave a sell signal →
       Sperandeo initiated short position
  - **The crash trigger** ("straw that broke the camel's back"):
    - Secretary of State **James Baker** disputes with Germany,
      urging Germany to stimulate its economy
    - Germany refuses; Baker makes a weekend announcement that
      the US is prepared to **"let the dollar slide"**
    - Unknown magnitude of devaluation = foreign holders of
      dollar-denominated securities must sell until they know
      what "slide" means
    - "In my opinion, there's no doubt that this statement was
      the trigger for the October 19 stock market collapse."
  - Added to short **on Monday morning, October 19** even
    though the Dow opened **over 200 points lower** — was
    "absolutely convinced that the stock market was going to
    collapse because of this unknown dollar devaluation"

<!-- page 336: 0 new nuggets (1987 crash content continues from page 335, captured in N249) -->

<!-- page 337: 1 nugget -->

### The 1989 Minicrash — Same Statistical Framework

- [SHOULD KNOW][N250] **October 1989 minicrash — Sperandeo's
  statistical approach applied again**:
  - Analysis similar to 1987: market had been up **200 days**
    without an intermediate downtrend (defined as a decline of
    15+ days) vs. historical median of **107 days**
  - Market was up over **24%** — historical studies showed
    **7 out of 8 times** when the market rose that much, a
    correction followed that brought prices back below that level
  - "Statistically, this market was like an **eighty-seven-
    year-old**."
  - Position: already out of longs, very attuned to signals
    that the market was ready to die
  - Illustrates: the same statistical/duration framework that
    flagged 1987 also flagged 1989 — not a one-off lucky call

<!-- page 338: 2 nuggets -->

### Final Words — Life Balance and Schwager's Postscript

- [NICE TO KNOW][N251] **Sperandeo's regret — trading consumed
  the years he should have spent with his daughter**:
  - "Being involved in this business requires tremendous
    dedication and desire. However, you shouldn't make trading
    your whole life."
  - Personal story: while doing his exhaustive historical stock
    trend research, his daughter **Jennifer** was in preschool
    — "a critical age for the child in terms of development
    and a wonderful age for parents to enjoy their children"
  - He was so absorbed that when Jennifer wandered into his
    office, "I had no time to share with her. It was a mistake
    that I regret to this day."
  - Warning: "Some traders make this business their entire life
    and, as a result, they may make more money, but at the
    expense of living a more rounded, balanced, and satisfying
    life."

- [SHOULD KNOW][N252] **Schwager's postscript on Sperandeo —
  the actuarial edge and the bet-sizing key**:
  - Sperandeo's core edge: approached odds like an actuary —
    "Just as insurance companies guarantee that the odds are in
    their favor by classifying policyholders according to risk,
    Sperandeo categorizes the stock market by risk."
  - Bet sizing varies considerably based on perceived odds:
    largest positions when market appears to be in early stages
    of a new trend AND multiple indicators confirm → trade much
    larger than in ambiguous situations
  - The corollary (from N233): when trading large, the position
    must go immediately in your favor — otherwise pare down
    quickly
  - Intelligence: Sperandeo concluded it was "virtually
    irrelevant in predicting success" based on 38-trainee
    experience; far more important = ability to admit mistakes
  - Buy-and-hold myth: "One sacred cow that Sperandeo believes
    is really a bum steer" — provides historical examples of
    very extended periods when it was disastrous

