## New Chapter: Tom Basso — "Mr. Serenity"

<!-- page 339: 1 nugget (Part V section divider + Tom Basso chapter introduction) -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N253] **Tom Basso — "Mr. Serenity" — why
  psychological success may matter more than numbers**:
  - **Background**: Engineer at **Monsanto Company** → found
    the job didn't fully absorb his energies → began dabbling
    in investments
  - **Track record**:
    - Stock accounts: **16% annually since 1980** (~5% above
      S&P 500) — respectable but not spectacular; majority of
      managers underperform the index
    - Futures fund: **20% annually since 1987**, moderate
      volatility — better than industry average but not
      extraordinary
  - Schwager's framing: not on his original interview list due
    to non-legendary numbers; "And yet, in an important sense,
    Basso is perhaps the most successful trader I have
    interviewed."
  - Key distinction: Basso has learned to accept losses "not
    only in an intellectual sense but on an **emotional level**
    as well." His exuberance about trading and life "bubble
    right out of him."
  - "He has managed to be a profitable trader while apparently
    maintaining complete peace of mind and experiencing great
    joy. In this sense, I can't think of a more worthy role
    model for a trader."
  - Schwager's meta-lesson: selecting interview subjects
    "strictly on the basis of numbers was a somewhat myopic
    approach"
  - **Career path**:
    - 1975: First trade — $2,000 commodity account, bought 2
      corn contracts, immediately lost $600. "My stomach was
      turning. I couldn't concentrate on my work... I called my
      broker every hour."
    - Took many years before trading futures profitably — but
      persisted until he succeeded
    - 1980: began managing equity accounts (outgrowth of
      investment club involvement)
    - 1984: expanded to futures accounts; problem = small
      account sizes (many as small as $25k) created excessive
      volatility
    - 1987: raised minimum account size to **$1 million**,
      returned all smaller accounts — the only way to achieve
      adequate diversification without excessive volatility
  - Met Schwager at a **psychology investment seminar** run by
    **Dr. Van Tharp** and **Adrienne Toghraie** (Newark, NJ)
  - First trade motivation: a "rather naive study" where a
    certain infrequent chart pattern had been followed by a
    price advance 100% of the time — classic well-chosen example

<!-- page 340: 1 nugget -->

### The "Well-Chosen Example" Problem in System Testing

- [MUST KNOW][N254] **The "well-chosen example" trap —
  virtually any system can look perfect with the right
  historical cherry-pick**:
  - Basso's first trade ("naive study") reminded Schwager of
    his own experience with this exact trap
  - **Schwager's discovery**: read an article about a simple
    two-condition trading system that appeared to dramatically
    outperform his own complex system — for one market
    illustration
  - One condition: already tested by Schwager and **Norman
    Strahm** (partner in system development) → net winner,
    but only moderately performing
  - Second condition: they had dismissed it as having "such a
    poor prospect of success that we never even bothered
    testing it"
  - Testing the second condition: performance for the market
    in the article was exactly as claimed — BUT:
    - The time period selected was the **single best year** in
      a 10-year test period for that market
    - In fact, it was the **single best year for any of the
      25 markets** they tested over 10 years = the single
      best of **250 possible market/year combinations**
    - "No wonder the system had appeared to be that good — we
      were looking at the single best case out of the 250
      possible market and year combinations we tested."
  - Full picture: the system **"did dismally in the remainder
    of the test sample"** — **17 out of 25 markets actually
    lost money** once transaction costs were taken into account
  - Practical rule: whenever a system is illustrated with just
    one market or one time period, assume it is the best-case
    example — test across ALL markets and ALL periods before
    drawing any conclusions
  - "Ever since that time, I have had an ingrained [suspicion
    of well-chosen examples]" — passage continues on next page

<!-- pp. 339-360 -->

## Chapter: Tom Basso — Serenity (continued)

### Biographical Context

<!-- page 339: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N234] **Tom Basso's background**:
  - Former engineer; opened first commodity account in 1975
  - Lost money for roughly five consecutive years before
    becoming profitable — but never quit because losses were
    always manageable (never risked more than he could afford
    to lose)
  - 1980: began managing equity accounts from an investment
    club
  - 1984: expanded to futures accounts; accounts as small as
    $25,000 caused excessive volatility
  - 1987: raised minimum account size to **$1 million** and
    returned all smaller client funds — recognized that
    diversification requires scale
  - Met Schwager at a **Dr. Van Tharp** and **Adrienne
    Toghraie** psychology investment seminar in Newark, NJ

### The "Well-Chosen Example" Problem in System Selling

<!-- page 340: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N235] **The well-chosen example trap — Basso's
  first trade** (1975):
  - Opened commodity account for $2,000; bought two corn
    contracts; immediately lost $600
  - Stomach turning, couldn't concentrate on work, called
    broker every hour
  - Trade was based on "a rather naive study" showing a
    certain infrequent chart pattern was followed by a price
    advance **100 percent of the time** — a textbook
    well-chosen example
  - Years later heard Schwager describe "the well-chosen
    example" as a pet peeve — recognized his own first trade
    was the epitome of it

<!-- page 341: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N236] **Schwager's editorial on the well-chosen
  example — how systems are sold using cherry-picked history**:
  - Mid-1980s: Schwager read an article on a simple, fully
    disclosed trading system that appeared to massively
    outperform his own far more complex system
  - Re-examined carefully: system had only two conditions;
    one condition Schwager and partner **Norman Strahm** had
    tested before and found only moderately performing; the
    other condition they had dismissed as so poor they never
    even tested it
  - They tested the second condition: for the specific market
    and year shown in the article, performance was exactly as
    claimed
  - But: the article's selected time period was **the single
    best year in a ten-year test period** for that market —
    AND the single best year for any of the **twenty-five
    markets** they tested over ten years
  - In other words: the article had selected the #1 best
    case out of **250 possible market/year combinations**
  - Mechanism of deception: not necessarily intentional fraud
    — system developers "define system conditions that are
    unrealistically restrictive" to maximize past performance,
    and the future never looks exactly like the past

<!-- page 342: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N237] **System cherry-picking results when
  stress-tested broadly**:
  - After Schwager's full test: "the system did dismally in
    the remainder of the test sample"
  - **Seventeen out of twenty-five markets** actually lost
    money once transaction costs were taken into account
  - Core lesson: any system can be made to look great by
    illustrating it with the one market and period where it
    worked best — "What a coincidence! I'm sure hindsight had
    nothing to do with it."
  - Basso's takeaway: ingrained skepticism toward illustrations
    used in trading system articles and advertisements

### Flexible Models vs. Overfitted Systems

<!-- page 343: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N238] **Why well-chosen example systems fail
  in real trading — and Basso's solution**:
  - Two types of system sellers: (a) those who know they're
    selling garbage, (b) those who genuinely believe in
    systems that won't work — "self-delusion occurs frequently,
    because it's psychologically comforting to construct a
    system that looks very good in its past performance"
  - Overfitting mechanism: in desire for superior past
    performance, developers make system conditions
    "unrealistically restrictive" — conditions so specific
    they will never recur
  - Solution: **keep models as flexible as possible** — design
    for robustness across many scenarios, not optimization for
    one scenario
  - Basso's stress-test method: mentally imagine scenarios
    "that would almost make good movie plots" — e.g., U.S.
    government defaults on T-bill obligations, dollar drops
    50% overnight — and live through what happens to
    positions

- [SHOULD KNOW][N239] **Purpose of extreme scenario thinking
  — not system design, but mental preparation**:
  - Basso doesn't design systems to cope with catastrophes;
    he mentally simulates what will happen to positions if
    they occur
  - Effect: "I know what I'll do in any given situation" —
    preparation converts panic into protocol

### The College of Trading — Losing as Tuition

<!-- page 344: 2 nuggets -->

<!-- page 345: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N240] **Basso's reframe: losses as tuition at
  the "College of Trading"**:
  - After his first corn trade loss: "I realized that every
    time I had a loss, I needed to learn something from the
    experience and view the loss as tuition at the College of
    Trading"
  - Key corollary: "As long as you learn something from a
    loss, it's not really a loss"
  - Adopted this attitude almost immediately after the first
    trade — "By adopting that perspective, I stopped looking
    at the losses as problems and started viewing them as
    opportunities to elevate myself to the next plateau"
  - Over the next five years: gradually improved and lost less
    each year — never thought he wasn't cut out for trading

- [SHOULD KNOW][N241] **Staying in the game during the
  learning years — Basso's survival rule**:
  - Never stopped trading before losing all allotted capital
    because he "never took a trade that would jeopardize my
    ability to continue trading"
  - Always limited risk per trade to a level that permitted
    re-entry if wrong
  - Reinforcement came from SHRINKING losses, not from
    profits — gradual progress toward breakeven was enough
    signal to persist
  - Always traded a very small account — "an amount that I
    could afford to lose without affecting my life-style"

### The 1979 Silver Miss — Taking Every Signal

<!-- page 346: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N242] **The 1979 silver opportunity loss —
  why Basso commits to taking every system signal**:
  - Was following a system and had taken EVERY trade for nine
    consecutive months
  - His parents came to visit for a week; he fell behind in
    his work updating charts
  - That same week: **silver broke out violently on the
    upside** — he missed the buy signal because he wasn't
    current
  - Account size at the time: ~$5,000
  - Over the next few months, silver skyrocketed; missing the
    single trade cost him ~**$30,000 profit per contract** —
    approximately a **sixfold increase** in his account
  - Resolution: "From that point on, no matter what system I
    was using, I always made certain that I would take all the
    trading signals"
  - Implication: one missed trade from a system you believe
    in can cost more than years of losses from bad trades

### The 1987 Silver Explosion — Volatility Management

<!-- page 347: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N243] **The 1987 silver trade — learning to
  manage equity swings**:
  - Basso and wife had account at ~$130,000 equity
  - Were long several silver contracts when the market
    "exploded"
  - Account went up ~$500,000 in one month — then
    surrendered **80 percent of that profit** in only a week
  - Emotional lesson: "When your account has these massive
    swings up and down, there's a tendency to feel a rush
    when the market is going your way and devastation when
    it's going against you. These emotions do absolutely
    nothing to make you a good trader."
  - Resolution: adopted a formula that **limits both profits
    AND drawdowns** by notching back the number of contracts
    traded in each market
  - Key mechanic: **number of contracts fluctuates with
    each market's volatility** — more volatile market = fewer
    contracts, keeping dollar risk constant

### Why Basso Has No Emotional Reaction to Market Moves

<!-- page 348: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N244] **Basso's formula for emotional
  equanimity — constant risk exposure**:
  - "When I come to work each day, I know that the risk and
    volatility in my portfolio is exactly the same as it was
    yesterday, last week, and last month. So why should I let
    my emotions go up and down if I'm in exactly the same
    exposure all the time?"
  - The volatility normalization formula (from N243) is what
    makes this possible — by keeping portfolio risk constant,
    market moves feel the same every day regardless of P&L

- [MUST KNOW][N245] **Why bought systems almost always fail
  their users — and the confidence problem**:
  - Even if a user buys a genuinely good system, they almost
    never have the confidence to stay with it through the
    first major drawdown — "every system in the world will
    have a drawdown"
  - If given Basso's exact system as a black box, users would
    quickly modify it to fit their own ideas, then abandon
    it at the first losing streak ("Tom may be a nice guy,
    but how do I know he just didn't develop this system off
    of some well-chosen examples?")
  - Root cause: confidence in a system requires
    **understanding** of the system — you must own the
    intellectual construction, not just the rules
  - Mechanism: a system you understand feels different from a
    black box; you can separate "the system is in a drawdown"
    from "the system is broken"

### The One-Thousand-Trades Framework

<!-- page 349: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N246] **Cure for trade-by-trade anxiety —
  think in terms of 1,000 trades**:
  - For traders who are profitable overall, use stops, but
    still feel anxiety and "can't stand to lose": think of
    each trade as **one of the next one thousand trades**
    you're going to make
  - Effect: "all of a sudden you've made any single trade
    seem very inconsequential. Who cares if a particular
    trade is a winner or a loser? It's just another trade."
  - This is the probabilistic framework: individual outcomes
    are noise; the system's edge manifests over large samples
  - Practical implication: emotional reaction to any single
    trade is a signal that you're NOT thinking in
    probabilities — it means you're treating the trade as
    unique when it's actually a member of a large series

### Mental Rehearsal and Morning Preparation

<!-- page 350: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N247] **Basso's morning mental rehearsal
  routine**:
  - Does MORE mental exercises as he became more
    computerized, not fewer
  - Every morning while driving to work: makes a conscious
    effort to relax; mentally rehearses any conflict that
    might happen that day
  - Effect: arrives at work in a "very positive frame of
    mind"
  - This is pre-competition visualization — identical to
    what elite athletes do before performance

- [MUST KNOW][N248] **Gulf War test of mental preparation —
  January 16–17, 1991**:
  - Evening of January 16, 1991: Basso was at the office for
    computer work; news broke that U.S. launched air war
    against Iraq; crude oil shot to $40; had substantial long
    position
  - First thought: "We're going to have a lot of volatility
    and risk control alarms going off tomorrow, and we better
    be prepared" — called managing director **George** at
    home
  - Next morning (January 17): heard crude oil was at $22 —
    thought he'd misheard ($42? $32?); confirmed: it was $22
    — overnight swing from +$8 (vs previous close) to down
    $10 from that $40 peak

<!-- page 351: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N249] **Gulf War outcome — lost 15%, felt
  phenomenally good**:
  - Basso and George came in early, ran all programs, worked
    out risk alarms, placed all orders before the opening
  - By 9:30 AM: everything done — all orders placed and
    executed, new position balances calculated
  - Estimated loss: ~**15 percent** of portfolio overnight
  - Basso's emotional state: "I sat there for a moment and
    thought about all the events and actions of the previous
    evening and that morning. I realized that there was not
    a single thing I would have done differently. Despite
    having just lost 15 percent in our portfolios overnight,
    I felt phenomenally good at that point."
  - Key insight: **the source of peace is not the outcome
    but the process** — if you executed your plan perfectly,
    a loss doesn't diminish you

<!-- page 352: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N250] **Why process-focus makes losses
  tolerable — the Gulf War lesson**:
  - Schwager confirms: "Because you had done everything that
    you were supposed to do?" Basso: "Exactly."
  - Basso had gone to sleep the night before with crude at
    $40+ (long position), woke up to find it at $22 —
    profit from evening had flipped to a massive loss
  - His gut response: curiosity about whether his programs
    would handle the situation correctly — not hurt or
    depression
  - Contrast: most traders would have intense negative
    emotions at seeing profits turn to a 15% loss overnight
  - Mechanism: Basso had mentally rehearsed catastrophic
    scenarios; so when one arrived, "you feel like you've
    already seen this movie once before, and you wonder
    whether it's going to come out the same way"

### The Triage Doctor Metaphor

<!-- page 353: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N251] **Basso's mental image for catastrophic
  market events — the triage doctor**:
  - Mental rehearsal model: "picture a doctor in a triage
    situation. He's in a battlefield emergency operating
    room. In come fifty bodies. Some are going to live; some
    are going to die. The doctor has been trained to handle
    the situation. He's going to make all the necessary
    decisions. 'This patient goes to operating room number
    one.' 'This patient gets pushed aside.' He's calm and
    collected, not nervous. He knows that he has the best
    chance of saving the maximum number of lives. He knows
    that he can't save them all, but he's going to do the
    best he can with what he has."
  - Application to trading: focus entirely on executing
    your protocol — allocation of attention (which positions
    need action now, which can wait) rather than P&L
  - Result: focus shuts out negative emotions because there's
    no bandwidth left for them

### The Emotional Spring Metaphor

- [SHOULD KNOW][N252] **Why emotional volatility burns
  traders out — the spring metaphor**:
  - Basso likens trader emotions to a spring being stretched
    up and down: "While it's going up and down, it's kind of
    thrilling, but eventually the spring wears out. Burnout
    sets in."
  - Counter-intuition: being thrilled on gains is just as
    problematic as being devastated on losses — both are
    stretching the spring
  - Solution: "keep your emotions in balance in the middle —
    it's actually a whole lot more fun"
  - Schwager quotes another trader's description: "When I'm
    losing money, I'm upset because I'm losing. When I'm
    making money, I'm anxious because I worry that I won't
    be able to keep it up" — illustrating how emotional
    reactivity is negative in BOTH directions
  - Basso's focus principle: "I focus my total attention on
    trading well, and let the results take care of themselves"

### The Observer Technique — Self-Disassociation

<!-- page 354: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N253] **The observer technique — watching
  yourself rather than being yourself**:
  - Origin: Basso had extreme fear of public speaking in high
    school — knees would literally tremble
  - Solution he developed: **disassociation** — observing
    himself from the outside. An internal observer would
    comment: "Why are you shaking, Tom? Just relax. You're
    talking too fast. Slow down a little bit."
  - The observer became a permanent presence: "If you're
    watching yourself doing everything, you get pretty close
    to watching a movie. The observer is watching you play
    a role in this movie called *Life*."
  - Trading application: instead of saying "I'm going to do
    this trade," say "I'm going to **watch myself** do this
    trade" — "all of a sudden you find that the process is
    a lot easier"

<!-- page 355: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N254] **The observer's practical function in
  trading decisions**:
  - The observer can catch real-time behavioral traps:
    "You're getting greedy on this trade, watch out." Or
    when indicators conflict: "How about doing nothing? You
    don't have to trade."
  - Key implication: the observer creates a pause between
    impulse and action — this is the psychological mechanism
    behind the ability to NOT trade when signals are unclear
  - Basso recommends this "not only for trading but for life
    in general. There's no reason why you have to struggle
    and strain and claw your way through life."
  - Pushback: "But if you're observing your life, you're not
    living it." Basso: "Think of life as a movie in which
    you'll see what you're seeing right now only one time.
    You'll never see it exactly the same way again. Absorb
    it; be aware of it; enjoy it."

### Stress Management and the Life-as-Movie Framework

- [SHOULD KNOW][N255] **Basso's framework for avoiding
  stress — keeping things in cosmic perspective**:
  - "The universe is overwhelming. It was here before you
    were born, and it will be here after you die. In the
    general scheme of things, your problems are not that
    important."
  - Life-as-movie analogy: if you rent a horror movie, you
    let yourself be horrified — but it's not stressful
    because you know it's just a movie. What if you had the
    same attitude about life?
  - Not passivity: Schwager clarifies Basso is suggesting
    you "enjoy and experience your life with the same
    involvement and intensity you would an engrossing movie,
    but at the same time, maintain the sense of perspective
    you would have if you were watching a movie"

### The 50-60 Year Trading Horizon

<!-- page 356: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N256] **Basso's long-horizon perspective —
  trading for 50-60 years**:
  - Projects trading for fifty or sixty years total: "over
    that time span, I'll see devastating declines, spectacular
    advances that I virtually can't believe, and everything
    in between"
  - Effect: any single dramatic event (Gulf War oil crash,
    etc.) is just one data point in a very long series —
    "you become curious" rather than panicked
  - Story: wife asked what he would do if he retired — he
    described setting up computers at home, trading, research,
    reading the Wall Street Journal. Wife said: "That sounds
    exactly like what you're doing right now. The only
    difference is that now you have a staff to back you up
    when you want to go on vacation." — He realized he was
    already doing what he loved

### Mechanical vs. Discretionary — Why Basso Trusts Systems Over Intuition

<!-- page 357: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N257] **Why Basso follows systems over
  intuition even when intuition is often right**:
  - "My intuitive feelings about the markets are probably
    right more often than they are wrong."
  - BUT: "sometimes the trading signals that I'm intuitively
    most nervous about turn out to be the best trades"
  - Conclusion: "over the long run, I think my performance
    is best served by following my systems unquestioningly"
  - The cases where intuition outperforms are outweighed by
    the cases where intuition fires false alarms on the most
    important trades
  - Second reason for pure systematization: **systems get
    the monkey off your back** — "If you lose money today,
    it's not your fault; it's the system that had the
    problem. There is an element of disassociation involved.
    Even though you designed the system, you start taking
    losses less personally."
  - Once fully computerized: "I found that I was less and
    less emotionally involved in each trade"

- [MUST KNOW][N258] **Basso's priority hierarchy for
  aspiring traders — psychology first**:
  - His actual learning order: (1) trading system, (2) risk
    management and volatility control, (3) psychology
  - What he would do in reverse if starting over:
    (1) **Investment psychology** — by far the most important
    (2) **Risk control**
    (3) **Entry/exit decisions** — the least important
  - Matches the recurring theme across Market Wizards — where
    you buy and sell matters far less than how you think
    and how you manage risk

### Schwager's Closing Commentary on Basso

<!-- page 358: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N259] **Schwager's meta-lesson from Basso —
  success without happiness is failure**:
  - "If trading (or any other job or endeavor) is a source
    of anxiety, fear, frustration, depression, or anger,
    something is wrong — even if you are successful in a
    conventional sense, and especially if you are not. You
    have to enjoy trading, because if trading is a source
    of negative emotions, you have probably already lost
    the game, even if you make money."
  - Schwager notes Basso is "far from being the most
    successful trader I have interviewed in terms of
    performance statistics, but he is probably one of the
    first I would choose as a role model"
  - Basso's prescription: combine enthusiasm, energy, focus,
    devotion, and discipline to become the best trader you
    can be — then stop agonizing over details. Maintain
    perspective. View market events as a movie. Learn from
    losses as tuition. Never risk more than you can afford.

---

## Chapter: Linda Bradford Raschke — Reading the Music of the Markets

### Biographical Background

<!-- page 359: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N260] **Linda Bradford Raschke — background
  and route to trading**:
  - Serious enough about trading that she put on a trade
    approximately **three hours after giving birth** to her
    daughter (she was short currency contracts expiring that
    day and rolled the position into the next month)
  - Could not land a stockbroker job after graduating college
    (rejected by every brokerage in San Francisco — told to
    come back in four or five years)
  - Took job as financial analyst at **Crown Zellerbach** (a
    paper company); office was two blocks from the
    **Pacific Coast Stock Exchange** which opened at 7:30
    — started spending every morning hour at the exchange
  - One floor trader explained options pricing to her; she
    thought "I can do that" — he was impressed by her
    enthusiasm and quick grasp of concepts; offered to back
    her with a **$25,000 stake** in a **50/50 partnership**
    on profits
  - She chose floor trading over getting an MBA — "It wasn't
    a hard decision"
  - Key insight on what backs traders: **level of interest** —
    "If someone has a strong enough interest or desire, it
    usually overcomes other obstacles"

- [SHOULD KNOW][N261] **Raschke's early floor trading
  results and transition off the floor**:
  - Spent six years as a floor trader: first at **Pacific
    Coast Stock Exchange**, then **Philadelphia Stock
    Exchange**
  - Made money steadily throughout, with one catastrophic
    exception early on (Cities Service options disaster,
    covered separately)
  - Suffered injuries in an accident in late 1986; forced to
    trade from an office — discovered she **preferred
    off-the-floor trading**
  - Set up trading office at home; her first year off the
    floor was her **best year ever**
  - Had **Epstein-Barr Syndrome** (chronic energy loss) at
    time of Schwager interview — had pushed herself too hard
    simultaneously trading full-time, caring for a newborn,
    managing home renovation, and training/riding horses

<!-- page 360: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N262] **Raschke's early floor trading
  method and the Cities Service disaster (setup)**:
  - Early method: buy underpriced options or sell overpriced
    options, hedge with other options or stock
  - First three months on the floor: made approximately
    $25,000 — options market was "incredibly inefficient in
    the early 1980s. You didn't really need an IQ over 100
    to make money."
  - Got involved selling calls on **Cities Service** because
    options were overpriced — reason for overpricing: the
    stock was a **takeover candidate**
  - Stock was at $32; calculated she'd be okay as long as
    price stayed below $55 — [continues into next batch]
  - Context: Cities Service takeover disaster was
    Raschke's one "catastrophic event" as a floor trader;
    the full story carries into the next pages
  - Background: Occidental College program — ten students
    selected each year to manage a trust set up by an
    anonymous donor; Raschke was one of them; made
    decisions "almost strictly on fundamentals" — mainly
    learned it was "an awful lot of fun"

<!-- pp. 359-380 -->

## Chapter: Linda Bradford Raschke (continued)

### Getting Started on the Floor

<!-- page 359: 3 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N262] **Raschke's entry into trading**: She wandered onto the floor of the
  Pacific Coast Stock Exchange as an observer; traders went out of their
  way to explain things to her. One trader explained options pricing — she
  thought "Gee, I can do that." Later that same trader offered to back her
  rather than have her go to business school for an MBA.
- [SHOULD KNOW][N263] **Formal education irrelevant on the floor**: Raschke observed that
  traders came from all walks of life — "some of the best traders were
  surf bums." Formal education had little to do with skill as a trader.
  - Practical implication: domain-specific pattern recognition matters far
    more than academic credentials
- [MUST KNOW][N264] **What earns a backer's trust**: The key quality that impressed her
  eventual backer — and that she looks for when backing others — was
  **level of interest and desire**. "If someone has a strong enough interest
  or desire, it usually overcomes other obstacles." Passion was the
  selection criterion, not credentials or intelligence.

> "If someone has a strong enough interest or desire, it usually overcomes other obstacles."
> — Linda Bradford Raschke

### The Cities Service Disaster

<!-- page 360: 3 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N265] **Partnership structure for floor traders**: Standard procedure was a
  limited partnership — the backer put up capital (here $25,000) as
  limited partner; the trader was general partner with a 50/50 profit
  split. The general partner bore full personal liability for losses
  beyond the capital.
- [SHOULD KNOW][N266] **Early floor trading edge — market inefficiency**: In the early 1980s
  options markets were "incredibly inefficient." Raschke's basic strategy:
  buy underpriced options or sell overpriced options, hedge with other
  options or stock. After her first three months she had made ~$25,000.
  - Key insight: she did NOT need superior intelligence — the market was
    simply mispriced. This edge no longer exists in the same form.
- [MUST KNOW][N267] **Cities Service disaster — the tail-risk lesson**: Raschke sold calls
  on **Cities Service** because options were overpriced (stock was a
  takeover candidate, hence high implied volatility). Stock was at $32;
  she calculated she'd be safe as long as it stayed below $55.
  - The takeover was announced the afternoon BEFORE options expiration
  - Stock jumped from ~$34 to $65 overnight — past her break-even
  - She lost $80,000 overnight, wiping out $25,000 profit + $25,000
    capital + leaving her $30,000 in the hole
  - As general partner, she was personally responsible for the full deficit
  - She was 22 years old and still had $10,000 in student loan debt
  - **Core lesson**: "Overpriced" options on takeover candidates are
    overpriced for a reason — the tail risk IS the reason for the premium.
    Selling volatility into known event risk is asymmetric in the wrong
    direction.

<!-- page 361: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N268] **Emotional resilience after catastrophic loss**: Raschke did not
  collapse psychologically after losing $80,000 overnight at age 22.
  Reasons she could absorb it emotionally:
  1. She had seen other floor traders lose "several million dollars" in
     similar sudden takeover situations — and survive. Her loss felt
     smaller in context.
  2. She believed: "In any business where you could lose money that
     quickly, you had to be able to make it back."
  3. Finding a new backer and recovering gave her **permanent confidence**:
     "Overcoming that experience gave me the confidence that I could
     overcome anything that might happen in the future."
  - Practical implication: early catastrophic loss that is survived and
    recovered from can be psychologically fortifying rather than
    permanently damaging

> "Overcoming that experience gave me the confidence that I could
> overcome anything that might happen in the future."
> — Linda Bradford Raschke

### Transition to Off-Floor Trading

<!-- page 362: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N269] **Accident forced evolution**: In late 1986 a bad horse-riding
  accident (fractured ribs, punctured lung, dislocated shoulder) made it
  physically impossible to stand on the floor. She started trading from
  upstairs off a quote machine — and loved it: multiple indicators,
  multiple markets visible simultaneously. She evolved a new style in
  **S&P futures**.
- [MUST KNOW][N270] **Short-term is the only predictable horizon — chaos theory**:
  Raschke's core belief: only short-term price swings can be predicted
  with precision. Accuracy drops dramatically the further out the forecast.
  She is a "strong believer in **chaos theory**."
  - **Chaos theory explanation** (Schwager's note): Aperiodic systems
    (markets, weather) never exactly repeat; they never reach a steady
    state. Slight differences in initial conditions get magnified over
    time — the **Butterfly Effect** (from James Gleick's *Chaos: Making a
    New Science*): "a butterfly stirring the air today in Peking can
    transform storm systems next month in New York."
  - Implication: too many unpredictable variables accumulate beyond two
    months. Her ideal trade lasts 10 days; she approaches every trade as
    if she'll hold it only 2–3 days.

### Raschke's Trading System

<!-- page 363: 4 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N271] **Predict direction, not magnitude — no price targets**: Raschke
  believes in predicting price direction but explicitly NOT magnitude.
  She sets NO price targets. She gets out when market action tells her
  it's time — not based on how far price has traveled.
  - "You have to be willing to take what the market gives you. If it
    doesn't give you very much, you can't hesitate to get out with a
    small profit."

- [MUST KNOW][N272] **Entry price is the #1 skill**: She puts "a great deal of effort
  into getting the best entry price possible" — considers it probably
  her strongest skill. Mechanism:
  - Good entry price **buys time** to see how market will react
  - If you buy expecting a bounce and the market only goes sideways,
    you get out — the entry timing told you the thesis was wrong
  - "If your entry timing is good enough, you won't lose much even
    when you're wrong."
  - Practical implication: entry precision is a risk management tool,
    not just a profit maximization tool

- [MUST KNOW][N273] **The rubber band model**: Short-term price fluctuations around
  equilibrium are like a **rubber band being stretched** — the further it
  is pulled, the more certainly it will snap back. Raschke waits for
  the extreme point, then trades the snap.
  - "Some of the best trades come when everyone gets very panicky.
    The crowd can often act very stupidly in the markets."
  - Contrarian discipline: she sells when a multi-day rally has made
    the market look good to everyone; she buys when a multi-day decline
    has made everyone bearish

- [SHOULD KNOW][N274] **2-to-4-day cycle pattern**: One of Raschke's favorite patterns —
  markets tend to move from relative lows to relative highs and back
  **every two to four days**. This is a "function of human behavior":
  it takes several days of rallying before a market looks good enough
  that everyone wants to buy it — which is precisely when
  professionals are selling.

> "Some of the best trades come when everyone gets very panicky. The
> crowd can often act very stupidly in the markets."
> — Linda Bradford Raschke

### Indicators and Execution

<!-- page 364: 3 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N275] **Key indicators: tick, TRIN, premium**:
  - **Tick**: difference between number of issues whose most recent trade
    was an uptick vs. downtick — measures breadth momentum
  - **TRIN** (Arms Index): relates price and volume of advancing issues
    to declining issues — measures intensity of selling or buying pressure
  - **Premium/discount**: S&P futures vs. theoretically equivalent cash
    index — measures futures market sentiment relative to cash
  - Example signal: tick at extreme and falling (–480, –485, –490, –495)
    then PAUSES (–495, –495, –495) while other indicators also oversold →
    she buys at market. "Sometimes I've actually bought the low tick of
    the day using this method."

- [MUST KNOW][N276] **Rule #1: Don't try to make a profit on a bad trade — find the
  best exit**: When wrong, Raschke does NOT immediately dump the position.
  She has patience because:
  1. The reason she entered was that the market was extremely overdone
  2. A reaction seems overdue — that thesis still applies even when
     the trade goes against her initially
  3. She can usually get out at a better price with a little patience
  - "Perhaps my number one rule is: Don't try to make a profit on a bad
    trade, just try to find the best place to get out."
  - After exiting, she can re-enter the same direction at a higher/lower
    price — she treats it as a fresh trade with no psychological baggage

- [SHOULD KNOW][N277] **No fear of buying breaks or selling rallies**: Because she waits
  for sufficient extremes, even when the market continues against her,
  it will "often snap back enough to let me out near even." Willingness
  to trade INTO momentum extremes is what makes the strategy work —
  half-hearted entries at less extreme levels do not have the same
  snap-back probability.

### Isolation and October 1987

<!-- page 365: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N278] **Isolation is the hidden cost of home trading**: After leaving the
  floor, Raschke found the first four years at home ideal — no
  distractions. But eventually isolation became a serious problem. She
  tried: phone calls with other traders (distracting, hurt productivity),
  sharing office with another trader (worked until he moved), hiring an
  assistant (didn't improve bottom line). She now uses outside projects
  (Market Technicians Association meetings, neural network development)
  to maintain connection to the world.
  - Key insight: social structure of the trading floor provides
    psychological benefits that are easy to underestimate; solo traders
    must actively engineer substitutes

- [SHOULD KNOW][N279] **Neural networks as a trading tool**: Raschke worked with a
  programmer to develop **neural network trading indicators** she now uses
  in her process. Key characteristic: they are NOT static — they evolve
  as they "learn" from the data. Contrasts with traditional fixed
  indicators.

<!-- page 366: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N280] **Stopping at the top — Raschke and the 1987 crash**: In 1987,
  Raschke had a phenomenal year to that point — over $500,000 in
  profits, nearly twice her prior year. She felt she had been "too hot"
  and shouldn't press her luck. She stopped trading about a month before
  the October crash to apprentice with a horse trainer.
  - She had no positions at the start of crash week
  - **Critical psychological trait**: the discipline to stop when hot,
    rather than pressing — rare and valuable. Most traders increase size
    after a winning streak.

<!-- page 367: 3 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N281] **Buying the 1987 crash — maximum contrarianism**: On the morning of
  October 19, 1987, Raschke's husband (a market maker on the
  Philadelphia Stock Exchange) called her and said "All the world markets
  have crashed, and it looks like the Dow Jones is going to open 200
  points lower." Her thought: "Boy, this is great. This is the buying
  opportunity I've been waiting for."
  - She watched news coverage of universal panic and thought: "This is
    terrific. Let's see how low we can open the market."
  - Forced herself to hold back — waited until early afternoon, then
    bought 1 S&P futures contract, kept buying into the decline through
    the final hour, ended the day long 10 contracts
  - By close she was down ~$100,000

- [SHOULD KNOW][N282] **Closing conviction despite paper losses**: She was "not really
  concerned about the initial loss." Her reasoning: futures were at such
  a large **discount to cash** that she was certain they would open higher
  the next day — which they did. She took profits on part of the
  position on the higher opening but stayed long, believing the crash
  was a **selling exhaustion point**.
  - Evidence for the thesis: Salomon Brothers stock had traded at $32
    when she first went to the Philadelphia floor, ran to $60+, and
    on crash day traded at $22. "It seemed ridiculous that people were
    pricing stocks that way."

- [MUST KNOW][N283] **The hidden risk she missed — systemic failure**: In retrospect,
  Raschke acknowledges she "was naive in having faith that the markets,
  clearing firms, and banks would continue to function." If the Fed had
  been less aggressive, her clearing firm — along with many others —
  could have gone bankrupt, obliterating her account equity entirely.
  - Key lesson: in extreme crises, **counterparty and systemic risk**
    are real. Being right on direction doesn't protect you if the
    infrastructure itself fails. Buying maximum panic is valid only
    when the system itself holds together.

### Mistakes and Performance Statistics

<!-- page 368: 3 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N284] **Core weakness: premature entry**. Raschke's self-identified primary
  weakness: being too early into positions. Her discipline: "Patience,
  patience, patience." Mechanics she uses:
  - Wait until things set up "just right" before trading
  - Count to ten slowly before picking up the phone to place the order
  - Mantra: "It's better to have the wrong idea and good timing than the
    right idea and bad timing."
  - Pioneer metaphor: "The pioneers are the ones with the arrows in
    their backs."

- [MUST KNOW][N285] **Small positions cause big losses — complacency trap**: A recurring
  mistake: participating in too many markets at once → sloppy trading.
  Paradox: her SMALLEST positions cause her BIGGEST losses because
  they get neglected.
  - "It's natural to be cautious and attentive to big positions. With the
    small positions, it's easy to fall into the trap of being complacent."
  - Practical implication: position size inversely affects attention.
    Traders systematically over-monitor large positions and neglect small
    ones — the small ones then drift into large losses unnoticed.

- [MUST KNOW][N286] **Performance statistics**:
  - Win rate: ~**70%** of trades profitable
  - Average win (short-term, per contract): ~**$450**
  - Average loss: ~**$200**
  - Win rate × average win vs. average loss: 0.70 × $450 = $315 expected
    value per trade (vs. 0.30 × $200 = $60 expected loss) → massive
    positive edge
  - Result: profitable nearly every WEEK; philosophy is to try to be
    profitable every DAY
  - Watches 20 markets, trades no more than ~6 at any one time

> "My philosophy is to try to be profitable every day! Of course, I
> don't quite achieve that consistency, but that's my goal."
> — Linda Bradford Raschke

<!-- page 369: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N287] **Trading as craft, not science**: Raschke frames trading as a
  **craft** analogous to playing piano. Like any craft, perfection is
  unachievable (never play a piece perfectly; never buy the exact low
  and sell the exact high), but **consistency is achievable** through
  practice day in and day out. Measurement is skill, not dollars.
- [SHOULD KNOW][N288] **Market focus limit**: Analyzes ~20 markets but trades no more
  than ~6 at any given time. Ideal would be every market every day —
  physically impossible. Could not successfully delegate this to an
  assistant (trained one for a year; he had no passion, couldn't pull
  the trigger — would watch the trade he planned to make go up without
  entering it).

### Training Others — What Can't Be Taught

<!-- page 370: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N289] **Passion cannot be trained**: Raschke hired and trained an assistant
  for a year — hardworking, loyal, even-keeled, physically disciplined
  (karate daily). On paper: ideal candidate. Failed completely as a
  trader because he had "no passion for trading" and couldn't "pull the
  trigger" — he didn't like taking risks.
  - Illustrative exchange: "OK Steve, what's your game plan today?" →
    "I think I'm going to buy wheat." → End of day: "Did you buy the
    wheat?" → "No." → "What did you do?" → "I watched it go up."
  - **Key lesson**: all the intellectual preparation, good character, and
    work ethic in the world cannot substitute for genuine risk appetite
    and trading passion. These qualities are either present or absent —
    they cannot be installed through training.

> "He didn't seem to have any passion for trading. He couldn't pull
> the trigger. I think he didn't like the idea of taking risks."
> — Linda Bradford Raschke

### Pattern Recognition and Music

<!-- page 371: 3 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N290] **Most important skill: pattern recognition**: Raschke's view of her
  primary trading edge is an ability to perceive patterns in the market.
  She attributes this to heavy musical involvement from ages 5–21 —
  practiced piano several hours every single day. College major: economics
  and **musical composition**.
  - Musical scores are "just symbols and patterns" — hours of daily
    analysis developed the brain's pattern-recognition circuitry
  - Daily instrument practice also built **discipline and concentration** —
    both essential for trading

- [MUST KNOW][N291] **The music-market analogy** (her most developed framework):
  | Music | Markets |
  |---|---|
  | Repeating patterns with variations | Price patterns repeat with variations |
  | Quiet interludes | Price consolidations |
  | Theme development | Major trends |
  | Crescendo to climax | Runaway moves to tops/bottoms |
  | Patience as piece unfolds | Patience waiting for trade setup |
  | Practice improves but never perfects | Trading improves but never perfects |
  | Relaxed → better performance | Relaxed → better trading |
  | Go with the flow | Go with the flow |
  | Read measure by measure | Short-term focus precedes long-term |
  - "All you can hope to do is play a piece (or trade) better than
    before."
  - Short-term focus (measure by measure) → explains why she gravitates
    toward short-term trading rather than long-term analysis

<!-- page 372: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N292] **Women as traders — Raschke's perspective**:
  - Initially felt she had to work twice as hard to gain respect; in
    retrospect, believes this was her own perception, not reality — if
    anything, people went out of their way to be helpful because she was
    rare
  - Trading is a **bottom-line business**: "They don't care if you're a
    man or a woman. If you perform well, you'll get financial backing."
  - **Potential women's advantage**: less likely to trade as ego trips;
    not prone to "macho-type trades" (large position intended to feel
    power in moving the market) — a pattern she saw lead to financial
    ruin in male traders
  - Women may be more **intuitive**: more socially acceptable to rely on
    intuition vs. rigid rule adherence; intuition comes into play
    significantly in trading (e.g., reading price action gestalt rather
    than mechanical signals)

### Secrets, Advice, and Philosophy

<!-- page 373: 3 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N293] **Why sharing methods doesn't hurt**: Raschke believes she could
  give away all her secrets and it wouldn't matter. Two reasons:
  1. "Most people can't control their emotions or follow a system"
  2. "Most traders wouldn't follow my system, even if I gave them
     step-by-step instructions, because my approach wouldn't feel right
     to them" — they lack the same confidence and comfort in the method
  - Bonus: if sharing did change her patterns, "new ones would be
    created, and I'm confident that I would find them."
  - **Deeper implication**: trading success is not a function of knowing
    the right rules. Execution, emotional control, and internalized
    confidence are what actually differentiate traders.

- [MUST KNOW][N294] **Advice for novice traders** (dense, important):
  1. **Immerse completely** — learning takes years; give up everything
     else; get as close to successful traders as possible; work for one
     for free if necessary
  2. **Find a niche and specialize** — pick one market or pattern, learn
     it inside out before expanding
  3. **Manual observation exercise**: pick one market; without intraday
     charts, jot down price every five minutes from open to close for an
     entire week. Questions to ask: Where are support/resistance levels?
     How does price act at those levels? What happens in the last 30
     minutes? How long does each intraday move last?
  4. **Stay actively involved** — don't passively watch; old successful
     traders still construct their own point-and-figure charts by hand
     intraday. Develop a routine.
  5. **Never be greedy** — it's OK to leave money on the table; if you
     can't get in at a favorable price, let it go
  6. **Never fear mistakes** — if you make one, don't complicate with
     hedges; just get out

<!-- page 374: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N295] **The independence litmus test**: Raschke's most insightful
  observation (echoed by Schwager as particularly notable): "If you ever
  find yourself tempted to seek out someone else's opinion on a trade,
  that's usually a sure sign that you should get out of your position."
  - Mechanism: the impulse to seek validation is your subconscious
    already telling you the trade isn't working. A trader who needs
    external confirmation has already lost objectivity.
  - Real traders do their own work, make their own game plans, make their
    own decisions — this is what makes it possible to know when a trade
    isn't working out.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N296] **Raschke's self-measurement**: She measures progress not in
  dollars but in **skill** — specifically how close she can come to
  pinpointing entries and exits to actual market turns. "I believe I can
  go into any market with just a quote machine and out-trade 98 percent
  of the other traders." This is not arrogance — it is the deep
  confidence Schwager identifies as common to all great traders.

### Schwager's Postscript on Raschke

<!-- page 375: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N297] **Confidence as prerequisite for great trading**: Schwager's
  meta-observation — "one of the primary common characteristics I have
  found among the great traders is an almost compelling sense of
  confidence in their ability to succeed." Raschke exemplifies this
  more than almost any other interviewee.
  - The chicken-and-egg question: do traders like Raschke succeed because
    they are confident, or are they confident because they succeed?
    Schwager's answer: "Probably a little bit of both. However, the key
    point is that exuberant confidence appears to be one of the essential
    elements in exceptional achievement."

- [MUST KNOW][N298] **Short-term predictability vs. long-term — Raschke reversed
  Schwager's prior belief**: Schwager had long assumed markets might be
  predictable long-term but short-term movements are largely random.
  Raschke holds the exact opposite: short-term predictions can be quite
  accurate; long-term forecasting is "a virtual impossibility" (chaos
  theory). Her consistency at short-term trading "would defy the laws
  of probability, if indeed there were no patterns in these movements."
  - "Raschke has made me a believer. Clearly, there are predictable
    movements in price even over periods as short as a few days or a
    single day."

### Raschke's Essential Characteristics (Schwager's Summary)

<!-- page 375: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N299] **The essential traits Raschke cites for being a good trader**:
  1. **Passion for trading**
  2. **Self-reliance** in developing trading ideas and making decisions
  3. **Willingness to take risk**
  4. **Ability to correct mistakes immediately** (because mistakes are
     inevitable)
  5. **Patience, patience, patience**

