## Chapter: CRT — The Trading Machine

### Introduction to CRT

<!-- page 377: 4 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N300] **CRT (Chicago Research and Trading)** — one of the most
  extraordinary trading operations ever built. **Joe Ritchie** (founder)
  fifteen years earlier had been so broke he had to borrow his brother
  **Mark Ritchie**'s wholesale suit to visit the Chicago Board of Trade.
  By the time of this interview: 800-employee trading company, primary
  dealer in government securities, estimated ~**$1 billion** in trading
  profits on over **$10 trillion in transactions**.
  - A floor broker's comment: "You guys make money every day" — only a
    slight exaggeration. CRT is profitable "virtually every month, if
    not every week."

- [MUST KNOW][N301] **CRT's core philosophy — exploit small inefficiencies, not home
  runs**: CRT is "in the business of extracting the many modest profit
  opportunities that are continually created by the small inefficiencies
  in the marketplace." Examples:
  - Options on one exchange slightly higher-priced than equivalent
    options on another → buy low, sell high
  - Options providing better risk/reward than underlying → trade one
    against the other
  - Hundreds/thousands of variations on this theme
  - CRT tracks ~**75 different markets** on **19 worldwide exchanges**
    simultaneously

- [MUST KNOW][N302] **CRT's operational model — real-time pricing sheets + instant
  hedging**:
  - Floor brokers carry sheets generated by proprietary computer models
    showing exactly what price to bid/offer for each option at each
    market price level
  - Sheets incorporate not just option value but how each option
    interrelates with the firm's overall position (e.g., if buying a
    particular option would reduce net risk exposure, the bid is skewed
    upward)
  - After any option trade, the floor trader immediately offsets with an
    equivalent opposite trade in the outright market (e.g., sell T-bond
    calls → instantly buy equivalent T-bond futures)
  - Hedge is implemented via hand signals to an arbitrage clerk 50 feet
    away — within 1–2 seconds. Initial hedge virtually eliminates
    near-term risk.
  - On very volatile days, runners bring revised computer sheets to floor
    traders as often as **20 times in one day**

- [SHOULD KNOW][N303] **Upstairs position management**: After initial trade + hedge are
  booked, responsibility shifts to an **upstairs position manager** whose
  sole job is to keep firm risk exposure as close to zero as possible
  as the portfolio constantly changes. CRT also maintains a **backup risk
  control group** specifically to handle extraordinary events (1987 crash,
  Gulf War price moves).

<!-- page 378: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N304] **Technology infrastructure**: CRT's operation requires an entire
  floor of its building devoted to computers. Departments for developing
  new proprietary software; even its own on-site hardware repair unit.
  Software philosophy: translate all data into **graphic/visual displays**,
  based on the belief that the human mind processes visual information
  more readily. New generation of software (in development at time of
  visit): **3D displays** showing a firm's real-time option positions
  across all strike prices AND all expiration dates simultaneously, with
  colors indicating concentration of positions.

- [MUST KNOW][N305] **Teamwork as the true source of CRT's edge**: Despite all the
  technology, CRT employees unanimously identify **teamwork** as the key
  to success. The culture:
  - No blame culture: traders trusted that upstairs staff wouldn't blame
    them for losses, enabling faster and more decisive action
  - Contrast: at other firms, screaming matches when things went wrong
    (broker blamed clerk, clerk blamed phone person, phone person blamed
    customer) — caused paralysis and inability to act
  - A CRT floor trader could use discretion on a 500-lot order; at
    competing firms a trader had to call upstairs for permission on
    anything over 50 lots → CRT could act 10x faster
  - Compensation: Joe Ritchie described as "absolutely committed to
    sharing the wealth" — more concerned with splitting profits fairly
    than minimizing payroll

<!-- page 379: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N306] **No-blame culture yields better execution**: A former CRT floor
  trader described competing firms where, when things went wrong, the
  broker blamed the clerk, clerk blamed phone staff, phone staff blamed
  customer — screaming matches common. At CRT, the trust eliminated
  this entirely. The trader could act immediately on large orders,
  whereas competitors had to call upstairs for permission, introducing
  delays that cost them execution quality.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N307] **The quiet trading floor**: Schwager's personal observation
  visiting CRT — the trading floor was **quiet**. No shouting, no turmoil,
  no anxiety, no swearing. It resembled a university or law firm more
  than a trading operation. This is structurally significant: the
  emotional absence means decisions are made analytically, not
  reactively. The usual trading floor noise is emotion — and emotion
  is the enemy of systematic arbitrage.

<!-- page 380: partial — chapter introduction content continues -->
<!-- page 380: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N308] **CRT's relationship-based clerk management**: One CRT trader
  described getting better service from his clerk by making the clerk
  feel valued and trusted than a competing broker got by handing his
  clerk a $100 bill on expiration day. "I let him know that I believe
  in him and the company values him." The relationship/culture approach
  outperformed the transactional approach — directly impacting execution
  quality on the most critical trading days.

<!-- pp. 379-400 -->

## Chapter: CRT (Conclusion)

### CRT Risk Management Infrastructure

<!-- page 379: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N263] **CRT's institutional risk control architecture**:
  - Dedicated **upstairs position manager** whose sole job is to
    keep the firm's aggregate risk exposure as close to zero
    as possible at all times — across hundreds of traders and
    thousands of interrelationships between instruments worldwide
  - CRT has a **backup risk control group** specifically to
    ensure readiness for extraordinary events — explicitly
    cited: October 1987 crash and Gulf War price moves
  - Nearly an entire floor of the office building devoted to
    computers; on-site hardware repair unit; departments for
    proprietary software development
  - New 3D visualization software (in development at time
    of interview): would display an option market's real-time
    position across ALL strike prices AND all expiration dates
    simultaneously, with color-coding for concentration —
    "human mind can draw information much more readily if it
    is in visual form"
  - Implication: institutional edge is partly organizational
    and technological infrastructure, not just trader skill

<!-- page 380: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N264] **CRT's teamwork philosophy as competitive
  advantage — no-blame culture and discretion**:
  - Former CRT floor trader: "If you have the confidence that
    the person upstairs is not going to blame you, it makes life
    so much easier… When I worked on the floor, I often saw
    screaming matches between people trying to make sure the
    blame didn't get laid on them."
  - Result: CRT traders could act on **500-lot orders with
    immediate discretion**; traders at competing firms had to
    call upstairs for permission on anything above 50 lots —
    massive speed disadvantage in fast markets
  - Second CRT trader: "Our competition… will hand the clerk
    a $100 bill to give them special attention on expiration
    day. I don't have to hand my clerk a $100 bill. I let him
    know that I believe in him and the company values him. I get
    better service… by making him feel that way."
  - Compensation: **Joe Ritchie** described as "absolutely
    committed to sharing the wealth… more concerned about how he
    can split up the profits fairly" than minimizing payouts
  - Observable signal of culture: Schwager noted the trading
    floor was **completely quiet** — "you might have thought we
    had toured the offices of a university or law firm" — no
    shouting, turmoil, anxiety, or stress

- [NICE TO KNOW][N265] **CRT's physical environment as culture
  signal**:
  - Casual dress (jeans, sport shirts standard), home-cooked
    cafeteria on the trading floor (not typical cafeteria food),
    lounge area on the floor
  - Interpretation: environment reflects philosophy — calm,
    collaborative, long-term-oriented

<!-- page 381: 1 nugget -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N266] **CRT founders — Mark and Joe Ritchie**:
  - Four founding members; next two chapters interview
    **Mark Ritchie** and **Joe Ritchie**
  - Mark drifted away from CRT years before interview,
    preferring to trade independently without managerial
    obligations
  - Joe remained CRT's **primary visionary and guiding force**;
    day-to-day operations transferred to senior management team
  - Mark's interview placed first to maintain original
    conversation order

