## Chapter: Mark Ritchie — God in the Pits

<!-- page 382: 2 nuggets -->

### Background

- [SHOULD KNOW][N267] **Mark Ritchie's background and
  off-floor trading performance**:
  - Attended **divinity school** (philosophy of religion),
    working as correctional officer (midnight-to-8 shift) and
    truck driver during school — so poor he sometimes couldn't
    fill his truck's gas tank
  - Got hooked on trading when brother **Joe Ritchie** brought
    him to the **Chicago Board of Trade** to explore silver
    arbitrage in the early 1970s
  - Spent most of career on CBOT floor, specializing in
    **soybean crush** trading
  - Transitioned to office trading approximately 5 years before
    interview; first year off the floor was difficult (many blind
    alleys) but ultimately highly successful:
    - **$1 million account** in 1987 → **50% average annual
      return** over the next four years
  - Philanthropic work: extended stays living with a primitive
    **Amazonian tribe**; compiled narratives from the Indians'
    perspectives into manuscript *Victim of Delusion*
  - Book: ***God in the Pits*** [MacMillan, 1989] — described
    as "a highly unusual blend of spiritual revelations, exotic
    experiences, and trading stories." Title refers to Ritchie's
    conviction of God's presence in his life, not deity-like
    trading prowess.

<!-- page 383: 1 nugget -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N268] **Schwager's impression of Mark Ritchie**:
  - Found him "very personable, low-key, and sincere"
  - Interview conducted over multiple sessions in CRT's offices

<!-- page 384: 2 nuggets -->

### Ethics on the Floor

- [SHOULD KNOW][N269] **Ritchie on ethics and culture variation
  across trading pits**:
  - "Each pit is almost a culture in itself. A pit has its own
    personality."
  - Soybean meal pit (where Ritchie spent 90% of his time):
    "some of the nicest and most honest people I've known in my
    life"
  - "In one sense, you have to be awfully honest to be in this
    business, where huge transactions take place with a nod of
    the head."
  - BUT: "there's tremendous opportunity to cheat"
  - Ritchie's view: "I don't believe there are any more cheats
    in this business than, say, among plumbers or lawyers"
  - Key distinction: **the payoff for a dishonest act** is
    far higher than in most industries — some brokers would
    fill orders for nothing (or even pay) just for the
    opportunity to **bucket trades** (trade against customer
    orders for their own account)
  - One broker Ritchie knew had such severe conflict of interest
    between trading own account and filling customer orders that
    "the only way he could sleep at night was to refuse to ever
    do any customer orders"

<!-- page 385: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N270] **Ritchie on the FBI sting operation**:
  - "All the honest people in this business were thankful that
    something was finally done."

> "For years, I've been saying that if we didn't clean up our
> own business, someone else would do it for us, and we wouldn't
> like the result. Instead of a scalpel, they'd likely use a
> dull chainsaw."
> — Mark Ritchie

- [MUST KNOW][N271] **Floor trading success rate — Ritchie's
  estimate**:
  - Roughly **10% do well**, and maybe **1% do extremely well**
  - Qualified as "a wild guess" — willingness to accept other
    percentages
  - Implication: the floor, despite appearing accessible, is
    highly Darwinian

<!-- page 386: 2 nuggets -->

### Why Traders Fail

- [MUST KNOW][N272] **Primary reason traders fail: overtrading
  — the $25K/50-contract case study**:
  - Ritchie encountered a trader who published articles and was
    "often quoted by the press" yet "hardly knows the first thing
    about successful trading"
  - This trader was holding a **50-contract position in a
    volatile interest rate spread** with only **$25,000 in his
    account** — money he "couldn't afford to lose"
  - Ritchie at the same time held **only twice the position
    size** ($100 contracts) with **$1,000,000 in his account**
    — and was *still worried* about the size
  - Ritchie told the trader: "Overtrading, wouldn't even begin
    to communicate it"
  - Trader cut position in half and asked "I'm OK now, right?"
    Ritchie: "You're not OK. You haven't even begun."
  - Ritchie warned the clearing firm: the trader was "a walking
    time bomb" — he eventually self-destructed, leaving the
    clearing firm with a large debit he had to work off
  - Key insight: leverage perception is completely distorted by
    emotional attachment to the money at risk

<!-- page 387: 3 nuggets -->

### Position Sizing and Greed

- [MUST KNOW][N273] **The "pillow test" — Ritchie's personal
  position size rule**:
  - "Whenever I'm still thinking about my position when I lay
    my head on my pillow at night, I begin liquidation the
    next morning."
  - Stronger version: "If I find myself praying about a
    position at any time, I liquidate it immediately. That's
    a sure sign of disaster."
  - "God is not a market manipulator. I knew a trader once who
    thought he was. He went broke — the trader, I mean."
  - Mechanism: position anxiety is a signal that size has
    exceeded the psychological threshold where rational
    management is possible

- [MUST KNOW][N274] **Position size as the most undervalued
  factor in trading**:
  - "Magnitude of losses and profits is purely a matter of
    position size. Controlling position size is indispensable
    to success."
  - "Of all the traits necessary to trade successfully, this
    factor is the **most undervalued**."
  - Corollary: position size is linked directly to greed —
    the failed trader held 50 contracts on $25K because of
    greed, not ignorance of risk math

- [MUST KNOW][N275] **Ritchie on greed as disqualifier**:
  - "The successful trader must be able to recognize and
    control his greed."

> "If you get a buzz from profits and are depressed by losses,
> you belong in Las Vegas, not the markets."
> — Mark Ritchie

  - Implication: emotional reactivity to P&L is a
    fundamental incompatibility with disciplined trading

<!-- page 388: 2 nuggets -->

### Traits for Success — Contrarianism and Panic Clarity

- [MUST KNOW][N276] **Ritchie: panic markets are the best
  opportunity — but require acting when others freeze**:
  - "The markets that go wild are the ones with the best
    opportunity. Traditionally, what happens in a market that
    goes berserk is that even veteran traders will tend to stand
    aside. That's your opportunity to make the money."
  - Mechanism: when professionals step back, spreads widen,
    mispricings multiply, and decisive actors capture
    extraordinary returns
  - Prerequisite: the ability to think clearly and act
    decisively when everyone around you is in panic

- [MUST KNOW][N277] **"The battle cry of mediocrity" — Ritchie
  on contrarianism**:
  - "How many times have you heard someone put down an idea
    you're excited about by saying, 'If it's such a good idea,
    why isn't everyone doing it?' This is the **battle cry of
    mediocrity**."
  - Logic: "Any investment opportunity that everyone else is
    doing is by definition a bad idea… The reason markets get
    out of line is because everyone is doing the wrong thing."
  - Practical application: "The good trader always sticks with
    his own ideas and closes his ears to the why-isn't-everyone-
    doing-it cry of the crowd. He'll make a trade against the
    crowd at a conservative level he can afford, and then get
    out if he's wrong. That's what a stop is for."
  - Caveat acknowledged: "If it looks too good to be true, the
    rest of the market may know something that you don't" — both
    directions are possible. But "usually the way we miss
    opportunities in this business is by saying, 'It looks too
    good to be true,' and then not doing anything."

<!-- page 389: 2 nuggets -->

### The Flying Lesson — Acting Under Adverse Conditions

- [MUST KNOW][N278] **Ritchie's flying lesson analogy —
  implementing under adverse conditions**:
  - Story: taking flying lessons, second or third landing,
    wind gusts at 20-30 feet blew the plane all over the runway
  - Fought the plane down for "probably the worst landing ever
    in history" — was chuckling at how terrible it was when
    the instructor said: "Well, that was really impressive."
  - Instructor's explanation: "Any other beginner would have
    taken his hand off the stick, given up, and expected me to
    land the thing. You hung in there and implemented the
    program all the way until the landing was complete."
  - Insight: "I thought about that later and realized that
    that is the trait you have to have to trade."
  - Ritchie's definition: **"The ability to implement your
    ideas despite adverse conditions. There is no opportunity
    in the market that is not an adverse condition situation."**
  - Mechanism: markets always look scary when they're moving;
    that's exactly when your plan needs to be executed

- [SHOULD KNOW][N279] **Preparing for panic — game plans,
  not innate ability**:
  - Schwager: is acting calmly under pressure innate or
    trainable?
  - Ritchie: "I'm not sure, but I don't think it's innate.
    You can prepare for it by having a game plan."
  - Analogy: baseball coach who would yell "Have an idea!
    What are you going to do?" before every at-bat
  - "You have to know what you're going to do when the market
    gets out of line. Generally speaking, it's human nature
    to hesitate."
  - Method: "I go through a mental process. I decide what I'm
    going to do when X, Y, or Z happens. **If X, Y, or Z is a
    surprise, then you're part of the crowd.**"

<!-- page 390: 1 nugget -->

### God in the Pits — Honesty and Vulnerability

- [NICE TO KNOW][N280] **Ritchie on writing *God in the Pits*
  and personal vulnerability**:
  - Reviewer called the book "embarrassingly personal"
  - "In the first few months after the book was released, I
    could hardly look anybody in the eye who said they had
    read it, because I felt they knew more about me than was
    necessary."
  - Reason he wrote it: "My hope was that the book would make
    people take a long, honest look at themselves — at their
    relationship to others and their relationship to God. How
    could I expect people to honestly confront these questions
    unless I was willing to do the same."

<!-- page 391: 2 nuggets -->

### How Mark Ritchie Entered Trading

- [SHOULD KNOW][N281] **Origin story — Joe Ritchie's silver
  arbitrage and the CBOT visit**:
  - Early 1970s: Joe Ritchie was working for a **silver coin
    dealer in Los Angeles** exploring silver arbitrage between
    Chicago and New York markets
  - [Book explains arbitrage: silver traded in both markets;
    should theoretically be same price; temporary imbalances
    create windows; arbitrageurs buy low/sell high simultaneously
    locking virtually risk-free profits by acting very quickly]
  - Joe needed a suit to visit the exchange and borrowed Mark's
    good one (Mark only owned two, one ragged and one bought
    wholesale for $60)
  - Upon arriving at the CBOT president's office: exchange
    official slowly eyed them "from head to toe and back again"
    and said: **"You boys have got to be in the wrong place!"**
  - Watching the floor opening from the visitors' gallery:
    "The crowd in the pits flowed from the bottom up and back
    again in waves. Our mouths dropped open in astonishment.
    At that moment, both Joe and I decided that this was the
    place for us. It just looked like a barrel of fun."
  - Was attending seminary school at the time (philosophy of
    religion major), working correctional officer midnight-8am
    shift to cover rent and tuition. Took 7 years to complete
    the divinity degree.
  - Motivation for seminary: "I was mainly in seminary to
    answer some personal spiritual questions that were nagging
    me." Idealistic late-1960s/early-1970s worldview: "If you
    just did right, everything else would take care of itself."

<!-- page 392: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N282] **Ritchie's path onto the floor and
  first brush with the industry's dark side**:
  - About 6 months after CBOT visit: Joe's company sponsored
    him to start a **silver arbitrage floor operation**
  - Mark got a job as **phone clerk** on the floor — held for
    over a year until silver volatility declined sharply from
    the hectic 1973-74 levels and arbitrage opportunities dried
    up (or became too small to justify non-member clearing rates)
  - Company let him go; then found a job marketing **commodity
    options to the public** — which turned out to be a
    **boiler room operation**

<!-- page 393: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N283] **The commodity options boiler room —
  details and Ritchie's one-day tenure**:
  - Pre-exchange-traded options era: company was buying
    options in **London** and reselling to U.S. public at
    approximately **100% markup** ($2,000 buy → $4,000 sell)
  - Training program taught scripted cold-calling sales:
    "Hello Mr. so-and-so. We understand that you are a
    wealthy investor… You are obviously the type of intelligent
    person who can appreciate this kind of opportunity."
  - Ritchie in training: asked for the **track record** —
    trainer reacted with suspicion: "Why would you want to
    know about all that stuff?"
  - Trainer's stated goal: "The point is to sign the guy up"
  - "Specials": sometimes offered a $4,000 option at $3,000
    — likely an option bought at $2,000 that was running out
    of time and about to expire worthless; dumping it on
    new clients at a "discount"

<!-- page 394: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N284] **Ritchie's test for market legitimacy:
  ask for the other-side quote**:
  - Track record claim: "62 percent of our clients make money"
    — Ritchie suspected this meant 62% had made a profit *on
    a trade at some point*, not that 62% were net profitable
  - Decisive test: asked the instructor "I'm selling an option
    for $4,000. At what price can the buyer sell it back?"
  - "You can always tell very quickly whether a market is
    legitimate by simply asking for a quote on the other side."
  - Instructor evaded repeatedly; final response was essentially
    telling Ritchie to stop asking (analogized to asking a
    furniture store what the store paid for its furniture)
  - Other trainees didn't understand — one had already signed
    up after seeing a company salesman drive up in a **Corvette**
  - Ritchie left after making "a few half-hearted calls" on day
    one — "it had a feel of scam written all over it"

<!-- page 395: 2 nuggets -->

### Self-Deception and Dishonesty

- [SHOULD KNOW][N285] **Ritchie on how dishonesty begins
  with self-deception**:
  - "In fact, a lot of dishonesty in this business begins when
    people are dishonest with themselves."
  - "I believe the majority of crooks have told themselves
    enough lies that they begin to believe them. For example,
    the floor brokers indicted in the FBI sting… generally
    said, 'Hey, I wasn't doing anything anybody else wasn't
    doing.' It's not true, but I think they believe it."

### Memorable Trades

- [SHOULD KNOW][N286] **Falklands War trade — floor traders
  are the LAST to know**:
  - Common misconception: floor traders are the first to know
    what's driving the market
  - Reality: "Nothing could be further from the truth. The
    market erupts long before we ever get the news. **We're
    the last to hear what's driving the market.**"
  - On the day the Falklands War broke out: Ritchie had taken
    a large soybean meal position at what looked like a great
    price. By the time he got out, in approximately **one
    minute, he had lost $100,000**
  - Mechanism: market moved instantly on information that had
    already propagated to off-floor participants

<!-- page 396: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N287] **Gold 1979-80 trade — hesitation,
  disaster entry, right call, painful drawdown**:
  - Gold at ~**$400** when Iran took the hostages; Ritchie
    believed heightened tensions would push gold much higher
  - Market responded sluggishly → Ritchie hesitated (classic
    failure to act on conviction): "This was a classic example
    of not doing what you know should be done."
  - Gold went to ~$500 over the next month; Ritchie eventually
    bought at **just under $500**
  - Day he bought: gold went **locked limit-down**
  - Reason he held: Iran hostage situation completely
    unresolved; Soviet Union had invaded Afghanistan; still
    believed market would go higher. And it did go sharply
    higher.

<!-- page 397: no content (continuation of same trade into p.398) -->

<!-- page 398: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N288] **Open equity vs. closed equity — Ritchie's
  framework for surviving big moves**:
  - Exit plan on gold: get out whenever the market dropped
    **10% from its high**
  - When the market finally turned: it lost **25% of its value
    in one day** — deeply painful, but Ritchie still ended
    with a large profit on the trade
  - Key insight: "Most people don't distinguish between
    drawdowns in open equity and drawdowns in closed equity."
  - **Open equity** = unrealized profits on an existing
    position (paper gains)
  - **Closed equity** = realized profits/losses
  - "If I protected open equity [i.e., open profits] with the
    same care I protected closed equity, I would never be able
    to participate in a long-term move. Any sensible overall
    risk control measure could not withstand the normal
    volatility in such a move."
  - Ritchie: "I can't see any other way. If you get too careful
    about not risking your gains, you're not going to be able
    to extract a large profit."
  - Practical implication: use wider stops (based on % from
    high, not absolute loss) for trades where you're riding
    open profits; tighter stops for fresh entries

<!-- page 399: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N289] **Ritchie's 0.5% risk per trade rule and
  "trade too small" philosophy**:
  - Risk per trade (measured from trade initiation, NOT
    from peak equity): approximately **one-half of 1 percent**
  - "I think it's generally a good idea that when you put on a
    trade, it should be so small that it seems almost a waste
    of your time."
  - **"Always trade at a level that seems too small."**
  - Mechanism: sizing that feels too small prevents the
    emotional reactivity (greed buzz, loss depression) that
    distorts decision-making

<!-- page 400: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N290] **Ritchie's floor career consistency
  and why he left**:
  - Spent approximately the **first ten years of his trading
    career on the floor** — virtually **every month was
    profitable**
  - **90% of his weeks were profitable** during that period
  - Reasons for leaving despite that edge:
    1. Physical: "old age" (the floor is physically demanding)
    2. Reduced opportunity: soybean market had lost much of
       its volatility, reducing trading opportunities
  - Implication: even a 90% weekly win rate edge doesn't last
    forever — market structure changes eliminate edges;
    adaptability is essential

<!-- pp. 399-420 -->

<!-- page 399: covered in previous batch — continuity overlap only -->
<!-- page 400: covered in previous batch — continuity overlap only -->

<!-- page 401: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N291] **Perpetual contract data: a hidden trap for system developers**:
  - When Ritchie transitioned off the floor, he purchased
    price data marketed as a **"perpetual" contract** —
    a series interpolated between the nearest two actual
    futures contracts to always be a constant time forward
    (e.g., 90 days out)
  - **The problem**: the perpetual series can show large
    price moves implying profits **you could not have
    realized in the real market** — it's a theoretical
    hybrid of two different contracts, not a tradeable
    instrument
  - Ritchie used this data for **over six months** before
    discovering the flaw: "I almost fell off my chair.
    I couldn't understand how anyone who had ever traded
    anything could have constructed this type of series."
  - Key lesson: "I never again trusted anyone else's work"
  - Implication: system developers routinely backtest on
    data that doesn't reflect real-world execution — a
    fundamental and widespread error

- [SHOULD KNOW][N292] **Advisory services and the reliability
  of industry "pros"**:
  - Ritchie found advisory services "often not worth the
    time it took to listen to the phone tape"
  - His conclusion: the unreliability of information from
    so-called industry professionals is striking
  - Drove him to develop his own systems and trust only
    his own research

<!-- page 402: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N293] **Optimization/curve-fitting: the fatal flaw
  in commercial trading systems**:
  - Ritchie bought a commercial simulation package —
    assumed that optimizing systems across historical data
    would be "a thousand times more effective than charts"
  - What he found: the software **"allowed you to optimize
    the market to death"** — the vendor even recommended
    reoptimizing every week
  - Definition: **optimization** = testing many parameter
    combinations and picking the one that worked best for
    past history → **wildly overinflates implied future
    performance**
  - Weekly reoptimization = "curve-fit the program to last
    week so the trades this week will match what should
    have been done the previous week"
  - Red flag: the company salesman said he had a friend
    photocopy the *Wall Street Journal* price page for
    him because he couldn't afford to buy it — "Here's a
    guy marketing a program as the premiere trading system
    software and he doesn't even have enough money to buy
    the *Wall Street Journal*"
  - Outcome: results were "spasmodic"; Ritchie swore never
    to purchase a **black box** system again
  - Black box defined: computer software that generates
    buy/sell signals without revealing how they are
    generated

<!-- page 403: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N294] **Ritchie's core advice: don't trade unless
  you genuinely know what you're doing**:
  > "My advice to people has always been: Stay out of the
  > business; stay completely away from the market. For
  > novices to come in and try to generate profit in this
  > incredibly complex industry is like me trying to do
  > brain surgery on the weekends to pick up a little
  > extra cash."
  - Illustrative anecdote: three doctors bought a stud
    race horse, discovered upon delivery it was a gelding
    — Ritchie's point is that even if they'd caught the
    obvious thing they should have checked, **they would
    still make the next laughable mistake** because the
    underlying knowledge gap remains
  - "DON'T INVEST WHERE YOU DON'T KNOW WHAT YOU'RE DOING"
  - The lesson is NOT "inspect the horse next time" — it's
    that amateur investors will always find a new way to
    make an equivalent error in their domain of ignorance

<!-- page 404: 1 nugget -->

<!-- page 405: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N295] **Willingness to lose as prerequisite to
  winning**:
  - "I don't think you can make money unless you're willing
    to lose it. Unless you have money that you can afford
    to lose and still sleep at night, you don't belong in
    the market."
  - "My willingness to lose is fundamental to my ability
    to make money in the markets."
  - Most retail participants enter **without willingness
    to lose** and with **unrealistic expectations**
  - Consequence: even if they pick a good trading advisor,
    they pull money at the first drawdown → "they end up
    losing even though they may have been in a winning
    situation"

- [MUST KNOW][N296] **Schwager's empirical finding: investor
  timing destroys returns even from winning advisors**:
  - Schwager's own research: evaluated outside trading
    advisors professionally; found a handful who had made
    money **every single year**
  - Key finding: **less than 50% of their closed client
    accounts showed a net profit**
  - Mechanism: investors naturally **invest after hot
    streaks and withdraw after losing streaks** — the
    exact opposite of optimal timing
  - This is not anecdotal — it's documented across a
    select group of consistently profitable managers
  - Implication: behavioral timing errors can fully negate
    the alpha of an excellent manager

<!-- page 406: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N297] **Ritchie's advice for serious trading
  aspirants**:
  - Tells them to read Schwager's *The Complete Guide to
    the Futures Markets* — then come back after they've
    digested half of it
  - Knows most won't do it: "the book gives people a good
    idea of the amount of work it takes to become
    competent in this business"
  - This is his filter — the book's difficulty is the point

<!-- page 407: 1 nugget -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N298] **Ritchie's "investing in the poor"
  philosophy (not charity)**:
  - Distinguishes between **charity** (handouts → dependency)
    and **investing in the poor** (capital for
    self-sufficiency)
  - "Charity tends to spawn dependency. That's why the
    Great Society war on poverty was such a failure."
  - Goal: establish cottage industries that allow
    self-sufficiency; the sign of success is when someone
    can return the capital
  - Income allocation: roughly **one-third to taxes, one-
    third back into trading account, one-third to these
    projects**
  - Motivation for continuing to trade: without the
    charitable aspect he's not sure he'd still be trading

<!-- page 408: no content (Amazon Indian village detail — not trading-relevant) -->

<!-- page 409: 1 nugget -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N299] **Debunking romantic views of tribal
  life (Indian "Bee" anecdote)**:
  - Western academics extol the beauty/virtues of
    isolated indigenous culture
  - An Indian named **Bee**, shown a newspaper article
    praising his "beautiful culture," responded: "Where
    does this man live that he could be so foolish? Why
    doesn't he come down here with his family and join us?
    Then we can all enjoy this beautiful place together."
  - Ritchie: "I agree with the Indians" — the people
    actually living the life want progress; romanticizing
    poverty is a luxury of comfort
  - Schwager references Ritchie's book *Victim of
    Delusion* as documenting the "unimaginable brutality"
    of life in these societies

<!-- page 410: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N300] **Ritchie's trading frequency and
  best-ever trade**:
  - Signals a shift from long to short (or vice versa)
    between **1 and 5 times per year** per market
  - "I would prefer only one trade per year."
  - Best trade ever: held for **over four years** —
    long soybean meal / short soybean oil, kept rolling
    the position; stayed in because it kept generating
    **monthly profits**
  - Approach not sensitive to volume (why managing public
    funds made sense): the long-term nature means large
    size doesn't degrade execution meaningfully

<!-- page 411: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N301] **Mark Ritchie's 5 core trading principles
  (chapter summary)**:
  1. **Do your own research** — never trust others' data,
     systems, or analysis without independent verification
  2. **Keep position size so small it almost seems a waste
     of time** — prevents emotional interference
  3. **Have the patience to stay with a winning position
     as long as it's working**, even if that means holding
     for years
  4. **View open profit risk differently from starting-
     equity risk** — riding winners requires tolerating
     larger swings in open profits than you'd accept as
     initial risk; this is the only way to capture large
     moves
  5. **Recognize and control your greed**

