## Chapter: Joe Ritchie — The Intuitive Theoretician

<!-- page 412: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N302] **Joe Ritchie's background and CRT's
  founding character**:
  - **Joe Ritchie**: founder and driving force of **CRT
    (Chicago Research and Trading)**
  - Described by many as a math genius — a natural — though
    he has **never taken an advanced math course**; he
    describes math as something he "almost feels or
    intuitively understands"
  - Decision-making style: makes major business decisions
    based on **gut feeling about people** — has walked
    away from lucrative ventures due to discomfort with
    the people involved; conversely has initiated major
    operations "on not much more than a handshake"
  - Example: launched a computer company in the former
    Soviet Union (software, hardware import, distribution)
    based entirely on being impressed by one entrepreneurial
    Russian — asked him to write a contract he thought was
    fair, signed it on the next trip to Moscow — while
    other U.S. companies were writing 300-page legal docs
  - Key employee quote: "Joe believes in empowering people.
    He trusts people. One of the reasons we can make so
    much money is that Joe makes us feel absolutely
    comfortable to risk his money."

- [SHOULD KNOW][N303] **CRT's silver arbitrage edge: better
  pricing models + aggressive volume**:
  - When Joe Ritchie started silver arbitrage, others were
    already doing it — he differentiated by:
    1. Better understanding of **interrelationships between
       markets** and probability assessment
    2. Trading more aggressively at **narrower margins**
       than competitors
  - Same strategy repeated in options years later: used
    more accurate pricing models → could quote such tight
    bid/ask spreads that competitors assumed the markets
    were "too tight to be profitable"
  - Core principle: **"If you really have the mechanics or
    theoretical value nailed, you can do a lot more volume
    at a smaller margin"**
  - This is the low-margin, high-volume market-making
    playbook applied to arbitrage

<!-- page 413: 1 nugget -->

<!-- page 414: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N304] **CRT's speed advantage in silver
  arbitrage — phone clerk innovation**:
  - In early silver arbitrage days, many competitors did
    not even have a phone clerk — used a flashing phone
    light to signal price changes from NY to Chicago, then
    a broker would **run to the phone, get the quote, hang
    up, and run back to the pit**
  - About half the market used phone clerks but still had
    **3–10 second transmission times**
  - CRT: hired the best phone clerks, motivated them
    properly → cut transmission time to **about 2 seconds**
  - Result: CRT could do trades before the spread widened
    to the point where others would act — made CRT
    "very unpopular"; floor tried to throw them off
  - Pretext: phone clerks verbally calling orders into the
    pit violated rules (orders supposed to be written and
    carried by runner) — but this rule was standard
    operating procedure for everyone; exchange dropped
    the complaint when they realized selective enforcement
    wasn't workable
  - Lesson: **operational execution advantages** (not just
    analytical edge) can be the source of an arbitrage
    edge

<!-- page 415: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N305] **Soybean crush mechanics and CRT's
  move into soybeans**:
  - Silver arbitrage was highly profitable in **1973–74**
    due to extreme volatility; when volatility died in
    1975, the business dried up
  - **Soybean crush**: soybeans crushed into **meal** and
    **oil**; the crush trader tries to exploit the
    relationship between soybean prices and product prices
    - If soybeans cheap relative to products → buy
      soybeans, sell meal+oil (locking in processor margin)
    - If soybeans expensive relative to products → reverse
    - Economic forces keep the relationship broadly stable
      over time via crushing plant activity
  - CRT's edge in crush: same formula as silver —
    better clerks, higher pay, and **crude slide rules**
    showing implied soybean price given different meal/oil
    price combinations → could **instantaneously calculate
    market value** and act faster than competitors

<!-- page 416: 1 nugget -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N306] **CRT's asymmetric 50/50 deal (heads
  win fifty, tails lose one hundred)**:
  - When Ritchie convinced his employer to switch from
    silver to soybean crush, the deal was: Ritchie
    responsible for **all losses**, splits profits 50/50
  - Schwager's characterization: "heads you win fifty,
    tails you lose one hundred — not a very good deal"
  - Ritchie stayed out of "inertia and loyalty" for giving
    him his start; eventually went on his own
  - Initial firm name: **Chicago Board Crushers** →
    changed to **Chicago Research and Trading** after
    secretary tired of explaining they couldn't crush
    people's boards

<!-- page 417: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N307] **CRT's core organizational principle:
  follow the opportunity**:
  - From the beginning, CRT's guiding principle was
    **"not being tied to any one business but rather
    moving to the markets where something interesting
    was going on"**
  - Applied consistently: silver → soybean crush → back
    to silver in 1979–80 → options
  - In the 1979–80 silver market CRT traded
    "almost exclusively arbitrage" — but one directional
    trade emerged from intelligence gathering (see below)

<!-- page 418: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N308] **Joe Ritchie's 1979 silver calls trade:
  "better to be dumb and make a profit than smart and
  take a loss"**:
  - In early 1979, a mystery buyer purchased **20,000
    contracts of silver** — Ritchie did intelligence work
    tracing the buyer to a Pakistani connected to Saudi
    money management
  - Bought a **"ridiculously large"** position in
    out-of-the-money silver calls in London at very low
    prices (illiquid market) — took a position much larger
    than appropriate relative to equity base
  - **The actual buyer**: had no connection to Saudis —
    was someone who had passed a **$20 million bad check**
    to a Dallas brokerage; when caught, his position was
    liquidated and silver dropped under $6
  - Ritchie couldn't exit because the position was so
    illiquid — "essentially married to the position"
  - Rescue: went on vacation; the **Hunt buying** began
    and pushed silver sharply higher while he was away
  - "I'm sure that if I were around, I would have gotten
    out at the first opportunity of breaking even"
  - Eventually sold too early at $7.25 (locked in some
    profit against calls), liquidated entire position —
    silver went to ~$8.50 by expiry; then Hunt squeeze
    took it to $50
  - Core lesson: **his intelligence was completely wrong,
    his position sizing was reckless** — and he still
    made money due to luck (vacation + Hunt). Humbling
    counterexample to the idea that analytical work always
    drives results

<!-- page 419: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N309] **How professional silver traders
  handled the 1979–80 bull market: nobody got out at
  the top**:
  - Silver went from ~$5/oz to **$50/oz** in just over
    a year — one of the greatest commodity bull markets
    ever
  - Ritchie: "$10 per ounce seemed extremely far-fetched"
    at the beginning
  - Pattern of traders who entered at $3, $4, $5, $6:
    "Either, by the time silver got up to $7, $8, or $9
    they got out, or they rode the position all the way
    up and all the way down"
  - "I don't know anybody who bought silver at relatively
    low prices and got out at over $20"
  - Traders who **shorted at $9 or $10** (because price
    "seemed so ridiculously high") rode the position
    until they had **lost their entire net worth** — "that
    happened to some of the best professionals I knew in
    the silver market"
  - Key insight: **extreme moves feel absurd at every
    stage** — both longs who exit too early and shorts
    who enter too early systematically misread the scale
    of what markets can do

- [SHOULD KNOW][N310] **The Hunt silver squeeze: exchanges
  were right to intervene**:
  - The Hunt brothers stood for delivery of April silver
    while forward contracts traded at **huge discounts**
  - Economically rational alternatives the Hunts ignored:
    1. Roll into discounted forward months (save money
       AND free up capital)
    2. Buy silver coins in the free market at **$35/oz**
       when the April futures were at **$50/oz**
  - Refusing economically sensible alternatives and
    insisting on April delivery signals **market
    manipulation, not investment**
  - Ritchie: "That's not what these markets are here for.
    I feel that the Hunts got exactly what they had
    coming to them."
  - **Collateral damage example**: a Peruvian mine with
    $5/oz production cost hedged two years of production
    at $15 (made economic sense) → as silver rose to
    $20, $25, $30, $35 they had to keep posting variation
    margin on their short → eventually **ran out of money
    and went broke** despite having a legitimate hedge
  - The Hunt manipulation destroyed innocent commercial
    hedgers even as it rewarded speculators

<!-- page 420: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N311] **Joe Ritchie on directional trading
  alongside arbitrage**:
  - CRT's core business is **option arbitrage**, but
    Ritchie has always believed technical trading works
    and has periodically used it for his own account
  - Note: this begins a new section of the interview —
    content continues beyond this batch

<!-- pp. 419-440 -->

## Chapter: Joe Ritchie (CRT) — continued

### Silver Vacation Trade & Premature Exit

<!-- page 419: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N291] **Absence as accidental discipline — the silver vacation
  trade**:
  - Ritchie had bought silver calls, then went on vacation
  - While away, the **Hunt buying** pushed silver sharply
    higher — if he had been around, he says he would have
    exited "at the first opportunity of breaking even"
  - Returned to find calls deeply in the money
  - Even then, couldn't stand the volatility — went to the
    silver pit "just to get a feel," promised himself hands
    in pockets; silver was at $7.25; ended up selling 25
    contracts against his calls "just to lock in some
    profits" — and accidentally liquidated his entire
    position
  - Silver ultimately went to ~$8.50 by expiration
  - Lesson: the emotional need to "lock in gains" is often
    the mechanism that kills otherwise winning trades;
    physical absence removed the stimulus and let the trade
    work

- [MUST KNOW][N292] **The 1979-80 silver bull market: almost
  no one extracted the full move**:
  - Silver soared from ~$5 to ~$50/oz in ~14 months
  - Ritchie: "I don't know anybody who bought silver at
    relatively low prices and got out at over $20"
  - Traders who bought at $3-$6 did one of two things:
    1. Got out at $7-$9 (left 80-90% of the move on the
       table), OR
    2. Rode it all the way up AND all the way back down
  - Even the best floor professionals who went **short at
    $9-$10** (thinking the price was "ridiculously high")
    lost their entire net worth riding the short into the
    $50 top
  - Key insight: human intuition about "too high/too low"
    is nearly useless in true bubble markets; both the
    timid exits and the contrarian shorts were wrong

### The Hunt Silver Squeeze

<!-- page 420: 1 nugget -->
<!-- page 421: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N293] **Why the exchanges were RIGHT to
  intervene in the Hunt silver squeeze**:
  - Ritchie argues exchanges had full legal authority to
    spread delivery or allow liquidation-only trading
  - Key evidence that Hunts were playing games, NOT
    genuine economic need:
    - Forward contracts were trading at **huge discounts**
      to spot; rational owners wanting silver ownership
      could have rolled April contracts to cheaper forward
      months, saving enormous capital
    - Silver coins available in free market at **$35/oz**
      while April futures were at **$50** — genuine owners
      would have bought the physical at a $15 discount
    - Instead, Hunts insisted on April delivery at full
      price: "No, no, no, we want to take delivery of the
      silver in April" — this signals manipulation, not
      investment
  - "So I feel that the Hunts got exactly what they had
    coming to them"

- [MUST KNOW][N294] **Collateral damage from the Hunt corner:
  innocent hedgers destroyed**:
  - A Peruvian silver mine with production cost under
    $5/oz rationally hedged **two years of forward
    production** when price hit $15 — locking in a
    massive profit at that level
  - As Hunt buying pushed price to $20, $25, $30, $35+,
    the mine had to post ever-larger **variation margin**
    on its short futures position
  - Eventually ran out of cash and was **forced to
    liquidate the hedge** — went broke in the process
  - The mine did everything right economically; the
    manipulation by the Hunts made their rational hedge
    into a death sentence
  - Implication for traders: extreme market dislocations
    driven by non-economic actors can destroy even
    properly-hedged positions; size and liquidity of
    counterparty matters

### CRT's Directional Technical Trading System

<!-- page 422: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N295] **CRT's in-house technical trading
  system: 40% avg annual gains, almost killed by position
  sizing**:
  - Ritchie believed in technical trading but didn't want
    it distracting him — turned the ideas over to CRT
    staff who had both the interest and technical skills
  - System ran for **5 years**: profitable in **4 of 5**,
    average annual gain of **~40%** over the period
  - The one losing year: system was "really smoking"
    halfway through; they **tripled position size rapidly**
    — "that was the only time we increased trading size
    rapidly"
  - If position size had been held constant, that year
    would have been a winner
  - Classic lesson: expanding size after a winning streak
    at exactly the wrong moment (when mean reversion
    begins) is the most common way to turn a winning
    system into a losing year

- [SHOULD KNOW][N296] **Human judgment in the technical
  system: "unbelievably detrimental"**:
  - Early on (~6-month period), human judgment was
    permitted to override the system
  - Result: "Unbelievably detrimental"
  - Schwager notes this is confirmed across virtually
    every mechanical system trader he has interviewed

### Fundamental vs. Technical Analysis — The Richard Dennis Story

<!-- page 423: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N297] **Richard Dennis on fundamental vs.
  technical information — and his disarming humility**:
  - Late 1970s seminar: Ritchie sat with **Richard Dennis**
    at lunch; asked him what percentage of his trading was
    technical vs. fundamental
  - Dennis: **"I use zero percent fundamental information"**
    — answered with scorn
  - Ritchie: "How do you escape the argument that all
    fundamental information is already in the market?"
  - Ritchie: "How do you escape the argument that all
    technical information is already in the market?"
  - Dennis: **"I never thought of that."**
  - Ritchie: "I admired him for that. He had a humility
    about him that I think explains a lot of his success."
  - Key takeaway: Dennis's greatness wasn't that he had
    the perfect answer — it was that he was genuinely
    open to a question he hadn't considered

- [SHOULD KNOW][N298] **Ritchie's resolution of the
  fundamental/technical debate**:
  - Both camps use publicly available information; the
    edge comes from who uses it BETTER, not from having
    exclusive data
  - "Just because all the information is in the market
    doesn't mean that one trader can't use it better than
    the next guy"
  - Applies equally to fundamentals AND technicals
  - Implication: the EMH argument that "all information
    is priced in" doesn't mean all traders extract equal
    value from that information

### CRT's Options Business — Origins and Edge

<!-- page 424: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N299] **Joe Ritchie: intuitive math without
  formal training — philosophy major running world's
  largest options firm**:
  - **CRT** (*Chicago Research and Trading*) described as
    the world's largest options trader at the time of
    the interview
  - Ritchie: **"I have never had a course in math beyond
    high school algebra"**
  - "I feel math in an intuitive way that many quants
    don't seem to. When I think about pricing an option,
    I may not know calculus, but in my mind I can draw a
    picture of how you would price an option that looks
    exactly like the theoretical pricing models in the
    textbooks"
  - First got seriously involved in futures options when
    that market opened; in 1975 hand-programmed the
    **Black-Scholes formula** into a **TI-52 hand-held
    calculator** — gave one option price in ~13 seconds
    after manually entering all variables
  - "It was pretty crude, but in the land of the blind,
    I was the guy with one eye"

- [SHOULD KNOW][N300] **CRT's early edge: embryonic option
  markets with extreme mispricings**:
  - First day in the **T-bond futures options** pit (market
    only a few months old): gave a **50-point bid/ask
    spread on a 100-lot order** — apologized, said it was
    his first day and he didn't normally trade back months
  - The other trader's jaw dropped: couldn't believe anyone
    was making that TIGHT a market
  - Spreads were actually far wider than 50 ticks at the
    time — Ritchie's spread was considered remarkably
    narrow by the standard of the day
  - "In the land of the blind, I was the guy with one eye"
    (repeats from above — consistent theme)

<!-- page 425: 1 nugget (subsumed above) -->

### Option Arbitrage Mechanics and Market Efficiency

<!-- page 426: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N301] **How CRT hedged early option
  positions**:
  - First hedge: take opposite position in the **outright
    (futures) market** — much broader volume, tighter
    spreads than the option itself
  - Then "whittle down" the option-specific risk by
    becoming the best bidder for offsetting option
    positions in the pit
  - Example: sold a call → hedge in futures → then
    actively seek to buy other calls to reduce net
    options exposure
  - The strategy exploits the fact that the futures
    market for hedging was much more liquid than the
    options market itself in the early years

- [MUST KNOW][N302] **Why mispricings ALWAYS exist in
  options markets — the market-making argument**:
  - "The notion that the market will trade at its precise
    theoretical fair value implies that someone will hold
    it there without getting paid. Why should anyone do
    that?"
  - Market making is labor with risk — like waiting
    tables; someone must be paid to do it
  - The **bid/ask spread IS the payment** — without it,
    no one would take the other side of large orders
  - Implication: the option market will NEVER be perfectly
    efficient because perfect efficiency would eliminate
    the compensation that makes market making viable
  - Even as competition increased, margins merely
    compressed — they can never go to zero

<!-- page 427: 1 nugget (subsumed above) -->

### Black-Scholes Flaws and CRT's Theoretical Edge

<!-- page 428: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N303] **Black-Scholes flaw: fat tails —
  and how CRT profited from it**:
  - Standard **Black-Scholes model** assumes log-normal
    price distribution — **underweights probability of
    extreme moves** (thin tails vs. reality)
  - Actual price distributions have **fatter tails** than
    the normal curve implies
  - Result: standard models underprice
    **out-of-the-money options** (which pay off in extreme
    moves)
  - Early on, even CRT's biggest competitors hadn't
    figured this out: "a lot of our profits came through
    that crack"
  - By the time of the interview, all serious players had
    figured it out and most commercially available models
    incorporated fat tails
  - But knowing tails are fat doesn't solve the problem —
    you still need to know HOW fat, which distribution
    to use, whether to account for hedging strategy
    changes, whether additional variables matter, and
    whether adding them makes the model "unwieldy"
  - "Knowing that the distribution isn't log-normal only
    opens a can of worms"

- [SHOULD KNOW][N304] **The danger of trusting mathematical
  models over market reality**:
  - Ritchie: "I think that probably explains a lot of it,
    but it presupposes that reality matches the curve in
    a mathematician's head. **Believing that can get
    expensive.**"
  - The academic appeal of tractable mathematical
    distributions (normal curve) led professionals to
    ignore empirical reality for years
  - This is a meta-lesson about all quantitative models:
    elegance in math ≠ accuracy in markets

<!-- page 429: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N305] **CRT's pricing models are price-
  neutral — directional bets kept separate**:
  - There was a market rumor that CRT secretly made money
    on direction while claiming to be delta-neutral;
    Ritchie confirms this was NOT true but they didn't
    correct it (it made counterparties more willing to
    trade with them)
  - CRT's option pricing models are **genuinely price
    neutral** — they assume unchanged price as the base
  - Ritchie's principle: if you have a directional view,
    set up a **separate account** to express it — keep
    options arbitrage P&L and directional P&L cleanly
    segregated so you can measure each

- [MUST KNOW][N306] **Implied volatility beats historical
  volatility as a predictor — conceptually obvious**:
  - **Historical volatility**: past realized price
    movement — tells you what happened
  - **Implied volatility**: the volatility assumption
    embedded in current market option prices — tells
    you what the market's collective judgment is about
    future volatility
  - Ritchie: "Implied volatility seems better to me"
  - Why conceptually obvious: "The implied volatility is a
    statement of what all the players in the market,
    having cast their votes, believe is a fair price for
    future volatility. Historical volatility is nothing
    more than a number representing past volatility."
  - Implication: using historical vol to identify
    "overpriced" or "underpriced" options may be
    completely misleading if implied vol is the better
    forecast — the option may be priced "above historical
    vol fair value" precisely because the market correctly
    anticipates higher future vol

<!-- page 430: 1 nugget (subsumed above) -->

### Relative Value vs. Absolute Value in Options

<!-- page 431: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N307] **CRT never compared options to
  historical vol — only to other options (relative value)**:
  - Even in the early inefficient days, CRT never assumed
    historical volatility was the true benchmark
  - Their method: make a bid/ask spread where you believe
    you can find another trade to "lay off the volatility
    risk and still leave a profit margin"
  - They were making judgments about whether **one option
    was mispriced relative to another option** — not
    whether any option was mispriced vs. the underlying
  - This is pure **relative value arbitrage**: exploit
    inconsistencies within the options surface, not
    absolute price level vs. a model

- [SHOULD KNOW][N308] **Why option pricing models still
  matter even if implied vol is the better benchmark**:
  - The model is needed to determine **relative values**
    between options — which is over/underpriced vs. which
    other options
  - Two models that disagree about relative values → their
    users will trade with each other → one will be right
    and one wrong
  - The model doesn't need to correctly identify absolute
    cheap/expensive — it needs to correctly rank relative
    value among options to give a trading edge

### CRT's Organizational Philosophy and People Edge

<!-- page 432: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N309] **CRT's true competitive advantage:
  people and team culture, not models**:
  - Ritchie: "The models are important, but the **critical
    element is the people**. To make a company this size
    work like a clock takes extremely unusual people."
  - Other firms: hired for technical competence
    (education, experience)
  - CRT: hired for technical competence **AND** how well
    the person interrelates with the work group — equal
    or greater emphasis on the latter
  - CRT employees (Gene Frost, Gus Pellizzi, Niel
    Nielson) join the interview and confirm: "just trying
    [to hire for culture] seems to have put us ahead"

- [MUST KNOW][N310] **CRT's hiring philosophy: "character,
  character, and character"**:
  - Example: hired a **world champion go player** —
    brilliant, "like putting a human computer in the pit"
  - One day he made a bad trade, panicked, couldn't bring
    himself to cover and admit the mistake; waited until
    end of day; cost CRT **$100,000**
  - Gene Frost: "Whether this guy cracked because of some
    insecurity in not wanting to look bad, or because
    of some other reason, in the end it comes down to a
    **character flaw**"
  - Joe Ritchie: "I would rather have a good trader I
    could trust than a brilliant trader I couldn't"
  - CRT hiring formula: **"character, character, and
    character"**
  - What they look for: people who "take as much pleasure
    in the success of their group as they do in their
    individual success" — not self-sacrifice, but genuine
    enjoyment of collective wins
  - "When you put together people who are not worried
    about whether they, individually, are getting enough
    credit, you have a tremendous advantage over the
    competition"

<!-- page 433: 1 nugget (subsumed above) -->

### Evaluating People and Trader Traits

<!-- page 434: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N311] **Self-evaluation works better than
  external monitoring — at CRT**:
  - Ritchie's method for performance reviews: after an
    hour of hearing an employee describe activities and
    priorities, he simply asks: **"How do you think you
    have been doing? How would you rate yourself? Not how
    busy have you been. Not how hard have you worked. But
    how have you really been doing?"**
  - Employee in the example said: "Well, I think I should
    have been fired" — then gave a complete, honest
    evaluation including shortcomings
  - Ritchie: "He knows how he's doing, better than if I
    were looking over his shoulder, and he really wants
    to do the right thing"
  - Principle: self-centered people resist self-evaluation;
    team-oriented people tend to be their own toughest
    critics, making oversight easier and more accurate

<!-- page 435: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N312] **The most analytical people tend to
  be the worst traders**:
  - Joe Ritchie: "Successful traders tend to be instinctive
    rather than overly analytical"
  - "The most analytical people tend to be the worst
    traders" — analytical thinking **masks** intuitive
    traits and abilities that good trading requires
  - Second key trait of successful traders: **humility —
    the ability to admit when they're wrong**
  - These two traits (instinct + humility) echo across
    virtually every Market Wizard interview

- [SHOULD KNOW][N313] **Margin compression handled by
  volume increase — the efficient market equilibrium**:
  - As options markets became more competitive, CRT's
    margins per trade fell significantly
  - Response: volume increased proportionally
  - Ritchie's economic logic: "The margin can't shrink so
    much that an efficient person in that business can't
    put bread on the table. Therefore, if you can be the
    most efficient, there should always be a profit
    margin."
  - Implication: in any competitive market, being the
    lowest-cost and most efficient operator is a durable
    moat even as margins compress toward zero for
    everyone else

### CRT's Organizational Secret

<!-- page 436: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N314] **CRT's organizational secret: people
  working for each other, not for individual credit**:
  - Ritchie: "At CRT we believe in the philosophy that
    people work best when they work **for each other**"
  - "People think that CRT's success is due to some secret
    computer model. However, I believe that CRT has
    succeeded because we build teams."
  - The formula: give people real authority + pay them
    what they're worth + trust them
  - "I'm willing to tell people because (A) they won't
    believe me and (B) even if they did believe me, they
    couldn't train themselves to do the same thing — to
    trust people and to give up absolute control"
  - Other firms approach options trading "too
    mechanically" — they believe getting the formula right
    solves everything; it doesn't
  - "To build a machine that uses many different brains
    that are qualitatively contributing different things
    is an art. Most people don't want to do it that way."
  - The typical organizational failure: someone wants to
    believe it's HIS thinking making things run; won't
    share credit or responsibility

- [NICE TO KNOW][N315] **Schwager's conclusion on Joe
  Ritchie**:
  - "Joe Ritchie provides living proof that creative
    thinking can be more powerful than complex analysis"
  - No formal math training, yet developed an options
    pricing model that, judging by CRT's performance,
    "must be better than all the academically derived
    models commonly in use"
  - The primary lessons from this interview are
    **managerial**, not trading-related: share
    responsibility and profits; this policy "makes so
    much common sense that you wonder why more companies
    don't use it"

