## Chapter: Blair Hull — Getting the Edge

<!-- page 437: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N316] **Blair Hull's background: blackjack
  to options — the throughline of probability and edge**:
  - **Blair Hull** founded **Hull Trading Company (HTC)**
    — a major option market-making firm
  - Came to trading through blackjack — "This is not as
    strange as it may sound, since there are actually very
    strong parallels between the two activities"
  - The parallel: consistent winning in both blackjack
    and trading requires **strategy and discipline, not
    luck** — luck is only relevant short-term and its
    impact must be neutralized by money management
  - After casinos identified and banned his blackjack
    team, Hull sought another avenue for applying
    probability theory; found option mispricings matched
    the same general principles

<!-- page 438: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N317] **Hull Trading Company's performance
  record**:
  - Hull started with **$25,000 in late 1976** → multiplied
    it **20x by start of 1979** (~2.3 years)
  - Subsequent years: averaged **~100% per year**
    (excluding sabbatical years)
  - **HTC launched 1985** — starting stake of **$1 million**
    → **$90 million by mid-1991** (after expenses); gross
    trading profits were ~$137 million over that period
  - Profitable in **58 of 69 months** (after expenses);
    only **5 months** had net trading losses (before
    expense allocations)
  - Scale: ~100 employees by mid-1991; market maker on
    CBOE, CME, AMEX, NYSE, and foreign exchanges;
    accounts for **>10% of total trading volume** in
    several options markets
  - Real-time monitoring with **2-second lag** for all
    positions; strategies continuously revised for price
    and position changes

- [SHOULD KNOW][N318] **Blair Hull's early career path and
  why he rejected fundamental analysis**:
  - Background: business school at **Santa Clara University**;
    hired as security analyst at **Blair and Company** —
    fired after 3 months when West Coast research dept.
    was eliminated in the 1969 bear market
  - Learned about **Graham and Dodd** and fundamental
    analysis; concluded it was "too subjective — it
    couldn't be quantified or systematized. So I didn't
    want to have anything to do with it."
  - Preference for quantifiable, systematic approaches
    predates his blackjack and options career — it's a
    deep personal trait, not a learned lesson

### Hull's Blackjack Years (1971-1975)

<!-- page 439: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N319] **How Ed Thorp's *Beat the Dealer*
  launched Blair Hull's trading career**:
  - While working in operations research for **Kaiser
    Cement**, Hull read ***Beat the Dealer*** by
    **Ed Thorp** — became fascinated with blackjack
    card counting
  - From **1971 to 1975**, spent ~5 days per month in
    Nevada casinos while living in California
  - Hull: "In a sense, I owe everything that I have to
    the state of Nevada. It not only provided me with my
    original trading stake, but the betting experience
    taught me a lot of things that allowed me to become
    a successful trader."
  - Key transferable lessons from blackjack: thinking in
    probabilities, managing bankroll, only betting when
    the edge is favorable, patience waiting for
    advantageous conditions

- [SHOULD KNOW][N320] **The blackjack edge mechanics —
  card counting and back-counting**:
  - Basic blackjack: house has a small edge
  - When deck is rich in **tens and aces** (many small
    cards already dealt), the odds shift to the bettor
    by ~1-2%
  - Hull's method: **back-counting** — stand back from the
    table, count cards, only sit down and bet when the
    count favored him; bet **$1-$4** range to avoid
    casino detection
  - Placed roughly **5 bets per hour** using this selective
    approach (vs. continuous betting at the table)
  - Allows concentration of bets only when edge exists —
    the same principle that drives option arbitrage: only
    trade when the mispricing exceeds your costs

<!-- page 440: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N321] **The sample size lesson from Hull's
  first blackjack attempt**:
  - First attempt: made ~**50 bets** — ended with a net
    loss
  - Initial reaction: doubt about the system
  - Key insight Schwager surfaces: 50 bets was "too small
    a number and still left the odds of winning too close
    to even"
  - Hull then calculated how many bets it would take to
    be confident he'd be ahead over the long run
  - This is the statistical law of large numbers applied
    to trading: a positive-expectancy strategy can easily
    lose over small sample sizes; you need enough
    repetitions for the edge to manifest
  - Started with a **$120 base**, betting $1-$4 per hand;
    after **two years** was ahead ~**$10,000**
  - Implication: a strategy that looks broken after 50
    trials may be working perfectly — the failure is in
    expecting results too soon, not in the strategy

- [NICE TO KNOW][N322] **Hull's blackjack team
  recruitment — comedic failure**:
  - Tried to form his own blackjack team; one candidate
    didn't know which Sahara to show up at — went to
    **Sahara in Lake Tahoe** while the team was in Las
    Vegas; entire weekend passed before they figured out
    where he was
  - Eventually connected with a professional, secretive
    blackjack team whose organizer contacted Hull about
    a year after a mutual friend was killed in a car
    accident
  - The team approach was sought for capital pooling and
    risk diversification — same logic as hedge fund
    structures in trading

<!-- pp. 459-480 -->

## Chapter: Blair Hull (continued) — conclusion

### Gulf War Directional Trade

<!-- page 459: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N323] **Hull's Gulf War S&P trade — uncertainty-resolution
  thesis**:
  - Date: morning of **January 15, 1991** — day of Bush's midnight
    ultimatum deadline to Hussein before Desert Storm
  - Consensus view: market would fall 150 points if war started
  - Hull's thesis: uncertainty had to diminish regardless of outcome;
    therefore he had to be long
  - Strategy: put on **half the position** before the deadline,
    **other half after** war started (to manage the fear/uncertainty)
  - He never added the second half because the market opened
    **sharply higher** — no dip to buy into
  - Result: ~**$4 million in about thirty minutes** on the S&P trade
  - His head trader booked it in the **LTG account** as a joke —
    Hull had earlier criticized someone for taking a net directional
    position, saying "What do you think, you have a line to God?"
  - Lesson: the trade was about **resolving uncertainty**, not
    predicting direction with precision; the thesis was that
    uncertainty itself was priced in, and any resolution would
    be positive

- [SHOULD KNOW][N324] **Directional trades = only ~5% of Hull's
  profits**:
  - The Gulf War S&P trade was spectacular but exceptional
  - Directional trades account for **only about 5 percent** of the
    firm's total trading profits
  - Core methodology remains: buy undervalued, sell overvalued
    securities (the blackjack edge philosophy applied at scale)
  - "If you have the edge, in the long run, you'll make more money
    by doing a lot of transactions"

### Market Evolution: Speed and Intermarket Arbitrage

<!-- page 460: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N325] **The arms race in arbitrage — "unless you're
  running very fast, they're going to catch you"**:
  - Strategies have become more complex; **speed** has become
    much more important
  - Easy single-market mispricings have disappeared —
    intramarket plays now have very small return on capital
  - Hull: "This is always a horse race, and unless you're running
    very fast, they're going to catch you"
  - **Intermarket trades** (across less-correlated markets) are
    now required to generate meaningful returns
  - Key driver: institutions need to alter their risk profile;
    Hull/firms take the other side — the institution is the
    ultimate source of the edge

- [SHOULD KNOW][N326] **Hull's "less obvious offsets" strategy —
  competitive advantage from being slow on the floor**:
  - Hull was one of the **slowest floor traders** — others always
    got to primary markets first
  - Forced him to look for hedges in **less correlated markets**
  - Example: OEX options are based on S&P 100 (highly correlated
    with S&P 500 but less so with NYFE and MMI); when OEX
    traders were sellers and hit the S&P 500, Hull would hedge
    in **NYFE** [New York Futures Exchange] and **MMI** instead
  - His slowness as a floor trader became an institutional
    competitive advantage — he learned to find less-contested
    offset markets
  - Now automated, but the firm still looks for "less obvious
    markets in which to offset trades"

### Zero Delta Portfolio and Air Traffic Control

<!-- page 461: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N327] **Zero delta portfolio with 2-second rebalancing
  — air traffic controller model**:
  - Hull tries to maintain a **zero delta portfolio** at all times
    (net position neutralized vs. directional market moves)
  - The firm's net delta (contract-equivalent net long or short)
    is **reevaluated within two seconds** every time any of 25
    traders makes a new trade
  - Each trader sees this information instantaneously and knows
    which direction to "lean" when seeking the next trade
  - If net position is long → traders lean toward finding
    mispricings that require a bearish bias to implement
  - One designated trader among the 25 acts as the **"air traffic
    controller"** — responsible solely for hedging (keeping net
    exposure as close to zero as possible)
  - Individual traders can take one-sided positions (whatever
    looks cheap or expensive) because the air traffic controller
    absorbs the directional risk at the firm level
  - "When there's an edge on a trade, part of the cost of taking
    that trade is that you have to give up some of that edge to
    hedge it. The beauty of this system is that the cost of
    hedging is very small."

### Covered Call Fraud

<!-- page 462: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N328] **Covered call = short put — Hull calls
  promoting covered calls a "fraud" on the public**:
  - A **covered call** (buy stock + sell call against it) is
    **mathematically identical** to selling a put
  - Brokers promote covered calls as a strategy because (a) it
    generates **double commissions** and (b) it *sounds* less
    risky than "selling a put" (semantic framing, not economic
    difference)
  - Hull: "I almost feel guilty when taking the other side of a
    covered call position, because it's obvious that the customer
    is operating under a misconception."
  - His direct verdict: "If you want to guarantee an inferior
    strategy, do covered calls." (The covered call underperforms
    the short put because of the extra commission and execution
    friction on the stock leg)
  - Implication: the *name* of a strategy can systematically
    mislead investors about its risk profile

### Strike Pinning at Expiration

<!-- page 463: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N329] **Stock price "pinning" at expiration —
  statistically ~2x more likely to finish near strike**:
  - Hull's statistical research (quoted in the *Wall Street
    Journal*): stocks are **about twice as likely** to finish
    within **one-quarter of a point of the nearest strike price**
    at option expiration vs. what would be expected by chance
  - Mechanism: speculators tend to hold slightly in-the-money
    calls; as expiration approaches, they sell (don't want to
    exercise); market makers on the other side must sell the
    stock to hedge → pushes stock toward the strike
  - Example: stock at 60 1/2, most open interest in 60 calls;
    public sells calls near expiration → market maker buys
    calls, sells stock → stock drifts toward 60
  - Hull actively **plays this strategy** because it provides
    a measurable edge
  - Context: two smaller market makers on the Pacific Stock
    Exchange once tried to intentionally pin a stock to a
    strike; they enlisted a large market maker who instead
    took the opposite position and "took them both out of
    the game"

### OEX RAES System — Public's Hidden Edge

<!-- page 464: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N330] **OEX RAES system gives retail traders an
  edge during volatile markets**:
  - **OEX RAES** (Remote Automatic Execution System): provides
    automatic execution within ~10 seconds; accepts orders of
    **10 contracts or less**
  - Market makers have agreed to honor these automated quotes;
    during extreme volatility, market makers **cannot update
    quotes fast enough**
  - Result: the public customer has a **"tremendous edge"** during
    the most volatile markets — they get filled at stale quotes
    that market makers can no longer defend
  - Hull's advice: if order size ≤ 10 contracts, always use RAES
    rather than open outcry

### Hull's Trader Profile and Common Misconceptions

<!-- page 465: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N331] **Hull's key traits of a successful options
  trader**:
  - "You can't listen to the news. You have to go with the facts."
  - Logical approach + discipline to apply it
  - Emotional control
  - **Consistency**: "go for the small theoretical edges instead
    of home runs"
  - Personality profile: successful **blackjack, chess, and
    bridge** players are more likely to fit the profile of a
    good options trader (games of incomplete information +
    mathematical strategy)
  - Common misconception of market participants: "They tend to
    listen to rumors. They're too interested in who's buying or
    selling. They think that type of information is important;
    yet it rarely means anything."

- [MUST KNOW][N332] **The money management myth destroyed —
  "even a poor system can make money with good money management"
  is complete nonsense**:
  - Hull's direct refutation: "All that good money management
    will do for a poor strategy is to assure that you will lose
    money more slowly."
  - Proof by roulette: no money management system can make
    roulette profitable — the zero and double zero give the
    house a decisive edge regardless of bet sizing
  - Corollary: with a **negative-edge strategy**, your **best**
    chance of coming out ahead is to apply the *worst* money
    management — risk everything on one trade — because the
    longer you play a negative edge, the more certain eventual
    ruin becomes
  - The correct formulation: **"Even a good system can lose money
    with poor money management"** — you still need bet-sizing
    discipline to survive variance even when you have an edge
  - Most basic requirement for trading success: a **well-defined
    method that gives you an edge**; without it, trading is
    as much a gamble as casino betting
  - Contrast with casinos: casinos can bar winning players;
    markets cannot eliminate skillful traders — the market
    "confounds the greatest number of people the greatest amount
    of time" rather than expelling them

<!-- page 466: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N333] **Schwager's inference: Hull's rules for
  directional trading** (not explicitly stated in interview):
  1. Trade **infrequently** and only when you have a strong idea
  2. Trade the **opposite side** of the predominant news stories
  3. **Time** the trade to coincide with an event that has the
     potential to lead to a **panic climax**
  - These rules are implicit in the Gulf War trade (strong thesis,
    against consensus fear, timed to the ultimatum deadline)
  - Reference: **Ken Uston**, *The Big Player* (book about the
    blackjack team Hull described) — illustrates the same
    theme of applying a winning method with discipline through
    losing periods while managing bet size relative to equity

