## Chapter: Jeff Yass — The Mathematics of Strategy

<!-- page 468: 2 nuggets -->

### Background and Philosophy

- [MUST KNOW][N334] **Jeff Yass — Susquehanna Investment Group —
  market as a giant poker game**:
  - Started as option trader on the **Philadelphia Stock Exchange**
    in **1981**
  - Enticed college friends into trading; trained **six of them**
    in early 1980s
  - In **1987**, formed **Susquehanna Investment Group** with those
    friends; firm grew to **175 employees, 90 traders**
  - One of the largest option trading firms in the world and one
    of the largest entities in **program trading**
  - Core approach: not necessarily having a better pricing model,
    but applying **mathematical game theory** to maximize winnings
  - Key insight: "the market is like a giant **poker game**, and
    you have to pay very close attention to the **skill level of
    your opponents**"
  - Yass's poker analogy: "If you're the sixth best poker player
    in the world and you play with the five best players, you're
    going to lose. On the other hand, if your skills are only
    average, but you play against weak opponents, you're going
    to win."
  - Practical implication: Yass factors in his **perception of
    the counterparty's knowledge** and willingness to revise
    his own market views based on actions of better-informed
    traders — a form of conditional updating

### Early History

<!-- page 469: 2 nuggets -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N335] **Yass's early stock experiences — pattern
  of losing**:
  - First stock at age **11**: bought **Campbell Soup** (because
    he loved Swanson's TV dinners, which Campbell owned);
    stock went nowhere for years
  - At **13**: bought Eastern Airlines (liked flying to Florida)
    + a realty company that went **bankrupt**; "I always lost"
  - Father's wisdom: "The stock was around a long time before
    you bought it. Just because you bought it now doesn't mean
    that it suddenly has to go up." — early lesson against
    recency bias / anchoring to one's own entry

- [SHOULD KNOW][N336] **Yass discovers options in high school —
  and learns about stale newspaper quotes**:
  - Discovered options from his father's company issuing
    **warrants** when it went public; understood warrants =
    long-term options
  - In high school, found what appeared to be **huge mispricings**
    in newspaper closing option prices; example: **Alcoa** at
    $49, 45 call trading only $2.50 above the 50 call — a
    bull spread with asymmetric risk/reward
  - Convinced father to execute the trade; it worked — but then
    discovered that newspaper closing option prices are **stale**
    (last sale printed, not current bid/ask); an option might
    have closed 11 bid/12 offered but printed at 13 if that
    was the last sale
  - Once he understood the quotes were not real, "most of the
    trading opportunities I found were really nonexistent"
  - Lesson in data quality: know your data source and its
    limitations before acting on apparent mispricings

<!-- page 470: 1 nugget -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N337] **Yass's failed job interview — the beta
  anecdote**:
  - Interviewed at unnamed investment house for options department
    job after college
  - Head of options dept asked: "Do you know this year's high and
    low for IBM?" Yass said it was irrelevant
  - Interviewer didn't know the definition of **beta** (stock
    volatility relative to market); said "I don't bother myself
    with that kind of stuff"
  - Yass replied: "Terrific! Just hire me and I'll explain it to
    you and show you how to use it." — didn't get the job
  - Illustrates the gap between incumbent practitioners and
    mathematically rigorous traders entering the field; also
    Yass's confidence in quantitative thinking over price-history
    trivia

### Poker as Training Ground for Options

<!-- page 471: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N338] **Poker strategy = betting strategy, not
  odds memorization — direct parallel to options trading**:
  - Yass and college friends took poker "very seriously" as a
    game of **enormous skill and complexity**, not luck
  - Yass wins approximately **55% of poker sessions** (not hands
    — sessions); does not second-guess himself when losing
    because "in the short run most of the fluctuations are due
    to luck, not skill"
  - Key insight: "The really great poker players have an
    understanding of **proper betting strategy**. What information
    do you get when your opponent bets? What information do you
    give up when you bet? What information do you give up when
    you don't bet?"
  - Memorizing odds for hand combinations = "very small role";
    knowing which hands to play = basic knowledge only
  - **Susquehanna trains all option traders using poker strategy**
    because "the parallels are very strong" — if you can teach
    the right way to think about poker, you can teach the right
    way to trade options
  - The transferable skill: strategic information theory —
    what does your action signal, and what does your opponent's
    action reveal?

- [MUST KNOW][N339] **Poker/options lesson 1 — checking with the
  best hand when opponent likely to bet**:
  - Scenario: you have the best hand, last card dealt; novice
    says "bet the limit"; this is often **not the right move**
  - If opponent has >50% probability of betting, checking is
    superior: when he bets, you raise → win **double** the bet;
    when you bet directly, you win only **single** the bet (if
    he calls) or nothing (if he folds)
  - Mathematical result: in the long run, checking (inducing
    the bet) extracts more value than betting directly
  - **Options analogy**: the primary object is not winning the
    most trades, but **maximizing gains**
  - Worked example: option worth 3 1/4, can buy at 3 (certain
    $2,500 profit on 100 calls) OR try to buy at 2 3/4 (60%
    chance, miss 40% of the time, but profit = $5,000 when
    filled) → expected value at 2 3/4 = **$3,000 > $2,500**
    certain gain; the "miss" risk is worth accepting for the
    higher expected value

<!-- page 472: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N340] **Poker/options lesson 2 — four aces and
  the informed counterparty problem**:
  - Poker scenario: 7-card stud, last round, you have four aces
    showing (high hand); opponent's board shows potential
    straight flush (4, 5, 6, 7 of clubs showing with 3 hole
    cards)
  - Typical answer: "bet as much as I can — I have four aces"
  - **Correct answer: PASS** — if he can't beat you, he folds
    and you win nothing extra; if he can beat you (has the
    straight flush in the hole), he **raises** and you lose
    more; you can only lose by betting
  - **Options analogy** (the informed broker problem):
    - Option worth $3; normal market = 2 7/8 / 3 1/8
    - Broker who may have superior information asks for a
      quote → no benefit from making a tight market: if priced
      right, he passes; if priced wrong, he trades and you lose
    - If a broker with suspected superior information is bidding
      **significantly more** than you think the option is worth,
      there's a very good chance he knows something you don't →
      don't take the other side just because it looks like an
      attractive sale
  - Core principle: **option trading decisions should be based
    on conditional probability** — the other party's willingness
    to trade at a price is itself information that should update
    your estimate of value
  - "The lesson we try to teach our traders is that anything
    that seems very obvious should be double-checked."

### The Monty Hall Problem — Conditional Probability Made Concrete

<!-- page 473: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N341] **The Monty Hall problem — conditional
  probability and why you should always switch**:
  - Posed to Yass by **Fisher Black** (of Black-Scholes fame) as
    an illustration of conditional probability in trading
  - Setup: 3 doors, prize behind one; you pick door 1; Monty
    (who *knows* where prize is) opens door 2 (empty); offers
    you the chance to switch to door 3
  - **Wrong intuition**: doesn't matter, 50/50
  - **Correct answer**: **always switch** — probability of
    winning = **2/3** by switching vs. 1/3 by staying
  - Reasoning: the probability that prize is behind one of the
    two *unchosen* doors was originally 2/3; Monty always opens
    the wrong one (non-random) → all of that 2/3 probability
    now resides in door 3
  - **Key distinction**: if Monty randomly chose a door and
    happened to reveal a goat, it would be 50/50 — but then
    sometimes the prize would be behind the opened door, which
    never happens; his non-random selection is what changes the
    math
  - **100-door version** (reader letter from Cory Franklin,
    Chicago, July 23, 1991): makes it intuitive — 1/100 chance
    you picked right; Monty opens 98 empty doors; the remaining
    door has 99/100 chance of being the winner → obviously
    switch
  - Alternative framing (Karl V. Amatneek, San Diego, July 22,
    1991): 2/3 of the time you initially picked a goat; after
    one goat door is removed, the other door has the car 2/3
    of the time when you started with a goat → switching wins
    2/3 of the time
  - **Trading application**: conditional probability routinely
    confounds even expert intuition; **Marilyn Vos Savant**
    answered correctly in *Parade* column and received ~**1,000
    critical letters from Ph.D.s** (mostly mathematicians and
    scientists) who were wrong — even experts failed to account
    for the non-random nature of Monty's door selection
  - Direct options lesson: when market participants act in ways
    that seem to contradict your valuation, ask whether their
    action carries information that should update your estimate
    — the "switch" is often the correct move, even when staying
    feels intuitively right

<!-- page 474: no content (letters to editor, supporting material) -->
<!-- page 475: no content (Monty Hall letters continued) -->

<!-- page 476: no content -->
<!-- page 477: no content -->
<!-- page 478: no content -->
<!-- page 479: 1 nugget (Monty Hall explanation continued — captured in N341) -->
<!-- page 480: 1 nugget (reader letters — captured in N341) -->

<!-- pp. 479-500 -->

<!-- page 479: no content (Monty Hall explanation continued — captured in N341) -->
<!-- page 480: no content (reader letters — captured in N341) -->
<!-- page 481: no content (Cory Franklin/Karl Amatneek letters — context for N341) -->
<!-- page 482: 1 nugget (Lore Segal letter + Yass trading moral from Monty Hall) -->

- [MUST KNOW][N342] **Trading moral of the Monty Hall puzzle — beware
  the obvious**:
  - Lore Segal (New York, July 22, 1991) humorously pointed out that
    no one asked whether you *wanted* the car vs. the goat — a reminder
    that framing the question matters
  - Yass's synthesis: "Your senses deceive you. Your simplistic impulse
    is to say that the probabilities are 50/50... On careful analysis,
    however, you realize that there is a huge advantage to switching,
    even though it was not at all obvious at first."
  - **Core trading lesson**: In trading it's important to examine the
    situation from as many angles as possible, because initial impulses
    are probably going to be wrong
  - **"There is never any money to be made in the obvious
    conclusions."** — Jeff Yass

<!-- page 483: 2 nuggets (options below intrinsic value; obvious decision wrong) -->

### Options Below Intrinsic Value — The Information Signal

- [MUST KNOW][N343] **When an institution sells options below intrinsic
  value, they almost always know something you don't**:
  - Example: stock at $50, institution offers to sell 500 of the 45
    calls at $4.50 (intrinsic value = $5; option offered at discount)
  - **Obvious response**: "Great! Buy the calls at $4.50, sell the stock
    at $50, lock in $0.50 profit."
  - **Reality**: nine times out of ten the institution is offering the
    call cheap because it's fairly certain the stock is going lower
    (i.e., to below $49.50)
  - **Mechanism**: institution may have 1 million shares to sell, knows
    it will have to move price to $49 to clear that quantity — they're
    not naive, they know something
  - **Conditional probability framing**: given that the institution
    *wants* this trade, which is more likely — they're so naive they're
    writing you a risk-free check for $25,000, or they know something
    you don't? Answer is obvious.
  - **Yass's learning experience**: when he first started, he would
    always buy options offered below intrinsic value, thinking he had
    a locked-in profit; couldn't understand why smart floor traders
    weren't rushing to do the same; eventually realized those trades
    were on average losing propositions
  - **Common strategy**: institutions regularly sell calls prior to
    liquidating large positions to cushion slippage — "it's a common
    strategy, but the market makers have wised up"

<!-- page 484: 1 nugget (why sell below intrinsic; conditional probability) -->

<!-- page 485: 2 nuggets (evolution of options market; volatility arbitrage era) -->

### Evolution of the Options Market

- [SHOULD KNOW][N344] **How the options market evolved from 1981 to
  early 1990s — three phases of edge**:
  - **Phase 1 (1981–early 1980s)**: basic **Black-Scholes** +
    common sense was sufficient; the dominant strategy was buying
    an option at low **implied volatility** and selling a related
    option in the same stock at a higher volatility
    - Example: large buy order pushes one strike's implied vol to 28%;
      another call in the same stock trades at 25% — sell the higher,
      buy the lower, pocket the spread
    - Many traders still didn't understand that you could buy an
      underpriced call while being *bearish* on the stock by
      simultaneously shorting the stock (combined position = long put)
    - Once this was understood, cross-option volatility arbitrage
      eliminated those discrepancies
  - **Phase 2 (mid/late 1980s)**: everybody understood volatility
    relationships; different options in same stock no longer trade at
    significantly different volatilities unless there's a fundamental
    reason
  - **Phase 3 (now)**: the major battle has shifted to **skewness** in
    option pricing — the new edge is understanding *which direction*
    vol is skewed and why

<!-- page 486: 2 nuggets (skewness definition; OTM puts vs calls) -->

### Option Skewness — Why Puts Cost More Than Calls

- [MUST KNOW][N345] **Skewness: out-of-the-money puts are almost
  always priced higher than equivalently OTM calls — and this is
  rational**:
  - Example (at interview time): OEX index at 355; the 345 puts are
    priced much higher than the 365 calls, even though standard
    pricing models would price the 365 calls *slightly higher* than
    the 345 puts
  - **Two reasons for the directional bias toward expensive puts**:
    1. **Financial panic asymmetry**: much greater probability of
       a financial panic on the downside than an overnight runaway
       euphoria on the upside — "once in a great while, you may get
       a day with the Dow down 500 points, but it's far less likely
       that the Dow will go up 500 points"
    2. **A second reason Yass won't disclose** ("I can't tell you")
       — implies proprietary edge
  - **Historical note**: the market did NOT price puts significantly
    higher than calls until *after* the October 1987 crash — even
    though the asymmetry always logically existed; the market learned
    the lesson the hard way
  - **Yass's view**: he had always felt the chance of a huge downmove
    was greater than an equivalent upmove — so he was early to
    recognize the correct skew before it was priced in
  - **Exception**: if a stock is subject to a **takeover rumor**,
    OTM calls will be priced *higher* than OTM puts (upside surprise
    > downside surprise in that context)
  - **Scope**: applies to most major individual stocks (downside
    surprises tend to be much greater than upside surprises), not
    just stock index options

<!-- page 487: 2 nuggets (directional bias; takeover exception; model vs judgment) -->

- [MUST KNOW][N346] **No option pricing model can adequately describe
  the real world — and the market beats every model**:
  - Yass's own model, like all pricing models, is "too simplistic to
    adequately describe the real world"
  - "There's no way you can construct a model that can come close to
    being as informed as the market"
  - **Training philosophy at Susquehanna**: teach traders the basic
    model assumptions, then teach *why* those assumptions are too
    simplistic, then teach more sophisticated assumptions and their
    price implications
  - Result: pricing decisions are always a judgment call about which
    assumptions are appropriate — model output is the starting point,
    not the conclusion
  - **"Traders are made, not born."** — Yass; they can train any
    intelligent, quick-thinking person to be a trader

<!-- page 488: 2 nuggets (NCR takeover — model failure; seat of pants beats math) -->

### When Models Fail: Takeover Situations

- [MUST KNOW][N347] **Takeover situations expose the deepest flaw in
  standard option models — the 50/50 tick assumption collapses**:
  - Real example: **NCR** as takeover target of **AT&T**; AT&T bid
    at $110, stock trading at ~$106
  - Standard model assumes any individual tick is 50/50 up or down
  - **Reality of the binary outcome**:
    - If takeover succeeds: stock goes to ~$110; buyer makes ~$2
      (half the $4 spread represents interest costs of carrying stock)
    - If takeover fails: stock drops to ~$75 (market estimate)
  - **Implication for option pricing**:
    - OTM calls near $110 are essentially worthless — stock won't
      go above the bid
    - OTM 90 puts have significant value — they capture the downside
      if deal falls through
    - Correct pricing: OTM puts priced much higher than equivalent
      OTM calls (opposite of model's prediction in that range)
  - **Historical edge**: mathematical traders would sell OTM puts
    because they appeared overpriced by their models; seat-of-the-pants
    traders recognized real possibility of a large decline and bought
    those puts from the math types — "taking them to the cleaners"
  - Eventually mathematical types caught on, eliminating this edge

<!-- page 489: 2 nuggets (respect for market opinion; information flow to options) -->

### Respecting Market Opinion and Information Flow

- [MUST KNOW][N348] **When a knowledgeable market maker bids away from
  your model, assume they know more — and adjust your entire book**:
  - Susquehanna's core operating principle: "tremendous respect for
    market opinion"
  - If they value an option at $2 and a knowledgeable market maker
    bids $2.50: assume 9 times out of 10 he's right, because he
    trades that one stock all day and they trade 500
  - Process:
    1. Try to identify *why* he's bidding $2.50
    2. If they can identify the reason and disagree with it → may sell
       the option as overpriced
    3. If they can't identify or agree the reason is valid → adjust
       their entire valuation of that stock's options upward; may buy
       other options in that stock or the stock itself
  - **Key insight**: the signal in his price tells you something about
    the stock — update everything, not just that one strike

- [MUST KNOW][N349] **Information flows to the options market first —
  and market makers are on the wrong side of informed trades**:
  - "Information doesn't flow perfectly, like they teach you in
    Finance 101. Frequently, the information will show up first in the
    option market."
  - "A lot of these insider trading cases involve options, and we're
    the people who lose the money."
  - **Why options attract informed traders**: leverage is vastly
    higher than stock; example — Marion Labs employee with advance
    knowledge of a Dow takeover bid
    - Bought 500 of the July 25 calls at $1 (total cost: $50,000)
    - Next day the calls were worth $10 (total position value:
      $500,000) — a 10× return overnight
    - Pre-options era: same information → buy stock on 50% margin →
      profit percentage far smaller
  - **The Santa Fe example (1981)**: Santa Fe oil company targeted by
    Kuwaitis; stock at $25; floor traders filled order for 1,000 35
    calls at $1/16; stock jumped from $25 to $45; options went from
    $1/16 to $10; floor traders suffered ~$1 million overnight loss
    - Eventually recovered money, but waited years; "If you're a
      market maker and you're broke, waiting to get your capital back
      is not pleasant"

<!-- page 490: 2 nuggets (Marion Labs insider; information first in options) -->
<!-- page 491: 2 nuggets (Santa Fe 1981; Combustion Engineering) -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N350] **Combustion Engineering — being picked off by
  a board member, and the structural dilemma of market making**:
  - Combustion Engineering options on Pacific Coast Exchange rarely
    traded; morning call from board broker: order to buy several
    hundred options
  - Susquehanna sold 300 of the 25 calls at ~$2.50; 10 minutes later
    trading was halted; announcement that company was being taken over
    by European corporation; stock reopened at $39
  - Loss: over $350,000 in minutes; buyer turned out to be a board
    director of the acquiring company
  - Resolution: SEC identified buyer quickly; high-level foreign
    executive who didn't realize he was breaking the law; returned
    money without complications
  - **Structural dilemma**: "If you're too conservative, you won't do
    any trades; if you're too aggressive, you're going to get picked
    off a lot. The trick is to try to strike a balance between the
    two."
  - Susquehanna normally hedges position after filling option order by
    buying the stock — Combustion Engineering stock halted before they
    could hedge, making the loss total rather than partial

<!-- page 492: 2 nuggets (Combustion Engineering full story; SEC enforcement cycle) -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N351] **The perverse economics of SEC enforcement —
  better enforcement means wider bid/ask spreads for everyone**:
  - Every option trade carries a risk premium for the possibility the
    other side has inside information
  - "The more successful the SEC is in catching people trading on
    inside information... the tighter the bid/ask spreads will be"
  - Counterintuitive: if everyone believes the SEC will catch all
    insider traders, market makers will price away that risk premium →
    tighter spreads → better prices for regular investors
  - But in practice: "it's really the average investor who ends up
    paying for insider trading through the wider bid/ask spreads"
  - **Signal**: large option volume almost always precedes major
    corporate events — "if you go over the volume data for stocks
    that were taken over, you'll find that there was almost always a
    flurry of option trading before the event"

<!-- page 493: 2 nuggets (balance of aggressiveness; SEC spread dynamics) -->
<!-- page 494: 2 nuggets (no directional trading; efficient markets belief) -->

### Yass on Efficient Markets and Directional Trading

- [MUST KNOW][N352] **Yass does zero directional trading — and the
  efficient market argument he makes is unusually strong**:
  - "None. It's my firm belief that the market's wisdom is far greater
    than mine. In my opinion, the market's pricing of an item is the
    best measure of its value."
  - The test: "The odd thing about believing in efficient markets is
    that you have to surrender your beliefs and ego to the markets."
  - **OMB director example**: a director of the Office of Management
    and Budget claimed budget projections should assume long-term
    interest rates would decline to 5–6%, when rates were over 8%
    - The market-implied interest rate level reflects "the net
      intelligence of thousands of traders battling it out daily in
      the bond market"
    - "In comparison, the OMB director's personal opinion doesn't mean
      anything. If he's basing government policy on the assumption that
      long-term interest rates will be 5 to 6 percent, when the
      market's best guess is 8 percent, he's doing grave harm to
      society."
  - **Yass's simplified version**: "My guess about where interest
    rates will be in the next twenty years is better than that of
    almost any economist, because all I have to do is look at where
    the bond market is trading."
  - Principle: "If you can give up your ego and listen to what the
    markets are telling you, you can have a huge source of
    information."

<!-- page 495: 2 nuggets (diversification; Schwager summary of Yass) -->

- [MUST KNOW][N353] **Diversification is the only free lunch —
  and not diversifying is literally incinerating money**:
  - "If you invest and don't diversify, you're literally throwing out
    money."
  - Counter-intuitive mechanism: diversification is beneficial even if
    it *reduces* your return, because it reduces your *risk even more*
  - Therefore: diversify, then use margin to lever back up to the same
    risk level as an undiversified portfolio → higher return for same
    risk
  - Yass's formulation: "The way I would put it is that not
    diversifying is like throwing your lunch out the window. If you
    have a portfolio and are not diversifying, you're incinerating
    money every year."
  - Schwager's summary of the Yass chapter: most important points for
    ordinary traders:
    1. **Maximize gains, not number of wins** — sizing up on
       higher-probability trades
    2. **Initial impressions are often wrong** — beware of acting on
       the obvious ("there is never any money in the obvious
       conclusions")

