## Chapter: Zen and the Art of Trading (Anonymous Trader)

<!-- page 497: begins Zen chapter -->
<!-- page 498: 3 nuggets (anonymous trader; intuitive trading; paper trading rule) -->

- [MUST KNOW][N355] **Anonymous trader: intuitive trading that defies
  systematic description — and what to do when intuition goes away**:
  - Background: one of the traders Schwager interviewed had made
    several hundred million dollars in trading profits for his firm;
    the chapter contained material on intuition, dreams, Eastern
    philosophy, and trading anecdotes; the trader withheld permission
    to use the material (worried about corporate clients) — Schwager
    published anonymously with permission for this excerpt
  - Trading method: "There was no system to it. It was nothing more
    than, 'I think the market is going up, so I'm going to buy.' 'It's
    gone up enough, so I'm going to sell.' It was completely impulsive.
    I didn't sit down and formulate any trading plan."
  - **When intuition disappears — the three-loss rule**:
    - Recognition signal: being wrong three times in a row → call time
      out
    - Response: switch to **paper trading** until he feels in sync with
      the market again
    - "Every market has a rhythm, and our job as traders is to get in
      sync with that rhythm."

- [MUST KNOW][N356] **The Zen trading state — ego-less execution,
  the difference between *wanting* and *knowing***:
  - When truly in sync: "There's buying and selling going on, but
    it's just going *through* me. It's like my personality and ego
    are not there. I don't even get a sense of satisfaction on these
    trades. It's absolutely objective."
  - Reference: *Zen and the Art of Archery* — "you have to learn to
    let the arrow shoot itself. There's no ego involved. It's not,
    'I'm shooting the arrow, and I'm releasing it.' Rather, the arrow
    is shot, and it's always right."
  - Applied to trading: "There's no sense of self at all. There's just
    an awareness of what will happen. The trick is to differentiate
    between what you *want* to happen and what you *know* will happen.
    The intuition knows what will happen."
  - **Effort as a negative signal**: "In trading, just as in archery,
    whenever there is effort, force, straining, struggling, or trying,
    it's wrong. You're out of sync; you're out of harmony with the
    market. The perfect trade is one that requires no effort."

<!-- page 499: 2 nuggets (Zen archery analogy; Freudian slip trade trigger) -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N357] **The Freudian slip as a trading signal**:
  - Example: trader was talking about the yen/mark rate when it was
    trading at 87.80; he kept referring to the price as 77.80
  - The other trader finally asked "What are you talking about?" and
    he realized he was off by 10 big figures in his price references
  - Interpretation: "Obviously, there was some part of me that was
    looking for the rate to go down to that level. It was literally
    bubbling out of me."
  - The subconscious conviction expressed itself as a slip before the
    conscious mind formulated a trade thesis
  - This triggered the position in mark/yen — and the decline
    subsequently occurred

