## Chapter: Charles Faulkner — The Mind of an Achiever

<!-- page 499: Charles Faulkner chapter begins -->
<!-- page 500: 3 nuggets (NLP definition; skiing analogy; language processes literally) -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N358] **Charles Faulkner and NLP — background and
  core definition**:
  - **Charles Faulkner**: abandoned graduate studies in
    psycholinguistics at **Northwestern University** after discovering
    books by **Richard Bandler** (information scientist) and
    **John Grinder** (professor of linguistics), cofounders of
    **Neuro-Linguistic Programming (NLP)**
  - Studied extensively with Grinder and Bandler from 1981; certified
    NLP trainer 1987; focus on modeling human excellence applied to
    accelerated learning, physician decision-making, and futures trading
  - NLP's definition: "the study of human excellence" — how great
    achievers use their brains to produce results
  - Started with extraordinarily talented therapists; expanded to
    managers, negotiators, athletes, artists
  - **Faulkner's preferred name**: "Natural Learning Processes" would
    have been better than Neuro-Linguistic Programming
  - **Core metaphor**: "NLP is software for the brain" — mental
    programs that allow you to develop new abilities

- [SHOULD KNOW][N359] **NLP's founding insight: excellence can be
  modeled and taught — the skiing analogy**:
  - Until the 1950s, skiing was considered natural talent — you either
    had it or you didn't
  - Films were made of Europe's great skiers; analysis revealed they
    all had certain techniques in common
  - Discovery: those techniques could be taught to anyone; great
    skiing was no longer considered innate talent but a learnable model
  - NLP applies the same logic to any domain of human excellence:
    identify the mental "essence" (the model) and teach it

- [MUST KNOW][N360] **The brain processes language literally — why
  "don't think about X" makes things worse, and the trading
  implication**:
  - "How do our brains process language? The answer is very, very
    literally."
  - "Don't worry" or "Don't think about it" — the brain must first
    access the concept of worry/the problem in order to understand
    what NOT to do; the result is that you think about the very thing
    you're trying to avoid
  - **Trading application**: experienced traders telling new traders
    "Don't think about the money. Remember, don't think about the
    money" → new traders repeat this until they are literally obsessing
    about the money
  - **NLP prescription**: restate negative instructions in positive
    terms — state what you *do* want, not what you don't want
    - Wrong: "Don't think about the money"
    - Right: "Focus your attention on following your method"
  - This is a general principle of effective coaching and
    self-instruction in any domain

<!-- page 500: 1 nugget (NLP five presuppositions — begins, cut off) -->
- Note: page 500 ends mid-sentence with the first of five NLP presuppositions
  ("The map is...") — content continues in next batch

<!-- pp. 499-520 -->

## Chapter: Charles Faulkner — The Mental Edge (NLP Applied to Trading)

*Charles Faulkner is a Neuro-Linguistic Programming (NLP) trainer
who studied great traders to build models of trading excellence.
Schwager met him in a private conference room near an airport;
Faulkner used NLP's cue-reading (gestures, eye movements, language,
voice intonation) throughout. His work began in 1987 at the request
of Steve Bianucci, a floor trader transitioning to position trading
under Pete Steidlmayer's Market Logic School.*

---

### What Is NLP?

- [MUST KNOW][N342] **NLP (Neuro-Linguistic Programming) — "software
  for the brain"**: founded by **Richard Bandler** (information
  scientist) and **John Grinder** (linguistics professor); "Natural
  Learning Processes" might have been a better name
  - Core method: study extraordinary achievers → identify what mental
    programs they use → extract the essence ("model") → teach it to
    anyone
  - Origin: began with extraordinarily talented therapists who
    consistently produced change; expanded to managers, negotiators,
    athletes, artists
  - Analogy: 1950s skiing — once films were made of Europe's great
    skiers, their common techniques were identified and taught to
    anyone; excellence is teachable once you extract the model
  - Key claim: if ONE person can do it, ANYONE can learn to do it —
    excellence has a structure that can be copied

- [SHOULD KNOW][N343] **The five core presuppositions of NLP**:
  1. **The map is not the territory** — we respond to our thoughts
     and feelings (map), not to reality (territory); a better map =
     better results; all maps are distorted or selected views of
     reality
  2. **Experience has a structure** — how memories are arranged in
     the mind determines their meaning and emotional impact; change
     the structure → the experience changes automatically
  3. **If one person can do it, anyone can learn to do it** — the
     great promise of NLP; excellence is not born talent but
     learnable structure
  4. **Mind and body are part of the same system** — change your mind
     → your body changes; change your posture or breathing → your
     thoughts change. Psychiatrist **R.D. Laing**: "Change your mind,
     change your body. Change your body, change your mind."
  5. **People have all the resources they need** — inner images,
     sounds, feelings (confidence, will, challenge) — even if only
     felt once in a lifetime — can be transferred to any situation

---

### Language: The Brain Processes Literally

- [MUST KNOW][N344] **The brain cannot process negative commands**:
  - "Don't think about the problem" forces the brain to think about
    the problem first in order to know what not to think about
  - Trading example: experienced traders telling beginners "Don't
    think about the money" causes them to obsess about it
  - **NLP fix**: restate negatives as positives — instead of "don't
    think about the money," say "focus your attention on following
    your method"
  - Practical implication: trading rules, self-talk, and mental
    rehearsal should ALL be stated as what you WANT to do, not what
    you want to avoid

---

### The Map Is Not the Territory — Applied to Trading

- [MUST KNOW][N345] **Regression to the mean as a map distortion for
  traders**:
  - Statistical reality: extreme performance (good or bad) tends to
    revert toward average in the next period — this is math, not
    skill change
  - Trader distortion: after a great period + average period, trader
    feels like he failed; after a bad period + average period, trader
    feels dramatically improved — in both cases, he attributes the
    change to his system or feelings, not to a statistical tendency
  - **The trap**: switching systems after a poor period → likely to
    do better anyway (regression to mean) even if the new system is
    equal or inferior → trader falsely credits the new system
  - Same phenomenon explains motivational seminar results: people
    attend when they're at a low point; they improve afterwards
    statistically regardless; they credit the seminar
  - **Supertrader edge**: they understand regression to the mean and
    USE it instead of being misled by it

---

### The Placebo Effect — Reframed

- [NICE TO KNOW][N346] **NLP's reframe of the placebo effect**:
  - Medical science treats placebo as a nuisance to rule out
  - Bandler & Grinder viewed it as a **natural human ability** —
    the brain's power to heal the body
  - NLP position: if results are partially placebo, that is the
    brain's natural ability to affect how we feel, heal, and function
    — use it deliberately, don't discard it
  - Broader implication: self-belief effects in trading are not
    "irrational" — they are real causal mechanisms

---

### NLP Technique Examples

- [SHOULD KNOW][N347] **Anchoring — and how to neutralize bad trading
  memories**:
  - **Anchoring**: conditioned emotional responses to situations
    (like Pavlov's dogs); a trader who had a big loss may feel
    anxiety when in a similar position weeks later — the association
    is automatic
  - **Visual de-anchoring technique** (for disturbing memories):
    1. Run through the memory like a movie; pick one still-frame that
       symbolizes the disappointment
    2. Pull back in your mind's eye until you can see your earlier
       self in the scene (dissociated perspective)
    3. Imagine it rendered as a famous painting — a Renoir, van Gogh,
       Lichtenstein
    4. Add a frame (gold, steel) and museum light; appreciate it as a
       framed memory
    5. Notice feelings — most people find disturbance greatly reduced
       or eliminated
  - **Auditory technique** (for disturbing memories):
    1. Run the memory like a movie to the beginning
    2. Play mismatching music over it (circus music, William Tell
       Overture/"Lone Ranger" theme)
    3. Rewind and replay; play again without music
    4. For many, the incident becomes humorous or the distress is
       neutralized
  - Visual technique suits visual thinkers; auditory suits auditory
    thinkers

- [SHOULD KNOW][N348] **Confidence transfer — anchoring positive
  states to future situations**:
  - Recall a specific memory of feeling abundantly confident; relive
    it fully (see and hear what you saw and heard)
  - As confidence builds, imagine a colored circle on the floor;
    step out of it as you exhale, leaving the feeling in the circle
  - Think of a specific future time when you want confidence; as you
    think of it, step back into the circle → spontaneously feel
    confidence from the past
  - The anchor becomes automatic — that future situation will
    trigger confidence without conscious effort
  - Can be repeated for multiple future situations and multiple
    emotional states

---

### Why Success Doesn't Bring Happiness

- [SHOULD KNOW][N349] **The wrong-goals problem: pursuing
  society's definition of success, not your own**:
  - Most people base success on outdated models from family, media,
    culture (the right house, car, spouse) — never asking "Do I
    actually want to live this life?"
  - NLP exercise: mentally step INTO that future life and experience
    several weeks or months — not just visualize from outside it
  - When people do this, they often find they want to change things
    before working harder to reach them
  - **End-of-life exercise**: imagine yourself at end of a long,
    healthy life; look back on what you accomplished; ask "What is
    really worth the time of your life?"
  - Source: Faulkner worked as an orderly in a hospital ward with
    elderly patients for three years in college; spoke to hundreds
    near death; found:
    - Falling in love at 19 mattered; willingness to take risks into
      the unknown mattered
    - Retiring because of age was among the biggest regrets
    - **Not one person regretted what they had done — they regretted
      what they hadn't done**; wasted time on petty pursuits; failed
      to identify and live their values
  - NLP lesson: if we don't live true to our values and fulfill
    them, we experience disappointment and emptiness regardless
    of external success

---

### Motivation: Toward vs. Away From

- [MUST KNOW][N350] **Two directions of motivation — and why
  traders need BOTH**:
  - **Toward motivation**: moves toward goals, pleasure, rewards;
    wakes up excited; makes career moves to seize opportunities;
    "self-motivated," "go-getter" in the language of job ads
  - **Away From motivation**: moves away from pain, discomfort,
    negative consequences; motivated by problems; stays put until
    the pain exceeds the threshold
  - **Common misconception**: Toward is always better. Not true.
    Away From people are often brilliant problem-solvers. **Martin
    Zwieg** — manages over a billion dollars, one of the most
    respected stock market forecasters — uses Away From motivation:
    "DON'T fight interest rate trends. DON'T fight market momentum."
    **Paul Tudor Jones**: "I have a short-term horizon for pain."
    Many outstanding traders reveal Away From motivation in their
    emphasis on "protecting themselves" and "playing great defense."
  - **Disadvantages of Away From**: runs hot and cold (motivation
    fades as distance from the problem grows); can cause excessive
    anxiety and health problems; person doesn't always know where
    they're going, only what they're escaping
  - **Disadvantages of Toward**: so focused on goals they may miss
    problems and risks ahead; poor at preparing for contingencies
  - [MUST KNOW] **Trading specifically requires BOTH directions**:
    traders typically enter markets with Toward motivation (toward
    success, money) but "it's nearly impossible to be a really
    successful trader without the motivation to get away from
    excessive risk" — Away From motivation is essential for risk
    management and money management
  - Away From + positively stated goal can work together: motivation
    is "away from poverty"; goal is "set a personal record in profits
    this year"

---

### Five Conditions for an Achievable Goal

- [SHOULD KNOW][N351] **NLP's five conditions for a goal to be
  achievable**:
  1. **Stated in positive terms** — not "I don't want to lose money"
     but "I want to protect my assets"; the brain can't run toward
     a negation
  2. **The goal must be yours** — "They want me to trade larger" is
     not your goal; "When market conditions warrant, I'm going to
     double my trading size" is your goal
  3. **Must be specific** — richly detailed: what you'll see, hear,
     feel when you get it; "nothing ever happens in general"
  4. **Include when, where, and with whom**
  5. **Anticipate the effects** — is it worthwhile and desirable in
     itself and in its consequences? Does it align with your values?
  - Note: Away From motivation can still work if the GOAL is stated
    positively — the motivation direction and the goal statement are
    separate things

---

### What Differentiates Exceptional Traders

- [MUST KNOW][N352] **Five elements of trading excellence (from NLP
  modeling research)**:

  **1. Beliefs** — **Charles Garfield** (*Peak Performers*): the
  key shared element among peak performers is total belief in the
  likelihood of their own success. **Dr. Michael Lerner**
  (*Surplus Powerlessness*): most people feel they have very little
  power over their lives (from thousands of interviews across
  occupations). Supertraders have absolute confidence in their
  ability to win — confidence confirmed by competence, not just
  self-talk. Average traders lack confidence in their system and
  tend to blame others (broker, floor traders) for results.

  **2. Perceptual filter** — a methodology, approach, or system for
  understanding market behavior. Elliott Wave, classical charting,
  Gann, Market Profile — the TYPE of filter doesn't matter much;
  what matters is knowing it thoroughly and following it.
  - Schwager's explanation (which Faulkner endorses): many methods
    are essentially different-colored glasses for looking at price —
    "nonprescription sunglasses: they change the view but don't
    necessarily improve the vision." Methods appear to work mainly
    because users develop intuitive experience about price patterns
    while using them. The perceptual filter must match the trader's
    thinking style — it has more to do with the trader's mental
    strategy than with how accurately it models market activity.

  **3. Effective trading strategy (in the NLP sense)** — a series
  of internal representations, mental pictures, words, and feelings
  leading to a desired outcome. Five characteristics:
  - Automatic: trader knows what to do without second-guessing
  - Congruent: no internal conflict
  - Incorporates Away From motivation via specific risk-control plans
  - Involves imagining the trade from the perspective of already
    being in it — asking what might be wrong BEFORE entering
  - Provides specific evidence that allows the trader to evaluate
    the merits of a trade

  **4. Emotional state management** — exceptional traders stand up
  to anything; when things go wrong, they remain calm and act in
  accordance with their approach. **Pete Steidlmayer's** attitude
  when a position goes against him: "Hmm, look at that." Scientific
  detachment. This allows proper feedback about whether the approach
  needs revision.
  - This can be intrinsic (natural detachment) or learned (military-
    like control); either works
  - **Warning**: trading attracts people who are ill-suited to this
    requirement — those enamored with money, risk-seekers, people
    who react to the world with emotional intensity
  - NLP technique for detachment: physically get up, step back, and
    imagine seeing yourself sitting in your chair, calmly observing
    the trading as if watching someone else
  - **Cautionary case**: one of Faulkner's earliest clients was an
    emotional trader with a good system he couldn't follow; NLP
    techniques made him emotionally detached; he made $7,000 in a
    few hours; he turned to Faulkner and said "This is boring" — and
    eventually blew out. He knew how to be detached but didn't want
    to be there. **Lesson**: some traders are in markets for the
    excitement; removing excitement removes their reason to be there.
    Solution: help them find excitement elsewhere in their lives so
    trading can be calm.

  **5. Operating metaphors** — how a trader describes the market
  shapes their beliefs, actions, and lifestyle in profound ways:
  - **Game/puzzle metaphors** (used by great traders): **Richard
    Dennis**: "It's like playing a hundred chess games at once."
    **Pete Steidlmayer**: "solving the markets." **Paul Tudor Jones**:
    "It's a game, and money is a way to keep score."
  - **Adversarial metaphors** (common on trading floors): "I got
    torn up today" → market is a beast of prey; "We took a hit" →
    speaker is a wounded participant
  - **Why game metaphors are superior**: in a game, survival isn't
    at stake — there are winners and losers, but you keep playing;
    this promotes objective analysis, learning long-term strategies,
    and practicing tactics. The survival/war metaphor triggers
    fight-or-flight responses that cloud judgment.

- [MUST KNOW][N353] **The best single predictor of trading success:
  improving with experience**:
  - After years of studying traders: the best predictor is simply
    whether the person is improving with time and experience
  - Many traders unconsciously acknowledge their lack of progress
    by jumping from one system to another — they end up with "one
    year of experience six times instead of six years of experience"
  - Superior traders gravitate to a single approach and become
    extremely adept at it (the specific approach is secondary)
  - This aligns with the perceptual filter point: depth of
    experience with ONE method builds genuine intuition

---

### Conscious vs. Unconscious Mind (NLP View)

- [SHOULD KNOW][N354] **The conscious mind evaluates; the unconscious
  runs programs — horse-and-rider analogy**:
  - The conscious mind's role is reflection and evaluation: "Am I
    where I want to be?" "Is this a good trade?"
  - It is NOT the property of the conscious mind to change things —
    that's the unconscious
  - Analogy: Faulkner rented a horse in Colorado; horses know the
    paths and try to run riders through trees to knock them off and
    return to the stable. His friend, unfamiliar with horses, jumped
    off when the horse ran toward trees — "The stupid horse was going
    to run into a tree, so I had to jump off." He mistook the horse
    for a car and grabbed the wrong model.
  - Point: when the unconscious (horse) goes somewhere the conscious
    mind (rider) doesn't want to go, it doesn't mean the unconscious
    is bad or broken — it's running its OWN programs. The solution
    is to reprogram those programs (NLP's job), not fight the
    unconscious.
  - Trading implication: traders who self-sabotage aren't weak-
    willed — their unconscious is running programs that have
    unexamined logic (e.g., the trader who gave back profits because
    he associated success with becoming a "not wonderful human
    being"; resolving that inner conflict tripled his position size)

---

### Case Study: Inner Conflict Sabotaging Performance

- [SHOULD KNOW][N355] **Classic NLP trader case — unconscious
  success avoidance**:
  - A good trader who consistently gave back profits; in working with
    Faulkner, revealed he knew many financially successful traders
    who were "not wonderful human beings"
  - His unconscious solution: don't become too successful, so he
    wouldn't become like them
  - Secondary belief: trading meant he couldn't have a personal life
    or family
  - NLP resolved both: helped him see that financial success and
    being a good person weren't mutually exclusive; that he could
    have both trading and a personal life
  - Result: he tripled his position size to over 1,000 contracts;
    freed mental power previously consumed by inner conflict
  - **Key mechanism**: inner conflict consumes cognitive bandwidth;
    resolving it doesn't just help psychology — it improves actual
    decision-making and opportunity recognition

