## Chapter: Robert Krausz — The Role of the Subconscious

### Background and Biography

- [NICE TO KNOW][N363] **Robert Krausz — biographical context**:
  - Schwager first heard of Krausz through *Club 3000* (publication of
    subscriber letters about trading); a trader reported that
    subliminal tapes from Krausz, a member of the **British Hypnotist
    Examiners Council (BHEC)**, had dramatically improved his trading
  - Krausz's life story has a Dickensian quality: childhood in a
    Hungarian ghetto during WWII; at age 8, escaped a forced march to
    a death camp by bolting into woods with a friend; returned to the
    ghetto; lived in orphanages after the war, including one in South
    Africa where a diamond magnate adopted him
  - As an adult: committed Zionist → joined Israeli armed forces as a
    paratrooper in the 1956 war with Egypt → troubled by socialist
    undertone of Israeli economy → emigrated to Great Britain
  - In London: dress designer during the Carnaby Street/Beatles era;
    later expanded to fabric design and Far East manufacturing
  - Early 1988: gave up his clothing business and moved to the US as
    a full-time trader
  - Methodology: **Symmetrics** — price bars of varying widths based
    on assumed symmetry of price and time, invented by **Joe Rondinone**,
    one of **W.D. Gann's** first students; Krausz manually maintained
    large hand-drawn charts
  - Krausz's view of trading:
    > "This is the best business in the world! There is no other
    > profession that is so black and white; you're either right or
    > wrong. Trading also appeals to me because you're totally
    > dependent on your own talents and abilities."

---

### How Krausz Got Into Trading and Hypnosis

- [SHOULD KNOW][N364] **Krausz's origin story — the classic beginner
  trap and how it led to hypnosis**:
  - First exposure: 1979-80 gold bull market; a friend who claimed
    expertise (and later went totally broke) persuaded him to open a
    brokerage account
  - **Classic beginner pattern**: first trade winner → second trade
    winner → third breakeven → fourth winner → fifth gave it all back
    → sixth lost MORE than all previous wins combined
  - Kept meeting margin calls, believing "the market is going to turn"
    — it never did; lost considerably more than starting equity
  - Had a pre-set loss cutoff (was 50% shareholder in garment business;
    would not let losses endanger the business)
  - **Aftermath**: not just financial loss — "a tremendous infliction
    of pain to my ego. I was a reasonably successful businessman who
    up to that point had never failed in any venture."
  - Decision: figure out what makes the market tick, or walk away
    permanently. Chose to figure it out.
  - Used Far East business travel (lots of free time in remote regions)
    to read voraciously on technical analysis; had partner telex daily
    gold OHLC prices; eventually read **Charles Drummond's**
    *How To Make Money in the Commodity Markets—and Lots of It!*
    — introduced to Drummond's **point-and-line charting** methodology

- [SHOULD KNOW][N365] **Krausz on harping on past mistakes**:
  - "One of the most damaging things a person can do is to harp on
    past mistakes"
  - If you constantly repeat "I shouldn't have done that," it's like
    a cartwheel going over the same tracks — eventually the negative
    message gets so embedded in the psyche that it becomes very
    difficult to change course
  - **Practical implication**: acknowledge the loss, extract the lesson,
    then actively stop the repetitive self-criticism loop

- [SHOULD KNOW][N366] **The "freeze" — how Krausz discovered hypnosis**:
  - Returned to trading using Drummond's point-and-line methodology;
    found himself HESITATING to take trades — the fear of loss had
    arrived
  - Communicated with Drummond (by telex); Drummond diagnosed:
    "You're experiencing what is known as the 'freeze,' which is purely
    a psychological problem"
  - By chance, met an acquaintance in severe distress (divorce, failing
    business); saw same man three weeks later laughing and jovial —
    transformation traced to a hypnotherapist
  - Sought out that hypnotherapist specifically for trading psychology;
    hypnotherapist had no concept of trading but helped anyway
  - **Result**: trading went to breakeven quickly; the "freeze" was gone
  - So impressed he trained himself in hypnotherapy through BHEC:
    two-weekend beginner course → one year observing instructor
    **John Cross** → advanced BHEC course → BHEC qualification exam

---

### Krausz's Model: Hypnosis and the Subconscious

- [MUST KNOW][N367] **Who hypnosis can and cannot help — critical
  prerequisite**:
  - Hypnosis can only help traders who ALREADY have a valid methodology
    that their conscious mind is happy with and have adequately
    back-tested
  - The only thing hypnosis can do is inform the subconscious that the
    person NOW has a valid methodology the conscious mind has already
    accepted
  - **Analogy**: a novice trader trying to become expert through hypnosis
    is like a novice chess player seeking to become a master through
    hypnosis — a certain proficiency level is necessary first
  - Krausz's intake process: ~30 questions to determine if the person
    has a methodology. If not: "Go home; find yourself a methodology;
    then see me if you still need to."
  - **"Hypnosis is not a crutch."** If you don't have a methodology
    and trading plan, all the hypnosis in the world won't help

- [MUST KNOW][N368] **Hypnotizability facts**:
  - About **85% of the population is hypnotizable**
  - Contrary to popular belief, **intelligent and creative people are
    the EASIEST to hypnotize** (not the hardest)
  - Stage hypnosis "fools of themselves" dynamic: volunteers are
    natural performers who want to show off — hypnosis merely bypasses
    the behavioral controls of the conscious mind to release their
    natural inclinations
  - It is virtually impossible to get a naturally shy person to do
    silly things under hypnosis — you cannot make people do what they
    wouldn't do in a normal waking state
  - Test for genuine hypnosis: tell subject their arm is a rigid piece
    of steel; instruct them to extend it horizontally; if truly
    hypnotized, the arm cannot be pushed down regardless of force —
    even from a physically weak person

- [MUST KNOW][N369] **Subconscious sabotage mechanism — why experienced
  traders with new methods still can't execute**:
  - Every losing trading plan leaves a memory etched in the
    subconscious. More losses = deeper impression, deeper pain
  - Scenario: trade Methodology A → take many losses → stop trading
    1-2 years → develop new Methodology B (conscious mind convinced
    it's valid) → BUT losses from Methodology A are so ingrained in
    subconscious that the moment you contemplate a trade, adrenaline
    starts flowing, fear of executing arises
  - Some traders are literally IMMOBILIZED by this fear at the moment
    they need to act — this is the "freeze"
  - **Key insight**: the problem will persist until you convince the
    subconscious in a very direct manner that the new methodology is
    valid AND that it has to forget the old methodology
  - The conscious mind can already be convinced; the bottleneck is
    the subconscious

- [MUST KNOW][N370] **How hypnosis transforms the subconscious — the
  mechanism**:
  - Must erase the previous "pictures of impending financial disaster"
    and paint new pictures in beautiful colors: happy, confident,
    successful trader
  - Deep relaxation (achieved through hypnosis) bypasses the **critical
    faculty of the conscious mind** and establishes a direct connection
    to the subconscious
  - Hypnosis = a state of mind, NOT a state of sleep
  - Because the subconscious mind is **nonjudgmental**, it will accept
    new input as facts when delivered directly
  - Tell the subconscious the old fears are no longer valid; the trader
    now has a well-tested and confident plan → subconscious begins to
    accept this new reality
  - "One cannot truly be a winner until the subconscious mind is fully
    in tune with what the conscious mind has set out to achieve."

- [MUST KNOW][N371] **The single most important belief for keeping
  winnings: "I deserve to win"**:
  - To GENERATE winnings, conscious and subconscious must be in harmony
  - To KEEP those winnings, the subconscious must believe only one
    thing: **that you deserve your winnings**
  - Without this belief, Krausz argues, traders lose even when they
    have a valid methodology

  > "Robert, I feel I deserve my winnings again."
  > — Krausz's client, under hypnosis, explaining what had changed

---

### Case Studies: The Subconscious at Work

- [MUST KNOW][N372] **30-year veteran losing because of personal trauma
  — case study**:
  - A trader with 30+ years of consistent success suddenly started
    losing six-figure amounts monthly for 5 consecutive months
  - Onset of losing streak coincided exactly with his wife leaving him
    for another man (she was a much younger woman)
  - Krausz helped him realize: the breakup was not his fault; his
    wife's affections went only as deep as his pocketbook
  - **Result**: within 3 days of resolving this, he was breaking even;
    within another 3 days, making money again
  - Under hypnosis after recovery: Krausz asked if he had changed his
    method → "No." More confident? → "Yes." What made the difference?
    → "I feel I deserve my winnings again."
  - **Root cause revealed**: the trader subconsciously believed the
    marriage breakup was due to his inability to perform sexually as
    he had when younger → felt he had failed his wife → was
    subconsciously punishing himself by losing in the markets
  - **Mechanism**: subconscious self-punishment expressed through
    deliberate (but unconscious) losing; had NOTHING to do with
    trading methodology

- [MUST KNOW][N373] **The two reasons traders lose — Krausz's
  framework**:
  - Some people lose because they feel they don't DESERVE to win
    (psychological/subconscious block — addressed by hypnosis)
  - But MORE people lose because they never perform the BASIC TASKS
    necessary to become a winning trader:
    1. Develop a competent analytical methodology
    2. Extract a reasonable trading plan from this methodology
    3. Formulate rules for this plan that incorporate money management
       techniques
    4. Back-test the plan over a sufficiently long period
    5. Exercise self-management so that you adhere to the plan — "The
       best plan in the world cannot work if you don't act on it"
  - **Critical sequencing**: must complete these 5 tasks FIRST; only
    then can hypnosis help with execution and belief issues

- [MUST KNOW][N374] **Some people use trading to punish themselves —
  alarming regularity**:
  - Under hypnosis, Krausz finds with "alarming regularity" that some
    people subconsciously do NOT want to be traders — they are using
    the market as a vehicle for self-punishment
  - Motivations: retribution for a real or imagined wrong done to
    another person; the channel for self-punishment varies by person
    (suicide, doing one's job poorly, or losing money in markets)
  - These people will lose money even when they consciously believe
    they want to trade and even when they know better
  - **Krausz's response**: bring them out of hypnosis, tell them what
    transpired, and advise them to stop trading entirely
  - Reaction: "Total horror" — Krausz has been threatened with
    physical violence by clients receiving this advice
  - **Case example**: a professionally successful man trading for 2
    years with steady losses. Under hypnosis, revealed he hated his
    wife but lacked courage to divorce her. His subconscious strategy:
    lose enough money in the markets to become "church-mouse poor" so
    she would leave him. He couldn't allow himself to look bad in his
    actual profession (too good at it), so every month he earned X
    thousand in his work and gave back X thousand plus in markets.
    When asked under hypnosis "Are you getting back at your wife?
    Do you feel if you lose enough money she'll walk out?" — he
    exclaimed, "That's the idea!" His conscious mind would never
    have admitted this motivation.

---

