## Chapter: Ari Kiev / Krausz (continued) — Self-Sabotage & Hypnotherapy

### Traders Who Shouldn't Be Trading

- [MUST KNOW][N356] **Self-punishment through market losses — subconscious
  retribution**:
  - Some people try to punish themselves through the market on a
    subconscious level — making retribution for a real or imagined wrong
    done to another person
  - The channel for self-destruction varies by person: suicide, doing
    their job poorly on purpose, or losing money in the markets —
    even if they know better consciously
  - Krausz's test: under hypnosis, some clients reveal they are
    genuinely NOT comfortable trading — it's simply not their calling
  - His response: "Here's your money; I can't help you. Do yourself
    and your family a great favor and forget trading."
  - When told to quit, typical reaction: **total horror** — one person
    threatened physical violence ("I'll bash your f–ing head in!")
  - Implication: people can be deeply attached to trading even when
    it is actively destroying them — the attachment itself is evidence
    of the psychological problem

### Case Study: Man Using Losses to Escape His Marriage

- [MUST KNOW][N357] **Subconscious motivation: market losses as a divorce
  strategy**:
  - A successful professional man had been trading for two years,
    losing money steadily; his wife had sent him to Krausz
  - Under hypnosis, revealed: he hated his wife but lacked the
    courage to divorce her; his only way out was to make himself
    "church-mouse poor" so she would walk out on him
  - He couldn't make himself look bad through his profession (too
    good at it), so every month he earned X thousand in his
    profession and gave back X thousand plus in commodity markets
  - Under hypnosis Krausz asked directly: "Are you getting back at
    your wife? Do you feel that if you lose enough money she'll
    walk out on you?" — Response: "That's the idea!"
  - His **conscious mind would never have admitted this motivation** —
    he sincerely believed he loved trading and preferred it to his
    profession
  - Lesson: trading losses can serve hidden psychological purposes
    that have nothing to do with markets; the losses are actually
    "working" for the person at a subconscious level

### Case Study: The Man Who Hid His Own Brokerage Statements

- [SHOULD KNOW][N358] **Classic self-deception case — hiding losses from
  oneself**:
  - A London client came to Krausz claiming his wife was
    intercepting his brokerage statements (hiding them from him)
  - The man was so absorbed in trading (watching the quote screen)
    that he couldn't even step outside to beat the mailman
  - Krausz called the wife, who said: "I never touch his mail — he
    hides them himself. He never opens them."
  - Under hypnosis, the man confirmed: he was hiding his own
    statements because he didn't want to see his mistakes; his
    broker would call for margin, he'd send more money ("I have to,
    otherwise I'd have to stop trading")
  - His motivation for trading: **pure thrills** — a boring civil service
    job, trading was how he sought excitement
  - What stopped him: the wife, under the agreement Krausz
    orchestrated, would hand over ALL back statements; realizing
    she knew where he hid them (the only way she could hand them
    over), his conscious mind grasped what had actually been
    happening — he pulled out the statements himself, put them on
    the dining table, and closed his account the next day
  - **Key mechanism**: the subconscious uses elaborate fictions
    (blaming the wife) to protect itself from information that would
    force a rational decision to stop. The deception wasn't just
    from others — it was self-directed.

### Krausz's Deepest Insight from Hypnotherapy Work

- [MUST KNOW][N359] **The most surprising discovery: people fool themselves
  constantly — beliefs ARE reality to the believer**:
  - "How ready we are to fool ourselves. I learned that people's
    perceptions of reality and true reality are not the same thing.
    It's a person's belief system, not reality, that really counts."
  - The more Krausz worked with hypnosis, the more he found that
    lives are warped and misdirected by **invalid beliefs that have
    their origins in childhood** — these beliefs cause people to live
    with a distorted view of reality for their entire lives
  - Example: boy of 5 picks up father's tool; father shouts at him
    to put it down (afraid of injury). Filed subconsciously as: "If I
    touch these tools, my father will shout at me. Therefore it's bad."
  - At age 12, father asks the boy to help fix the car — the boy
    feels inexplicably uncomfortable with tools, performs poorly;
    father concludes he's clumsy; boy is now **convinced for life
    that he's useless with tools**
  - The original fear (being shouted at) was rational at 5; the
    belief persists long after it is no longer relevant
  - Trading analogy: a trader may believe on a subconscious level
    that he is a poor trader based on PAST experiences, even after
    developing an effective methodology — the subconscious belief
    generates fear that is no longer appropriate

### Why Most Traders Lose: The Trading Plan as Root Cause

- [MUST KNOW][N360] **Krausz's workshop finding — no trading plan is the
  single root of all trader failure**:
  - Conducted a two-day workshop with 30 part-time traders; asked
    them to rank their greatest weaknesses in the markets from a
    list of 8 items
  - 90% of participants independently chose the SAME top four:
    1. Lack of confidence
    2. No trading plan
    3. Execution (pulling the trigger)
    4. Fear of loss
  - Krausz's insight: what is the single element that causes all
    three others? **NO TRADING PLAN**
  - Lack of confidence disappears in direct relation to the validity
    of a back-tested trading plan
  - "This is the basic feature that separates losing traders from
    winning traders."
  - **Key implication**: fear of loss, poor execution, and lack of
    confidence are SYMPTOMS — the trading plan is the cure,
    not psychological work in isolation

### Creative Visualization

- [SHOULD KNOW][N361] **Creative visualization as subconscious reprogramming**:
  - In a deep-relaxation state, you see yourself applying your
    trading methodology and then see it succeeding
  - By mentally playing through these images, you can alter the
    negative beliefs in your subconscious and enhance your chance
    of success
  - Same methodology used by many top athletes
  - Traders without goals find it much harder to achieve high
    returns than traders who set specific targets
  - **Goal-setting + visualization = conscious programming of the
    subconscious toward desired outcomes**

### Krausz's Core Principle

- [MUST KNOW][N362] **"We become what we believe"**:
  - Krausz's unstated motto: subconscious beliefs will dictate
    a person's actions, even after the conscious mind knows better
  - If the subconscious believes a trader is a loser based on prior
    experiences, it will continue to hold that view even after an
    effective methodology has been developed
  - These outdated beliefs generate fears that Charles Drummond
    (Krausz's mentor) called "the freeze" — paralysis at the moment
    of action
  - Once an effective trading plan is developed, it is critical to
    convince the subconscious mind of the new reality through
    hypnosis, deep relaxation, or visualization
  - **Greater harmony between conscious and subconscious =
    greater chance of trading success**
  - Happiness, per Krausz: "The single most important factor is
    having control of your own life. Everything else is secondary."

---

## Part VIII: Closing Bell — Market Wiz(ar)dom
### Schwager's 42 Observations on Trading Success

> Note: This is Schwager's synthesis chapter. These are his distilled
> observations after two books of interviews with exceptional traders.
> They represent cross-validated principles — things that held true
> across wildly different trading styles, timeframes, and markets.

- [MUST KNOW][N363] **The methods of exceptional traders are
  extraordinarily diverse** — pure fundamentalists, pure technicians,
  hybrids; some consider 2 days "long term," others 2 months "short
  term." Yet despite this diversity, certain principles hold across
  all of them. What follows is Schwager's list of 42.

---

### Observation 1: First Things First

- [SHOULD KNOW][N364] **Be sure you really want to trade** — both Krausz
  and Faulkner confirmed it is common for people who THINK they
  want to trade to discover, through psychological work, that they
  actually don't
  - This is a non-trivial filter: entering trading with the wrong
    motivation is a setup for self-sabotage

---

### Observation 2: Examine Your Motives

- [MUST KNOW][N365] **Motive-method alignment is essential**:
  - If you want to trade for the excitement, you might be better
    off riding a roller coaster or taking up hang gliding
  - Schwager's own example: his underlying motive for trading was
    serenity and puzzle-solving — but trading's visceral emotional
    characteristics conflicted with both. He resolved this by
    focusing exclusively on mechanical systems (eliminated
    emotionality; kept the puzzle-solving)
  - **"You need to do almost everything right to win. If parts of
    you are pulling in opposite directions, the game is lost before
    you start."**
  - Inner conflict between motives and activity = guaranteed
    underperformance

---

### Observation 3: Match the Trading Method to Your Personality

- [MUST KNOW][N366] **The method must fit the person — personality
  mismatch is a primary reason traders fail even with good systems**:
  - If you can't stand giving back significant profits → trend
    following will be a disaster (you will never follow it)
  - If you can't watch screens all day → don't use day-trading
  - If you can't stand emotional strain → develop a mechanical system
  - **Randy McKay**: "Virtually every successful trader I know
    ultimately ended up with a trading style suited to his
    personality."
  - Why purchased trading systems rarely make profits: not only is
    the chance of buying a winning system less than 50/50, the odds
    of it also fitting your personality are smaller still. Compound
    those probabilities and the chance of using it effectively is
    very small.

---

### Observation 4: It Is Absolutely Necessary to Have an Edge

- [MUST KNOW][N367] **Without an edge, perfect discipline and money
  management will only guarantee a slow death**:
  - If discipline alone were enough, you could win at roulette with
    perfect risk control — you can't, because the house has the edge
  - "If you don't know what your edge is, you don't have one."
  - Money management and discipline, applied to a method with no
    edge, simply guarantees you will "gradually bleed to death"
  - The edge must be identified and defined — not assumed

---

### Observation 5: Derive a Method

- [SHOULD KNOW][N368] **The type of method is irrelevant — having one is
  critical**:
  - Among supertraders: pure fundamentalists, pure technicians,
    hybrids, tape readers, chartists, mechanical system traders,
    Elliott Wave analysts, Gann analysts, etc.
  - The method doesn't matter; the edge within it does
  - Having no method is the same as having no edge

---

### Observation 6: Developing a Method Is Hard Work

- [SHOULD KNOW][N369] **Shortcuts rarely lead to trading success**:
  - Developing your own approach requires research, observation,
    and thought — expect dead ends and multiple failures
  - "You are playing against tens of thousands of professionals.
    Why should you be any better? If it were that easy, there
    would be a lot more millionaire traders."

---

### Observation 7: Skill Versus Hard Work

- [SHOULD KNOW][N370] **Both talent AND hard work are required for
  exceptional performance — hard work alone has a ceiling**:
  - Marathon analogy: virtually anyone can run a marathon with
    commitment and work; but only a small fraction will ever run
    a 2:12 marathon regardless of effort
  - Similarly: virtually anyone can become a NET PROFITABLE trader,
    but only a few have the inborn talent to become supertraders
  - "It may be possible to teach trading success, but only up to a
    point. Be realistic in your goals."
  - Caveat: this is NOT a counsel of despair — net profitability is
    achievable through hard work. Supertrader-level performance
    requires innate talent.

---

### Observation 8: Good Trading Should Be Effortless

- [MUST KNOW][N371] **The paradox: hard work refers to preparation,
  not to the act of trading itself**:
  - Hard work = the research, observation, and preparation phase
  - The actual trading process, for good traders, should feel
    natural and effortless — NOT a struggle
  - "On the contrary, the more effortless and natural the trading
    process, the better the chances for success."

> "In trading, just as in archery, whenever there is effort, force,
> straining, struggling, or trying, it's wrong. You're out of sync;
> you're out of harmony with the market. The perfect trade is one
> that requires no effort."
> — Anonymous trader in *Zen and the Art of Trading*

  - Analogy: a world-class distance runner clicks off miles at a
    5-minute pace with graceful ease; an out-of-shape person
    struggles through one mile at 10 minutes. The professional
    puts in the hard work during **training** — the actual race is
    where that preparation pays off in apparent effortlessness
  - If trading feels like constant struggle, that is diagnostic
    information — something is wrong at a deeper level

---

### Observation 9: Money Management and Risk Control

- [MUST KNOW][N372] **Money management is more important than the
  trading method — and it's not complicated**:
  - Nearly every trader interviewed rated money management as MORE
    important than the trading method itself
  - Many potentially successful systems have led to disaster because
    the trader lacked risk control
  - **Schwager's three-step risk control framework**:
    1. Never risk more than 1–2% of capital on any trade (up to 5%
       max; strongly advises against anything over 5%)
    2. Predetermine your exit point BEFORE you get into a trade
       (cited by many interviewees as a critical rule)
    3. If you lose a predetermined amount of starting capital (e.g.,
       10–20%), take a breather, analyze what went wrong, wait
       until you feel confident and have a high-probability idea
       before trading again. For large accounts, trading very small
       is a reasonable alternative to a full hiatus.
  - **Cutting size during losing streaks** was mentioned by many
    traders interviewed as a key discipline

---

### Observation 10: The Trading Plan

- [MUST KNOW][N373] **No trading plan = inevitable costly mistakes**:
  - "Trying to win in the markets without a trading plan is like
    trying to build a house without blueprints."
  - A trading plan = personal trading method + specific money
    management rules + trade entry rules
  - Krausz: absence of a trading plan is the root of all principal
    difficulties traders encounter
  - **Richard Driehaus**: the trading plan must reflect a personal
    core philosophy — without a core philosophy, you won't hold
    positions or stick with the plan during really difficult times
  - The plan provides the psychological anchor to follow through
    when it's emotionally hardest

---

### Observation 11: Discipline

- [MUST KNOW][N374] **Discipline — the most frequently cited word by
  exceptional traders — has two distinct functions**:
  - Usually mentioned "almost apologetically" — traders felt they
    were stating the obvious, but emphasized it was critical anyway
  - Function 1: prerequisite for maintaining effective risk control
  - Function 2: applying your method without second-guessing or
    cherry-picking which trades to take
  - **"I guarantee that you will almost always pick the wrong ones.
    Why? Because you will tend to pick the comfortable trades, and
    as Eckhardt explained, 'What feels good is often the wrong
    thing to do.'"**
  - Final note: bad trading habits are never eliminated — the best
    you can do is keep them **latent**. Laziness or sloppiness
    will bring them back.

---

### Observation 12: Understand That You Are Responsible

- [SHOULD KNOW][N375] **Radical personal responsibility — no successful
  trader blamed others for losses**:
  - Whether you win or lose, you are responsible for your own
    results
  - Even if you lost on a broker's tip, advisory service, or a
    bought system signal — you made the decision to listen and act
  - "I have never met a successful trader who blamed others for
    his losses."
  - Implication: blame-shifting is not just ethically wrong — it
    prevents learning and guarantees repetition of errors

---

### Observation 13: The Need for Independence

- [MUST KNOW][N376] **Never listen to other opinions — not even from
  much better traders**:
  - Even if it occasionally helps on a trade or two, listening to
    others "invariably seems to end up costing you money — not to
    mention confusing your own market view"
  - **Michael Marcus** (*Market Wizards*): "You need to follow your
    own light. If you combine two traders, you will get the worst
    of each."
  - **Ed Seykota**: by the time a story makes the cover of national
    periodicals, the trend is probably near an end
  - Schwager's personal anecdote: had a strong short yen position;
    a much better trader called and gave 51 reasons why yen was
    oversold and due for a rally. Schwager closed his short.
    When he returned from a trip, yen had fallen 150 points.
    The same trader called again — he was now SHORT the yen.
  - "The point is not that this trader was trying to mislead me.
    On the contrary, he firmly believed each market opinion at the
    time he expressed it." But his timing meant he may have made
    money on BOTH sides. Schwager, by listening, ended up with
    nothing even though he had the original move right.
  - **Moral: even advice from a much better trader can lead to
    detrimental results**

---

### Observation 14: Confidence

- [SHOULD KNOW][N377] **Unwavering confidence in one's ability to win is
  a nearly universal characteristic of exceptional traders**:
  - **Dr. Van Tharp** (psychologist, researched traders, interviewed
    in *Market Wizards*): one of the basic traits of winning traders
    is that they believe "they've won the game before the start"
  - Confidence enables losing trades to be accepted as inevitable
    rather than devastating — because the trader knows they will
    make it back

---

### Observation 15: Losing Is Part of the Game

- [MUST KNOW][N378] **Accepting losses as inevitable — not just
  intellectually but emotionally — is essential**:
  - Great traders fully realize losing is an intrinsic element of
    the game; individual losses appear inevitable, not horrible
  - **Linda Raschke**: "It never bothered me to lose, because I
    always knew that I would make it right back."
  - Fear of losing creates a destructive feedback loop: either you
    end up taking large losses (can't cut them) or you miss great
    opportunities (afraid to enter). Either flaw sinks success.
  - "There is no more certain recipe for losing than having a fear
    of losing."

---

### Observation 16: Lack of Confidence and Time-Outs

- [SHOULD KNOW][N379] **Negative self-talk is a signal to stop trading**:
  - Warning signs: "I just can't seem to do anything right" or
    "I bet I get stopped out right near the low again"
  - When thinking in such negative terms → take a break immediately
  - Re-enter trading slowly; test the water before plunging back in
  - "Think of trading as a cold ocean."

---

### Observation 17: The Urge to Seek Advice

- [SHOULD KNOW][N380] **Wanting someone else's opinion on a trade is
  a diagnostic signal — get out of the position**:
  - **Linda Raschke**: "If you ever find yourself tempted to seek
    out someone else's opinion on a trade, that's usually a sure
    sign that you should get out of your position."
  - The urge to seek advice betrays a lack of confidence in the
    trade — which means the trade is already compromised

---

### Observation 18: The Virtue of Patience

- [MUST KNOW][N381] **Waiting for the right opportunity — not always
  being in the market — is a mark of professional trading**:
  - **Edwin Lefevre** (*Reminiscences of a Stock Operator*): "There
    is the plain fool who does the wrong thing at all times
    anywhere, but there is the Wall Street fool who thinks he must
    trade all the time."
  - **Jim Rogers** (*Market Wizards*): "I just wait until there is
    money lying in the corner, and all I have to do is go over
    there and pick it up." — Until so sure it's that obvious, he
    does nothing.
  - **Mark Weinstein** (*Market Wizards*): "Although the cheetah is
    the fastest animal in the world... it will wait until it is
    absolutely sure it can catch its prey. It may hide in the bush
    for a week, waiting for just the right moment. It will wait for
    a baby antelope, and not just any baby antelope, but
    preferably one that is also sick or lame. Only then, when
    there is no chance it can lose its prey, does it attack. That,
    to me, is the epitome of professional trading."
  - **Vengeance trading** — trading aggressively to win back prior
    losses — is a sure recipe for failure. Guard against it.

---

### Observation 19: The Importance of Sitting

- [MUST KNOW][N382] **Staying with winning trades is as important as
  entering correctly — failure to sit is a key profit-limiting
  factor**:
  - **Lefevre** (*Reminiscences*): "It never was my thinking that
    made big money for me. It was always my sitting. Got that? My
    sitting tight!"
  - **Eckhardt**: "One common adage ... that is completely
    wrongheaded is: You can't go broke taking profits. That's
    precisely how many traders DO go broke. While amateurs go
    broke by taking large losses, professionals go broke by taking
    small profits."
  - The failure to adequately profit from correct trades is a KEY
    profit-limiting factor — cutting winners short is as damaging
    as letting losers run

---

### Observation 20: Developing a Low-Risk Idea

- [SHOULD KNOW][N383] **Low-risk idea as a framework combining patience
  and risk control**:
  - **Dr. Van Tharp** uses this exercise in his seminars: write down
    your ideas for low-risk trades
  - A low-risk idea combines two elements: (1) patience (only a
    small portion of ideas will qualify) and (2) risk control
    (inherent in the definition of "low-risk")
  - The specific ideas vary widely by trader, market, and methodology
  - Example: a trade where the market movement required to prove you
    wrong is very small (tight invalidation point)
  - Humorous example from the seminar: "Open a doughnut shop next
    door to a police station."

---

### Observation 21: The Importance of Varying Bet Size

- [MUST KNOW][N384] **Bet sizing by conviction level is mathematically
  optimal and separates good from exceptional returns**:
  - All winning traders have an edge, but the edge varies from trade
    to trade — some setups are much stronger than others
  - It can be mathematically demonstrated that in any wager game
    with varying probabilities, **winnings are maximized by
    adjusting the bet size in accordance with the perceived chance
    of a successful outcome** (optimal blackjack betting strategy
    illustrates this — see Hull chapter)
  - **Stanley Druckenmiller**: "The way to build [superior] long-term
    returns is through preservation of capital and home runs…
    When you have tremendous conviction on a trade, you have to go
    for the jugular. It takes courage to be a pig."
  - For many Market Wizards, the willingness to really step on the
    accelerator when conviction is high was instrumental in
    achieving **exceptional** (not merely good) returns
  - **Randy McKay**: varied position size by as much as a factor of
    100 to 1 depending on performance period — reducing risk during
    losing periods, enhancing during winning periods

---

### Observation 22: Scaling In and Out of Trades

- [SHOULD KNOW][N385] **Scaling provides flexibility that all-in/all-out
  thinking sacrifices**:
  - You don't have to get in or out of a position all at once
  - Scaling broadens the set of alternative choices and allows
    fine-tuning
  - Most traders sacrifice this flexibility due to the innate human
    desire to be completely right (all-in/all-out feels decisive)
  - By definition, scaling means some portions will be entered or
    exited at worse prices — but the flexibility is worth it
  - **Bill Lipschutz**: scaling enabled him to stay with long-term
    winners much longer than most traders stay with positions

---

### Observation 23: Being Right Is More Important Than Being a Genius

- [SHOULD KNOW][N386] **Don't try to pick tops and bottoms — go for
  consistency over brilliance**:
  - Many traders pick tops and bottoms to prove how smart they are
  - Think about winning rather than being a hero
  - Judge trading success by how well you pick individual trades
    with merit based on favorable risk/return — not by how close
    you come to major tops and bottoms
  - Go for consistency on a trade-to-trade basis, not perfect trades

---

### Observation 24: Don't Worry About Looking Stupid

- [SHOULD KNOW][N387] **(Content begins at bottom of p. 560 — continues
  in next batch)**
  - Section heading visible; full content not yet captured

