## Appendix: Glossary of Trading Terms (continued)

*Pages 579–583 contain the back-matter glossary — standard reference
definitions. These are the terms most relevant to understanding the
interviews in this book.*

### Key Glossary Definitions

- [NICE TO KNOW][N388] **Leverage** — the ability to control a dollar
  amount of a commodity or financial instrument greater than the
  capital employed; greater leverage = greater potential profit OR loss
  - Core risk concept underlying most of the traders' warnings about
    position sizing and risk management throughout the book

- [NICE TO KNOW][N389] **Limit price move** — many futures exchanges
  specify a maximum daily price change; when free-market forces would
  push price outside this range, market moves to the limit and
  **virtually ceases to trade**
  - *Locked limit-up* (advancing) / *locked limit-down* (declining)
  - Practical implication: traders can be trapped in losing positions
    with no exit — a key risk in futures not present in stocks

- [NICE TO KNOW][N390] **Liquid market** — sufficiently large daily
  trade volume so that most reasonably sized buy/sell orders can be
  executed without significantly moving prices; provides ease of entry
  and exit
  - Liquidity is a precondition for the scaling strategies praised
    throughout the interviews

- [NICE TO KNOW][N391] **Mark to the market** — valuation of open
  positions at prevailing settlement prices; no distinction between
  realized and unrealized losses (or gains)
  - Important: unrealized losses are real losses in futures — margin
    calls don't wait for positions to be closed

- [NICE TO KNOW][N392] **Mechanical system** — a computerized trading
  system that generates buy/sell signals; trader follows signals
  without personal market assessment
  - Contrasts with discretionary trading; multiple wizards use
    systematic approaches while others are purely discretionary

- [NICE TO KNOW][N393] **Moving average** — smooths prices to discern
  trends; *simple moving average* = average price over fixed number of
  days; crossovers (price crossing MA, or two MAs crossing) used as
  buy/sell signals in trend-following systems

- [NICE TO KNOW][N394] **Open interest** — in futures, total number of
  open long AND short positions are always equal; total (long or short)
  is the open interest; builds from zero when contract begins trading,
  declines as expiration approaches as positions are liquidated

- [NICE TO KNOW][N395] **Overbought/oversold indicator** — technical
  indicator attempting to define when prices have risen/fallen too far,
  too fast, making a reversal likely; also used as contrary opinion
  measure (too many bulls = overbought)

- [NICE TO KNOW][N396] **Put/call ratio** — volume of puts divided by
  volume of calls; a contrary opinion indicator
  - High ratio (more puts) = too many bears = **bullish** signal
  - Low ratio (fewer puts) = too many bulls = **bearish** signal

- [NICE TO KNOW][N397] **Pyramiding** — using unrealized profits on an
  existing position as margin to increase position size; increases
  leverage, profit potential, AND risk simultaneously

- [NICE TO KNOW][N398] **Reversal day** — day on which market reaches
  new high (low) then reverses, closing below (above) one or more
  preceding daily closes; most significant ("key reversal") when
  accompanied by high volume and wide price range

- [NICE TO KNOW][N399] **Risk/reward ratio** — estimated potential loss
  divided by estimated potential gain; theoretically should incorporate
  probability of each outcome, but frequently calculated naively on
  magnitudes alone (ignoring probabilities)
  - Schwager notes this naive calculation as a limitation throughout
    the book — good traders think about probability-weighted outcomes

- [NICE TO KNOW][N400] **Scalper** — floor broker trading own account
  for profit from very small price fluctuations; profits from the
  bid-ask spread; also provides liquidity to the market

- [NICE TO KNOW][N401] **Skid (Slippage)** — difference between
  theoretical execution price (e.g., midpoint of opening range) and
  actual fill price; a real transaction cost that erodes system
  performance

- [NICE TO KNOW][N402] **Spike** — price high (low) sharply above
  (below) highs (lows) of preceding and succeeding days; represents
  at least temporary climax in buying (selling) pressure; may be major
  top or bottom

- [NICE TO KNOW][N403] **Stop order (stop-loss order)** — buy order
  placed above market (or sell order below) that becomes a market
  order when specified price is reached; most frequently used to limit
  losses; critical tool for all the traders in this book

- [NICE TO KNOW][N404] **Trend-following system** — generates buy/sell
  signals in the direction of a newly defined trend, assuming a trend
  once established will continue

- [NICE TO KNOW][N405] **Whipsaw** — repeated, abrupt reversals in
  trend; in choppy/trendless markets, trend-following systems generate
  buy signals just before downside reversals and sell signals just
  before upside reversals — the principal failure mode of systematic
  trend-following

