## Back-Matter Footnotes (Substantive Content)

*Pages 594–600 contain footnotes to earlier chapters. Several contain
genuinely useful context.*

### Elliott Wave — Origins and Subjectivity

- [SHOULD KNOW][N406] **Elliott Wave Principle** — formally developed
  by **R. N. Elliott**, an accountant turned market student
  - Definitive work: *Nature's Law — The Secret of the Universe*,
    published in 1946, only two years before his death
  - "The application of the theory is unavoidably subjective, with
    numerous interpretations appearing in scores of volumes"
  - Source cited: **John J. Murphy**, *Technical Analysis of the
    Futures Markets*, New York Institute of Finance, 1986
  - Key caveat: subjectivity is acknowledged even in formal
    definitions — not just a practitioner complaint

### Lipschutz / Salomon Bond-Buying Scandal Footnote

- [SHOULD KNOW][N407] **Lipschutz interview was conducted before the
  Salomon Brothers government bond-buying scandal**
  - Lipschutz had used the words *honesty* and *integrity* to describe
    Salomon; after the scandal broke, Schwager asked if he wished to
    revise
  - Lipschutz chose to maintain his original description
  - His reconciliation: "I believe it was more a matter of ego on the
    part of a single individual, which ran counter to the qualities
    embodied by **Gutfreund** and the firm"
  - Interesting window into how insiders vs. outsiders assess
    institutional integrity — Lipschutz distinguished the institution
    from one rogue actor

### Magazine Cover Rule — Origin

- [NICE TO KNOW][N408] The **"magazine cover rule"** (when a trader or
  market appears on a major magazine cover, it's a contrary indicator
  — a signal the trend is exhausted) was proposed by **Ed Seykota**
  in *Market Wizards* (the first volume)
  - Previously noted as Observation #13 (Independence); this footnote
    confirms Seykota as the originator

### Sharpe Ratio — Technical Footnote

- [NICE TO KNOW][N409] The **Sharpe ratio** calculation technically
  requires subtracting the **risk-free return** (e.g., T-bill rate)
  from portfolio return before dividing by standard deviation
  - Schwager's footnote acknowledges that simplified presentations
    throughout the book omit this adjustment for clarity
  - Practical implication: true Sharpe ratios are slightly lower than
    the simplified calculation suggests, especially in high-rate
    environments

### Options Notation

- Pages 598–600 contain chapter-level notes directing options-unfamiliar
  readers to the Appendix; no substantive trading content
- Note on final pages: the Market Wiz(ar)dom chapter was adapted from
  **Jack Schwager**, *Market Wizards* (New York: New York Institute of
  Finance, 1989)

