## Interview: Bill Lipschutz (continued)

### The 24-Hour FX Market and Information Edge

- [MUST KNOW][N005] **FX is a 24-hour market — being plugged in
  around the clock is a structural edge, not a lifestyle choice**
  - Lipschutz was the first person at **Salomon Brothers** to have a
    **Telerate** terminal at home — colleagues thought he was crazy
  - His reasoning: "Foreign exchange is a twenty-four-hour market.
    It doesn't go to sleep when you leave at 5 P.M. The market is
    really there all night, and it moves!"
  - Implication: traders who treat FX like an exchange-hours market
    are systematically disadvantaged — the interbank market never
    closes, and significant moves happen overnight

- [MUST KNOW][N006] **Access to the interbank network is a primary
  differentiator between profitable and unprofitable FX traders**
  - "Those of us who did well were generally the ones who were
    accepted by the interbank circle"
  - Traders who stayed aloof from this network "tended to be the
    ones who couldn't make any money trading foreign exchange"
  - The contrast: isolated traders would call a clerk on the
    **Chicago Mercantile Exchange (Merc)** floor — a futures market
    that is "an active but still far smaller market than the
    interbank market" — and ask "So, how does the Swissy look?"
  - A Merc floor clerk has no meaningful insight into what is
    actually driving the international currency market
  - Lipschutz, by contrast, was talking to bankers throughout the
    day and night — in **Tokyo, London, Frankfurt, and New York**
  - Mechanism: the interbank market is where real order flow,
    central bank activity, and institutional positioning are
    visible; retail/futures-only traders see only a shadow of this

- [SHOULD KNOW][N007] **"That's what foreign exchange trading is
  all about"** — Lipschutz's direct answer when asked if he was
  trading off the information flow
  - Distills his entire FX edge to one thing: superior real-time
    information from being embedded in the global interbank network
  - Not technical analysis, not macro models in isolation — the
    flow of information from practitioners around the world

- [SHOULD KNOW][N008] **Berlin Wall case study begins** — Lipschutz
  uses the fall of the Berlin Wall as a concrete example of how
  information flow translates into a trading edge
  - General market sentiment at the time: everyone expected money
    to flood into East Germany on the ground (page continues)

<!-- page 41: 4 nuggets (24-hour FX market edge; interbank network
     access as primary differentiator; information flow as core FX
     edge; Berlin Wall case study setup) -->

<!-- pp. 42-42 -->

- [MUST KNOW][N005] **Market narrative shift: Deutsche mark / German
  reunification trade** — continuation of currency trade example
  illustrating how macro trades reverse when the underlying narrative
  changes:
  - Initial thesis: large capital flows into Eastern Europe would most
    directly benefit the **Deutsche mark** (wall comes down → DM
    strengthens)
  - Reversal trigger: realization sets in that absorbing East Germany
    into a unified Germany would take far longer than expected
  - Confirming signals accumulating: **Helmut Kohl** makes a statement;
    **James Baker** makes a comment; statistics reveal very high East
    German unemployment
  - Attitudinal evidence: East Germans (lifetime under socialism) begin
    saying "We don't want to work as hard as those West Germans, and by
    the way, how come the state is not paying for our medical bills
    anymore?" — signals deep structural drag on reunification
  - Result: investment community concludes rebuilding Eastern Europe is
    a long haul → capital starts moving OUT of the Deutsche mark
  - Mechanism: the same fundamentals that initially argued FOR the DM
    eventually argued AGAINST it once the market processed the full
    implementation complexity

- [MUST KNOW][N006] **"Being right early is the same as being wrong"
  — the narrative timing problem** — interviewer challenges: "You could
  have made all those same arguments when the wall first came down."
  - Trader's response: most people did NOT see it that way at the time,
    but more importantly — **that's not what matters**
  - What matters: **"What is important is to assess what the market is
    focusing on at the given moment"**
  - Core insight: the analytical correctness of a macro argument is
    secondary to whether the market is CURRENTLY pricing that argument
    — a trade only works when the market is ready to act on the
    narrative you've identified
  - Practical implication: early contrarian identification of a thesis
    does not create edge; edge comes from identifying WHEN the market
    shifts to pricing that thesis

- [MUST KNOW][N007] **How to know what the market is focusing on:
  talk to participants** — method for gauging current market narrative:
  - "You need to be plugged into the news and to know what the market
    is looking at"
  - Primary channel: **talking to lots of participants in the foreign
    exchange market** — not models, not charts alone, but human
    intelligence from active participants
  - Key insight: not everyone interprets information the same way at
    the same time — understanding the distribution of interpretation
    across participants IS the edge

- [SHOULD KNOW][N008] **FX market focus shifts day to day —
  interest rate differentials vs. capital appreciation** — concrete
  example of how the market's focal variable can flip entirely:
  - One day the FX market may focus on **interest rate differentials**
    (investors shift money to higher-yielding currency)
  - The next day the market may focus on **potential for capital
    appreciation** — which is exactly the OPPOSITE dynamic (a currency
    expected to appreciate attracts capital regardless of yield)
  - A focus on interest rate differentials implies investors shift to
    the higher-rate currency; a focus on capital appreciation implies
    the opposite currency may be bid up
  - Practical implication: the same macro data point (e.g. a rate
    hike) can be bullish OR bearish for a currency depending on which
    lens the market is currently applying — knowing the active lens is
    the critical variable

<!-- page 42: 4 nuggets (DM reunification narrative reversal;
     narrative timing — what market focuses on matters more than
     analytical correctness; talk-to-participants method for gauging
     focus; FX market focus shifts between interest rate differentials
     and capital appreciation — opposite implications) -->

<!-- pp. 43-43 -->

## [Continuation — Currency Trader Interview]

### Capital Appreciation vs. Yield in Currency Investing

- [SHOULD KNOW][N005] **Yield vs. capital appreciation split in
  currency strategy** — closing context from previous page:
  - Yield-focused investors chase **highest interest rate countries**
    (industrialized nations with high yields)
  - Capital appreciation-focused investors place money in countries
    with **strongest economic and political outlooks** — which
    typically have *lower* interest rates
  - Practical implication: the two objectives can point in opposite
    directions; traders must know which objective governs their
    position

---

### Early Currency Options Arbitrage Trade (1983)

- [MUST KNOW][N006] **Mispriced deep-in-the-money option arbitrage
  — Philadelphia Stock Exchange, 1983** — one of the earliest and
  clearest examples of exploiting a specialist's arithmetic error:
  - Context: **very early days of currency options trading** on the
    **Philadelphia Stock Exchange**; daily volume in the market was
    only ~2–3 hundred contracts total
  - A specialist was quoting a particular **deep-in-the-money option**
    ~100 points off fair value (underpriced)
  - The trader bought 50 contracts, immediately sold the underlying
    currency market, and **locked in a risk-free profit**
  - **Why deep-in-the-money options are nearly equivalent to
    outright contracts**: they behave like the underlying position
    but carry limited downside if the market makes an extreme move —
    maximum risk is theoretically capped
  - After confirming the specialist was still offering at the same
    wrong price, the trader kept buying in lots of 50: another 50,
    another 50, another 50
  - **Goldman Sachs** noticed and also bought 50 — institutional
    validation that the mispricing was real
  - By the time the specialist realized his error, he had sold
    **300–400 contracts** into a market where normal daily volume
    was only 200–300 — he had sold multiples of the entire daily
    market in a single session
  - The specialist thought he had a **locked-in arbitrage profit**
    but had done his arithmetic wrong — the trader knew exactly
    what was happening and the specialist did not
  - Trader escalated: asked broker to offer the specialist a chance
    to do **1,000 contracts** at the same price — the specialist
    backed off but was still ~100 points off in his quote
  - Story ends mid-page: specialist called the trader directly,
    said "What are you doing?" — trader responded "What are *you*
    doing?" (implying the specialist was the one making the error)
  - **Key lessons**:
    - Early/illiquid markets create outsized mispricings because
      specialists lack sophisticated pricing tools or experience
    - Recognizing *why* a price is wrong and having the conviction
      to scale aggressively into it is the edge — most traders
      would have taken the first 50 and stopped
    - The fact that Goldman Sachs followed signals external
      validation; but the original trader was already multiple
      rounds in before they arrived
    - Risk-free arbitrage exists briefly in new markets; the window
      closes as participants gain sophistication

