<!-- pp. 44-44 -->

## (Continuing interview — unnamed Salomon Brothers trader / options market maker)

### The Broken Trade Decision

- [MUST KNOW][N005] **Letting a specialist off the hook — long-term
  business reasoning over short-term profit**
  - Context: a specialist had made a catastrophic pricing error on
    currency options (off by a "big figure"), resulting in ~1,000
    contracts traded at wrong prices; **Goldman Sachs** held all 150
    of their contracts, refusing to break any
  - Trader broke all trades after the first 50 — forfeiting a large
    windfall gain — reasoning: holding the specialist to the full
    trade "would put him out of business," which would be:
    1. Bad for the exchange
    2. Terrible for the **currency options** product, which was "just
       beginning to trade in a significant way" — i.e., market
       ecosystem health mattered more than one score
  - Long-term consequence of Goldman NOT breaking: years later, after
    the specialist company failed, the individual specialist became
    head trader for the **largest market maker on the floor** — and
    "always made it very difficult for Goldman on the floor"
  - Mechanism: **reciprocity and reputation compound over time** in
    tight professional markets; a one-time gain can generate
    persistent adversarial friction worth far more in aggregate losses
  - Practical implication: in illiquid or specialist markets, how you
    treat counterparties in extremis defines your long-term dealing
    relationships — the "hard-assed" reputation that works in
    normal trading can become a liability when it prevents strategic
    mercy

- [SHOULD KNOW][N006] **Internal conflict: reputation vs. judgment**
  - The trader describes himself as having "a reputation as being one
    of the most — if not the most — hard-assed players in the market"
  - His consistent rule: *never* cut anybody a break, because at
    **Salomon Brothers** everyone was trying to knock them off; assumed
    if tables were reversed, no one would give Salomon a break
  - Yet he broke the trades anyway — and "agonized over it for years
    afterward"
  - This tension illustrates: **rules work until they don't** —
    even deeply held operating principles require situational judgment
    when systemic consequences outweigh the personal rule
  - Caveats: he framed the break as a "long-term business decision,
    which I didn't think about a lot at the time" — suggesting the
    decision was partly intuitive, rationalized afterward; the
    agonizing came from the clash with his identity, not regret about
    the outcome

<!-- page 44: 2 nuggets (broken trade decision — ecosystem reasoning
     over profit; internal conflict between hard-assed reputation and
     strategic mercy) -->

<!-- pp. 45-45 -->

## Bill Lipschutz Interview (continued)

### Back-Office Operations Philosophy

- [MUST KNOW][N005] **Zero-error back-office standard** — Lipschutz
  operated with an explicit policy of zero back-office errors, achieved
  through extensive fail-safe systems rather than accepting errors as
  inevitable:
  - When Merrill Lynch asked how much to budget for back-office errors,
    Lipschutz answered: "Zero. We don't have back-office errors."
  - Merrill reacted with disbelief ("of course you have back-office
    errors") — Lipschutz's counterpoint: "If you put in enough
    fail-safes, you don't make errors"
  - Practical mechanism: "We've put a lot of money and thought into our
    back-office operations. We've instituted numerous fail-safe
    measures."
  - Real-world test: in all his time at Salomon, they never missed an
    options expiration — not once
  - Contrast to counterparties: traders would call late on expired OTC
    options with excuses ("I tried to get through earlier," "I
    forgot," "just a few minutes late") — Lipschutz held the line
    because he knew if Salomon called late, no one would grant them
    an exception
  - Key principle: **reciprocity-based rule enforcement** — only hold
    yourself to rules you'd face from the other side

- [SHOULD KNOW][N006] **Rule-following as identity, not policy** —
  Lipschutz's colleagues would say "why do you have to be such a
  hard-ass about everything?" His answer: "These are the rules; that's
  the way the game is played"
  - This rigidity made the decision to let the specialist off the hook
    (discussed in prior pages) especially notable — it was explicitly
    "very much out of character" for him
  - The specialist exception was a **long-term business decision**:
    holding him to the trade would have been bad for business
    (protecting a fledgling exchange/product relationship), not a
    softening of his principles

<!-- page 45: 2 nuggets (zero back-office errors philosophy; rule-following as identity vs. the specialist exception as deliberate long-term business decision) -->

<!-- pp. 46-46 -->

## Interview Continuation: Currency Options / Philadelphia Stock Exchange

### Self-Interest as the Real Motive for Honoring a Bad Trade

- [SHOULD KNOW][N005] **Honoring an erroneous trade = marketplace
  self-preservation, not altruism**
  - Context: interviewee's currency options business was "exploding"
    and the **Philadelphia Stock Exchange** was the primary venue
    (OTC currency options market was only just starting at the time)
  - When asked if honoring the bad trade was done "to protect the
    exchange," the interviewee corrects: *"No, I did it to protect me."*
  - Clarified as protecting *his* marketplace — the exchange was his
    business infrastructure; its survival was his survival
  - Explicit hypothetical test: if the exchange had been established
    for ten years with huge volume and the trade would not have
    affected its survival, he would have made a **different decision**
    — i.e., would NOT have honored it
  - Punchline: *"It wasn't charity."*
  - Practical implication: professional ethics in markets are often
    rationally self-interested, not purely principled — understanding
    the incentive structure behind "honorable" behavior reveals when
    it will and won't apply

- [SHOULD KNOW][N006] **Three-question due-diligence protocol before
  accepting a counterparty's bad price**
  - Even while deciding to honor the trade, the interviewee told the
    broker to run three checks on the other party:
    1. *"Ask him to check his price."*
    2. *"Ask him if he is sure."*
    3. *"Ask him if he wants to do another fifty."*
  - The third question is the tell: offering to do another 50
    contracts at the same erroneous price is a classic market test —
    if the counterparty eagerly accepts more, they either don't know
    it's wrong or think they have an edge; if they hesitate, the
    error is exposed
  - This protocol gives the counterparty every chance to catch their
    own mistake before being held to it — simultaneously generous and
    strategically revealing
  - Key mechanism: the willingness to "do another fifty" at that
    price signals whether you genuinely believe the price is fair or
    are exploiting an error

<!-- page 46: 2 nuggets (self-interest as motive for honoring bad
     trade; three-question broker protocol to test counterparty
     awareness of pricing error) -->

<!-- pp. 47-47 -->

## Interview: Bill Lipschutz (continued)

### Interbank Market — Erroneous Quotes and Professional Conduct

- [SHOULD KNOW][N005] **"Honest attempt" convention for off-market quotes**
  - In fast-moving interbank markets, dealers sometimes quote a price
    one full **big figure** off (e.g., 1.9140 instead of 1.9240)
  - Convention: the receiving party must give the dealer a chance to
    correct the error — e.g., "1.9140. Are you sure? Please check
    your price."
  - **If the dealer confirms** ("Yeah, yeah, yeah, I'm sure. Do you
    want to deal or don't you?") — the quote stands and the trade
    must be honored
  - Key principle: the obligation to honor an erroneous quote is
    conditioned on the quoting party being given an explicit
    opportunity to correct it and then affirming the price anyway
  - Practical implication: in OTC interbank FX, there is no exchange
    backstop — professional norms and bilateral trust govern error
    resolution

- [SHOULD KNOW][N006] **Lipschutz's real-world experience with
  erroneous quotes — refusing to cancel or split**
  - Lipschutz confirms this has happened to him personally; every
    time, the other institution came back wanting to either cancel the
    trade or **split the difference**
  - He refused both — because he had already asked them to verify the
    price before dealing
  - Implication: professional traders enforce their contractual and
    conventional rights strictly; compassion or accommodation on
    pricing errors is not standard — if you followed the protocol, the
    trade stands
  - This illustrates a harder edge to interbank professionalism:
    courtesy (checking the price) is required; charity (giving back
    windfall gains) is not

### Editorial Note — Off-the-Record Segment

- [NICE TO KNOW][N007] **Off-the-record digression during meal break**
  - During a break for Chinese food, the interview continues
    informally; one topic is explicitly stated to be off the record
    because it contains references to a specific exchange
  - Schwager notes he eventually persuaded Lipschutz to allow
    inclusion — the page ends mid-sentence on this transition
  - Signals that the subsequent section contains more candid, pointed
    commentary than the formal Q&A

