## Anonymous Trader Interview (continued)

### Stop-Gunning in Exchange-Traded Markets

- [MUST KNOW][N005] **Stop-gunning is real but unpredictable in
  direction** — the Salomon trader anecdote illustrates that floor
  traders will run stops opportunistically, but they choose WHICH
  stops to gun based on ease of execution, not which direction the
  rumor pointed:
  - Setup: market in narrow range, liquidity drying up; large cluster
    of stops had accumulated ~40-50 ticks **above** the market
  - Clerk tip: floor talk was they would "gun for the stops above
    the market" on a low-liquidity holiday session the next day
  - Trader's plan: sell heavily once upside stops were hit, expecting
    the breakout to be artificial and the market to reverse
  - What actually happened: market moved **down** 50, then 100, then
    200+ points — floor traders went for the stops **below** the
    market instead (200 points away), ignoring the upside stops (only
    50 ticks away)
  - Key lesson: even when you have advance intelligence about
    stop-gunning intentions, the floor can and will pivot to the
    easier or more profitable target — the direction of the raid is
    not guaranteed

- [SHOULD KNOW][N006] **Low-liquidity sessions amplify stop-run risk**
  — the episode specifically occurred on a day when cash market
  holiday reduced liquidity substantially below normal
  - Mechanism: thin markets require less capital to move prices to
    stop levels; floor traders exploit holiday/thin sessions
    deliberately because the cost of running stops is lower
  - Practical implication: be especially cautious holding stops near
    clustered levels on known low-liquidity days (holidays, early
    closes, option expiry thin periods)

- [SHOULD KNOW][N007] **Interbank and specialist-system exchanges vs.
  pit exchanges** — the anonymous trader notes he does "very little
  trading on exchanges with trading pits," preferring the
  **interbank market** and the **Philadelphia Exchange** (specialist
  system)
  - Implicit claim: pit-traded markets are more vulnerable to
    deliberate stop-gunning because floor traders have informational
    and positional advantages over off-floor participants
  - Specialist systems and OTC interbank markets have different
    (though not absent) dynamics around stop visibility

<!-- page 48: 3 nuggets (stop-gunning direction unpredictability —
     Salomon anecdote; low-liquidity amplification of stop-run risk;
     pit vs. interbank/specialist market vulnerability) -->

<!-- pp. 49-49 -->

### Fast Market Conditions and Floor Broker Execution

- [MUST KNOW][N005] **"Fast market" designation as execution shield** —
  when an exchange officially declares **fast market** conditions,
  floor brokers are legally exempt from being held responsible for
  failing to fill orders that were within the day's traded price range
  - Mechanism: during rapid price movement, a trading pit can
    simultaneously trade at several different prices at the same
    moment — floor brokers can legally look past bids/offers and
    claim they couldn't get fills
  - Practical implication: a trader bidding aggressively at multiple
    price levels (100–200 points below market) during a sharp break
    may get filled on only a fraction of their order (e.g., 50
    contracts when bidding for hundreds) even though thousands of
    lots traded at those exact levels
  - The interviewee's acid summary: *"A fast market gives the floor
    brokers a special license to steal, above and beyond their normal
    license to steal"* — framed as opinion, not allegation, since
    proving intent is impossible

- [SHOULD KNOW][N006] **Crowded-long setup → downside vulnerability**
  — the setup context for the sharp break described:
  - Everyone was positioned long in anticipation of a rally that
    would take out upside stops
  - Because the long side was crowded, the direction of **greatest
    price vulnerability** was actually to the downside
  - When the break came, it was sharp and overextended — creating
    the buying opportunity the interviewee's friend tried to exploit
  - Lesson: consensus positioning reveals where the market is most
    fragile; the crowded trade creates asymmetric risk in the
    opposite direction

> "A fast market gives the floor brokers a special license to steal,
> above and beyond their normal license to steal."
> — Interview subject (unnamed friend anecdote)

<!-- page 49: 2 nuggets (fast market execution shield mechanic;
     crowded-long → downside vulnerability setup) -->

<!-- pp. 50-50 -->

## Mispriced Bond Arbitrage Trade (continued)

- [MUST KNOW][N005] **Classic arbitrage: mispriced dual-currency bond**
  — interviewee describes a bond issued by **Salomon Brothers** (one of
  the lead underwriters) that was "grossly mispriced," allowing for
  redemption in either **sterling (British pounds)** or **U.S. dollars**
  - The mispricing was so obvious the interviewee "wanted to buy the
    whole issue"
  - **Mechanism of the mispricing:**
    - At the time, **U.K. interest rates were significantly lower than
      U.S. rates**
    - When two countries have different interest rates, forward months
      of the **lower-rate currency** invariably trade at a **premium to
      the spot rate** — because if they didn't, arbitrageurs would
      borrow cheaply in the low-rate country, invest at higher rates
      abroad, and hedge back with forward currency, closing the gap
    - **Interest rate parity** enforces this: forward premiums on the
      lower-rate currency exactly offset the interest rate differential
  - **The flaw**: the bond was priced as if sterling's redemption option
    assumed **no premium over spot**, despite forward sterling trading
    at a **huge premium**
  - **The trade**: buy the bond, sell sterling forward at the large
    premium — over the bond's life, the forward rate converges to spot,
    locking in the premium as profit regardless of rate moves
  - **Bond structure**: matured in four tranches — 5, 7, 9, and 12
    years — giving a laddered exposure to the arbitrage

- [SHOULD KNOW][N006] **Interest rate parity footnote (mechanism)**
  — Schwager inserts an explanatory bracket clarifying the forward
  currency premium rule:
  - If forward premiums on the lower-rate currency did NOT exist,
    a risk-free arbitrage would be available: borrow in low-rate
    country → convert → invest in high-rate country → hedge currency
    risk with forward purchase of low-rate currency
  - Arbitrageurs eliminate this gap, ensuring forward premiums exactly
    offset interest rate differentials — a foundational concept in
    international finance and currency markets
  - Practical implication: when a structured product fails to reflect
    forward pricing correctly, it creates a locked-in edge — not a
    speculative bet but a **mathematical certainty of profit**

<!-- page 50: 2 nuggets (dual-currency bond mispricing trade — Salomon
     Brothers underpricing; sterling forward premium arbitrage; interest
     rate parity mechanism explained) -->

<!-- pp. 51-51 -->

### Mispriced Sterling Bond Issue — Acquisition Story (continued)

- [SHOULD KNOW][N005] **Long-dated currency hedging mechanics** —
  interviewer expresses surprise that a currency can be hedged "that
  far forward"; interviewee clarifies two routes exist:
  - **Forward market**: for liquid pairs like **sterling/dollar**,
    term forward markets existed out to at least **ten years**
  - **Interest rate swap**: if a forward market is unavailable or
    illiquid, the same economic position can be constructed synthetically
  - Practical implication: liquidity in currency hedging extends much
    further than most traders assume; exotic or illiquid pairs can still
    be hedged via swap structures

- [SHOULD KNOW][N006] **Bond issue size** — two tranches:
  **$100 million** (first) and **$50 million** (second), total
  **$150 million** outstanding

- [MUST KNOW][N007] **Institutional resistance to mispricing
  identification** — when the interviewee flagged the issue as
  "grossly mispriced," the initial institutional response was
  **disbelief and pushback**, not action:
  - Firm spent **nine hours** running the analysis past every
    **"quant jock in the house"** before accepting he was right
  - Key insight: large institutions default to assuming an outside
    analyst is wrong rather than that their own pricing models missed
    something; being right early requires persistence through
    bureaucratic skepticism
  - This is a recurring Market Wizards pattern: **the edge often
    exists precisely because the market/institution resists accepting
    the correct price**

- [SHOULD KNOW][N008] **Execution friction eroded the opportunity** —
  by the time institutional approval came through, **$50 million of
  the first tranche had already been sold**; the interviewee then:
  - Spent **one to two years** patiently bidding in the **secondary
    market** with a standing bid always in place
  - Used a **salesperson contact** who knew where the original issue
    had been placed to locate sellers
  - Ultimately acquired **$135 million of the $150 million
    outstanding** — capturing **90% of the total issue**
  - Lesson: when conviction is high on a mispriced instrument,
    maintaining a persistent bid over an extended period can
    accumulate a position even after the primary window closes

