## Continuation: British Pound Forward Sterling Trade (Interview — Bill Lipschutz or similar currency trader)

### Forward Market Hedge Mechanics

- [MUST KNOW][N005] **Synthetic call/put structure via forward market**:
  after buying a bond issue redeemable in sterling, the trader
  immediately sold 50% of the total amount in the **forward sterling
  market** to hedge currency risk
  - Spot rate (= bond redemption rate): **$1.3470**
  - Seven-year forward sterling: ~**$1.47**
  - Twelve-year forward sterling: ~**$1.60**
  - Forward pound was at a **large premium** to spot — selling forward
    at premium locked in favorable exchange rate above redemption price
  - Structural result: selling half in forward market converted that
    half into a **proxy put on the British pound**, while the original
    bond issue was effectively a **call position**
  - Net position: **half call, half put** — but the put was established
    at a much higher price than the call
  - This **price gap = locked-in profit** with potential for even
    greater profit if forward pound moved below $1.3470 OR above $1.47
    (seven-year tranche)
  - Practical implication: combining a cash instrument with a forward
    sale can synthetically create an options-like structure with an
    embedded profit floor — this is a classic cross-instrument
    structural arbitrage

### Trade Outcome

- [SHOULD KNOW][N006] **Resolution — U.K./U.S. interest rate reversal**:
  U.K. interest rates eventually reversed from **below** to **above**
  U.S. rates, causing British pound forward rates to invert from a
  **premium** to a **discount** to spot
  - When forward rates inverted, the trader covered the whole position
    at a **huge profit**
  - Mechanism: the rate differential reversal eliminated the forward
    premium that had been locked in on the short side, compressing
    forward prices — the short forward position gained as forward GBP
    fell relative to where it was sold

### G-7 September 1985 Trade Setup

- [MUST KNOW][N007] **Plaza Accord / G-7 September 1985 meeting** —
  identified as one of the most memorable trades in the interviewee's
  career:
  - The **G-7 meeting in September 1985** involved "major structural
    changes that set the tone in the currency markets for the next
    five years"
  - Schwager footnote clarifies: this was the meeting at which **major
    industrialized nations agreed to a coordinated policy aimed at
    lowering the value of the dollar** — the **Plaza Accord**
  - Context: this was a watershed macro event; traders positioned
    correctly ahead of or at the announcement captured enormous
    multi-year currency trends
  - Interviewer follow-up question ("You were obviously very closely
    tied into the currency markets. Did you have any idea that such a
    major policy change was at hand?") signals that the next page will
    reveal how the trader anticipated or reacted to this event —
    suggesting either anticipatory positioning or rapid read of the
    announcement

