### The Plaza Accord — Market Reaction and Information Lag

- [SHOULD KNOW][N005] **Markets initially failed to price the Plaza Accord correctly** —
  even after the G-7 meeting results were publicly reported, the dollar's
  reaction was muted and misleading:
  - Initial sell-off in New Zealand and Australia
  - Dollar then **rebounded modestly in Tokyo** — the opposite of what
    the coordinated policy was designed to produce
  - The massive dollar decline that followed occurred in **ensuing months**,
    not immediately — the full magnitude was not priced in at the announcement
  - Practical implication: major policy shifts that eventually move markets
    enormously can look like non-events at first; initial price action
    after a fundamental catalyst can be deceptive

- [SHOULD KNOW][N006] **Crowd skepticism as a contrarian setup** — the market's
  indifferent reaction was explained by conditioned disbelief:
  - General attitude at the time: *"Oh, another central bank intervention"*
  - This meeting followed **years of ineffective central bank intervention**,
    so traders discounted it as more of the same
  - Key mechanism: repeated failed interventions train the market to ignore
    the next one — even when it is structurally different
  - The trader who recognized the qualitative difference (coordinated
    multilateral policy vs. unilateral intervention) had an enormous edge
    precisely because consensus was dismissive

- [SHOULD KNOW][N007] **What made the G-7 Plaza Accord different** — the
  interviewee identifies the distinguishing feature:
  - *"This was the first time that we saw a coordinated policy statement
    from the seven industrialized nations"*
  - Prior interventions were unilateral or poorly coordinated — this was
    genuinely novel in scale and political commitment
  - Implication: when evaluating policy events, the question is not
    "has this happened before?" but "is this structurally different from
    prior attempts?" — novelty of coordination was the key variable

- [NICE TO KNOW][N008] **Interviewee was in Sardinia during the G-7 meeting** —
  anecdote illustrating the information environment of the era:
  - Had taken a rare vacation after a very good year
  - Sardinia described as "fairly isolated" — making an overseas call
    took approximately two hours
  - He did not even know what the G-7 was at the time of the meeting
  - The meeting initially seemed like *"just a bunch of bureaucrats getting
    together to talk down the value of the dollar"* — no special significance
    apparent in real time
  - Retrospective lesson: the trades that produce outsized returns are
    often ones where the significance of a development is only clear in
    hindsight; being physically or informationally isolated from the noise
    did not prevent capturing the opportunity

