## Bill Lipschutz Interview (continued)

### Andy's New Zealand Trade — Exploiting Thin Markets and Relationships

- [MUST KNOW][N005] **Trading thin markets through established
  relationships** — when the Plaza Accord / major currency news broke
  on a weekend, **Andy** (Lipschutz's colleague) went into the
  **New Zealand dollar market**, the first currency market to open
  after the weekend:
  - Very few participants traded that time zone; market was extremely
    thin — price quotes barely available to most traders
  - **Salomon Brothers** had established relationships there because
    they regularly traded $20–$50 million on Monday mornings in New
    Zealand — a volume so consistent that NZ banks welcomed their
    calls even in off-hours
  - Key mechanism: **relationship capital built through consistent
    volume** created access that competitors (New Yorkers, Europeans)
    simply did not have — this is a structural edge, not an
    analytical one
  - Practical implication: institutions that regularly trade in
    off-hours or illiquid markets gain privileged access precisely
    when those markets matter most (i.e., during crises)

- [MUST KNOW][N006] **Hitting bids aggressively into a confused,
  wide-spread market** — Andy sold **$60 million** in New Zealand
  dollars into spreads that were "literally two big figures wide
  because everyone was confused":
  - The reconstructed conversation shows Andy systematically hitting
    bids and continuing to ask "how do you remain?" — pulling the
    market down six big figures below Friday's close
  - Lipschutz: *"Andy was hitting bids six big figures below
    Friday's close. I was really impressed that he had that type
    of insight. I wouldn't have done that."*
  - Key insight: Lipschutz — himself a master currency trader —
    openly admits he would NOT have taken that trade; Andy's read
    of the market's confusion and willingness to hit thin bids
    aggressively was a distinct, superior judgment call
  - Counterargument/caveat: this only works when you have size and
    relationships allowing execution; retail traders cannot replicate
    this in thin illiquid markets

### Crisis Trading Infrastructure — Improvised Coordination Under Pressure

- [SHOULD KNOW][N007] **Six-hour open phone line across three
  continents** — Lipschutz managed the position from a hotel room
  in **Sardinia** while Andy was home sick in **Englewood, New
  Jersey**, coordinating across:
  - Open line: Sardinia hotel ↔ Andy (flat on his back, sick)
  - Andy's second open line: to the floor of the **Philadelphia
    Stock Exchange** (currency options trading)
  - Third line: borrowed from a neighbor's house to reach their
    **spot dollar/mark broker** for cash market trading
  - Andy's wife was dispatched to **Radio Shack** to buy extra-long
    telephone cord extensions to physically make this work
  - They traded this improvised three-line setup for six hours
  - Lesson: **execution infrastructure under crisis conditions** —
    world-class traders find creative, ad hoc solutions to stay
    connected to markets when normal infrastructure fails; commitment
    to the trade trumps comfort

