  position, sized and timed correctly, can dominate a year's P&L
  - Context: Lipschutz was trading from a **luxury resort in Sardinia**
    frequented by wealthy Europeans — illustrates that elite trading
    requires no physical proximity to markets; presence + information
    edge matters more than location
  - Humorous detail: two "industrial magnate" German men kept
    interrupting his room every ten minutes asking (via his wife
    translating) what was happening in FX — they sensed something
    significant was moving but had no access to real-time information;
    Sardinia's isolation meant all newspapers were at least two days old
  - Practical implication: **information edge** — being plugged in
    while others are two days behind is a structural advantage, even
    in a pre-internet world

### 1987 Japanese Yen Bull Call Spread — "Virtual Poker Game"

- [MUST KNOW][N006] **Massive bull call spread in Japanese yen, 1987**:
  - Position: long **23,000** Japanese yen 54 calls, short **23,000**
    yen 55 calls
  - At expiration in-the-money, **each side of the spread** would
    represent nearly **$800 million** — described as "an enormous
    position at the time"
  - When initiated, the calls were **well out of the money** — a
    speculative, high-conviction directional bet structured as a spread
    to reduce cost

- [MUST KNOW][N007] **Bull call spread mechanics** (Schwager's
  bracketed editorial explanation):
  - Structure: long lower-strike call + short equal-size higher-strike
    call on same underlying / expiry
  - **Profit condition**: underlying must rise above the lower strike
    (54) by enough to at least offset the net cost of the spread
  - **Profit cap**: maximum profit capped at **one full point per
    contract** — the long 54 call's upside is offset by the short 55
    call above 55
  - **Cost reduction**: selling the 55 call generates premium income
    that **substantially reduces** the cost vs. buying the 54 call
    outright — the core trade-off: you give up unlimited upside to
    dramatically lower your breakeven
  - **Max profit formula**: one full point ($625 per contract) minus
    the net cost of the spread at initiation
  - **Max profit trigger**: realized at **any price above 55** at
    expiration — not just at 55, anywhere above
  - Given 23,000 contracts, maximum gross profit = ~$14.375M minus
    spread cost (implied by the numbers described)

