## Continuation: Large Options Spread Trade (Bull Call Spread — Nikkei/Tokyo Market)

### The "Between Strikes" Risk at Expiration

- [MUST KNOW][N005] **The binary assignment problem** — the critical
  hidden risk of a large bull call spread at expiration:
  - If the market settles **between** the two strikes (in this case,
    between 54 and 55), the **long 54 call** is in the money and will
    be exercised, but the **short 55 call** is NOT exercised — so
    there is no offsetting call-away
  - Result: trader ends up net **long ~$800 million** in the
    underlying over the weekend (Tokyo market doesn't open until
    Sunday night) — extreme unhedged overnight gap risk
  - Even though this scenario implies a profit at expiration (long
    call is in the money), the **weekend carry risk** is enormous and
    unacceptable at that position size
  - Mechanism: exercise is automatic on the long side; assignment on
    the short side only occurs if the counterparty exercises — and
    near-the-money shorts may or may not be exercised

### Why Hedging Near Expiration Is Impossible at This Scale

- [MUST KNOW][N006] **The expiration hedge paradox** — hedging a
  massive near-the-money spread near expiration creates a no-win
  binary dilemma:
  - **Scenario A**: You DON'T hedge the long 54 call (assuming market
    will expire above 55, triggering the short 55 call exercise) →
    market closes BELOW 55 → you carry a massive net long position
    over the weekend with no offsetting short
  - **Scenario B**: You DO hedge the long 54 call (assuming market
    will close below 55) → market closes ABOVE 55, short 55 call IS
    exercised → you end up net **short the entire face amount** over
    the weekend
  - Core insight: **the binary uncertainty of whether the short call
    will be exercised makes it structurally impossible to hedge
    correctly** — hedging is a bet on expiration outcome, not a
    true risk reduction
  - This is a distinct risk category from directional or vol risk —
    it's **assignment uncertainty risk**, which cannot be resolved
    until settlement

- [SHOULD KNOW][N007] **Bid/offer spread cost at scale** — a second
  reason hedging is impractical: liquidating **23,000 contracts** in
  the final hours of trading would require paying away a tremendous
  amount in bid/offer spread to exit a position of that size
  - Practical implication: liquidity risk compounds assignment
    uncertainty risk at extreme position sizes — the two together
    make near-expiration risk management nearly intractable
  - The position was so large that even trying to unwind it would
    move the market against the trader

### Counterparty Concentration Risk

- [SHOULD KNOW][N008] **Single market maker on the other side of
  ~20,000 lots** — one particularly notable element of the trade:
  a single market maker was the counterparty to approximately 20,000
  of the ~23,000 contract spread position
  - (Page cuts off — context suggests this introduces a counterparty
    concentration / conflict-of-interest dynamic to be continued)
  - Implication flagged for follow-up: when one counterparty holds
    that much of the opposite side, their hedging behavior and
    exercise decisions near expiration can directly influence the
    settlement outcome — a structural conflict

<!-- page 57: 4 nuggets (between-strikes assignment risk; expiration
     hedge paradox — binary assignment uncertainty; bid/offer
     liquidation cost at 23k contracts; single market maker
     counterparty on ~20k lots) -->

