## Interview: Bill Eckhardt / Turtle Traders (continued)

### Richard Dennis Interview Anecdote

- [SHOULD KNOW][N005] **The Dennis interview naivety anecdote** —
  the interviewee (a Turtle candidate) recalls being unusual among
  applicants in knowing virtually nothing about **Richard Dennis**,
  who was already famous as one of the world's great technical traders
  - During the interview, candidate asked: *"Do you trade the markets
    fundamentally or technically?"* — an unknowingly naive question
    to Dennis
  - Dennis answered: *"We trade technically."*
  - Candidate followed up: *"Is fundamental analysis dead?"*
  - Dennis replied, with a smile: *"We certainly hope not."*
  - Implication: Dennis's humor signals that fundamental traders
    provide the "wrong side" of trades — technical traders **need**
    fundamental believers to be on the other side of their positions;
    fundamental analysis's persistence is good for technicians

### Turtle Selection — Experience Distribution

- [SHOULD KNOW][N006] **Turtle program applicant experience mix** —
  of the thirteen people ultimately selected for the Turtle program:
  - One-third had **no** trading experience
  - One-third had **significant** trading experience
  - One-third had **a little** trading experience
  - Key implication: Dennis deliberately did NOT filter for experience;
    the experiment was partly about whether trading skill could be
    taught regardless of background — and the balanced mix was
    intentional

### Core Psychological Lesson from Turtle Training

- [MUST KNOW][N007] **"Don't worry about what the markets will do;
  worry about what YOU will do in response"** — described as a
  "nugget of advice valuable to anyone trading the markets"
  - This reframes trading psychology entirely: markets are
    uncontrollable; the only controllable variable is your own
    response/decision process
  - Practical implication: traders who obsess over market prediction
    are focused on the wrong variable — systematic traders focus
    entirely on pre-defining their own responses via rules, so
    in-the-moment emotion is removed from the equation
  - This is consistent with the Turtle system philosophy: rules
    specify the response in advance, so you never need to predict
    what markets will do

