## Blackjack Card Counting Interview (continued)

### Team Entry Requirements and Skill Gaps

- [SHOULD KNOW][N005] **Blackjack team had a formal testing/qualification process** —
  the interviewee (a skilled player by self-assessment) initially
  **failed the team's entry test**, forcing deliberate skill improvement
  before gaining membership
  - Shortcomings identified were multi-dimensional: flaws in **basic
    strategy**, insufficient **counting speed**, and inaccurate **deck
    size estimation**
  - Practical implication: elite trading/edge-seeking teams set
    objective performance bars — self-assessed skill is unreliable;
    structured testing reveals actual gaps

### Deck Size Estimation

- [SHOULD KNOW][N006] **Estimating remaining deck size is a distinct,
  trainable skill** — casinos typically used **four-deck shoes**; the
  team trained on **eight different deck sizes in half-deck increments**
  - Players practiced until they could identify remaining deck size
    **from across the room** — a purely perceptual, repeatable skill
    built through deliberate practice
  - Market analogy: position sizing and edge calculation depend on
    knowing "how much game is left" — misjudging remaining opportunity
    distorts bet sizing just as misjudging remaining deck distorts card
    count normalization

### Card Counting Method: Revere Advance Point Count

- [MUST KNOW][N007] **Revere Advance Point Count** — the specific
  counting system used by the team; assigns weighted values to each
  card rank:
  - Twos, threes, sixes → **+2**
  - Fours → **+3**
  - Fives → **+4**
  - Sevens → **+1**
  - Eights → **0**
  - Nines → **−2**
  - Tens → **−3**
  - **Aces kept as a separate count** (not folded into the running
    total)
  - **Higher count = more high cards remaining undealt = more
    favorable odds for players**
  - The system is more granular than simple Hi-Lo (+1/−1) — fives
    carry the highest positive weight (+4) because their removal most
    dramatically shifts odds toward the house; tens carry the highest
    negative weight (−3) because their concentration most favors
    players
  - Market analogy: not all signals carry equal weight — a
    sophisticated edge-tracking system assigns differential importance
    to different indicators, just as Revere assigns differential point
    values rather than treating all cards as equal

### Count Normalization (question introduced, answer on next page)

- [SHOULD KNOW][N008] **Raw count must be standardized by cards
  remaining** — the question "did the count have to be standardized by
  the number of cards remaining?" signals the concept of the **true
  count**: a raw running count means little without normalizing for how
  many cards are left in the shoe
  - A +12 count with 2 decks remaining is less significant than +12
    with half a deck remaining — the true count adjusts for this
  - Market parallel: an absolute signal reading means less without
    context of how much of the move has already occurred or how much
    volatility/liquidity remains

<!-- page 442: 4 nuggets (team entry test + failure; deck size
     estimation as trainable perceptual skill; Revere Advance Point
     Count card weights with full breakdown; true count normalization
     concept introduced) -->

<!-- pp. 443-443 -->

## Blackjack Card Counting — True Count, System Evolution, Execution

### True Count Calculation

- [MUST KNOW][N005] **True count = raw count ÷ decks remaining** —
  normalizes the running count for how much of the shoe is left:
  - Raw count 10, two decks remaining → true count = **+5**
  - Raw count 10, half deck remaining → true count = **+20**
  - What actually matters is the **density of high cards in the
    undealt deck**, not the absolute raw count — the true count
    captures this density directly
  - Market analogy: like adjusting a signal for the size of the
    opportunity set; a signal means more when fewer alternatives
    remain

### Thorp's Counting System Evolution

- [SHOULD KNOW][N006] **Edward Thorp's counting system evolved
  across two books**:
  - First book: simple **ten vs. non-ten count** (binary split)
  - Second book: more granular — twos through sixes = **+1**;
    sevens, eights, nines = **neutral (0)**; tens and aces = **−1**
  - The **Revere Advance Point Count** (discussed in prior pages)
    was a further refinement — a more sophisticated version of the
    Thorp approach
  - Progression illustrates how edge-seeking systems iterate toward
    greater precision over time

### Accuracy vs. Complexity Trade-off

- [MUST KNOW][N007] **Fundamental trade-off: accuracy vs. execution
  error** — even the Revere system is not the theoretically optimal
  solution per probability theory, but complexity has a real cost:
  - More complicated counting methods → **more counting errors**
    under real-world casino conditions
  - The optimal system in theory is NOT optimal in practice if
    execution degrades
  - **Practical implication for trading**: a more sophisticated
    model that a trader cannot execute faithfully is worse than a
    simpler model executed with discipline — model complexity must
    be matched to the operator's capacity

### Keeping the Count Under Pressure

- [SHOULD KNOW][N008] **True count tracking requires practice,
  discipline, and mental shortcuts**:
  - Not just memorization — practitioners develop **chunked
    pattern recognition**: e.g., seeing a five (+4) and a ten (−3)
    together, the combination is immediately read as **net +1**
    (plus) without sequential arithmetic
  - This mirrors how expert traders learn to read price action —
    chunked pattern recognition replaces slow deliberate calculation
  - Discipline is as important as intelligence in execution

<!-- page 443: 4 nuggets (true count formula + density concept;
     Thorp system evolution ten/non-ten → granular; accuracy vs.
     complexity trade-off with practical implication; mental
     shortcuts for count tracking under pressure) -->

<!-- pp. 444-444 -->

## Blackjack Team Operations (continued interview)

### Skill Development Timeline

- [SHOULD KNOW][N005] **Skill acquisition curve for card counting**:
  initially requires substantial time investment; once mastered,
  maintenance drops to a couple of hours of practice before each
  casino trip
  - Implication: edge-based skills have high front-loaded learning
    costs but low ongoing maintenance costs — relevant to any
    systematic trading approach

### Team Testing and Entry Standards

- [SHOULD KNOW][N006] **Rigorous entry testing with remediation loop**:
  the team administered a **battery of ten different tests**; failing
  did not mean permanent exclusion — the interviewee was informed of
  knowledge gaps, practiced for approximately one month, retook the
  tests, and passed
  - Key mechanic: structured feedback → deliberate practice →
    retest; failure is diagnostic, not terminal
  - Parallel to trading: systematic review of what you got wrong,
    targeted practice, then re-entry

### Team Scale

- [NICE TO KNOW][N007] **Team size grew from 8 to ~20 members** over
  time — started small with tight trust relationships, scaled as
  operation proved viable

### Trust and Verification Mechanisms

- [MUST KNOW][N008] **Polygraph testing as institutional trust
  solution**: when the team combined bankrolls and players operated
  independently (making verification of results impossible from the
  outside), trust became a structural problem — solved in later stages
  by **mandatory polygraph tests**, both taken and administered by
  members including the interviewee
  - Early days: no polygraphs — relied on personal trust among a
    small group
  - Later stages: as team grew and stakes increased, informal trust
    was insufficient; polygraphs institutionalized accountability
  - Broader lesson: **when scale breaks informal trust, you need
    formal verification systems** — applies to any partnership where
    one party controls assets or information the other cannot
    directly observe (prop trading desks, fund structures, etc.)

<!-- page 444: 4 nuggets (skill curve, ten-test battery with remediation,
     team size 8→20, polygraph trust mechanism) -->

