## Blair Hull Interview (continued)

### Emotional Edge vs. Mathematical Edge in Poker/Trading

- [MUST KNOW][N005] **Mathematical edge is negated by emotional
  leakage** — even if you have a mathematical advantage, if opponents
  can read your emotions or you cannot read theirs, you lose the edge
  entirely
  - Mechanism: the psychological layer (bluffing, tells) sits on top
    of the mathematical layer; the math only pays off if emotional
    information is controlled
  - Evidence: Hull cites an unnamed mathematician who "has written
    some very good books on poker strategy" but has never been able
    to make money actually playing poker — intellectual mastery of
    the math without emotional/social mastery = losing player
  - **Bluffing** is described as "an essential element of the game"
    — not optional or peripheral
  - Trading implication: knowing theoretical value is necessary but
    not sufficient; reading counterparty desperation or concealing
    your own position is a real and separable skill

### Floor Traders and People-Reading

- [SHOULD KNOW][N006] **Successful floor traders profit partly by
  reading distressed counterparties** — Hull confirms that on the
  floor you can sometimes sense when another participant "is in
  trouble"
  - Mechanism: if a trader is offering at a quarter and you read that
    he needs to get out, you wait — knowing he will eventually reduce
    his offer to an eighth, improving your entry price
  - Hull's personal caveat: this was **not** an important element in
    his own trading — "most of the money I've made has been the
    result of being on the right side of the theoretical value"
  - Practical distinction: floor reading = exploiting others'
    emotional/positional weakness; Hull's edge = pure theoretical
    value arbitrage. Two different sources of edge, both real.

### Transition from Blackjack to Options Trading

- [SHOULD KNOW][N007] **Hull moved from blackjack to options while
  winding down blackjack involvement** — not a sudden pivot; the
  transition was gradual, beginning with work on option valuation
  models during the blackjack phase
  - By 1975 he was independently constructing an option pricing model,
    unaware that **Black-Scholes** had already published the canonical
    paper in **1973**
  - Significance: Hull reinvented core options pricing theory
    independently — demonstrates the depth of his quantitative
    grounding and that his edge was not borrowed from academic finance
    but self-derived

