## Options Trading — Relative Value & Model Pitfalls

### Relative Value as the Real Edge

- [MUST KNOW][N005] **Relative value over absolute model accuracy** —
  the key insight is that it doesn't matter which pricing model you use,
  as long as you apply it *consistently across all option prices*
  - The trader's concern is not whether the market is mispriced vs. the
    model in absolute terms, but whether **individual options within a
    specific market are mispriced relative to each other**
  - Method: adjust the model's volatility assumption so the
    **at-the-money implied price** matches the actual market price (e.g.,
    if model says ATM option = 3 but market trades at 3½, raise vol
    input until model also outputs 3½)
  - Once ATM is anchored to market, all other strikes should fall in
    line — any that don't (trading cheaper or more expensive than the
    adjusted model) represent **relative mispricings to trade**
  - Practical implication: buy options trading below the calibrated
    model value, sell those trading above — pure relative value, not a
    bet on whether implied vol is high or low in absolute terms
  - Early career note: interviewee says most of his early money was made
    in these intra-market relative value trades

### Fat Tails — The Core Flaw in Standard Option Models

- [MUST KNOW][N006] **Log-normal assumption understates tail risk** —
  most standard options pricing models (Black-Scholes and derivatives)
  assume stock/futures prices follow a **log-normal distribution**
  - Empirical reality: actual price distributions of virtually all
    financial markets have **fatter tails** than the log-normal
    distribution predicts
  - Consequence: standard models systematically **underprice far
    out-of-the-money options** (puts and calls) because they assign
    too little probability to extreme moves
  - This is a structural, persistent flaw — not a temporary market
    inefficiency — because the mathematical model itself is wrong about
    the shape of reality
  - Practical implication: traders who sell naked far OTM options
    believing they are "overpriced" per the model may be collecting
    insufficient premium for the true risk; buyers of deep OTM options
    may have a structural edge the model doesn't recognize

<!-- page 452: 2 nuggets (relative value ATM calibration method;
     fat tails / log-normal flaw in standard options models) -->

<!-- pp. 453-453 -->

- [MUST KNOW][N005] **Fat tails make deep OTM options systematically
  underpriced by standard models** — extreme price moves occur far
  more frequently in the real world than Black-Scholes and similar
  models assume; therefore deep out-of-the-money options are
  persistently **mispriced** relative to their true probability of
  expiring in-the-money
  - Practical implication: **buyers** of deep OTM options get
    better-than-model value; **sellers** are taking on more tail risk
    than they are being compensated for
  - Confirmed explicitly: "That's true — especially in potential
    takeover situations" (takeovers as archetypal fat-tail event that
    standard models cannot price correctly)

- [MUST KNOW][N006] **Selling deep OTM options = slow profits,
  catastrophic ruin** — the interviewee confirms he was making
  consistent money selling deep OTM options while trusting the model,
  but identifies the fatal flaw of the strategy explicitly:

> "That kind of strategy — selling deep out-of-the-money options —
> only leads to consistent profits until a catastrophe arises. Then
> you lose it all, plus some."

  - Mechanism: premium collection is small and steady; losses when a
    tail event hits are large and sudden, wiping accumulated gains and
    then some
  - This is a classic **negative-skew / short-gamma trap**: high win
    rate masks extreme downside; the strategy looks like alpha until
    it isn't
  - Key caveat: the interviewee acknowledged he was "consistently
    making money" doing this — meaning the danger is not immediately
    visible from P&L, which makes the trap especially seductive
  - Page ends on Schwager asking "Were you lucky not to hit a
    catastrophe using that approach?" — answer continues on next page

<!-- page 453: 2 nuggets (fat-tail mispricing of deep OTM options;
     catastrophic ruin from selling deep OTM options — consistent
     profits until total loss) -->

<!-- pp. 454-454 -->

- [MUST KNOW][N005] **Catastrophic loss from short out-of-the-money
  options in takeover situations** — two trades in 1981 wiped out
  approximately half of cumulative profits built over ~4.5 years of
  thousands of trades:
  - Trade 1: financed a trader on the **American Stock Exchange**
    selling out-of-the-money options in a takeover situation → lost
    ~one-third of capital in a single trade
  - Trade 2: short out-of-the-money call position in **Kennecott**
    (copper company) — position was relatively small but the overnight
    move was so enormous the loss was still substantial
  - Combined result: ~50% of total cumulative profits erased by just
    two trades
  - Key mechanism: **takeover events** create overnight gap moves of
    extreme magnitude — short out-of-the-money options that appeared
    safe (far from the money) become massively in-the-money
    instantaneously, with no opportunity to exit before the damage
  - Emotional note: handled first loss "very well" emotionally — but
    the second hit came only ~a week later, compounding the
    psychological and financial damage
  - [MUST KNOW] implication: **tail risk on short options is not
    theoretical** — even experienced, profitable traders can have years
    of gains erased by a single event-driven overnight move; position
    sizing and event exposure must account for gap risk, not just
    normal volatility

- [SHOULD KNOW][N006] **Performance context for the loss** — prior to
  these two trades, the interviewee had produced:
  - First two years: back-to-back **400 percent returns**
  - Years 3–4.5: averaged roughly **100 percent per year**
  - This context makes the "half my money" loss even more striking:
    it was not a novice mistake but a catastrophic event hitting an
    already highly successful, multi-year track record
  - Reinforces the theme that **even elite traders face ruin-adjacent
    events** and that survival (not being "taken out of the game") is
    itself a form of luck

> "I was lucky in hitting catastrophes that did not take me out of the
> game, even though that could have happened."

<!-- page 454: 2 nuggets (short OTM options takeover disaster — two
     trades wiped half of 4.5-year profits; Kennecott overnight gap;
     400% back-to-back years context) -->

<!-- pp. 455-455 -->

## Interview: Options Market Maker (continued)

### Resilience After Large Losses

- [SHOULD KNOW][N005] **Surviving big hits**: despite two major losing
  trades in 1981, the interviewee still ended the year with a **net
  profit** — underscoring that position sizing and diversification of
  edges can absorb severe individual losses without destroying annual
  performance

### Market Maker as Risk Transfer Agent

- [MUST KNOW][N006] **Market maker role = risk transfer, not
  speculation**: profits came strictly from exploiting **mispricings**,
  not from directional bets
  - Mechanism: speculators cluster on one side (e.g., buying
    out-of-the-money calls); institutions simultaneously operate on
    the other side (e.g., **buy writes** — selling long-term calls
    against long stock positions)
  - A smart market maker inserts between both flows: buys calls from
    institutions, sells calls to speculators, balancing the book so
    **net risk approaches zero**
  - Key insight: the edge comes from the **spread between mispriced
    implied volatilities** on each side, not from market direction —
    the market maker is indifferent to which way the stock moves

### Hedge Limitations in Takeover Situations

- [SHOULD KNOW][N007] **Takeover gap risk — the illusion of being
  hedged**: the interviewee tried to remain relatively hedged at all
  times, but flags a critical exception:
  - In a **takeover situation**, price gaps occur so suddenly that a
    position believed to be hedged becomes instantly unhedged
  - Practical implication: delta-neutral or ratio-hedged positions
    can become massively one-sided overnight when a gap-up takeover
    bid hits — no time to rebalance; this is the mechanism behind
    one of his two large 1981 losses
  - Lesson: **tail risk in options books is not fully hedgeable**
    when discontinuous price moves occur; gap risk is the residual
    exposure that survives all hedging frameworks

### Structural Buyer Bias in Options Markets

- [MUST KNOW][N008] **Speculators as structural option buyers —
  systematic mispricing**: question posed (answer on next page) frames
  a key market structure argument:
  - General public and speculators are **persistently net buyers** of
    options (calls and puts)
  - This demand imbalance may cause options to be **systematically
    overpriced** relative to fair value — creating a durable edge for
    disciplined sellers who can hedge the resulting delta exposure
  - This is the theoretical foundation for **volatility selling as a
    profession**: if human preference for optionality (lottery-ticket
    psychology) consistently inflates implied volatility above
    realized, the structural seller earns the volatility risk premium
    over time

<!-- page 455: 4 nuggets (net profit after big hits; market maker as
     risk transfer agent — mispricing edge, speculator vs institution
     flows; takeover gap risk — hedge illusion; structural buyer bias
     in options — systematic overpricing hypothesis) -->

<!-- pp. 456-456 -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N005] **Implied vs. historical volatility bias** —
  comparing historical graphs of implied volatility versus historical
  volatility across a spectrum of markets reveals a distinct tendency
  for implied volatility to be consistently higher than realized
  (historical) volatility
  - This pattern is not coincidental — it is structural evidence that
    options are systematically **overpriced** relative to what actual
    market movement delivers
  - Implication: the market persistently overestimates future
    volatility, creating a systematic edge for **option sellers**

- [SHOULD KNOW][N006] **Consistent option selling — edge vs. risk** —
  the speaker acknowledges there is a genuine edge to always being an
  option seller (given the implied-vs-realized volatility bias), but
  explicitly rejects it as a personal strategy because the **implied
  risk is too great**
  - Key distinction: having an edge and having an acceptable
    risk/reward profile are two separate questions — a strategy can be
    theoretically profitable in expectation but still be unacceptable
    due to tail-risk exposure (e.g., unlimited loss on naked short
    calls)
  - Bottom line stated directly: **the buyer of options has the
    disadvantage** — a clear, unhedged claim that options buyers are
    on the wrong side of the volatility premium on average

- [SHOULD KNOW][N007] **Takeover clues in options markets —
  pre-announcement signals** — in takeover situations, unusual options
  activity often precedes the public announcement:
  - Option suddenly trading **significantly beyond where it should be**
    given current fundamentals
  - **Unusual price moves** in the underlying stock
  - **Sudden increases in volume** in the underlying
  - **Jump in implied volatility** for out-of-the-money call options
    (the most sensitive instrument to takeover speculation)
  - These signals are used as warnings that informed buyers may be
    active — "there may be a wolf out there"

- [NICE TO KNOW][N008] **Wolf detectors** — named indicator systems
  used by certain traders to monitor markets for pre-takeover
  activity; combine the above signals (unusual price move, volume
  spike, OTM implied vol jump) into a composite warning flag
  - Regulators have begun monitoring similar patterns in recent years
    as part of insider trading surveillance
  - The speaker notes this as context but explicitly states it is
    **not his approach** — he does not rely on wolf detector signals

<!-- page 456: 4 nuggets (implied vol bias vs historical; selling edge
     vs tail risk; takeover pre-signals in options; wolf detectors) -->

<!-- pp. 457-457 -->

## Continuation: Options Trader Interview (Pacific Stock Exchange)

### High-Cap Stocks and Information Efficiency

- [SHOULD KNOW][N005] **High-capitalization stocks reduce surprise risk** —
  when trading individual stocks, the trader focuses on high-cap issues
  because information about them is already widely distributed in the
  marketplace; result: far fewer sudden, unexpected moves compared to
  small-caps
  - Practical implication: options traders who rely on relative value
    and volatility (not directional bets) prefer liquid, information-
    efficient underlyings where pricing surprises are rarer

### Directional Trades: Rare but High-Conviction

- [MUST KNOW][N006] **Infrequent directional trades = higher hit rate** —
  trader says he gets a strong idea for a directional trade only
  "maybe a couple of times a year"; crucially, "these types of trading
  ideas are infrequent, they're usually right"
  - Mechanism: selectivity filters out marginal ideas; only the highest-
    conviction setups survive to become actual trades
  - Implication: frequency of trading and quality of trades are often
    inversely correlated — professionals who trade less directionally
    tend to be more accurate when they do

### McDonnell Douglas DC-10 Calls: Contrarian Trade Example

- [MUST KNOW][N007] **DC-10 crash scare as contrarian opportunity** —
  while trading on the **Pacific Stock Exchange**, the trader bought
  thousands of calls in **McDonnell Douglas** during a period when
  multiple DC-10 crashes had generated public speculation that the
  aircraft would "never fly again"
  - His wife's reaction — "Those planes are never going to fly again.
    We're going to be broke." — captured the **climax of public fear**
  - Trader's read: maximum negative sentiment = maximum mispricing of
    calls; when nobody wants to touch the market, that is precisely the
    moment to step up
  - Outcome implied: the trade worked ("that trade taught me a lot
    about the marketplace")

> "When nobody wants to touch the market, that's the time you have to
> step up."

- [MUST KNOW][N008] **Wife-as-contrarian-indicator** — the trader credits
  his wife's horror at the position with teaching him contrarianism:
  "You could say my wife taught me to be a contrarian"
  - The anecdote illustrates a classic contrarian signal: when a trade
    is so scary that even a trusted non-market observer is certain of
    ruin, the fear is likely already fully priced in — and the actual
    downside is lower than perceived
  - Related principle: **climax of fear** (maximum bearish consensus)
    is often a better buy signal than any technical indicator, because
    it marks the point where sellers are exhausted and upside surprises
    become asymmetric

<!-- page 457: 4 nuggets (high-cap info efficiency; infrequent
     directional trades = higher accuracy; DC-10/McDonnell Douglas
     contrarian call trade; wife-as-contrarian-indicator / climax of
     fear principle) -->

<!-- pp. 458-458 -->

## 1987 Crash — Trading in the Pit the Day After

- [MUST KNOW][N005] **Going long during peak panic — October 20, 1987
  (day after the 508-point Dow crash)**
  - Context: due to pervasive fear + increased clearing firm capital
    requirements, the trader couldn't find anyone to execute orders in
    the **Major Market Index (MMI)** on the **Chicago Board of Trade**
    — was forced to go to the pit and trade himself
  - Heard rumors **CME** was considering calling a **trading halt**
    on the S&P 500 futures contract — a drastic, rare action
  - Sent desk to research historical trading halts; after ~30 minutes,
    no information found — sensed halt was imminent
  - Decision: instructed desk — *"Make sure that we're long on any
    trading halt"*
  - **Reasoning**: "The fear was all out of proportion to reality. I
    had to be a buyer." — embodiment of the liquidity-provision
    philosophy: always try to provide liquidity to the market
  - **Execution**: After the Merc halted trading, a commission house
    broker was trying to get a bid on a 100-lot sell order; market
    was at 290 with nobody bidding any size
    - Bid 285 → bought 100 lots; minutes later bought another 50 at
      285 — the ONLY trades transacted at 285
    - Market **closed at 400 that day** — massive intraday recovery
  - **Result**: bought the absolute low of the crash-aftermath session

- [MUST KNOW][N006] **The hindsight challenge — Schwager's follow-up
  question (page ends mid-Q&A)**
  - Schwager challenges the "fear out of proportion to reality"
    rationale: couldn't the same logic have justified going long the
    PREVIOUS day when the Dow collapsed 500+ points?
  - This is a critical epistemological trap for contrarian/panic-buying
    strategies: **how do you distinguish genuine panic overshoot from
    rational repricing of a fundamentally changed situation?**
  - Answer not yet captured on this page — continues on next page
  - Practical implication: the rationale "fear is excessive" is easy
    to apply in hindsight but requires a specific edge or framework
    to act on in real-time; the trader's answer to Schwager's
    challenge will reveal what that edge was

<!-- page 458: 2 nuggets (1987 crash pit trading — going long at MMI/
     S&P halt, bought 285 closed 400; Schwager challenge on
     distinguishing panic overshoot from rational repricing) -->

<!-- pp. 459-459 -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N005] **Gulf War trade — going long into uncertainty**:
  the interviewee went long the stock market on the morning of
  **January 15, 1991** — the day of **Bush**'s original midnight
  ultimatum deadline to **Hussein**
  - Consensus view: market would drop 150 points if war started
  - Contrarian reasoning: "How bad can this war be?" — the trader
    focused on **uncertainty reduction** as the key variable, not the
    war's direction per se
  - Strategy: put on **half the position** just before the deadline
    expired, and **the other half after the war had actually started**
  - Mechanism: uncertainty itself suppresses markets; once war begins,
    uncertainty diminishes regardless of outcome — so being long was
    the correct posture as the event resolved
  - Catalyst specificity: **CME was going to halt trading**, adding
    time-pressure urgency to the entry timing

- [NICE TO KNOW][N006] **"Line to God" anecdote** — the trader had
  previously criticized one of his arbitrage traders for wanting to
  take a net long position, saying "What do you think, you have a
  line to God?" — implying conviction about unknowable macro outcomes
  is arrogance
  - His head trader then booked the Gulf War long in the **LTG
    account** ("Line to God") as a joke at the boss's expense
  - Self-aware irony: the trader recognized he was doing exactly what
    he had criticized — making a directional macro bet on an
    unknowable event — but justified it via the uncertainty-reduction
    logic rather than outcome prediction

<!-- page 459: 2 nuggets (Gulf War long trade — uncertainty reduction
     rationale, half-position entry strategy, CME halt catalyst;
     "Line to God" account name anecdote — self-aware irony on macro
     conviction) -->

<!-- pp. 460-460 -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N005] **Directional trades are a small fraction of
  total profits** — the interviewee (context: options/arbitrage firm)
  notes that a dramatic S&P 500 directional trade (~$4 million profit
  in ~30 minutes) accounted for only ~**5 percent** of the firm's
  total trading profits
  - Core methodology remains: **buying undervalued securities and
    selling overvalued securities** — a relative-value, not
    directional, approach
  - Practical implication: spectacular one-off directional wins can
    distort perception of what actually drives a firm's edge; the
    bread-and-butter is systematic mispricing capture

- [MUST KNOW][N006] **Blackjack edge philosophy applied to trading**
  — interviewee explicitly ties firm's methodology back to the
  blackjack analogy: if you have the **edge**, in the long run you
  make more money by doing **a lot of transactions**
  - Volume of edge-positive transactions compounds the advantage;
    this is why high-frequency relative-value approaches dominate over
    infrequent directional bets
  - Mechanism: law of large numbers — edge expressed over many
    trades converges to expected value; few trades = high variance
    even with positive edge

- [SHOULD KNOW][N007] **Strategy evolution: speed and complexity
  replace simple arbitrage** — strategies have shifted from the basic
  concept of "buy cheap options, sell expensive ones" toward
  much faster execution and far more complex structures
  - Driver: competition — easier mispricings get arbed away as more
    players enter; surviving requires running faster than competitors
  - Quote captures the competitive dynamic:

> "This is always a horse race, and unless you're running very fast,
> they're going to catch you."

  - Implication for retail/individual traders: the simple
